HT_DATA.load("detail", {"dBnniua6-oM":{"wy":"The most-linked YouTube video on HN, recommended as an in-depth explanation of fructose metabolism.","q":{"x":"This is one of the best nutritional videos I've ever seen.","i":1056898,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":26239385,"author":"akurzon","month":"2021-02","story":"Fructose reprogrammes glutamine-dependent oxidative metabolism to support LPS","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed explanation of fructose metabolism and the obesity epidemic","quote":"I'd highly recommend these two talks by Robert Lustig on the subject.","text":"For anyone interested in a detailed explanation of fructose metabolism (and why it's bad for you), I'd highly recommend these two talks by Robert Lustig on the subject. The first one is about how the body reacts to fructose, and the second video is a follow-up that emphasizes how fructose has contributed to the obesity epidemic in the US and elsewhere. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":14573720,"author":"maxharris","month":"2017-06","story":"Coconut oil isn't healthy and it's never been healthy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Leading endocrinologist challenging dietary dogma on fructose","quote":"here's a leading endocrinologist that's overturning decades of dogma.","text":"If this article is correct, please explain why my blood pressure and cholesterol figures go through the roof when my diet includes fructose. Please also explain how and why everything goes back to normal when I cut fructose out entirely. I am an quite serious - I do not allow even trace amounts in sauces and mayo and the like, nor in hidden sources such as \"agave nectar\" (which is 50-90% fructose). Oh and BTW, I eat TONS of pasta, saturated fat (including butter), plants, and a really nice steak every month. I pour heavy whipping cream into my coffee every morning (not the usual trash, but the fancy expensive stuff free of polysorbate 80 and carrageenan.) Unless you're eating a fresh strawberry that hasn't been mutilated in a blender (and even then, not too many all at once), fructose makes you fat because it messes with your appetite regulation in the bad direction. Even a little fructose makes you hungry. Dietary fat (saturated or otherwise) does the exact opposite: all other things being equal, it makes you feel sated. Don't just take my experience over the last ten years into account - here's a leading endocrinologist that's overturning decades of dogma. He'll set you right: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13282603,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2016-12","story":"US government to withdraw longstanding warnings about cholesterol (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how sugar, not fat, drives arterial damage","quote":"I highly recommend Dr. Lustig's video (UCTV) to learn more","text":"I agree with you. This article also mentions they're focusing on fats/saturated fats; which is also a terrible focus. The real danger is not fat, even saturated/trans-fats, but starches and sugar. The increase in these types of carbohydrates in products like baby formula is actually leading to obesity in infants! Excess sugar can stress arteries and damage them. Cholesterol is carried to these artier by lipids (LDLs and HDLs, which are not actually cholesterol, but proteins that carry it through your bloodstream). If you eat a lot of sugar, cholesterol can build up between the walls of arteries as they fix the damage. They can eventually break down the wall, releasing plaque into the bloodstream and potentially causing heart complications. I highly recommend Dr. Lustig's video (UCTV) to learn more: [VIDEO] A few years back I switched to a keto normal fat/very low carb (excluding fiber) diet. I've lost weight, gained energy and have felt a lot better. Of course this isn't for everyone. Some people have a body type that lends itself to higher carb intake (I knew a runner who'd eat 30 bananas a week) and as always do your research, consult with your doctor, dietitian, etc. before drastic changes in your diet. It's a common myth that Adkins died of his own diet. He didn't, and Reuters rededicated their column implying that he did. I honestly wonder if the obesity epidemic would have gone down today if the world didn't dismiss his ideas, or the ideas of many nutritionist for decad"},{"id":6038554,"author":"colinprince","month":"2013-07","story":"High-fructose corn syrup prompts considerably more weight gain than sugar (2010)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains endocrinology of why bodies cannot keep up with sugar intake","quote":"See Robert H. Lustig's awesome expostion, Sugar: The Bitter Truth","text":"I think the point is that getting rid of the subsidies would let the price of HFCS increase and would be less attractive to food producers. Regarding the idea that sucrose is healthier than HFCS, I remain unconvinced. See Robert H. Lustig's awesome expostion, Sugar: The Bitter Truth: [VIDEO] He explains the endocrinology behind why our bodies cannot stay ahead of our sugar intake."},{"id":1056898,"author":"vixen99","month":"2010-01","story":"The Men Who Live Forever","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Peer-reviewed case for cutting out sugar","quote":"This is one of the best nutritional videos I've ever seen.","text":"If you want some peer-reviewed background on why it's smart to cut out sugar and the foods to which it's added by manufacturers, do take a look at [VIDEO] This is one of the best nutritional videos I've ever seen. There's a chunk of biochemistry in the middle but it's worth watching to the end! Don't miss it and add years to your life."},{"id":25078612,"author":"identity0","month":"2020-11","story":"Most-mentioned links in Hacker News comments, 2006–2015","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk, surprisingly the most-linked YouTube video on HN","quote":"It does have 11M views, however, and it is a great talk.","text":"I watched [VIDEO] a few days ago and was surprised to see is the #1 youtube link. It does have 11M views, however, and it is a great talk."},{"id":24773051,"author":"brian_cunnie","month":"2020-10","story":"Wim Hof’s teachings about breath work and the health benefits of cold plunges","stance":"rec","why":"Expert describes sugar metabolic pathways; fructose turned to fat by liver","quote":"Go to an expert: Dr. Robert Lustig, Endocrinologist at UCSF.","text":"Don't take my word for it (how bad sugar is). Go to an expert: Dr. Robert Lustig, Endocrinologist at UCSF. \"Sugar, the Bitter Truth\" [0]. He'll describe the metabolic pathways of sugar. Key take-away: fructose is processed by the liver, and much of its calories is turned to fat. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18795425,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-12","story":"The $35B race to cure a silent killer that affects 30M Americans","stance":"rec","why":"Compares metabolism of alcohol and fructose","quote":"Watch how the metabolism of alcohol and fructose compare","text":"For starters, stop ingesting all things that have fructose in them. The U.S. government is really kind of complicit in allowing the spread of diabetes and NASH to happen in the first place by allowing harmful substances such as high-fructose corn syrup and to be infused into anything you can find in a store or fast food. Sure, it's ultimately an issue of education, but when the vast majority of foods have this stuff in them, educating every person on non eating most of the available foods in stores becomes very difficult. Watch how the metabolism of alcohol and fructose compare: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14547328,"author":"ibejoeb","month":"2017-06","story":"The Liver: A ‘Blob’ That Runs the Body","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on sugar metabolism with real biochemistry","quote":"Despite the baity titles, it's a good talk with real biochemistry.","text":"Robert Lustig does a good talk about sugar metabolism in \"Sugar: The Bitter Truth\": [VIDEO] Also, \"The Trouble with Fructose\": [LINK] Despite the baity titles, it's a good talk with real biochemistry. Edit: better video added up top"},{"id":13736946,"author":"08-15","month":"2017-02","story":"DNA tests show Subway sandwiches could contain just 50% chicken","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed explanation of fructose versus glucose metabolism","quote":"Most of it is uncontroversial.","text":"This is almost right, but still manages to be dangerously wrong. Frustose and glucose are very different, one never gets converted to the other. All cells can and will take up and metabolize glucose, but only the liver can import and metabolize fructose. Metabolically, fructose does not behave like a carbohydrate, but like alcohol. Insulin affects mostly fat, muscle and liver cells. They react by storing the excess glucose, which they do harmlessly. Excess glucose does not cause insulin tolerance; fructose seems to do that, thought I don't remember the whole mechanism. Neither chitin nor lignin are carbohydrates, lignin isn't even chemically related. Even ruminants can't digest lignin, only a few funghi can do that. At any rate, the bottom line is that frustose is a problem, glucose isn't, and lumping both under \"carbs\" confuses matters. See [VIDEO] for lots of details. (But don't take Dr. Lustig's words at face value---check other sources. Most of it is uncontroversial.)"},{"id":10035882,"author":"pkulak","month":"2015-08","story":"Coca-Cola Funds Scientists Who Shift Blame for Obesity Away from Bad Diets","stance":"rec","why":"Video that started much public awareness of sugar issue","quote":"Here's the video that started a lot of the public awareness of the issue","text":"Here's the video that started a lot of the public awareness of the issue: [VIDEO] And there's also now a great documentary on Netflix called \"Fed Up\" ( [LINK] ), for anyone who has a subscription. It particularly tackles this silly idea that it's okay to eat and drink shitty food all day, so long as you exercise for a little bit too."},{"id":4892970,"author":"dools","month":"2012-12","story":"Why We Get Fat","stance":"rec","why":"Informative talk on sugar's impact on humans","quote":"This is a relevant and very informative video about the impact of sugar on humans","text":"This is a relevant and very informative video about the impact of sugar on humans: [VIDEO] I'd say that if you were to just dramatically reduce sugar you'll get results pretty quickly."},{"id":1219187,"author":"yanowitz","month":"2010-03","story":"Princeton researchers find that HFCS prompts considerably more weight gain","stance":"rec","why":"Long presentation on fructose metabolism and fat storage","quote":"If you want to watch a long presentation on what's going on, check this","text":"This is further confirmation of a well-known phenomenon. If you want to watch a long presentation on what's going on, check this: [VIDEO] Short version: Fructose is metabolized by your liver, results in a lot of nasty byproducts, your liver stores the excess energy as fat, etc."},{"id":38833684,"author":"pengaru","month":"2024-01","story":"What happens when you quit sugar? (2023) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Lustig's argument that fructose is poison like alcohol","quote":"Fructose is metabolized by the liver[0] ~identically to ethanol.","text":"> Fructose is usually considered healthier than sucrose or glucose - the whole \"natural fruit sugars are OK\" premise - though that seems likely to be nonsense given it's immediately and easily converted to glucose after you eat it. I don't know of any argument that says it's poisonous, or in any sense worse than glucose. That also seems unlikely to be true for the same reason - it's immediately converted to glucose. Fructose is metabolized by the liver[0] ~identically to ethanol. This is why Robert Lustig (the Sugar: The Bitter Truth[1] dude) argues fructose is a poison, and taxing it like alcohol. AIUI it's arguably the cause of non-alcoholic fatty liver disease: [LINK] Your comment strikes me as confusing several factors... fructose does not turn into glucose upon eating it. But something like that is applicable to Sucrose vs. HFCS, rendering them ~identical in consumption (a split of glucose and fructose). That's orthogonal to what the body does with the fructose though. Furthermore it's intact fruit that is seen as healthier because of the dietary fiber, not because of the fructose. The fructose is always a problem for the liver to deal with when absorbed. Fiber helps by slowing/preventing its absorption, in the best case letting it reach the large intestine to turn into flatulence via guttural flora before the body can absorb it. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35068343,"author":"orangepurple","month":"2023-03","story":"Fake sugars sneak into foods and disrupt metabolic health","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim fructose and ethanol share metabolic pathways","quote":"Fructose and ethanol follow the same biochemical metabolic pathways and are equally harmful.","text":"Fructose and ethanol follow the same biochemical metabolic pathways and are equally harmful. You just don't get buzzed from fructose. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12614665,"author":"theorique","month":"2016-09","story":"Saturated fats no longer the true enemy, experts say","stance":"reference","why":"Lustig as an authority speaking out against added sugar","quote":"Robert Lustig - Professor and pediatric endocrinologist at UCSF who speaks out against added dietary sugar.","text":"There have been several documentaries about this issue in the past few years: - Sugar Coated (2015) - That Sugar Film (2014) - Fed Up (2014) Some of the authorities who are found in these movies are: Robert Lustig - Professor and pediatric endocrinologist at UCSF who speaks out against added dietary sugar. See: [VIDEO] Gary Taubes - journalist and author of Good Calories, Bad Calories: Challenging the Conventional Wisdom on Diet, Weight Control, and Disease and Why We Get Fat: And What to Do About It Yoni Freedhoff - Medical doctor from Ottawa, Canada who criticizes the food industry's use of sugar. See: [LINK] It seems like a case is building, much like the case against the tobacco industry. Maybe there will be class-action lawsuits against soft drink and processed food companies based on recent suppression of potentially life-saving research."},{"id":10937593,"author":"poelzi","month":"2016-01","story":"A Former Dentist Uncovering Sugar's Rotten Secrets","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that fructose is metabolized like alcohol","quote":"Fructose is actually much more poisonous then glucose is.","text":"It is very important to distinguish between fructose and glucose sugars. Fructose is actually much more poisonous then glucose is. It seems metabolized mostly like alcohol. Now, with the high fructose corn syrup everywhere, makes the situation not better. [VIDEO] EDIT: fructose is not so problematic if it's still embedded in fibers like in fruit. Bacteria then eat most of it, before it is absorbed."},{"id":6049801,"author":"rfnslyr","month":"2013-07","story":"Is Sugar Really Toxic?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on sugar's effects, shared for opinions","quote":"I'd love to hear some opinions on the following talk from fellow HNers","text":"Man. Sugar is the worst. Take it from me. I moved to the city the moment I turned 18, I didn't know much about cooking. Back in the 'burbs you'd be lucky to live within 10km of a grocery store. Downtown however, totally different story. I went NUTS with sweets and pizza. I gained over 100 lbs in just under half a year. I was eating around 10k calories a day. I then stuck to only these foods and have only been eating this the past couple years: chicken lean beef kale + other greens various fruit cheese That's it. No seasoning, nothing. I'm so much happier now. Better sleeps, better mood, better everything. My mood and days fluctuated like crazy when I didn't keep track of my diet. I'd love to hear some opinions on the following talk from fellow HNers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1707609,"author":"rsheridan6","month":"2010-09","story":"'Corn Sugar' Makers Hope You'll Buy The New Name","stance":"reference","why":"Lustig argues HFCS and sugar are equally harmful in typical quantities","quote":"he says HFCS is no worse than sugar","text":"In the anti-sugar video by Donald Lustig, pediatric endocrinologist, that's been posted here a million times, he says HFCS is no worse than sugar - they're both terrible for you in the quantities people consume these days. This makes sense because they're chemically essentially the same thing (sucrose is a disaccharide of glucose and fructose, whereas HFCS is mix of glucose and fructose monosaccharides in proportions that may slightly skew towards fructose or glucose depending on the formulation. The disaccharides of sucrose are broken up in the body anyway, so in the end there's no clinically significant difference. Lustig didn't think the difference was important). If there's anything misleading going on here, it's that people think that \"real\" sugar is OK. I guess it'll give you natural diabetes. Obligatory link to the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1551885,"author":"zackattack","month":"2010-07","story":"Ask HN: Please, help me understand what I am doing wrong.","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture on evidence that sugar harms health","quote":"There is a lot of evidence that sugar is bad for you.","text":"There is a lot of evidence that sugar is bad for you. See [VIDEO] Nassim Taleb says carbs are one of the 3 most dangerous addictions, besides a paycheck and heroin. Obviously some carbohydrate is necessary and good for you but the less the better. Trader Joe's has these kickass \"a complete salad\" kits in their refrigerated produce section that I highly recommend. They have great nutritive value, taste great, aren't too expensive, and I feel aMaZiNg hours after eating them."}],"r":[["ceFyF9px20Y",2],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1],["uI1eRy0uBI8",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1],["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["-HFwok9SlQQ",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1],["WTYet-qf1jo",1],["tk862BbjWx4",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["lr4MmmWQtZM",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1]]},"otbnC2zE2rw":{"wy":"It is the go-to talk HN commenters recommend to anyone asking about bootstrapping, SaaS pricing, or choosing bootstrapping over VC.","q":{"x":"This talk is the holy grail on this topic","i":26688695,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":37438225,"author":"satvikpendem","month":"2023-09","story":"Every Software Project Is a Startup That Will Probably Fail","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Free resource on designing a bootstrapped business.","quote":"video on designing a bootstrapped business","text":"I looked into that book, looks like it's really for venture backed companies trying to form a founding team. I'd recommend that you instead look up micro-SaaS, where you just target making $10k+ monthly recurring revenue and be set, rather than going on the VC treadmill and trying to raise capital, hire a team, etc which is often overkill for first time founders. Rob Walling coined that term initially through his conference on it called MicroConf, he has a new book called [LINK] which I'd recommend. His older book Start Small Stay Small is also quite good. But to start off with a free resource, Jason Cohen's video on designing a bootstrapped business can't be beat: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36432230,"author":"mtlynch","month":"2023-06","story":"Biggest mistakes to avoid to build a better 1-person business","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Better version of the ideas, from someone who grew four businesses past $1M.","quote":"personal experience growing four businesses to over $1M/yr in revenue","text":"I think the better version of these ideas is in Jason Cohen's classic \"Designing the Ideal Bootstrapped Business\" talk.[0, 1] The difference is that Jason Cohen's advice is based on personal experience growing four businesses to over $1M/yr in revenue. I'm not seeing what qualifies this author to give such broad, sweeping advice about bootstrapping. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":32747950,"author":"robocat","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: Is it okay to just bootstrap it, even when VCs are knocking?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Solid bootstrapping advice drawn from the speaker's own experience.","quote":"Jason Cohen has an amazing talk on bootstrapping","text":"Jason Cohen has an amazing talk on bootstrapping, with some very solid advice from his own experiences: [VIDEO] Personally I think there is a lot of “kudos” for getting VC funding, so founders often get lured just for status reasons, joining the competition to become a unicorn. Unless you want to (or need to) “swing for the fences”, why give up ownership? Being a founder is already hella risky, and accepting VC money increases your leverage and your risk, which is just uneconomic for an average individual to do (unless they are already wealthy). Don’t double down on a risky bet. VCs get preferential shares, and they have a lot of different ways of taking control in the clauses of the contracts you sign which disempower you, and in return you get shitty common shares and often lose control for the worst reasons. I helped found a small business, and we retained full ownership which worked out great for us, although we will never get those mystical 7/8/9 figure payouts. The majority of founders only make wages[1]. We were in an incubator during our inception and I saw a lot of businesses get sidetracked by all the funding bullshit, control issues, stress, wasting time chasing investors, trying to follow conflicting or bad advice. If you do want to chase funding, consider applying for [LINK] which will help avoid you getting shafted. Other VC transactions are highly negotiation information asymmetric, because they have done many many transactions and you are doing your first, so "},{"id":31981864,"author":"philmcc","month":"2022-07","story":"The New American Micro-SaaS Dream","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Actionable bootstrapping advice the commenter followed to real success.","quote":"great, actionable, talk he did at the equally informative","text":"Jason Cohen is one of my favorite people speaking in this space. Here’s a great, actionable, talk he did at the equally informative Rob Walling’s conference. [VIDEO] (I have followed this advice, and it has brought me a level of success and comfort I didn’t know I could have, as an admittedly run of the mill developer)"},{"id":27133546,"author":"stevoski","month":"2021-05","story":"Show HN: Csvbox.io – CSV import button for a web app, SaaS or API","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recommends $49/$99/$249 pricing tiers and avoiding cheap plans.","quote":"classic and awesome talk","text":"A good place to start with pricing is tiers at $49/$99/$249. (As recommended in Jason Cohen’s classic and awesome talk “Designing the Ideal Bootstrapped Business”: [VIDEO] ) Customers at very low price points (such as $9/month) tend to churn quickly, and, counterintuitively, send more customer support interactions per customer than at higher points. You’ll never earn enough from them to cover your customer acquisition costs. Getting rid of the cheap plan also removes the, shall we say, more difficult customer."},{"id":26663483,"author":"chdaniel","month":"2021-04","story":"Ask HN: Where do you find potential customers to validate your idea / MVP?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Scratch-and-claw bootstrapping and 150 customers instead of 1000 true fans.","quote":"I highly highly recommend watching the video","text":"A couple of things need to be balanced, I think, as I'm doing just that with PriceUnlock ( [LINK] ) PriceUnlock will help SaaS owners finds the perfect pricing for their product, with more upside and less downside when trying new prices. Besides, it helps you stop leaving money on the table. * THE THINGS* 1. You can \"scratch and claw\", as Jason Cohen (founder of WPEngine) puts it. He talks more about this in the YT link below and he also puts out this idea of 150 customers, not \"1000 true fans\". I highly highly recommend watching the video, regardless of going VC/bootstrapped. He also talks about 2 other ways besides scratching and clawing [VIDEO] 2. Building in public is an upward trend these days. Basically you share as many milestones/ups/downs about your journey of building it. This can be a trap as well: it only works if your audience is A) interested in you sharing that, B) probably hackers/builders etc. I asked PG about this and he pretty much summed it up: [LINK] 3. As I'm building PriceUnlock, you'll also notice that I'm collecting emails (the ol' way) to get people's interest. I don't want to make a mailing list, I want to build my product, so I'll aim to be better doing the second rather than the first. But it doesn't hurt to, as Rob Walling did with Drip, launch with some MRR hopefully... or at least with some interest. [LINK] 4. The usual set of things hackers do when they launch their thing: ProductHunt, Betalist, etc. Don't forget to join my subreddit on [LINK]"},{"id":26688695,"author":"yoshyosh","month":"2021-04","story":"My experience releasing failed SaaS products","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ideal bootstrapped business traits: niche b2b, recurring cycles, $99 minimum pricing.","quote":"This talk is the holy grail on this topic","text":"This talk is the holy grail on this topic [VIDEO] niche b2b, w/ natural recurring cycles, $99 price point at a minimum, and not needing to be on call is most ideal"},{"id":26208916,"author":"shoo","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: Have you taken on debt to fund your startup?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jason Cohen on optimising SaaS cash flow with annual prepay.","quote":"you MUST watch Jason Cohen","text":"In Australia our major commercial banks offer unsecured loans to businesses based upon the business' cash flow. Example eligibility requirements: turnover of at least $75K per year ; have been operating as current registered business for 1 year+ ; you can provide 12 months of business financial data from your accounting software. You'd need to convince the bank that your business is a relatively reliable and sustainable cash generating machine. Example terms: 9% p.a. interest rate, borrow up to $250k, no fees. Another trick you can do, orthogonal to finance, is the annual-prepay trick. Offer the \"sign up for 12 months at a X% discount\" plan to customers. If you can get some fraction of customers to pay you for a 1 year subscription in advance at e.g. a 30% discount that might really help the business' cash flow. Edit: if your SaaS business is the one linked in your profile then since you are not currently offering your customers an annual prepaid plan then you MUST watch Jason Cohen's talk about optimising SaaS cashflow from his 2013 microconf talk \"Designing the Ideal Bootstrapped Business\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":34132623,"author":"grepLeigh","month":"2022-12","story":"1k True Fans (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Argues a few high-paying customers beat 1,000 true fans.","quote":"great talk by Jason Cohen","text":"This is tough to give general advice for, because you'll want to tap your personal strengths. Here's a great talk by Jason Cohen, if you're trying to design a bootstrapped business. He even mentions the 1,000 true fans goal as being unrealistic compared to a handful of high-paying customers: [VIDEO] Happy to brainstorm though. Here's a few questions to get the ball rolling: What're 2-3 things that people have come to you to learn? What are the 3 problems that are top-of-mind for the person who signs your paychecks? What are the top 3 sources of revenue for your current employer? How much is from expansion revenue (existing customers paying more over time) vs. new logos? Besides the all-in cost of personnel (salary, payroll taxes, health insurance, etc), what are the top expenses of your current employer? Follow the money! Once you start digging into how a company earns and spends money, you'll find ways to add revenue or reduce spend w/ software."},{"id":29315950,"author":"higgins","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How do you get started earning money on opensource?","stance":"rec","why":"Centering talk on building a bootstrapped business.","quote":"this talk is pretty centering","text":"This might be tangential bc it's not about strictly OSS, but this talk is pretty centering: [VIDEO] Patio11's talks at MicroConf too"},{"id":28861517,"author":"mostlystatic","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: Traits of a Desirable Business?","stance":"rec","why":"General traits of a desirable bootstrapped business.","quote":"discusses some of general traits of a desirable bootstrapped business","text":"There's a MicroConf talk from 2013 that discusses some of general traits of a desirable bootstrapped business: [VIDEO] (Notes here: [LINK] ) Regarding exits, I'd say low technical complexity also helps[1]. If your whole business is built on top of Wordpress it's probably easy for an acquirer to find someone to maintain it. If the product is complex and needs regular updates then they might need to hire an experienced full-time dev, so you need more revenue to make up for that. Obviously there's a trade-off, and a lot of custom tech also allows you to differentiate. Update: you might also find this book/podcast helpful [LINK] [1] applies to the typical bootstrapped business, there are also high-tech businesses that exit pre-revenue"},{"id":26825223,"author":"147","month":"2021-04","story":"Ask HN: My startup has more annual subscribers than monthly. What to make of it?","stance":"rec","why":"Why annual prepayment plans are great for bootstrapped SaaS.","quote":"Jason Cohen goes into why annual prepays are great for bootstrapped SaaS.","text":"Please watch this video at this time stamp: [VIDEO] Jason Cohen goes into why annual prepays are great for bootstrapped SaaS."},{"id":22202301,"author":"patio11","month":"2020-01","story":"My Second Year as a Solo Developer","stance":"rec","why":"Step-by-step approach to building a bootstrapped software business.","quote":"the best paint-by-numbers approach to it","text":"Two cents offered because this post really strikes a chord with me, and I also spent some time chasing down rabbit holes early in my software business: These products do not appear obviously commercializable and multiple years invested into them without materially improving the businesses does not decrease my confidence in that snap judgment. You could probably talk to business owners with problems, launch an (appropriately priced; hundreds to thousands of dollars per year) product against those problems, build contingent on getting 10 commits to buy, and be at +/- $100k in 12 to 18 months. Many people with less technical and writing ability have done this in e.g. the MicroConf community. If you want the best paint-by-numbers approach to it I've seen, c.f. [VIDEO] (I'll note that What Got Done is probably a viable boutique SaaS business if you somehow figured out distribution for it, at price points between $50 and $200 per month. My confidence on this approaches total. HNers skeptical because it is technically trivial should probably reflect for a moment on how much time is spent on standups at a company with 20 or 200 engineers, what one hour of their time is worth, and how likely that company is to put an engineer on this project specifically.) If you run an API-based business in the future: Usage-based billing is a really tricky business model for a solo developer. Note that you can say \"Usage-based billing but we have a minimum commitment\", and probably should prior to d"},{"id":41875182,"author":"robocat","month":"2024-10","story":"Is Matt Mullenweg defending WordPress or sabotaging it?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Jason Cohen understood the tradeoffs of using private equity versus bootstrapping.","text":"> he made a company backed by Private Equity look like a victim Jason Cohen understood the tradeoffs of using private equity versus bootstrapping. He goes into the decision in more depth elsewhere, but at 44s into this video about avoiding private equity he alludes to his decision: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41674006,"author":"authorfly","month":"2024-09","story":"WordPress Drama: From the Sidelines","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"rightfuly pointing out good fundamentals of business for bootstrapped developers","text":"I disagree with Matt's actions, but not why he is truly compelled to take action. It's been pretty clear for a long time that WP engine was founded to be a maximally profitable business model to fill in the security/reliability gap, with the founder identifying this as why he went into the business: [VIDEO] (note he doesn't mention Open Source there or extraction of value - he's just rightfuly pointing out good fundamentals of business for bootstrapped developers). He mentions how $50 or $100 a month is much better than $5 or $10 a month - and that leaves a bad taste in your mouth when you realise WP Engine still charges that today, a little. Where this comes to the sleazy part is that: A) WP Engine has kept prices the same or increased over the years B) But WordPress has became more secure and a lot of what WP Engine used to uniquely do they no longer do C) WP Engine contributes significantly more to sponsoring conferences seats, and PR spend, then actual contributions I think the combination of these things is what actually drove Matt to make his move: A) Awareness that the original founder made WP Engine to maximize profit; which Matt naturally feels relatively deprived of as a competitor B) The behavior of sponsoring conferences, but not giving back in WP Engine C) Rumors of suppression/avoiding working on Wordpress Core by WP Engine managemnet, which Matt seems to have genuinely been taken by (perhaps a few true WP engineers did reach out - no evidence that I've seen) D)"},{"id":41115864,"author":"Jugurtha","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: Book recommendations to improve XFN and engineering leadership as an IC","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The sort of skills that would eventually allow me to run my own company/team/org. These are different things. Running your own company more often than not implies you have started the company, and the skills you need to start and run a company are different in and of themselves, let alone the fact they're different from running a team in an established company/organization where you never think about how the power is still on. In smaller companies and startups, the opportunity to learn is yours if you want it and the incentive to do so is survival. The company is small enough that you can, if you're so inclined, be involved in and learn about everything. I answered a few questions on some topics you might find relevant: [LINK] There are a few replies there about communication and alignment. More ( [LINK] [LINK] ) A FAANG is so large that the acronym is just five letters, and procuring whiteboard markers to them could be the biggest contract another company ever had. What you want is to expand the spectrum of your involvement or \"ownership\". One way of doing that is to learn to sell into the enterprise. Imagine you are a startup and you want to sell your product to [insert your company]. How would you go about it? It teaches you many things: What problem does it solve? For whom is it a problem? Why is it a problem? Why now? Are they aware it is a problem? Have they tried solving that problem? What outcome and why wasn't it solved? What does winning look like (success criteri"},{"id":37364183,"author":"Jugurtha","month":"2023-09","story":"Ask HN: We're about to enter into a partnership with a company / Advice needed","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> We have one client already using our finished parts of the platform. They're paying us $250 monthly. Nice! I hope you realize that you're in a position few attain: someone paying you, and $250 at that. I wouldn't think in terms of the \"finished parts\" of the platform, more users will bring insights into what's worth implementing and what's not. There's already a user who's getting enough value to pay you $250 monthly. Please watch Gabriel Weinberg's (DuckDuckGo's founder) short videos, starting with this one (Number 10, Leaky Bucket): [LINK] Here's the unordered playlist: [LINK] I also recommend his book, \"Traction\", in which he goes over several channels you could test to get traction. You proved that you had someone pay. The next step is to have many pay. How did you get your first user? How did they sign-up and how did they pay? Has someone else signed for that purchase/subscription or was it self service? Did you walk them through/onboard them? How much did it cost you? Where did they come from? How can you attract similar users (same sector, same function/role)? Is the user willing to recommend the tool to their network of peers? Does the user go to a conference/events to meet with peers (same role/function or adjacent)? Would it be interesting to target that? I recommend David Skok's resource ' \"Zero to 100\" for High Growth SaaS – A workshop on how to build your Go-to-Market': [LINK] You're at step 3 (Prove it can be sold), the next step is to \"Find repeatable sales m"},{"id":30682666,"author":"shoo","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: What percent of my subscribers should be monthly vs. annual?","stance":"reference","why":"Annual-prepaid cash flow trick, starting around 13:20.","quote":"the annual-prepaid cashflow trick is discussed by Jason Cohen","text":"the annual-prepaid cashflow trick is discussed by Jason Cohen in this ancient 2013 microconf presentation, starting at around the 13:20 mark : [VIDEO]"},{"id":28374751,"author":"simonw","month":"2021-08","story":"Startup Bootstrapping Plan","stance":"reference","why":"Hour-long 2013 MicroConf talk that a Twitter thread summarizes.","quote":"summary of this hour-long MicroConf presentation by Jason Cohen from 2013","text":"The Twitter thread is a summary of this hour-long MicroConf presentation by Jason Cohen from 2013 [VIDEO] (Not intended as a criticism: summarizing an hour long talk into a Twitter thread is super-useful)"},{"id":22203056,"author":"mtlynch","month":"2020-01","story":"My Second Year as a Solo Developer","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thanks for reading, Patrick! I'm a big fan of your work, and your Indie Hackers interview was one of the big motivators that sent me down the solo developer path. > You could probably talk to business owners with problems, launch an (appropriately priced; hundreds to thousands of dollars per year) product against those problems, build contingent on getting 10 commits to buy, and be at +/- $100k in 12 to 18 months. Many people with less technical and writing ability have done this in e.g. the MicroConf community. If you want the best paint-by-numbers approach to it I've seen, c.f. [VIDEO] Thanks, I'll check out that video! I hear that advice a lot from people I respect, and I put a lot of effort into that over the past year, but I was never successful. The biggest problem I ran into was that there's this \"disconnect problem.\" I know I'm a competent developer and can build niche solutions to $10k problems, but I don't know which companies have $10k problems. I can guess at it, but they sometimes don't even realize their $10k problem has a software solution. The companies also don't have incentive to talk to me to explore the possibility if I'm just a developer off the street. In 2019, I tried to do this with stone quarries[1], sheet metal shops[2], and email copywriters[3], but the first two mostly wouldn't talk to me and the latter didn't seem to have enough opportunity for a niche business. > I would encourage you, to the maximum extent compatible with your sanity, to priorit"}],"r":[["wSdV1M7n4gQ",1],["ia7IKW0yuG0",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["plVk4NVIUh8",1],["u-x7PdnvCyI",1],["Jv7sLrON7QY",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["Mjx3S3UjmnA",1],["xqGAC5QCYuQ",1],["DEcwa68f-jY",1],["KZeIEiBrT_w",1],["6reLWfFNer0",1],["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["BTHvs3V8DBA",1],["cfwwHa-7Ux8",1],["Fy0aCDmgnxg",1]]},"F-QA2rkpBSY":{"wy":"A perennial HN recommendation for building intuition about exponential growth, cited whenever endless growth, energy or population come up.","q":{"x":"stick with it; it's really captivating and interesting","i":3165176,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":44828299,"author":"stouset","month":"2025-08","story":"The Sunlight Budget of Earth","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Riveting Bartlett lecture, less hyperbolic than its title","quote":"which is less hyperbole than you might think; the lecture is riveting","text":"From Tom Murphy's excellent Do the Math blog[1]. Only 70% of the incident sunlight enters the Earth’s energy budget—the rest immediately bounces off of clouds and atmosphere and land without being absorbed. Also, being land creatures, we might consider confining our solar panels to land, occupying 28% of the total globe. Finally, we note that solar photovoltaics and solar thermal plants tend to operate around 15% efficiency. Let’s assume 20% for this calculation. The net effect is about 7,000 TW, about 600 times our current use. Lots of headroom, yes? When would we run into this limit at a 2.3% growth rate? Recall that we expand by a factor of ten every hundred years, so in 200 years, we operate at 100 times the current level, and we reach 7,000 TW in 275 years. 275 years may seem long on a single human timescale, but it really is not that long for a civilization. And think about the world we have just created: every square meter of land is covered in photovoltaic panels! Where do we grow food? Seriously, if you haven't read his take on things yet, at least the first few posts are a must-read. It's on par with the Arithmetic, Population, and Energy lecture at UC Boulder by Al Bartlett (popularly titled \"The Most Important Video You'll Ever See\", which is less hyperbole than you might think; the lecture is riveting)[2]. To very TL;DR things: solar and tidal energy (and their derivatives like wind) are essentially the only sources of energy we can rely on as our energy requirem"},{"id":20255973,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2019-06","story":"The poisons released by melting Arctic ice","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Famous Bartlett lecture on limits of endless growth","quote":"Exponential growth is unintuitive, and chasing endless growth is not going to work out well for us.","text":"The bacteria are mentioned in Albert Bartlett's famous lecture here: [VIDEO] It's clickbait titled and split into 8 parts, for who knows what reasons, but that quote is the tl;dr version of it. Exponential growth is unintuitive, and chasing endless growth is not going to work out well for us. Even if the bacteria find another entire jar of open space - as much space as has ever been known, as much space as they have used in their entire history - a fully terraformed Mars hanging out in the back yard previously unseen - it only lasts a single doubling-time."},{"id":3123577,"author":"mark_up","month":"2011-10","story":"What is the Limit to Human Population Growth?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing Bartlett lecture on population and energy","quote":"It blew my mind when I first watched it two years ago","text":"Anyone really interested in this should watch Albert Bartlett's lecture \"Arithmetic, Population, and Energy\". It blew my mind when I first watched it two years ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3165176,"author":"tristanperry","month":"2011-10","story":"You are the nth person alive on earth","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Captivating series on how small growth rates double quickly","quote":"stick with it; it's really captivating and interesting","text":"Not sure if you've seen this series: [VIDEO] (despite looking/sounding boring at the start, stick with it; it's really captivating and interesting), but it is amazing just how much 'small' growth (even 2-3% p/a) can cause things to double in much less time than you'd imagine. Or if you haven't got the time to watch the whole thing (is close to an hour all in all), this touches on a couple of the topics the video covers: [LINK] (naturally doubling every minute is on a much faster scale to the Human population!)"},{"id":2841610,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2011-08","story":"Galactic-Scale Energy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bartlett's sobering lecture on arithmetic, population, energy","quote":"I strongly recommend it","text":"If you haven't previously seen Dr. Barrett's \"Arithmetic, Population, and Energy\", I strongly recommend it: [VIDEO] It doesn't address quite the same scale, but is very sobering."},{"id":20067667,"author":"sametmax","month":"2019-06","story":"The degrowth movement wants to intentionally shrink the economy","stance":"rec","why":"Math-oriented talk supporting degrowth argument","quote":"I'll post a fantastic math oriented conf on the topic","text":"I'm amazed it's such a sensitive topic. Some people are litterally angry in the comments out people suggesting degrowth. Since we have math lovers here, I'll post a fantastic math oriented conf on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11167158,"author":"porkradish","month":"2016-02","story":"This is the most depressing chart in the world","stance":"rec","why":"Video on unsustainable percent growth rates","quote":"Interesting video","text":"This \"growth rate\" measured in percent has always concerned me. It looks to be unsustainable. Interesting video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7633534,"author":"rasz_pl","month":"2014-04","story":"ARIN down to 1.00 /8 – Akamai got 104.64.0.0/10 yesterday","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why fractional solutions cannot save us from exponential growth","quote":"Watch this clip to understand why fractional solutions wont save us","text":">some fairly massive blocks that were allocated but unused Watch this clip to understand why fractional solutions wont save us : [VIDEO] Warning, its \"Perhaps the most boring video you'll ever see, and definitely the most important.\""},{"id":3700039,"author":"merkat","month":"2012-03","story":"The Rule of 72","stance":"rec","why":"Bartlett lecture on doubling time calculations","quote":"There's an interesting lecture from Albert Bartlett","text":"There's an interesting lecture from Albert Bartlett [ ] on the same subject, but he uses the value of 70 to calculate the years to double. [ ] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2201621,"author":"wazoox","month":"2011-02","story":"Lisp inventor John McCarthy on human progress and its sustainability","stance":"rec","why":"Bartlett videos use arithmetic to counter McCarthy","quote":"it makes quite a good usage of arithmetic to counter McCarthy point of view.","text":"Looks interesting. You should submit it on its own. In fact combined with The prof. Bartlett videos ( [VIDEO] ) it makes quite a good usage of arithmetic to counter McCarthy point of view."},{"id":1758758,"author":"SkyMarshal","month":"2010-10","story":"How Fake Money Saved Brazil","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent Bartlett lecture on exponential growth implications","quote":"has an excellent lecture on its implications for population growth, environmental degradation, and other big picture problems","text":"Great rule, should be the very first thing taught in high school when you get to exponents. Dr. Albert Bartlet of University of Colorado has an excellent lecture on its implications for population growth, environmental degradation, and other big picture problems: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1783449,"author":"cletus","month":"2010-10","story":"The middle class is over. It's not coming back.","stance":"rec","why":"A video suggesting population will eventually fall","quote":"Well worth watching.","text":"To temper this pessimism with some sobering reality, on another HN thread today I was pointed to this: [VIDEO] Well worth watching. Somehow, some time the population is going down. Way down."},{"id":1302247,"author":"dflock","month":"2010-04","story":"Peak Everything?","stance":"rec","why":"Bartlett lecture on inability to grasp exponential function","quote":"The Greatest Shortcoming of the Human Race is our inability to understand the exponential function","text":"There's only one planet Earth, finite in size - and therefore a finite amount of natural resources exist within it. Once we've split all the uranium and washed all the phosphorus into the ocean, that's it - you can't think up more Zinc, Rubidium or Lithium - once it's gone, it's gone. I'm sure we could do vastly more recycling, use alternatives for some things, improve efficiency, develop magical new technologies, etc, etc... but almost no-one I've spoken to about this appreciates just how terrifyingly rapidly we're approaching these limits. If we run out of available mine-able Zinc & Copper in 30 years - which is quite possible - what then? Do we have enough time to switch to coping strategies? Will that be a smooth ride? It seems to me that the front of the train has already hit the buffers, but we've still got our foot on the gas pedal, back here in the cab. Most of the useful mineral resources on Earth are only available in vanishingly tiny quantities elsewhere in the solar system. We currently do almost no recycling of anything, compared to our consumption. The human population is huge, growing in number, affluence and resource usage per capita. We're on course to run out of the minerals required to run our economy & infrastructure within most of our lifetimes. Nice infographic with some estimated timescales for various resources: [LINK] The Greatest Shortcoming of the Human Race is our inability to understand the exponential function: [VIDEO] I'm not being alarmist - so"},{"id":9953566,"author":"itistoday2","month":"2015-07","story":"Why slow thinking wins","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Instantly knew the answer thanks to having watched","text":"1. The impulse to say 10 cents was there, but solving the equation led to the right answer. 2. Got this right in a few moments by thinking about it (no math). 3. Instantly knew the answer thanks to having watched \"the most important video you'll ever see\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":4893682,"author":"grannyg00se","month":"2012-12","story":"The Most Dangerous Equation [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function","text":"Excellent. Reminds me of the lecture describing that \"the greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":3410658,"author":"bfrs","month":"2011-12","story":"A Googleplex for $1000 by 2040?","stance":"reference","why":"Mentioned as topic about underestimating exponentials","quote":"","text":"As Tyler Cowen points out in \"The Great Stagnation\", maybe these first 20 doublings or so were the low hanging fruit. Kurzweil keeps saying that people underestimate exponential processes (its also the topic of \"The most important video on math\" [1]), but I think it cuts both ways. It is by no means clear that the next 20 doublings will proceed at the same rate as the last 20. People might just say, \"Siri via the cloud is good enough for me, I don't need Siri running directly on my phone\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2905924,"author":"EGreg","month":"2011-08","story":"Women are rejecting marriage in Asia. The social implications are serious.","stance":"reference","why":"Longer version on population growth","quote":"Longer (but more boring) version","text":"Yes, an aging population might be worrisome on the surface, but I find overpopulation more worrying. Therefore, I am happy that people in really populated countries are having less babies overall! [LINK] Longer (but more boring) version: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2527757,"author":"carsongross","month":"2011-05","story":"Why Not All Earnings Are Equal; Microsoft Has the Wal-Mart Disease","stance":"reference","why":"Supports skepticism of endless exponential growth","quote":"Your discomfiture is well placed","text":"Your discomfiture is well placed: [VIDEO] The world is mostly logistic curves, but the ponzi needs us to all believe in exponential curves."},{"id":2199998,"author":"sseefried","month":"2011-02","story":"Lisp inventor John McCarthy on human progress and its sustainability","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on how fast exponential growth escalates energy use","quote":"Exponential functions grow very fast.","text":"I have read through these pages. Undoubtedly many of the things he states, particularly with respect to how long nuclear energy could supply us, are correct but only under the assumption that we don't continue to increase our usage of energy. It's hard to see how that assumption holds in a world that is continually growing its economies. Exponential functions grow very fast. (See [VIDEO] ) You may then counter that a decoupling of economic growth from its material and energy underpinnings is possible. This has yet to be effectively proven. We have only seen partial decoupling so far. For more information on this see the writings of Tim Jackson in his book Prosperity without Growth. I also urge you to download this spreadsheet (from the BP website) showing the growth in usage of fossil fuels ( [LINK] ). We may find alternatives to these but if we do, we will have to bring the alternatives online at roughly the same level to supply our societies with the energy they now require to function. This is no easy feat. It's good know, quantitatively, just what's required."},{"id":2159071,"author":"cletus","month":"2011-01","story":"Predictions for the World in 2030","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"My thoughts: 1. IMHO learning a second language will largely be no longer necessary because English will increasingly be the lingua franca. That being said, on the fly translation will clearly continue to get better. 2. 150 year life expectancy? This one I don't buy. Medicine here has to be differentiated in two areas: curing and preventing the causes of \"premature\" death (eg disease) that stop us reaching a \"natural\" age when we die. That lone won't get us to 150 years. We're probably not far off that limit now. To get to 150 we need to extend the age to which humans can live, which is getting into some fairly serious genetics. I can see such technology coming about but IMHO will be the province of the wealthy for some time and will probably take longer than expected (again IMHO). 3. I disagree with this. Despite all the efforts to stop poverty, even in developed nations, there is a segment of the population who remain poor. It might be controversial but a certain segment (IMHO) are poor and remain poor because they make poor choices. Also, the definition of \"poor\" constantly changes. Poverty in the developing world is lack of access to basic health care, food and clean drinking water. In the developed world, poverty seems to mean your iPhone is two generations old. And yes I realize there is true poverty in the developed world. There are people who are homeless but the scope of \"poverty\" in such discussions extends way beyond those when we're discussing social policy. 4. Po"}],"r":[["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1],["uxykI30fS54",1]]},"f84n5oFoZBc":{"wy":"HN returns to it constantly as permission and method for thinking deeply before coding, and as an antidote to always-working anxiety.","q":{"x":"Both of these talks are in my top 5.","i":11183901,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11183901,"author":"hire_charts","month":"2016-02","story":"Rich Hickey Fanclub","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In the commenter's top five talks.","quote":"Both of these talks are in my top 5.","text":"That and Hammock Driven Development: [VIDEO] Both of these talks are in my top 5. Edit (okay, here are the other 3): * All the Little Things by Sandi Metz [LINK] * Programming is terrible—Lessons learned from a life wasted. [LINK] * Some talk I saw by Dan McKinley on making Data-Driven decisions. I can't find the original video, but here's a really similar one: [LINK]"},{"id":29592532,"author":"the-alchemist","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Are most of us developers lying about how much work we do?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk arguing deep thinking time pays long-term dividends for projects.","quote":"Rich Hickey, of Clojure fame, has a talk you might find interesting.","text":"Rich Hickey, of Clojure fame, has a talk you might find interesting. It goes by multiple titles: * Step Away from the Computer * Hammock-Driven Development [VIDEO] The idea / hypothesis is that sometimes we need to sit down and think about something for an hour, a day... This seemingly \"wasted time\" can pay huge dividends in long-term health of a project. Like compound interest. As others have noted, this may seen \"lazy\" to managers, especially if LOC or sprint velocity is the metric they use."},{"id":23725844,"author":"bhntr3","month":"2020-07","story":"Ask HN: Did you take any fresh MOOCs as of 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"Rich Hickey talk on programming and design, suggested as stimulating alternative to courses.","quote":"Rich Hickey ( ) for programming and design","text":"In terms of mind-expanding or intellectually stimulating, I find it's easier to get that from Youtube. I recommend: 3blue1brown for math ( [LINK] ) Everyday Astronaut if you are casually interested in rockets ( [LINK] ) Bret Victor ( [LINK] ) or Rich Hickey ( [VIDEO] ) for programming and design. Doing a full college course is such a time investment and it usually takes a few weeks to start proving the interesting stuff. I find it more fun to look to shorter content for inspiration. I might realize I don't understand the fundamentals of the topic and then seek out a way to study it."},{"id":23271401,"author":"jugjug","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: How does one becomes a pro at Software Engineering","stance":"rec","why":"Overarching first principles for design and teamwork.","quote":"These are overarching first principles that help in designing and writing code","text":"I think a good starting point is to integrate the principles described in the Simple Made Easy[1] & Hammock Driven Development[2]. These are overarching first principles that help in designing and writing code, but also in communication & team work. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22402171,"author":"sgentle","month":"2020-02","story":"Ask HN: I feel like I should be working all the time and its killing me","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey as an antidote to constant-work anxiety.","quote":"I'd point you in the direction of two of my favourite Richards.","text":"The feeling you're describing is called anxiety. Some anxiety is normal, but constant anxiety that interferes with your life is a problem. I think it'd be worth trying that label on: call it anxiety, and ask \"how do I reduce my anxiety?\". About 1 in 4 people experience an anxiety disorder at some point in their lives, so there's a lot of research and good approaches out there. One thing that stands out to me is that you've made some pretty big and scary claims without a lot of evidence to back them up. Are you actually not a good developer? Are smart people really \"capturing\" the opportunity (ie there is less opportunity over time)? Do people who aren't working all the time fall behind and never make anything of significance? If so, prove it! Figure out what evidence you would need to definitively answer these questions, and research or experiment until you have that evidence. Anxious thoughts are like dreams: they seem perfectly reasonable in your head, but when you try to bring them into the real world they fall apart. As far as evidence goes, I'd point you in the direction of two of my favourite Richards. The one who did Clojure: [VIDEO] and the one who got a Nobel for Quantum Electrodynamics: [LINK]"},{"id":17429724,"author":"valw","month":"2018-06","story":"Readme-Driven Development (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Stay away from the computer; ask what the problem is.","quote":"Stay away from the computer\" - Start by asking: \"what is the problem?","text":"This makes me think of Rich Hickey's Hammock Driven Development: [VIDEO] - \"Stay away from the computer\" - Start by asking: \"what is the problem?\""},{"id":15389075,"author":"digaozao","month":"2017-10","story":"Streams: a new general purpose data structure in Redis","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on thinking long about problems.","quote":"A good talk about It, from the creator of clojure","text":"I like this kind of development also. But I don't think it is for everyone (the person has to keep thinking on the problem for long time). A good talk about It, from the creator of clojure: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13347222,"author":"udfalkso","month":"2017-01","story":"Lecture on Creativity (1991)","stance":"rec","why":"Work hard, then take a break to find solutions.","quote":"I found a lot of parallels with this \"Hammock Driven Development\" talk by Rich Hickey","text":"I have loved this talk for some time and have forced everyone at my company to watch the video. I found a lot of parallels with this \"Hammock Driven Development\" talk by Rich Hickey: [VIDEO] They both seem to suggest that you have to put in the effort, do the hard work and dig into the problem deeply. But then when you hit a wall, you should take a break. Relax. Play. Sleep. It is often during this \"open\" minded stage when the solution comes."},{"id":11897819,"author":"abc_lisper","month":"2016-06","story":"Ask HN: How many programmers out there keep a paper notebook for their projects?","stance":"rec","why":"Convinced the commenter to use notebooks.","quote":"I am ever more convinced of using one because of Rich hickey's talk","text":"I was searching for exactly the same stuff yesterday, and I found this: [LINK] I usually keep notebooks, but I am ever more convinced of using one because of Rich hickey's talk: [VIDEO] and we have a better chance of remembering something if we write than type. [LINK] . I have previously read that people like Martin Gardner had an awesome system of keeping notes. I would love to know more about your tips. How do keep your notes? How do you organize stuff, while retaining the freedom to doodle. How do you find stuff again(which is the killer feature for typed notes). Would love to learn from the masters - do you have any books to recommend?"},{"id":11107519,"author":"dyadic","month":"2016-02","story":"Building a Startup in 45 Minutes per Day While Deployed in Iraq","stance":"rec","why":"Rich Hickey on thinking time away from keyboard.","quote":"I recommend watching him talking about it here","text":"This is very similar to what Rich Hickey (creator of Clojure) talks about when he talks about Hammock Driven Development, I recommend watching him talking about it here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9210820,"author":"bjwbell","month":"2015-03","story":"Stanford study shows success of different programming styles in CS class","stance":"rec","why":"Helps avoid getting stuck on problems.","quote":"is IMO very good for actually not getting stuck in a bad place problem solving","text":"\"don't get stuck\" reminds me of the feynman method of problem solving: 1. Write down the problem. 2. Think very hard. 3. Write down the answer. Rich Hickey's Hammock Driven Development, [VIDEO] , is IMO very good for actually not getting stuck in a bad place problem solving."},{"id":8257907,"author":"cageface","month":"2014-09","story":"The End of Agile: Death by Over-Simplification","stance":"rec","why":"Rich Hickey on thinking before coding.","quote":"Personally I prefer Rich Hickey's \"Hammock Driven Development","text":"This is a classic and hilarious series of posts on the foolhardiness of the TDD approach to software design: [LINK] Personally I prefer Rich Hickey's \"Hammock Driven Development\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":41286505,"author":"juunpp","month":"2024-08","story":"The brain simulates actions and their consequences during REM sleep","stance":"reference","why":"Hammock-driven development linked to brain connecting ideas during sleep.","quote":"Hammock-driven development is a thing","text":"Hammock-driven development is a thing: [VIDEO] I think anyone can experience this even if you're unaware of the science behind it. The brain during sleep can also connect seemingly disconnected things in ways that your daily logical process would have a lot of trouble with. That's why things seem to \"click\" when you wake up from sleep. If your daily thinking is a gradient descent, your sleep thinking is a flood from all points and in all directions. But I don't have any science refs on this; just my experience."},{"id":40393352,"author":"tomjakubowski","month":"2024-05","story":"Third Time: a better way to work (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Cites hammock driven development alongside personal habit of walking to let problems simmer.","quote":"When I worked in an office I'd get up and go on walks","text":"Hammock driven development [VIDEO] When I worked in an office I'd get up and go on walks, or move to a sofa and let the problem simmer a bit, several times a day. I've been fortunate to work in companies which seem to, I think, recognize these are productive activities"},{"id":34892113,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2023-02","story":"Creation happens in silence","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies Hickey's well-known Clojure/Conj 2010 talk by that name.","quote":"He gave a well-known talk with that name","text":"He gave a well-known talk with that name - Hammock Driven Development , Rich Hickey, Closure Conj 2010 [VIDEO]"},{"id":33326592,"author":"stoeckley","month":"2022-10","story":"What “work” looks like","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey's hammock-driven development cited as parallel to thinking being most of work.","quote":"Rich Hickey talks about this, calling it","text":"Rich Hickey talks about this, calling it \"hammock-driven development.\" [VIDEO] Aaron Sorkin has also touched on this: > Most of the time, me writing looks—to the untrained eye—like someone watching ESPN. The truth is if you did a pie chart of the writing process, most of the time is spent thinking. When you’re loaded up and ready to go—when you’ve got that intention and obstacle for the first scene that’s all you need. For me at least, getting started is 90% of the battle. The difference between page zero and page two is all the difference in the world."},{"id":30003140,"author":"rektide","month":"2022-01","story":"The Parable of Two Programmers (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Mentioned among related submissions about time and commitment.","quote":"","text":"What a tale. This is such a key insight, such a core lesson on how the world tends to work. > At his next salary review Charles was given a raise which was about half the inflation over the period. He was not given a promotion. After about a year he became discouraged and left Consolidated. To be fair to Alan, spinning up an organization & knowledge system is real hard work. Legitimizing your projects is real hard work. But there's such tension over who steers, over how the work happens, and there's so many really good solid workers who slip through the cracks & never get appreciated. The corporate body is so often so very far away from the workers on the edge, sees the faults and problems, incompetent at appreciating and respecting many of the best acts done for it. Thanks for the share. This is a great article. I see a lot of relationships to other submissions. Jonathan Blow talking about work-life balance has some relationship here, on how we exert ourselves ( [LINK] [LINK] ). The \"midlife crisis of a programmer\" seems ultra-pertinent ( [LINK] [LINK] , 2 comments). \"Hammock-driven development\" made the rounds against ( [VIDEO] [LINK] ). All of these deal with questions of time-base & commitment & application, all of these feel like they could be informed by The Parable. Worth pointing out that the parable is reportedly from (1985)! It's somewhat confirming to hear the struggles of the lowly engineer to get work done & fit in are not new."},{"id":21554626,"author":"greenyouse","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: Does being practical act as a hinderance to one’s imagination?","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence against anxious thinking about productivity.","quote":"","text":"Nobody's stupid because they're not sure about something. It's totally cool to ask for help or opinions when you're thinking through a hard problem. To answer your question you should understand what creativity and practicality mean in your context. I would generally think that being practical and creative aren't at odds with each other. When working through a problem for work I tend to err on the side of practicality. We could be coming up with a new way to do data transformations, building out distributed tracing tools, or trying a new way of scaffolding our projects. Each of those solve a real problem and have practical implications. They also require creative thinking to imagine and design new tools and workflows. These projects are practical in the sense that they need to solve an actual problem (e.g. deliver a net benefit to customer UX or development velocity). Contrary to how they sound, they often aren't obvious projects to take on or straight-forward solutions. It usually required days or weeks of lateral thinking to cook up one of these. In this context, creative thinking complimented practical thinking and produced a better result than one of those modes taken in isolation. Sometimes it's helpful to circle around the problem you think you want to solve to better understand what your actual problem is. Be careful reading other's ideas since it will affect the way in which you see the problem. Your view of the problem may be skewed after reading other information. O"},{"id":38550249,"author":"fuzztester","month":"2023-12","story":"Why Is Jepsen Written in Clojure?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"if clojure is mentioned, can rich hickey be far behind?","text":"sorry guys, i don't know clojure, so i'll write this script in my favorite programming language: #/usr/bin/english if clojure is mentioned, can rich hickey be far behind? and if rich is mentioned, can this video of his be far behind? methinks not. lo, and behold, said video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9348275,"author":"lgas","month":"2015-04","story":"Rich Hickey's Clojure Bookshelf","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is a reference to Rich's talk \"Hammock Driven Development","text":"This was a joke, the only out of stock item on the list is the Hammock at the bottom. This is a reference to Rich's talk \"Hammock Driven Development\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["4jh94gowim0",1],["Tb823aqgX_0",1],["Uj6skZIxPuI",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["6Af6b_wyiwI",1],["csyL9EC0S0c",1],["8bZh5LMaSmE",1],["u6XAPnuFjJc",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1]]},"hUhisi2FBuw":{"wy":"It is a go-to recommendation whenever cans, good design or mass-produced objects come up, and many also recommend the whole channel.","q":{"x":"Whenever it’s about cans I can only recommend this video from the engineer guy","i":42789273,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":41911619,"author":"Gunax","month":"2024-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite YouTube video, ever?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite YouTube video","quote":"Design of the aluminum beverage can","text":"Design of the aluminum beverage can [VIDEO] For everyone else, thanks for sharing! Normally i think sharing videos is bad mojo--the Internet is so personalized that favorites are difficult to share. But I really enjoyed most of what has been shared so far!"},{"id":36894551,"author":"anamexis","month":"2023-07","story":"Wavy walls use fewer bricks than a straight wall (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great engineering explainer","quote":"All of his work is fantastic.","text":"Engineer Guy (Bill Hammack) has a great video about this. [VIDEO] Edit: Just realized this is the same video you referenced. All of his work is fantastic."},{"id":26046970,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2021-02","story":"I told a cat food company how much they’d save by using taller cans (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ingenious can design","quote":"Highly recommended related YT video","text":"Highly recommended related YT video: The Engineering Guy: The Ingenious Design of the Aluminum Beverage Can [VIDEO]"},{"id":12028304,"author":"Laforet","month":"2016-07","story":"The Chemical History of a Candle","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Engineering Guy explains design and manufacture of mundane objects","quote":"the Engineering Guy has a really good youtube channel.","text":"While we are at it, the Engineering Guy has a really good youtube channel. His videos are not very frequent most of them does a great job explaining how mundane objects are designed and manufactured. I thought I was familiar with some of his subjects but I always end up learning something new after watching. Personal favourites: Aluminium cans: [VIDEO] Wind-up music box: [LINK] Nerf guns: [LINK]"},{"id":11881715,"author":"MawNicker","month":"2016-06","story":"The Most and Least Expensive Cars to Maintain","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains can neck and two-mode pull tab","quote":"That was an awesome video.","text":"Wow. That was an awesome video. Thank you. I didn't know why the top had a neck. Further efficiency apparently. He has an excellent explanation of the two-mode pull tab towards the end. Direct link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42789273,"author":"larusso","month":"2025-01","story":"What are these bumps on the top of a pull-tab can?","stance":"rec","why":"Go-to video about cans","quote":"Whenever it’s about cans I can only recommend this video from the engineer guy","text":"Whenever it’s about cans I can only recommend this video from the engineer guy [1]. He makes no videos anymore sadly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29362224,"author":"cjtoth","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: What are the best-designed things you've ever used?","stance":"rec","why":"Aluminum cans as example of quality design","quote":"aluminum cans are a great example of quality design","text":"It feels pretty disingenuous to write off all real world design as trivial or impossible to make an impression. For instance, aluminum cans are a great example of quality design [VIDEO]"},{"id":28917584,"author":"jihadjihad","month":"2021-10","story":"Why soda bottles have gaps in their threads (2000)","stance":"rec","why":"How the can tab opens under pressure","quote":"Very clever and elegant solution to the problem of opening a can under pressure.","text":"In a similar vein, I found this explanation [0] of how and why a soda can's tab functions the way it does to be fascinating. Very clever and elegant solution to the problem of opening a can under pressure. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21661139,"author":"leobakerhytch","month":"2019-11","story":"Boeing 777X’s fuselage split dramatically during September stress test","stance":"rec","why":"Design and manufacturing of drink cans, tab as lever","quote":"Nice video on the design and manufacturing of aluminum drinks cans here","text":"Nice video on the design and manufacturing of aluminum drinks cans here: [VIDEO] The whole thing is pretty ingenious, but the way the internal pressure helps vent the can while the tab is acting as a second class lever, then as soon as it’s vented becomes a first class lever to push the scored section of the top into the can — that’s great engineering."},{"id":46614031,"author":"kentiko","month":"2026-01","story":"There's a ridiculous amount of tech in a disposable vape","stance":"reference","why":"Can as sophisticated mass produced object","quote":"it is still quite a sophisticated mass produced object.","text":"The complexity of a can isn't as extreme as a disposable ARM chip, but it is still quite a sophisticated mass produced object. [VIDEO] Many daily life, single use objects have a lot of thoughts put into them: [LINK]"},{"id":41392056,"author":"ForHackernews","month":"2024-08","story":"Can solar costs keep shrinking?","stance":"reference","why":"Can design refinements save aluminum","quote":"refinements in the design of soda cans (thinner walls, different shapes) since 1960 saves \"at least 90 million kilograms of aluminum annually.","text":"It's represented everywhere that efficiency counts. I haven't fact-checked it, but one source[0] claims that refinements in the design of soda cans (thinner walls, different shapes) since 1960 saves \"at least 90 million kilograms of aluminum annually.\" [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36399923,"author":"xoxxala","month":"2023-06","story":"Titanic tourist sub goes missing sparking search","stance":"reference","why":"Includes clip of someone standing on a can","quote":"has a video on the design and engineering of the aluminum can with a brief clip of someone standing on a can","text":"Bill Hammond, the Engineer Guy on youtube, has a video on the design and engineering of the aluminum can with a brief clip of someone standing on a can (around the 7-8 minute mark). [VIDEO]"},{"id":15349078,"author":"leon_sbt","month":"2017-09","story":"Show HN: Shelf – A better way to share knowledge across teams and organizations","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The site looks great and good job on shipping it out! I probably don't fit as your target customer, so please take my feedback with a grain of salt. After digging around your site for a bit, it seems like your \"special sauce\" is to bring incredibly powerful search features for multiple data types/sources all from a single authoritative place (Shelf.io) Based on your YouTube videos, I really like the ability to parse rich data on the internet and input it for my team to see. Seems like a great product marketing/engineering folks during the high level brainstorming phase. Use case: 1.Software Engineer: Reads a cool article on Medium about some software technique and wants their team to consider it for their their new beta project. 2.Marketing person: Gets inspired by a video/image for a new ad campaign that they are designing and wants to share it with their team. Also as an end user, I really like being able to see your actual product and how it works without needing to signup or give out my information. If the product seems like it can solve my pain point within 45 seconds of an intro video and 5 mins of playing with a demo site, then I'll commit to a email signup. Basecamp's \"How it works\" tab does a great job of this. On the flip side, I feel that Salesforce doesn't need to do this, since they already have market share and a great reputation for their products. I would take your \"Intro to Shelf\" YouTube video, cut the first 25 seconds out and embed it somewhere convenient o"}],"r":[["3-son3EJTrU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["HxSHIpEQRjs",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["Ai2HmvAXcU0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1],["XetplHcM7aQ",1],["pUF5esTscZI",1],["doz1QJ7LwjA",1]]},"-zRN7XLCRhc":{"wy":"HN points to it as the definitive, entertaining account of Sun's fate under Oracle, and quotes its Ellison lawnmower advice whenever Oracle behaves badly.","q":{"x":"If you're interested how it came to be check out this epic talk by Bryan Cantrill","i":13082671,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":22544985,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2020-03","story":"Oracle's history highlights a possible downside to its stance on API copyrights","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Commenter rewatches the Larry Ellison lawnmower rant regularly","quote":"it's once again time to re-watch the \"Never make the mistake of anthropomorphising Larry Ellison\" rant.","text":"Whenever I hear Oracle mentioned it reminds me that it's once again time to re-watch the \"Never make the mistake of anthropomorphising Larry Ellison\" rant. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16839529,"author":"antoncohen","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"SunOS/Solaris history then a brutally honest rant against Oracle","quote":"goes into a brutally honest rant against Oracle.","text":"\"Fork Yeah! The Rise and Development of illumos\" by Bryan Cantrill [VIDEO] He starts with a nice history of SunOS and Solaris, goes into open source, then midway through (33:00) he goes into a brutally honest rant against Oracle. Even better is that Oracle was one of the sponsors of the conference."},{"id":13082671,"author":"gbrown_","month":"2016-12","story":"\"Solaris being canned, at least 50% of teams to be RIF'd in short term\"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how the illumos fork came to be","quote":"If you're interested how it came to be check out this epic talk by Bryan Cantrill","text":"> not a leaked version of it. It's a fork. If you're interested how it came to be check out this epic talk by Bryan Cantrill [VIDEO]"},{"id":12638187,"author":"Malic","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Opinion on Sun to Oracle transition with an amusing Ellison rant","quote":"His Larry Ellison rant makes me smile","text":"grin Here we go... For \"laughing at ourselves\" and oddities of computer languages, there is \"Wat\" by Gary Bernhardt: [LINK] For an opinion on the Sun to Oracle transition, there is \"Fork Yeah! The Rise and Development of illumos\" by Bryan M. Cantrill, Joyent. His Larry Ellison rant makes me smile: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48991490,"author":"Sammi","month":"2026-07","story":"Five US tech giants' hidden debts soar to $1.65T on opaque AI funding","stance":"rec","why":"Cantrill's experience at Oracle as a warning","quote":"You need to listen to what Bryan Cantrill said about his experience at Oracle, before you go there.","text":"You need to listen to what Bryan Cantrill said about his experience at Oracle, before you go there. > What you think of Oracle is even truer than what you think it is: \"Shit mediocrity, inflict misery, lie our asses off, screw our customers, and make a whole shitload of money\" [VIDEO] > You need to think of Larry Ellison the way you think of a lawnmower. You don't anthropomorphize your lawnmower, the lawnmower just mows the lawn, you stick your hand in there and it'll chop it off, the end. You don't think 'oh, the lawnmower hates me' -- lawnmower doesn't give a shit about you, lawnmower can't hate you. Don't anthropomorphize the lawnmower. Don't fall into that trap about Oracle. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19108460,"author":"TAForObvReasons","month":"2019-02","story":"Reflecting on My Failure to Build a Billion-Dollar Company","stance":"rec","why":"Cantrill's discussion on Sun's legacy","quote":"Bryan Cantrill's discussion is also great","text":"The great Scott McNealy quote about Sun really resonated with me: > We kicked butt, had fun, didn't cheat, loved our customers and changed computing forever ... and in many ways, it seems like only a \"lifestyle business\" can really prioritize those over core revenue and growth metrics. Bryan Cantrill's discussion is also great: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15171458,"author":"richard_todd","month":"2017-09","story":"The sudden death and eternal life of Solaris","stance":"rec","why":"Solaris history, its troubles, Oracle's approach, and open-source forks","quote":"this talk says a lot about what was cool about Solaris, its place in history, the troubles it encountered","text":"If you are interested, this talk says a lot about what was cool about Solaris, its place in history, the troubles it encountered, and what was wrong with Oracle's approach, leading to little-known open-source forks. [VIDEO] (entitled: Fork yeah! The rise and development of Illumos)"},{"id":12417017,"author":"TazeTSchnitzel","month":"2016-09","story":"Contemplating the possible retirement of Apache OpenOffice","stance":"rec","why":"Informative account of Oracle closing OpenSolaris","quote":"This talk is informative","text":"Oracle killed OpenSolaris. They literally moved Solaris back to being closed-source. With no justification, even, beyond Oracle not liking open-source. Undoing the massive amount of work the Solaris team made in order to open-source it in the first place. There's no more source code releases for Solaris now. Unsurprisingly, almost all the core Solaris team walked. This talk is informative: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5848931,"author":"insaneirish","month":"2013-06","story":"Linux Performance Analysis and Tools","stance":"rec","why":"Context on Solaris, illumos and Oracle","quote":"You should also watch his \"Fork Yeah!\" [2] and \"Corporate Open Source Anti-Patterns\" [3] talks for full context.","text":"DTrace was deliberately licensed in a way to be incompatible with the Linux kernel. This was commented on directly by Bryan Cantrill on HN [1]. You should also watch his \"Fork Yeah!\" [2] and \"Corporate Open Source Anti-Patterns\" [3] talks for full context. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":48375034,"author":"tejtm","month":"2026-06","story":"Larry Ellison: \"Citizens will be on their best behavior because we’re recording\"","stance":"reference","why":"Classic lawnmower quote with timestamp","quote":"the classic \"Do not fall into the trap of anthropomorphizing Larry Ellison.","text":"the classic \"Do not fall into the trap of anthropomorphizing Larry Ellison. You need to think of Larry Ellison the way you think of a lawnmower. You don’t anthropomorphize your lawnmower, the lawnmower just mows the lawn - you stick your hand in there and it’ll chop it off, the end. You don’t think \"oh, the lawnmower hates me\" – lawnmower doesn’t give a shit about you, lawnmower can’t hate you. Don’t anthropomorphize the lawnmower. Don’t fall into that trap about Oracle.\" — Bryan Cantrill at about 34-39 minutes in: [ [VIDEO] ]"},{"id":45303349,"author":"fragmede","month":"2025-09","story":"U.S. investors, Trump close in on TikTok deal with China","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ah yes, famed billionaire Larry Ellison, 98% owner of the Hawaiian Island of Lānaʻi, who you famously should not anthropomorphisize [1][2] who has proposed using AI on mass surveillance cameras so that ‘citizens will be on their best behavior’[3] is definitely not into authoritarian government. He's totally not known as being an authoritarian CEO, with financhill describing his reign as CEO: > Of these four leadership styles, Larry Ellison’s style is decidedly autocratic. [4]. He may be a Jew, and the holocaust may be the worst thing the Nazis did, but it wasn't the only bad thing about their reign. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":44493498,"author":"hoherd","month":"2025-07","story":"Why Austin Is Falling Out of Favor for Tech Workers","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp of the famous Cantrill rant about Oracle","quote":"This is the video and timestamp where Bryan Cantrill goes off about how awful oracle is. It's kinda famous around here.","text":"This is the video and timestamp where Bryan Cantrill goes off about how awful oracle is. It's kinda famous around here. [VIDEO] Find more discussion about it by searching for \"oracle lawn mower\""},{"id":43485316,"author":"soraminazuki","month":"2025-03","story":"Google will develop Android OS behind closed doors starting next week","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for Oracle closing OpenSolaris development","quote":"","text":"It's called a bait and switch. Regarding this parent edit: > If you're going to argue it is a \"bait and switch\", that implies intent to deceive. Do you think that was the plan? Release the first version in 2007 and then wait 18 years and pull the ol switcheroo? Whether they had nefarious intentions from the get go is irrelevant. Oracle closed the once open source Open Solaris, stating at first that it will merely make real time development private just like Android. This was widely condemned, despite there being no intention to pull off a switcheroo when it was open sourced. > We will no longer distribute source code for the entirety of the Solaris operating system in real-time [VIDEO]"},{"id":40640077,"author":"Terr_","month":"2024-06","story":"Oracle Java police start knocking on Fortune 200's doors for first time","stance":"reference","why":"Funny quip about Oracle versus Sun","quote":"Reminds me of a funny quip that's stuck with me, about \"falling into a trap","text":"Reminds me of a funny quip that's stuck with me, about \"falling into a trap\": > [Oracle, as contrasted to Sun Microsystems] It's more like: \"Ship mediocrity, inflict misery, lie our asses off, screw our customers, and make a whole shitload of money .\" [...] > If you were to actually have a conversation with a bank --a friggin bank --[...]Whether you believe it or not, you will get a very societally-engaged answer. [...] You talk to Oracle, it's like \"No, we don't fuckin' make dreams happen, we make money , that's what we do!\" [...] > Do not fall into the trap of anthropomorphizing [Oracle CEO] Larry Ellison. You need to think of Larry Ellison the way you think of a lawnmower. You don't anthropomorphize your lawnmower, the lawnmower just doesn't--like, mows the lawn, you stick your hand in there and it'll chop it off, the end. You don't think \"Oh, the lawnmower hates me\"--lawnmower doesn't give a shit about you, lawnmower can't hate you. Don't anthropomorphize the lawnmower. Don't fall into that trap about Oracle. -- Brian Cantrill: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40776916,"author":"nordsieck","month":"2024-06","story":"CentOS Linux 7 will reach EOL on Sunday","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to explain why Oracle is not to be trusted","quote":"Oracle is a different beast[1].","text":"Oracle is a different beast[1]. If it were any other company, I might agree with you. But I just don't think I'd ever touch anything Oracle makes with a 10' pole except under duress. It's better to do the work up front of just going with the alternative than risk getting pulled into that particular tar baby. --- 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34878286,"author":"favorited","month":"2023-02","story":"Oracle is targeting users on Java compliance","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes Cantrill's lawnmower advice about dealing with Oracle","quote":"Bryan Cantrill's immortal advice about dealing with Oracle","text":"Bryan Cantrill's immortal advice about dealing with Oracle: \"Do not fall into the trap of anthropomorphising Larry Ellison. You need to think of Larry Ellison the way you think of a lawnmower. You don't anthropomorphize your lawnmower, the lawnmower just mows the lawn, you stick your hand in there and it'll chop it off, the end. You don't think 'oh, the lawnmower hates me' -- lawnmower doesn't give a shit about you, lawnmower can't hate you. Don't anthropomorphize the lawnmower. Don't fall into that trap about Oracle.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":24465268,"author":"yjftsjthsd-h","month":"2020-09","story":"Oracle Wins Bid for TikTok in U.S.","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes Cantrill's remark on Oracle's lack of nuance","quote":"There has been no entity in human history with less complexity or nuance to it than Oracle.","text":"> (that may be a bit too cynical) \"You actually don't need to be open-minded about Oracle, you are wasting the openness of your mind [...] As you know people, as you learn about things, you realize that these generalizations we have are, virtually to a generalization, false. Well, except for this one, as it turns out. What you think of Oracle, is even truer than you think it is. There has been no entity in human history with less complexity or nuance to it than Oracle.\" - Bryan Cantrill, [VIDEO]"},{"id":4262336,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2012-07","story":"Oracle launches \"a better alternative to CentOS\"","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for illumos innovations not backportable to Solaris","quote":"","text":"It depends on what you mean by \"significantly diverge.\" If you mean \"innovate\", then yes -- absolutely. We already have tons of evidence of differentiated innovation in illumos[1][2][3] -- none of which can be brought back into Solaris.[4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30752044,"author":"jnsaff2","month":"2022-03","story":"After 600 hours 64 workers at Ukraine's Chernobyl nuclear plant finally relieved","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"What is actually funny was after watching the show was .. huh, this was interesting .. but if the worst nuclear disaster we have has about a few hundred direct deaths, and a few thousand indirect .. with other health complications to some tens of thousands. Then compared to the respiratory illness related deaths caused by burning fossil fuels NOW and all the future wars and deaths and ecosystem collapses caused by climate change is absolutely nothing. Now I have read quite a few books on nuclear disasters before and was aware of the fact that the megaton explosion was not true. So I may not be a representative example of how people reacted to the show. What was scary to me was the relatively accurate representation of the behavior of the soviet brass. I even found their representation quite mild. And as we see in Ukraine these days, nothing has changed, russians have zero regard for civilians, nuclear power plants are being endangered with shelling, maternity wards are being bombed, bread lines are bombed. Post-soviet mafia does not care about anything but their own kleptocracy and looting. Every time I think about this I get reminded the Fork Yeah! talk by bcantrill. [VIDEO] \"An open mind is completely wasted on russians\" \"The lawnmower does not care\""},{"id":28296002,"author":"yakubin","month":"2021-08","story":"Googlespeak – How Google limits thought about antitrust","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Any large company with half-way competent legal counsel is going to tell their employees not to say, \"our goal is to crush our competitors, dominate the market, and hear the lamentation of their women.\" Instead they will tell their employees to focus on making life better for their customers. The lawn mower would like to have a word with you: < [VIDEO] >"}],"r":[["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1]]},"O5BJVO3PDeQ":{"wy":"Frequently recommended as an entertaining account of a failed launch and recovery, especially in threads about Star Citizen and over-promising.","q":{"x":"surprisingly a well told, heart-warming (albeit quite long) video telling the story of No Man's Sky","i":24128299,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":43744438,"author":"csense","month":"2025-04","story":"Cozy video games can quell stress and anxiety","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on how the game changed over the years","quote":"Internet Historian made an amazing documentary on how the game's changed over the years [1].","text":"Internet Historian made an amazing documentary on how the game's changed over the years [1]. They're still making massive changes to the game! If it's been years since you played it, you should give it a try. There have been a lot of bug/crash fixes, new mechanics, even redoing some core game systems (I think they recently made some big changes to planet generation). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34625658,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2023-02","story":"Bay 12 Games has made $7M from the Steam release of Dwarf Fortress","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Story of No Man's Sky's failed launch and years of updates","quote":"I highly recommend the Internet Historian video on it [1].","text":"I'm super happy for these guys. They really have struggled with donations for many years. It's nice to see them get the bag. For those commenting on the sales drop off, it is a problem for AAA titles that a half-finished product gets sold to the public and then abandoned because it's easier to just move onto the next thing. But I want to counter that with the story of No Man's Sky. I highly recommend the Internet Historian video on it [1]. The short version is for various reasons the game over-promised and under-delieved on launch but Sean and HelloGames have now spent years continuing to develop the game with free and significant updates. I can only imagine this still drives sales growth or it wouldn't be sustainable. But it's really repaired HG's and NMS's reputation. NMS has a real cult following now. And then of course there's Minecraft whose long tail of sales is truly mind-blowing. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30196572,"author":"cletus","month":"2022-02","story":"Star Citizen will limit its roadmap, as players are getting upset over delays","stance":"strong_rec","why":"No Man's Sky's terrible launch and recovery","quote":"I highly recommend this video [1]","text":"Watching Star Citizen has been a hobby for me like watching a train wreck is painfully slow motion. It came about at the same time as the revival of a beloved franchise Elite in the form of Elite Dangerous. ED didn't have the budget SC does but it stuck to quite narrow development goals and shipped years ago. It's progressively added updates, patches and expansions since. No Man's Sky is vaguely in the same vein and had a terrible launch (I highly recommend this video [1]) but, to their credit, Hello Games has plugged away at NMS adding more content and updates to the point where it's an enjoyable experience now and has a loyal fan base. But SC? By God. It's like a progenitor for NFT scams. Constantly raising even more money by selling limited run ships (for a game that still basically doesn't exist). I'd occasionally see a presentation on some new system under development (eg the prison system) and I always thought \"why on Earth are they talking about this before they even have a core game?\" It's an object lesson that too much money can be a bad thing. It's so easy for an embarrassment of riches to lead to hopeless feature creep and not really delivering anything. I'm honestly surprised so many still drink the Kool-Aid on SC. I really wonder where all the money has gone (and how much is actually gone). [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24128299,"author":"rgoulter","month":"2020-08","story":"Star Citizen Alpha 3.10","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well-told story of No Man's Sky hype and developer redemption","quote":"surprisingly a well told, heart-warming (albeit quite long) video telling the story of No Man's Sky","text":"Speaking of pre-release hype for over-promised scope, Internet Historian's \"The Engoodening of No Man's Sky\" is surprisingly a well told, heart-warming (albeit quite long) video telling the story of No Man's Sky, how it got so hyped, and the good-faith steps the developers took to do their best to make good on their promises. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42869150,"author":"ziddoap","month":"2025-01","story":"No Man's Sky's update introduces billions of new stars, planets, and more","stance":"rec","why":"Good review of how a shaky launch became a success","quote":"The Engoodening of No Man's Sky\" is a good review of the story.","text":"No Man's Sky is the poster child for turning a shitty launch into a success. You can really feel the passion of the staff at Hello Games in the current version of the game. It reminds me of the quote, which I'm about to butcher, that is along the lines of \"People don't remember the problem, they remember how you dealt with the problem\". If you haven't seen it, \"The Engoodening of No Man's Sky\" is a good review of the story. It's 5 years old now, and the engoodening hasn't stopped. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38213379,"author":"tmpz22","month":"2023-11","story":"No Man's Sky just keeps getting better","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining mini-documentary on the game","quote":"the engoodening of No Man's Sky is a entertaining mini-doc on the game.","text":"Yeah I got about 30 hours, paid about $30 (there have been many sales where its much cheaper - plus IIRC its on Xbox Game Pass now). Im more then happy at the cost/reward. Would seriously follow a new game by the same developer [1] the engoodening of No Man's Sky is a entertaining mini-doc on the game. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36155040,"author":"lyu07282","month":"2023-06","story":"No Man's Sky Now on Mac","stance":"rec","why":"Saga from launch failure to massive success","quote":"it goes over this entire saga from failure at launch to massive success","text":"Just in the off-chance that anyone hasn't seen this: [VIDEO] it goes over this entire saga from failure at launch to massive success"},{"id":30239273,"author":"snowgrove","month":"2022-02","story":"Worms or bust: The story of Britain’s most tenacious indie games company (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Hello Games' post-launch journey with No Man's Sky","quote":"The whole story is incredible and worth your time","text":"I love Worms, but if there’s a runner-up for “Britain’s most tenacious indie games company” then surely it is Hello Games. They launched No Man’s Sky in 2016 to universal disappointment and fan outrage, but kept working on it post-launch (under extreme stress and with little income to speak of) for years until they met & exceeded fan’s original expectations. Gratified fans eventually paid to put up a “Thank you Hello Games” billboard on a road near their office. The whole story is incredible and worth your time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27630218,"author":"cletus","month":"2021-06","story":"Star Citizen Progress Tracker v1.0","stance":"rec","why":"Redemption of No Man's Sky after over-promising at release","quote":"there's a great Internet Historian video [1] on the redemption of NMS after over-promising on release.","text":"Star Citizen is a cautionary tale about how too much money can be a curse. Star Citizen and Elite Dangerous came around at roughly the same time but Elite has been a complete game for years. Elite did things right in the sense that they released a complete game and added to it. I've seen videos on Star Citizen talking about new systems they're adding like being put in prison and escaping and all I can think of is that this sounds cool but why are you spending any effort on this before you have a core game? star Citizen development just seems to be completely unfocused with periodic sales of ships to top up the coffers. How much better could it have been had they decided on core features that were a complete game and then just added content releases every 6-12 months? For anyone who just wants to explore without the grittiness and over-realism of Elite Dangerous, check out No Man's Sky. Side note, there's a great Internet Historian video [1] on the redemption of NMS after over-promising on release. Also, I see another game that seems to be falling into the trap of being too ambitious before having core gameplay and that's Ashes of Creation, sadly. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22346864,"author":"Asooka","month":"2020-02","story":"Spore Prototypes (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Why No Man's Sky launched badly and later recovered","quote":"Here's a short story of how and why it had a bad launch","text":"NMS had problems of a completely different character at launch. Once they fixed the bugs, added the features that were missing and did a lot of polish, people really like the game. It's very well-received now. Here's a short story of how and why it had a bad launch [VIDEO] ."},{"id":37263371,"author":"ecuzzillo","month":"2023-08","story":"Factorio: Space Age","stance":"reference","why":"One rendition of the No Man's Sky story","quote":"See for one rendition of the story.","text":"Yes, they did go back. They basically used all the money they made from the hype and used it to make it real. Players bought them a billboard across from their office saying thank you. ( [LINK] ) See [VIDEO] for one rendition of the story."}],"r":[["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["zmCTmgavkrQ",1],["Pld8wTa16Jk",1],["Qam5BkXIEhQ",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["rDPj5zI66LA",1],["E3URhJx0NSw",1],["uFQ3sajIdaM",1],["5u7euN1HTuU",1],["_fQtxKmgJC8",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1],["469chceiiUQ",1],["kd2KEHvK-q8",1]]},"ZhuHCtR3xq8":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as the clearest and most correct beginner explanation of monads, with several commenters saying it finally made them click.","q":{"x":"This video is all you, as a practicing programmer, need to know about monads and it's just 1 hour long.","i":10058830,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":11552845,"author":"kika","month":"2016-04","story":"Functors, Applicatives, and Monads in Plain English","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Plain-English explanation of functors and monads from someone who knows the math.","quote":"I urge people spend an hour of their life and get the explanation of all this from someone who knows the math","text":"I don't know category theory, so I defer to more knowledgeable people here, but that sounds strange: \"Functor it understands how to take doStuff and execute it on the value inside itself, then spit out a new Optional. You don't lose the int-ness or optional-ness.\" So if I map a function, that takes an int and returns a string over an array of ints, this array stops being a functor? Or is it just a Swift thing? And, as always, I urge people spend an hour of their life and get the explanation of all this from someone who knows the math but still able to explain this in plain English: [VIDEO] To the best of my limited knowledge, it's all 100% correct."},{"id":10058830,"author":"kika","month":"2015-08","story":"Frege: A Haskell-Like Language for the JVM","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One hour covering all a practicing programmer needs to know about monads.","quote":"This video is all you, as a practicing programmer, need to know about monads and it's just 1 hour long.","text":"To be fair, (I think) you don't need to know category theory to successfully apply FP principles and/or use any functional language. I had a great (GREAT) success at using erlang not only not knowing what monad is, but even not knowing about existence of category theory at all, leave aside monads, monoids, functors, etc. You also don't need to know category theory to understand what monad is [0] This video is all you, as a practicing programmer, need to know about monads and it's just 1 hour long. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40559492,"author":"lkuty","month":"2024-06","story":"Monads are like burritos (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Appreciated alongside a monad tutorial.","quote":"watching \"Don't fear the Monad","text":"Good tutorial I think and I also appreciated reading the beginning of [LINK] and watching \"Don't fear the Monad\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":31653160,"author":"omginternets","month":"2022-06","story":"The Lazy Monad","stance":"rec","why":"Helped understand monads as a practical user without category theory.","quote":"I found Brian Beckman's video Don't Fear the Monad was extremely helpful for understanding monads as a practical user.","text":"[VIDEO] I found Brian Beckman's video Don't Fear the Monad was extremely helpful for understanding monads as a practical user. I now use them everywhere. I don't know the first thing about category theory."},{"id":17007576,"author":"mitchty","month":"2018-05","story":"Monads for functional programming (1995) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Softer introduction to monad concepts than the papers.","quote":"For a more soft introduction to the concepts this channel 9 video is probably a bit better","text":"Thats me here too! >.< Yeah the hidden subtext here was my presumption in that suggestion was someone was learning monads in Haskell and has at least a basic understanding of Haskell. Sorry if those papers threw you into the deep end. Monads really were thrown into play due to trying to make a pure functional programming language useful. I found the more mathematical explanation a lot easier than any analogies involving say burritos or pipes etc... At first glance to imperative programmers I will admit all of this just looks like a bunch of navel gazing. I thought the same thing, but once you let go of thinking of computation as a series of steps and at a bit of a higher abstraction this makes a lot more sense. For a more soft introduction to the concepts this channel 9 video is probably a bit better: [VIDEO] Another option might be to just get the haskell programming from first principles book: [LINK] Does a good job grounding you up to monads and gives copious amounts of reference material. Even though I already knew haskell when I read the book it helped clear up some gaps I didn't know I had. But monads are going to be really weird no matter if all you've ever seen is imperative programming. Despite the fact that you already use them and have probably discovered them without knowing you did. Much like learning stack based programming languages, sometimes the only way up is to climb off the computational hill you're familiar with to learn another way to the other hill side"},{"id":13270167,"author":"RossBencina","month":"2016-12","story":"What Is Abstract Algebra? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Nice intro to monoids and function composition.","quote":"Brian Beckman did a nice intro video to this at MS Channel 9 a few years back.[1]","text":"In a Group, every element has an inverse element. If you drop this property, you get a Monoid.[0] Functions under function composition form a Monoid (or Monoidal Category). Brian Beckman did a nice intro video to this at MS Channel 9 a few years back.[1] [0] [LINK] [1] \"Brian Beckman: Don't fear the Monad\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11460314,"author":"kika","month":"2016-04","story":"Monads Explained Quickly","stance":"rec","why":"Longer, more correct monad explanation needing only school-level math.","quote":"I'd suggest a longer (1 hour) but much more correct explanation.","text":"I'd suggest a longer (1 hour) but much more correct explanation. It doesn't use math beyond high (or even may be middle) school. Video, unfortunately. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9635718,"author":"zabi_rauf","month":"2015-05","story":"Monads in Elixir","stance":"rec","why":"Helped commenter understand the use case of monads.","quote":"it helped me in understanding the use case of monad and ultimately monad","text":"I've seen the same video before i knew what a monad was, it helped me in understanding the use case of monad and ultimately monad. Monad is just a generalization of that particular use case. Some resources that helped me in understanding monads 1. Brian Beckman: Don't fear the Monad - [VIDEO] 2. Functors, Applicatives, And Monads In Pictures - [LINK] 3. Three Useful Monads - [LINK] So its been not just one resource but multiple resource before it hit me that it was such a simple concept. So i would suggest to go over them (in order) and also look at other resources (mentioned by others)."},{"id":9148513,"author":"l4mbda","month":"2015-03","story":"Understanding Monoids using F#","stance":"rec","why":"Whole talk worth watching; includes simple monoid overview.","quote":"This whole video is worth watching","text":"This whole video is worth watching, but a simple monoid overview is here: [VIDEO] It's not a difficult concept."},{"id":8511063,"author":"emotionalcode","month":"2014-10","story":"On becoming an expert C programmer","stance":"rec","why":"Useful introduction to functional composition concepts for newcomers.","quote":"I found this introduction to the concepts of functional programming (functional composition) to be useful","text":"> and apparently I'm never going to understand Haskell I found this introduction to the concepts of functional programming (functional composition) to be useful: [VIDEO] Otherwise, it's just a different way of thinking about problems. Instead of building things structurally as an object of objects, and then creating functions that relate, transform, or describe the relationships between the object structures (like taking a pixel object, altering it's x coordinate, and returning the new pixel object with only the x coordinate altered), one has functions that can be composed with other functions to produce more complex object structures. A single base unit with a sequence of functional compositions can describe a very fancy, complex unit, with many fancy, complex characteristics. >> Monads have also been explained with a physical metaphor as assembly lines, where a conveyor belt transports data between functional units that transform it one step at a time.[2] They can also be seen as a functional design pattern to build generic types.[3] [LINK] Otherwise, it's syntax and semantics, which I generally find annoying, but there's always google and books. [LINK]"},{"id":4985241,"author":"chongli","month":"2012-12","story":"Monads are a Class of Hard Drugs (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Fairly simple explanation of monads as composable computations.","quote":"Here's a nice video that explains monads fairly simply","text":"Apples and oranges. A monad is just an idea; that of storing computations as values. This is useful because these computations can be composed together in a manner not unlike that of a pipeline in shell scripting. Different monads define different ways of composing these computations leading to vastly different semantics. Here's a nice video that explains monads fairly simply: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29674311,"author":"mwattsun","month":"2021-12","story":"Be curious, not judgmental","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I watched Brian Beckman: Don't fear the Monad ( ) but couldn't make heads or tails of it.","text":"Right, I understand where I wasn't clear. You're right, that idea has won and gone mainstream so I didn't think it was worth mentioning. I was emphasizing that I thought immutable data structures were inefficient and that it was the luxury of academics to abstract away stateful changes in the name of purity. I've got a couple of other ideas that I need to do more research on (I'm watching Erik Meijer videos today) 1) Class objects with methods and data are like small programs, so I don't get why people go on and on about having data structures with functions as being superior to class objects, which are essentially data structures with associated functions. 2) Similarly, Joe Armstrong seems to hate object oriented programming but Erlang instances are essentially class objects, albeit running in their own process so they have more isolation 3) From reading Erik Meijer's Confessions of a Used Programming Language Salesman, I'm assuming that Async/Await that he help put in C# and Dart are his attempt to bring Haskell continuation monads to the imperative world 4) I'm assuming Clojure Atoms and Actors are like Haskell monads but don't know that. I watched Brian Beckman: Don't fear the Monad ( [VIDEO] ) but couldn't make heads or tails of it. Something about functions calling first order functions and pure first order effects with stateful second order effects, or something... That was a few years ago so I will give it another chance. I'm probably wrong about these ideas but they'"},{"id":28941127,"author":"lkuty","month":"2021-10","story":"Functors, Applicatives, and Monads in Pictures (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"One item in a list of monad learning resources.","quote":"","text":"I would complement it with: - Scott Wlaschin's Railway Oriented Programming (ROP): [LINK] and [LINK] - Scott Wlaschin's The \"Map and Bind and Apply, Oh my!\" series at [LINK] and its concept of an \"elevated world\". - And for Elixir Programmers, Zohaib Rauf's \"Railway Oriented Programming in Elixir\" at [LINK] based on Scott Wlaschin stuff. - [LINK] . The introductory part (7 first pages of the pdf). - Brian Beckman: Don't fear the Monad [VIDEO] - Philip Wadler's \"Monads for Functional Programming\" at [LINK] - HN comment: [LINK] ROP is a good way to introduce the Either monad without ever talking about monads, functors and co. Moreover it is highly useful."},{"id":7971594,"author":"platz","month":"2014-07","story":"The Two Cultures of Mathematics (2000) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Beckman's aside on bottom-up versus top-down programmer camps.","quote":"Brian Beckman explained one such axis, as an aside, in \"Don't fear the Monad\" [1]","text":"I think there are several different axes one can split programmer culture down (which may or may not be orthogonal). Brian Beckman explained one such axis, as an aside, in \"Don't fear the Monad\" [1], about two differing views that born in the '70s and split two programmers into two camps: The bottom-up people, and the top-down people: - The bottom-up people start with the hardware and only add abstractions and trade performance where necessary (fortran, c, java) - The top-down people started with perfect abstraction/logic and reduced/removed abstraction to get access to the hardware and performance where necessary (lisp, ml, haskell) What is interesting these days, is that these two camps are trying to steal more and more ideas from each other [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] (previous discussion: [LINK] )"},{"id":7024483,"author":"platz","month":"2014-01","story":"Dijkstra on Haskell and Java (2001)","stance":"reference","why":"Beckman's aside on bottom-up versus top-down programmer camps.","quote":"The bottom-up people start with the hardware and only add abstractions and trade performance where necessary","text":"As Brian Beckman explained, as an aside, in \"Don't fear the Monad\" [1], these two differing views were born in the '70s and split two programmers into two camps: The bottom-up people, and the top-down people: - The bottom-up people start with the hardware and only add abstractions and trade performance where necessary (fortran, c, java) - The top-down people started with perfect abstraction/logic and reduced/removed abstraction to get access to the hardware and performance where necessary (lisp, ml, haskell) I don't think the two camps may ever get along. Kind of like our version of Conservative and Liberal. (nor should they, in the true spirit of democracy?) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9851983,"author":"88e282102ae2e5b","month":"2015-07","story":"Some Functional Programmers Are Arrogant","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The guy basically goes on some pseudo-mathematical journey that has only a tangential relationship with programming.","text":"Take videos like this: [VIDEO] The guy basically goes on some pseudo-mathematical journey that has only a tangential relationship with programming. \"It's easy to understand!\" he claims multiple times. And it was! I understood everything he said. But I had NO idea how to apply anything he said to my application. And so it goes with virtually every article I've ever read about functional programming. The truth is out there, but it's hidden in such a dense forest of obfuscation that I'm not sure I'll ever know what a monad is. The search goes on, but I'm afraid all I'll ever discover is yet another blog post where all the functions are named f(), g() and h(), or something about prime numbers. So I suppose arrogance may be assumed from seeing this concept explained so badly so many times. It's like people who understand functional programming are gatekeepers and do such a horrible job at letting people in that it seems like we've been locked out on purpose."}],"r":[["-K11rY57K7k",1],["Unzc731iCUY",1],["0vJJlVBVTFg",1],["ue-1JoJQaEg",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["lnnsDi7Sxq0",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["8ORLN_KwAgs",1],["4zgYG-_ha28",1]]},"Erp8IAUouus":{"wy":"HN reaches for it in every identity theft and data breach thread as the clearest, most enjoyable short explainer of why SSNs make bad identifiers.","q":{"x":"this is the most enjoyable explanation for it I've seen, by CGP Grey","i":15200929,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15200929,"author":"bmelton","month":"2017-09","story":"The Equifax breach may be the worst leak of personal info ever","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Most enjoyable explanation of how bad the SSN is","quote":"this is the most enjoyable explanation for it I've seen, by CGP Grey","text":"For anyone who wants an explanation at just how bad the social security number really is, this is the most enjoyable explanation for it I've seen, by CGP Grey [VIDEO]"},{"id":15211338,"author":"sagitariusrex","month":"2017-09","story":"The Equifax Breach Exposes America's Identity Crisis","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how SSCards came to be and why US lacks ID cards","quote":"He also explains why US citizens don't have an Identity Card as opposed to many European countries.","text":"For everybody who isn't acquainted with how Social Security Cards work in the US and how they came to be I highly recommend watching \"Social Security Cards Explained\" by CGP Grey [VIDEO] He also explains why US citizens don't have an Identity Card as opposed to many European countries."},{"id":41249401,"author":"quantumfissure","month":"2024-08","story":"Inside the \"3 billion people\" national public data breach","stance":"rec","why":"Short history of why SSN became an identifier despite not being one","quote":"has a great (and short) video about the history and why it's not technically an identifier, but has become one.","text":"For non-Americans (and Americans) that don't quite understand what SSN is and why it's a problem, CGP Grey [1] has a great (and short) video about the history and why it's not technically an identifier, but has become one. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38180960,"author":"SonicScrub","month":"2023-11","story":"U.S. military members' personal data being sold by online brokers","stance":"rec","why":"Quick five-minute primer on how SSNs grew beyond social security","quote":"Quick 5 minute primer","text":"There's an interesting story about how the social security number evolved to being much more than just for social security. Quick 5 minute primer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38245055,"author":"kalverra","month":"2023-11","story":"It's Still Easy for Anyone to Become You at Experian","stance":"rec","why":"Details the flaws in how SSNs are used","quote":"CGP Grey has an excellent video detailing the flaws of how SSNs are utilized.","text":"I'm late to the party, but CGP Grey has an excellent video detailing the flaws of how SSNs are utilized. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26614868,"author":"anitil","month":"2021-03","story":"No, I did not hack your MS Exchange server","stance":"rec","why":"Tells how the SSN evolved into a de facto ID","quote":"CGP Grey did a video about how it evolved [0].","text":"The story of how this happened is quite interesting. CGP Grey did a video about how it evolved [0]. I'm not American so I can't judge how likely it is to ever change because it seems to be politically radioactive to propose a government mandated ID. We had a similar issue in Australia, but our workaround is that your drivers license (or ID card from the equivalent of the DMV) typically acts as your ID. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23019801,"author":"remcob","month":"2020-04","story":"Having trouble with the IRS site? Try all caps","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why SSNs are poorly suited as national identity numbers","quote":"This video does a great job explaining it","text":"This video does a great job explaining it: [VIDEO] The summary is that the USA does not have an official national person identity number. The SSN is the closest approximation, so it is being (ab)used as one. This is despite that it is explicitly not designed for it and has some qualities which make it less suitable (the numbers are small and very predictable). In general, nearly every national-identity-system suffers from a contradiction between assigning identities and identifying. Where the number you get assigned is simultaneously your identity and a secret you use to establish your identity. There are initiatives to solve this using cryptography, but this is complicated by political attitudes towards privacy and government tracking. I dare say in the USA this is more pronounced due to both the problem and the political attitude against solutions being more pronounced."},{"id":21388322,"author":"manigandham","month":"2019-10","story":"Bank of the Underworld (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on SSNs never meant as IDs","quote":"CGP Grey recently made a great video about this exact problem with social security numbers","text":"CGP Grey recently made a great video about this exact problem with social security numbers: [VIDEO] They were started to keep of taxes and never meant to be used as IDs."},{"id":20424357,"author":"ocdtrekkie","month":"2019-07","story":"FTC Approves Roughly $5B Facebook Settlement","stance":"rec","why":"Good explainer on how SSN became misused as identification","quote":"Absolutely good explainer video about how this travesty took place historically","text":"Actually, the SSN is an account number, solely intended for tracking money paid into and paid out of an account. It isn't meant for identification, and the method it's used for identification is terrible. (Largely: If you know the number, you are identified.) It's treated like a secret, but as a 9-digit number you give to dozens of organizations in your life which follows a predictable and partially guessable pattern, it is an absolutely terrible secret. Absolutely good explainer video about how this travesty took place historically: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15658468,"author":"Qub3d","month":"2017-11","story":"Senators push to ditch social security numbers in light of Equifax hack","stance":"rec","why":"Pop science explanation of SSN problems, including sequential numbering","quote":"Here's a relevant pop science video by CGP Grey[0] that explains all the various problems with SSNs.","text":"Here's a relevant pop science video by CGP Grey[0] that explains all the various problems with SSNs. My favorite part is the fact that until quite recently (2011) you could decrement or increment a given SSN by 1 and get another valid SSN, since they were sequential. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15195829,"author":"alehul","month":"2017-09","story":"Cybersecurity Incident Involving Consumer Information","stance":"rec","why":"History of how SSNs became a de facto national ID","quote":"For anyone interested in more","text":"The SSN was never intended as a national ID. It was originally created alongside the Social Security Administration, to track what individuals put in and what they take out. People only received one upon becoming employed. Over time, the IRS realized that it could be used as a national ID, and adopted it for that purpose. They encouraged people to obtain one from a young age (even for their newborn children), and it was used to replace the original 'honor system' of 'How many children do you have?' tax discounts; now they'd need to register their child with a SSN. Companies eventually began piggybacking on the number as a national identifier (due to our lack of one), and voila. We're left with an awfully insecure identification system that shouldn't be an identification system for much in the first place. For anyone interested in more: [VIDEO] , and the sources below the video."},{"id":15196043,"author":"SimbaOnSteroids","month":"2017-09","story":"Cybersecurity Incident Involving Consumer Information","stance":"rec","why":"Makes the case for a national identification card","quote":"we need a national identification card, link to a video about it.","text":"You're right it was never meant to be a unique identifier, we need a national identification card, link to a video about it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15352097,"author":"vec","month":"2017-09","story":"Stop Calling it ‘Identity Theft’","stance":"rec","why":"Explains SSN as least terrible way to identify Americans","quote":"CGP Gray did a really good video[1] about this recently.","text":"CGP Gray did a really good video[1] about this recently. The short version is that it's the least terrible mechanism presently available for uniquely identifying and disambiguating American citizens. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14020049,"author":"mjevans","month":"2017-04","story":"Men Without Work","stance":"rec","why":"Short explainer on the flaws of US Social Security cards","quote":"A short (< 8 min) video by the ever informative CGP Grey on the inanity of US SS cards.","text":"Don't blame the immigrants, blame those who are fully or partly paying under the table. Of course, would you support a national ID card as a means of combating this variable you accuse? In the US we pretty much have such a thing already, just without any proper security or identification aspects... [VIDEO] (A short (< 8 min) video by the ever informative CGP Grey on the inanity of US SS cards.)"},{"id":29997212,"author":"dharmab","month":"2022-01","story":"IRS Will Soon Require Selfies for Online Access","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the lack of an American national ID card","quote":"The closest thing is a Social Security number, which has no security or biometric features.","text":"Americans have no national identity card. The closest thing is a Social Security number, which has no security or biometric features. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26657660,"author":"d4mi3n","month":"2021-04","story":"Security Breach at US Universities","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for SSA's request not to use SSNs as ID","quote":"the Social Security Administration asked very nicely for the rest of the country to not use them as a form of identification","text":"They are in fact not great IDs and the Social Security Administration asked very nicely for the rest of the country to not use them as a form of identification: [VIDEO] A few reasons why SSNs are bad as IDs: 1. There are a number of situations where people will not have a SSN. 2. SSNs are \"secrets\" that need to be broadly shared to participate in many parts of business and government in the US. 3. SSNs lack many security and authentication mechanisms most forms of ID have (e.g. photo ID) There are folks in the US who rally against the idea of a national ID, but I've always thought it was a silly argument considering how pervasive and problematic SSNs are as a form of identification."},{"id":22278573,"author":"ijpsud","month":"2020-02","story":"Ask HN: What is the single top-priority software engineering problem?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of a number repurposed as identification","quote":"social security numbers weren't originally intended to be used as unique identification","text":"I was talking about this with a friend the other day. I don't know about the technical feasibility (RE sybil attack[0]), but if it were possible, it would have a massive impact on the web. The current state of the art (in terms of new user signup) is using phone numbers as representing unique \"trustable\" people, which is kind of absurd. This reminds me of how social security numbers weren't originally intended to be used as unique identification, but the demand for some form of identification was so strong that organisations ended up hackily using it anyway.[1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16819773,"author":"htgb","month":"2018-04","story":"A privacy flaw in Comcast's automated telephone system","stance":"reference","why":"Gives background on SSNs being treated as secrets","quote":"reminds me of this video [1] by CGP Grey, giving some background of the issue.","text":"Yeah, I suppose that's right. And as you say, it's very practical. > Keep in mind that the social number in USA is apparently also supposed to be secret (like a password) which sounds absolutely crazy to me. That's very true, reminds me of this video [1] by CGP Grey, giving some background of the issue. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14024352,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-04","story":"For 18 years I thought she was stealing my identity, until I found her","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for why SSNs should not be used as identifiers","quote":"it should never be used as a identification number, see the latest GCP Greg","text":"This happened to a photography editor who sits in the cube across from me. He found out he had a ticket in Pittsburgh, a city he had never been to, when he tried to transfer his license to a new state. No lawyer would take the case either. He eventually went to an identity theft company, who took $400 and fixed the issue in three months. I use to work in Identity Management at a major university. A number of people came through our systems without SSNs. We had a complicated set of rules to make sure we didn't provision two accounts. We'd check if the names were reversed and we'd check both the month/date = date/month on the date of birth (people from other countries might enter it backwards without thinking about it). We even had rules for SSNs that were off by <2 digits with similar names. Every morning we'd have a set of \"manual provisions\" we'd have to stop and check. Occasionally duplicate accounts get created and we'd have to go through a big manual process of combining them and telling them \"You need to use this account; forget about the other one. It no longer exists,\" and get their stuff synced across all AD domains (Universities typically have several). What surprises me about this article is the NYC system doesn't use the SSN (which is good in a sense, it should never be used as a identification number, see the latest GCP Greg: [VIDEO] ). The idea of an identity and criminal record us that follows us digitally is a very new concept, one that didn't exist 150+ years "},{"id":13993190,"author":"pfranz","month":"2017-03","story":"You can’t buy Congress’s web history – that's not how any of this works","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how Social Security Numbers enable identifying anonymized data","quote":"CGPGrey posted a video today showing a layman's example with Social Security Numbers[1].","text":"You're right. My guess is most \"anonymized\" data is lazy--stripping out obvious identifying information. There are quite a few cases where you can pinpoint users from correlating to publicly available data. CGPGrey posted a video today showing a layman's example with Social Security Numbers[1]. One cool thing I heard Apple talk about[2] was injecting noise or subsampling to help mask individuals. Although, I don't see that mentioned in their public page on privacy[3] or their whitepaper[4]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"}],"r":[["r3snVCRo_bI",2],["NGFhc8R_uO4",2],["ZC5Zr3NC2PY",1],["6Cf7IL_eZ38",1],["LA_DrBwkiJA",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1],["wrwgIjBUYVc",1],["996OiexHze0",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["clnozSXyF4k",1],["7vsCAM17O-M",1],["iHzzSao6ypE",1],["v_UyVmITiYQ",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["6bqBsHSwPgw",1],["E9KJ_f7REGw",1]]},"vpTHi7O66pI":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up in climate and food threads as a hopeful, if contested, idea about grazing and soil carbon.","q":{"x":"This is the idea behind this fantastic TED talk","i":18644738,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18644738,"author":"monktastic1","month":"2018-12","story":"Can Dirt Save the Earth?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How managed grazing can restore land.","quote":"This is the idea behind this fantastic TED talk","text":"> This view ran counter to a lot of conservationist thought, as well as a great deal of evidence. Grazing has been blamed for turning vast swaths of the world into deserts. But from Creque’s perspective, how you graze makes all the difference. This is the idea behind this fantastic TED talk: [VIDEO] We're largely focused on high-tech ways to \"fix\" the Earth, but the Earth has known how to do it since forever. Finding ways to use her own intelligence seems like a great idea."},{"id":33781137,"author":"hitpointdrew","month":"2022-11","story":"Lab-Grown Meat Is Safe to Eat, FDA Says","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for holistic cattle farming for climate.","quote":"you can check his Ted talk here","text":"> I'm not entirely convinced by this argument. It’s been proven by Allen Savory and others, you can check his Ted talk here [VIDEO] Even if lab grown meat is the next best thing since sliced bread it would be a complete climate disaster to not continue, and expand, holistic cattle farming."},{"id":26326445,"author":"lcamach84","month":"2021-03","story":"Beyond Meat signs global supply deals with McDonald’s, KFC and Pizza Hut","stance":"rec","why":"Herds stimulate grass growth, a carbon sink.","quote":"there is a cool ted talk about this.","text":"When the herd eat grass it stimulates grass growth and grass is a huge carbon sink. there is a cool ted talk about this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24194209,"author":"wlib","month":"2020-08","story":"We should discuss soil as much as coal (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces why soil management matters.","quote":"a great related TED talk by Allan Savory","text":"Glad to see this, a great related TED talk by Allan Savory that introduces why this is true can be found here [VIDEO]"},{"id":19901628,"author":"yjhoney","month":"2019-05","story":"CO2 in the atmosphere just exceeded 415ppm for the first time in human history","stance":"rec","why":"Hopeful, plausible approach to reversing climate change.","quote":"This Ted talk gave me hope to reverse climate change and seems plausible","text":"This Ted talk gave me hope to reverse climate change and seems plausible: [VIDEO] Does anyone know / have experience whether this actually works?"},{"id":18286541,"author":"andersha","month":"2018-10","story":"Carbon Removal Technologies","stance":"rec","why":"Managed grazing builds soil and sequesters carbon.","quote":"The knowledge is available, but this ted talk only has 2 million views.","text":"We can manage grassland and savanah. Grasses exudate a lot of their sugars (carbon) into the ground feeding the microbes. Holistically managed grassing is all about timing the regrowth and recycling the nutrients and fibers through the cow. The prairies are amazing ecosystem pumping carbon into the ground when properly managed. The knowledge is available, but this ted talk only has 2 million views. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16123178,"author":"dx034","month":"2018-01","story":"Britain's Next Megaproject: A Coast-To-Coast Forest","stance":"rec","why":"Desertification linked to under-grazing.","quote":"Good video to watch","text":"But those are often desert. And desertification could (in part) be caused by overly protecting the area (e.g. not allowing cattle). Good video to watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8449560,"author":"xamolxix","month":"2014-10","story":"How Wolves Change Rivers [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Savory on herbivore behavior with and without predators.","quote":"Fascinating, really.","text":"Reminds me about this TED talk [VIDEO] where Allan Savory speaks about the behavior of herbivores with/without predators and the impact on flora. Fascinating, really. I hope the day will come when we understand the biosphere of this wonderful planet we live on."},{"id":5392870,"author":"boon","month":"2013-03","story":"Bills seek end to farm animal abuse videos","stance":"rec","why":"Savory on raising more animals to restore ecosystems.","quote":"Ever heard of Alan Savory? Check it out.","text":"I would eat dog if it wasn't raised as someone's pet. Actually I just got back from Vietnam, and I'm pretty sure I ate both dog and cat at some point. Didn't bother me because they didn't raise them as pets. They raised them with the intention of eating every little bit (without wasting any part of the animal, I might add). You can grow fond of plenty of the animals that we eat on a normal basis, pigs in particular. We also have a tendency to anthropomorphize animals which can lead to attachment. Factory farming is indeed a huge problem, because it requires so much fossil fuel, grain, and antibiotics, and destroys the land and causes global warming. But the fix is not to stop eating animals, the answer is to go back to the roots of the ecosystem and actually raise more animals. Ever heard of Alan Savory? Check it out. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34850524,"author":"rainbowzootsuit","month":"2023-02","story":"The second dust bowl cometh for America, supercomputer warns","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thanks for some info to catch up on. I've seen another step described where you bring in a mobile chicken coop a few days after the cows have departed to eat the insects that arrived to eat the poop. Also keeping the cows a little while means they eat up all the \"weed\" plants and not just the tastier stuff —\"eat your vegetables\" type of thing—while if they free graze they only take the best tasting grasses and move on. How to green the world's deserts and reverse climate change | Allan Savory (TED talk) [VIDEO]"},{"id":25536789,"author":"irthomasthomas","month":"2020-12","story":"2020 had some significant silver linings","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on desertification of grasslands.","quote":"in this quietly powerful talk","text":"It is a too large field for one to research alone, but I am familiar with the effort to track global leaf area and desertification. So here is my small contribution to the discussion: 2013: \"Desertification is a fancy word for land that is turning to desert\" begins Allan Savory in this quietly powerful talk. And terrifyingly, it's happening to about two-thirds of the world's grasslands, accelerating climate change and causing traditional grazing societies to descend into social chaos.\" [VIDEO] 2014: Global Greening Is Firm, Drivers Are Mixed - Harvard \"Evidence for global greening is converging, asserting an increase in CO2 uptake and biomass of the terrestrial biosphere. Evidence for these trends comes from firm empirical data.\" [LINK] 2016: Greening of the globe and its drivers - Nature \"Satellite records from 1982–2009 show a persistent and widespread increase of leaf area (greening) over 25% to 50% of the global vegetated area, whereas less than 4% of the globe shows decreasing leaf area (browning).\" [LINK] Elevated CO2 as a driver of global dryland greening - Nature \"Recent regional scale analyses using satellite based vegetation indices such as the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), have found extensive areas of “greening”\" [LINK] Rise in CO2 has 'greened Planet Earth' [LINK] \"Prof Judith Curry, the former chair of Earth and atmospheric sciences at the Georgia Institute of Technology, added: \"It is inappropriate to dismiss the arguments of the so-called cont"},{"id":24473804,"author":"maxharris","month":"2020-09","story":"Extremes become routine in an emerging new Arctic","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I think Allan Savory is also part of the answer.","text":"We have got to organize geoengineering efforts before the ocean currents stop. What I'm worried is a sudden shift in climate that kills billions of us, in an event that occurs within the next 10-30 years. Release of methane hydrates trapped in ice and methane bursting out of the tundra scares the hell out of me. Cooperation from governments is necessary to actually implement it, but I think it is a mistake to try to rely on those institutions. Why do I think this? Our government bungled development of reusable rockets for decades until SpaceX came along and just did it. You can quibble about how much NASA provided in seed money, but that's really not what I'm trying to get at here. My second example for why we can't rely on the government is the way the Coronavirus pandemic played out. We watch the slow-moving train wreck from January onward. Every single one of our institutions failed us! In fact, many of those institutions prevented individual action, with respect to testing: [LINK] Finally, I think Allan Savory is also part of the answer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24578883,"author":"dsirola","month":"2020-09","story":"Australia to produce seaweed cattle feed that reduces methane emissions by 80%","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's true that methane has a much greater impact, but only in the short term. Long term, CO2 is much more dangerous. The methane that cow produces is recycled as it circulates through the atmosphere -> plants -> cows -> atmosphere cycle. Fossil fuel burning on the other hand is not. It's released from the ground and there's no way for it to get back there. Nothing is ever black and white and to think that eliminating the animals as the food source would solve the problem is naive. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":20654881,"author":"ivanhoe","month":"2019-08","story":"Eat less meat: UN climate change report calls for change to human diet","stance":"reference","why":"Claim that cattle grazing stops desertification.","quote":"Number of serious scientists claim quite the opposite","text":"Number of serious scientists claim quite the opposite, that cattle grazing is the only solution to stop the erosion and turning land into deserts [VIDEO]"},{"id":20139381,"author":"captainredbeard","month":"2019-06","story":"Impossible Foods and Beyond Meat's plant-based burgers aren't healthier","stance":"reference","why":"Managed grazing improves land and reduces pollution.","quote":"If you let ruminant livestock graze in a carefully managed pattern instead of feeding them grain","text":"If you let ruminant livestock graze in a carefully managed pattern instead of feeding them grain (grown and transported from miles away), it improves the land and reduces the amount of pollution: [VIDEO] Cattle are not very efficient at converting grain to protein. It’s about 7:1. Pork is 2:1, in comparison. Poultry is close to 1:1."},{"id":19797102,"author":"mceoin","month":"2019-05","story":"Permafrost collapse is accelerating carbon release","stance":"reference","why":"Intensive grazing combats desertification in Africa.","quote":"Allan Savory talks about the use of intensive grazing as a method for combatting desertification in Africa","text":"Allan Savory talks about the use of intensive grazing as a method for combatting desertification in Africa [0]. Herd fertilize while eating grasses that would otherwise fall into senescence and create pockets of erosion. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19213007,"author":"jillesvangurp","month":"2019-02","story":"Is the Insect Apocalypse Upon Us?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's the classic variety which uses per-industrial practices to farm. This is indeed cute but hopelessly ineffective. And there's the modern variety which uses data and science driven approaches to farm while minimizing resources. E.g. watering and fertilizing based on drone observed growth patterns and other readings means you can use a lot less. Managing the soil in sane way that preserves it rather than destroys it gives you better yields and also improves water retention. Fighting parasites and pests is a lot easier if you understand the dynamics of their ecosystems and e.g. use predatory insects to counter that. Teaching small farmers some basics about how stuff works might help them improve their yields and put a stop to really destructive practices like burning forests to create land that is briefly usable for farming before it turns into desert. Things are definitely changing. 'Modern' mono-cultures are based on the state of the art fifty years ago when it was acceptable to just blindly plow, fertilize, and spray many acres of land without even bothering to measure the impact. It works, for a while, but it is not long term sustainable and there is a lot of farm land turning into barren desert world wide due to decades of mismanagement, abuse, and exploitation. The notion that you need to spend a lot on fertilizers, misc. poisons, genetically modified seeds and machines to plow (aka. destroy the soil) your land in order to farm is maybe a bit outdated. In the US, st"},{"id":18287434,"author":"dereke","month":"2018-10","story":"Carbon Removal Technologies","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've been watching waay too many youtube videos about reforestation, agriculture and land management recently and I think I've learned a little from it (links at the end). We no longer have the number of massive herds of animals that used to roam the plains/savannahs grazing, pooing and to a lesser extent escaping from predators. This has meant that the grasslands are no longer trampled on and \"fertilised\". This has caused the grasses to die back, the soil to degrade, to not hold water and to turn to desert. (see the sahara, the outback, parts of china and the usa). Subsequently we've tried to be really careful with the land and not over graze it etc. which tends to have the opposite effect than what is desired. Now I also looked into reforestation because I thought trees were the answer. Grow trees sequester carbon etc. But it turns out the cost of doing this £/$ and water (desalination) would actually be outweighed by both the albido effect (green trees absorb more sunlight than deserts that reflect it back) and that trees don't really grow fast enough to have the impact required. Getting back to the grasslands, it turns out that when you intensively drive a herd over grasslands the grass initially dies back but the root system expands, the plant grows quickly and sequesters carbon into the ground. It actually builds soil and traps carbon and it does it faster than previously thought. The ground is also more permeable to water so when big storms come it actually soaks up th"},{"id":15960050,"author":"mrarjen","month":"2017-12","story":"China’s Plan to Keep Sand from Swallowing the World","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Would like to see them try and do this, get the ground a bit more fertile and planting native grasses perhaps to get it going somewhat? [VIDEO]"},{"id":10273502,"author":"PopeOfNope","month":"2015-09","story":"It’s Time to Fix the Broken Food System","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It's very hard to get the general public to reduce animal product consumption. That's because animal proteins and animal fats are vital nutrients the body needs. It's also the most efficient method of transport for these nutrients; industrially grown crops cause far more damage than pastured livestock. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5guXaWwQpe4",1]]},"Ll6-eGDpimU":{"wy":"HN regards the dishwasher videos as must-watch practical explainers, linked whenever dishwashers, detergents or pods come up.","q":{"x":"the two Technology Connection videos on dishwashers are must watch","i":35097379,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35097379,"author":"lbutler","month":"2023-03","story":"I Splurged for the Fanciest Dishwasher and It Was Worth It","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"the two Technology Connection videos on dishwashers are must watch","text":"For those that haven't seen them, the two Technology Connection videos on dishwashers are must watch 1 - [LINK] 2 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30152236,"author":"chillingeffect","month":"2022-01","story":"My dishwasher won’t start until I let it update its firmware over the WiFi","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains European versus American dishwasher design.","quote":"This[0] and this[1] are the ultimate dishwasher talks.","text":"This[0] and this[1] are the ultimate dishwasher talks. From what I gather, European dishwashers heat the water, while American do not. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42499321,"author":"zahlman","month":"2024-12","story":"I just found out I've been using my dishwasher wrong for 7 years","stance":"rec","why":"Series on operating a dishwasher.","quote":"there is more to learn about operating a dishwasher","text":"Relevant - since there is more to learn about operating a dishwasher: Technology Connections' series on dishwashers - [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":38277129,"author":"raphlinus","month":"2023-11","story":"Gut epithelial barrier damage caused by dishwasher detergents and rinse aids","stance":"rec","why":"Explains rinse aids better.","quote":"If you want to understand rinse aids better, I recommend this Technology Connections video","text":"If you want to understand rinse aids better, I recommend this Technology Connections video (I've set the time stamp to the rinse aid section): [VIDEO]"},{"id":37461636,"author":"ComputerGuru","month":"2023-09","story":"To Make Dishwashers Great Again? (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why liquid detergent is poor.","quote":"The follow-up to the video is worth linking to, if only because it explains why liquid detergent is crap","text":"The follow-up to the video is worth linking to, if only because it explains why liquid detergent is crap: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36556253,"author":"harrisi","month":"2023-07","story":"Workers with less experience gain the most from generative AI","stance":"rec","why":"Useful dishwasher videos.","quote":"Some useful videos","text":"I've gone back and forth with dishwashers my whole life, spending years with followed by years without, etc. and I finally am team dishwasher. There are a few tips I've learned, such as immediately rinsing a dish after using (I don't bother scrubbing), running the hot water for a minute before starting, using the pre-wash detergent and shine stuff appropriately. I also don't bother entirely filling it up to run it - which may seem wasteful, but I'd imagine filling it completely and having to rewash some percent of them ends up being more wasteful. Also, just like getting a manual dishwashing routine figured out, dishwashers are all different and need to be learned like any tool. Understanding where pieces are, which cycles run when, which compartments open and close and even how they do it, are all important. It really is best to think of it as a tool. Some useful videos: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33826732,"author":"1MachineElf","month":"2022-12","story":"Gut epithelial barrier damage caused by dishwasher detergents and rinse aids","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why most dishwasher pods are overkill.","quote":"Here's an interesting video by Technology Connections on how most dishwasher \"pods\" are overkill","text":"Here's an interesting video by Technology Connections on how most dishwasher \"pods\" are overkill: [VIDEO] I see the bubbles foaming at the edges of my cups when I fill them with water, so I always rinse them before use. I would avoid most dishwasher detergent pods if I could, but I can't sell the idea to my significant other."},{"id":34199356,"author":"dns_snek","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: What is your system for dirty dishes?","stance":"rec","why":"Two helpful videos on dishwasher use.","quote":"Technology Connections released 2 videos on the topic of dishwashers that could be helpful","text":"It's made by a company called Candy (EU) but unfortunately I don't have the exact model number. I can't say how it's going to hold up over time since I've only had it for around 2 years since I moved in. The only advice I have in this regard is making sure you're loading it correctly and not overloading it to the point where water spray can't reach your dishes. You've probably come across these videos already but just in case you haven't, Technology Connections released 2 videos on the topic of dishwashers that could be helpful: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30232431,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Any piece of hardware that was more of game changer than you expected?","stance":"rec","why":"Follow-up on powder versus tablets.","quote":"Also, a follow-up video","text":"> Doesn't even need special tablets […] A thirty minute video explaining why tablets are bad and that you should simply use powder: * [LINK] Also, a follow-up video: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":30148526,"author":"slantyyz","month":"2022-01","story":"My dishwasher won’t start until I let it update its firmware over the WiFi","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting videos on how dishwashers work and getting the most from them.","quote":"pretty interesting videos on how dishwashers work and how to get the most out of them","text":"Sadly, I don't think dishwashers will last 20 years any more. Since 2005, I'm on my third dishwasher. The first one lasted 9 years, the second one around 5 (with one under warranty repair). At some point the repairs/parts cost more than what the original dishwasher is worth. Regarding the algorithm, I'm not sure they make a huge difference anyways. FWIW, The Technology Connections channel on YouTube has a couple of pretty interesting videos on how dishwashers work and how to get the most out of them: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45829107,"author":"masklinn","month":"2025-11","story":"I was right about dishwasher pods and now I can prove it [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Debunks claims of different dissolve rates in pods.","quote":"There is no \"different dissolve rate\" or \"dispensed in sequence\".","text":"Alec actually looked at that in a previous video of the dishwasher series. There is no \"different dissolve rate\" or \"dispensed in sequence\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":37461950,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-09","story":"To Make Dishwashers Great Again? (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Chemist comment on pod formulations being functionally identical.","quote":"","text":"> America's Test Kitchen, back when it was good (AKA when Chris was in control), said that the easiest thing to do was just use the pods that had as many colors of liquid as possible. 1. I don't see any/much difference in pre- and post-Christopher Kimball ATK. The rest of the senior staff is basically the same, so I'm not sure what would have changed. 2. What exactly is so special about the colored liquids? What is the chemical make-up of them and their purpose? And are the liquids the same between difference brands? Is the red of one the same as the red as another? Or is the red of one have an equivalent to another (brandA->red = brandB->blue)? Because unless you know what the liquids are/do, you're just cargo culting. By some account the liquids to nothing practical: > Chemistry expert and former detergent chemist here, chiming in. Modulo some small semantic differences, you're 100% spot on. Functionally, there is zero difference in the formulations. Every powder dish detergent on the market comprises the same functional components, though the exact chemicals selected may vary. It's always some combination of detergent, anti-deposition agent, water conditioners, strong base, oxidizers, enzymes, buffers, \"processing aids\", and what I call \"foo foo juice\" - colors and fragrances. * [VIDEO]"},{"id":37462120,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-09","story":"To Make Dishwashers Great Again? (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Linked comment from a chemist breaking down detergent formulation.","quote":"comment by a chemists that breaks things down","text":"> The direction completely changed. Now it's all about fancier foods and restaurant stuff. Compare an episode from 2010 to now. Complete difference. Is the fanciness of ATK any different that CK's Milk Street? Season six of the latter had Jordanian, Brazillian (pizza), Turkish, falafel, Mexican, Greek cuisine; season five had Ethopian, Japan, Ukraine, Crete, etc : * [LINK] If anything it's Kimball that seems to have gone in the high falutin' direction. Regardless: if fancy is where the viewers/audience numbers are now, then that's the direction that either/both shows have to go to. What was done in 2010, or whenever, is irrelevant if the landsacape has changed. > That's literally why I watch it: because they know better than me and they put in the work. That's fine, but the authority should at least explain why so the more curious viewer could dig into things more. The YT link to the Technology Connections videos is to a comment by a chemists that breaks things down: * [VIDEO] For example: > Some pods, such as our top pick and runner-up pick, contain additional liquid cleaning agents in separate chambers that release when the PVA film dissolves. This design keeps liquid and powder separate until they are dispensed, allowing detergent boosters that best operate in liquid form to combine with powder detergent during a dishwasher’s cycle, enhancing their effectiveness. * [LINK]"},{"id":32712822,"author":"codewench","month":"2022-09","story":"Interest in heat pumps has increased dramatically in recent years","stance":"reference","why":"Follow-up video covers when dishwasher salt is needed.","quote":"only homes with specific water types","text":"The salts issue was actually mentioned in a follow up video [0], with the note that only homes with specific water types (excessively hard water if I remember correctly) will require them. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31294497,"author":"jchw","month":"2022-05","story":"Shaving is an example of how consumer products extract more money","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that pods are overpriced versus powder.","quote":"","text":"Add dishwasher pods to the list. It’s literally just smoke and mirrors for nearly the same formula as powder, only less of it per package. All those fancy colors? It’s die. It’s the same formula across the entire pod. So… Yep: more cost, more waste, and it has some reasonable downsides. For “convenience,” since I guess deciding how much to put into the tray is just too much work. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30235344,"author":"YourCupOTea","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Any piece of hardware that was more of game changer than you expected?","stance":"reference","why":"Follow-up covers why powders beat gels.","quote":"Powders can have both enzymes and bleach unlike gels.","text":"[VIDEO] He doesn't like them in the follow-up video. Powders can have both enzymes and bleach unlike gels."},{"id":29258946,"author":"jldugger","month":"2021-11","story":"How to grow sodium chloride crystals at home","stance":"reference","why":"Notes dishwasher salt is a European thing.","quote":"","text":"The only brand I even recognize is Finish, and they're a UK firm, not American. Technology Connections mentions[1] it's a European thing: \"I have never ever encountered one, and I don't think I've ever seen dishwasher salt sold in a store.\" [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_rBO8neWw04",6],["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1],["NRoUC9P1PmA",1]]},"D6h5dFyyUX0":{"wy":"Repeatedly offered as the quickest, most convincing demo of structured editing and why Lisp programmers love paredit.","q":{"x":"it's well worth the 3:40.","i":6062364,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5869977,"author":"Kaali","month":"2013-06","story":"Clojure Cookbook is now accepting contributions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short enthusiastic video on paredit","quote":"For a short and enthusiastic video on paredit, see","text":"For those not familiar with Emacs, IntelliJ IDEA with La Clojure is also an okay choice. I use IDEA for Python, Java and Scala; and still switch to Emacs when I code in Clojure. The reason is that Emacs has really strong toolset for LISP, where many of the features are missing from other environments, or implemented partially. Slime/swank (nrepl replaces it, but I'm just slowly transitioning to it), provides code completion, documentation lookup, REPL, macro expansion and even live code updates on a running application; which can be a bit of a pain to setup on other editors. And the integration in Emacs is really great, as the repl buffer is really just a text buffer, the same as the one you are coding in. But the biggest reason for me to code LISP in Emacs is paredit. It really makes all the trouble with balancing parenthesis and moving around the sexps really easy. It might seem counter intuitive and a bit troublesome at first, but I really recommend you to try it. For a short and enthusiastic video on paredit, see [VIDEO] -- and maybe the other Emacs Rocks! videos to learn more about modern Emacs."},{"id":33759369,"author":"gabiruh","month":"2022-11","story":"Paredit 25 released (after 8 years)","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates how powerful paredit is for structural editing of Lisp.","quote":"It shows how incredible paredit is.","text":"Yeah... a couple GIFs would. If I was them I would've just embedded this video from \"Emacs Rocks!\": [VIDEO] It shows how incredible paredit is."},{"id":33047531,"author":"medo-bear","month":"2022-10","story":"The Hidden Origins of Lisp (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Good example of structured editing for Lisp","quote":"here is a good example of what i mean [0].","text":"not op but for me structured editing is one of the biggest pluses when it comes to editing lisp code. here is a good example of what i mean [0]. btw autolisp standard formating is kind of an anti-example of good lisp formating. in emacs lots of excellent tools exist to make parantheses an extremely helpful feature [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":14419449,"author":"peterhadlaw","month":"2017-05","story":"Is Clojure dying, and what has Ruby got to do with it?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows manipulating code as a tree with paredit","quote":"You end up manipulating the \"tree\" of code vs the characters on a line.","text":"The parentheses end up serving as a tool to speed up the development process with tool like paredit. You end up manipulating the \"tree\" of code vs the characters on a line. Check this out: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8205519,"author":"tikhonj","month":"2014-08","story":"An editor for composed programs","stance":"rec","why":"Paredit in action","quote":"Also, here's a great video of Paredit in action","text":"Emacs's structured editing, in the style of paredit[1], sounds very similar to a text-based version of syntax-directed editing (SDE). Designed for Lisp and S-expressions, it's a system that lets you edit and navigate around nested expressions at a syntactic rather than textual language. As you type, the correct level of nesting is maintained and there are commands for moving and editing code in terms of s-expressions rather than characters. In practice, it's very easy to start using and very powerful... but requires using Lisp. It'll seem odd if you're not regularly a Lisp user. Happily, this idea can generalize well enough to distinctly non-Lisp languages. Structured Haskell mode[2] does this for Haskell, which has a less regular and sparser syntax than s-expressions. The documentation has a bunch of handy animations showing the various features, giving you an idea of how it works without having to install it yourself. This doesn't address the meat of the blog post—composing languages and grammars by taking advantage of structured editing—but it shows that the necessary foundation is possible with a perfectly normal text editor. It would be interesting to see if some of the ideas from Eco could be added to structured editing modes like this without requiring you to use an external, self-contained editor. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] Also, here's a great video of Paredit in action: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6965139,"author":"6cxs2hd6","month":"2013-12","story":"Lamdu - towards the next generation IDE","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on paredit adoption and structured editing","quote":"Magnar Sveen has a great video about this at","text":"There's a structured editing mode for Emacs called Paredit, to use for s-expression languages like Lisp, Racket, Clojure. Since the AST for such languages is s-expressions, and some people struggle with parens, this is an interesting \"fun size\" example of editing the AST. The thing is, paredit is quite challenging for most people to adopt, due to what you mentioned about the \"flow\" they've already learned. Magnar Sveen has a great video about this at [VIDEO] Best quote: \"If you think paredit is not for you, then you need to become the kind of person that paredit is for.\" EDIT: To clarify, Magnar is quoting technomancy a.k.a. Phil Hagelberg."},{"id":6970905,"author":"JackMorgan","month":"2013-12","story":"Emacs users are like Terry Pratchett’s Igors","stance":"rec","why":"Short paredit explanation","quote":"And here is a short minute paredit mode explanation","text":"If you pair a majority of the time, sure, make a standardized workstation with your team. My team uses viemu in VS, which the rest of our office detests, but hey, we are the ones pairing 99% of the time. When I am not pairing for the majority, I setup my machine to my tastes, and simply install a \"default\" editor for the few times someone else actually needs to type on my machine. Emacs and IDEA both have good ways to revert everything to defaults temporarily. I do not believe, however, that a perfectly customized setup has only small gains. For example, consider two tools that I have never seen as defaults in any editor or IDE: rainbow parens/brackets and paredit mode. Rainbow parens just highlights pairs: [LINK] And here is a short minute paredit mode explanation: [VIDEO] When actually editing code, those two tools save me several hundred keystrokes an hour. And those are just the first two tools I thought to use as an example out of the entire toolbox that is emacs. If I was to now give up just those two tools, I would be sentencing myself to the mental equivalent of tying five pound weights to my legs for the marathon that is the rest of my programming career. Why would I intentionally require myself to type more keys per hour to do the same editing, rather than just learning a faster way to edit? This is, obviously, also my argument for learning the vim editing system (which I also use in emacs)."},{"id":6062364,"author":"wnesensohn","month":"2013-07","story":"A preview of LightTable 0.5","stance":"rec","why":"Short episode worth watching to learn paredit","quote":"it's well worth the 3:40.","text":"I'll be honest with you: I'm maybe not the one to give proper advice on paredit. I'm using C# on my day job, and as such have no use for paredit (I do use ViEmu for VS though, which makes VS bearable) for most of my programming tasks. Also, I've never done anything in clojure. I expect it to be a similar experience to CL, but the different types of parenthesis might have an effect on using paredit efficiently. That said, I found that the following mapping really makes a huge difference when working with sexps & evil-mode: C-l: paredit-forward-slurp-sexp C-k: paredit-backward-barf-sexp C-j: paredit-backward-slurp-sexp C-h: paredit-forward-barf-sexp Your mileage may vary, of course. I mapped it this way because my fingers dictated it, and it plays well with vim/evil. As to how to actually use these commands: Try them out on a few sexps, it'll be clear to you pretty soon. Also be sure to check out Emacs Rocks! ep14 ( [VIDEO] ), it's well worth the 3:40. I actually find learning paredit to be very similar to learning vim: You start with almost nothing (maybe hjkl in vim, but even that takes a while to get used to), and slowly add new tricks to your tool belt. Also like with vim, everyone has their own style of using the tools. Of course, there's a ton of other things in paredit which can improve your efficiency which I haven't added to my muscle-memory yet. It's just that I found barfing/slurping to be the most bang for the buck, so I added them first."},{"id":13578512,"author":"m1el","month":"2017-02","story":"Why Don't We Have a General-Purpose Tree Editor? (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Paredit demo","quote":"Emacs Rocks! Episode 14: Paredit","text":"Emacs has ParEdit minor mode which is a general-purpose tree editor. [LINK] edit: paredit demos: Productive Emacs: Paredit [LINK] Emacs Rocks! Episode 14: Paredit: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ruNFcH-KibY",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1]]},"zb7Bs98KmnY":{"wy":"HN's go-to link for correcting the lightbulb conspiracy myth, giving an engineer's view of the lifespan versus efficiency tradeoff.","q":{"x":"highly recommended if you want an engineers perspective, with no 'great conspiracy' hype.","i":40944255,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40944255,"author":"samat","month":"2024-07","story":"The Great Lightbulb Conspiracy (2014)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Engineer's view of the lightbulb cartel without conspiracy hype.","quote":"highly recommended if you want an engineers perspective, with no 'great conspiracy' hype.","text":"There is an excellent video about Lightbulb Cartel by Technology Connections, highly recommended if you want an engineers perspective, with no 'great conspiracy' hype. [VIDEO]"},{"id":49129743,"author":"mreome","month":"2026-07","story":"You have been mislead about lightbulbs","stance":"rec","why":"Deeper dive into why bulb lifespans were complicated.","quote":"For an even deeper dive into this topic...","text":"For an even deeper dive into this topic... Technology Connections: Longer-lasting light bulbs: it was complicated [VIDEO]"},{"id":42262871,"author":"RulerOf","month":"2024-11","story":"The capacitor that Apple soldered incorrectly at the factory","stance":"rec","why":"Lifetime limit sits near the sweet spot of lumens, life, energy.","quote":"Technology Connections explained this well in a video about a year ago","text":"It's true that the Phoebus cartel arranged to have light bulbs die after a certain number of hours, but bulb lifetime is a trade off between lumens, filament life, and energy consumption. The cartel-defined lifetime limit sits very close to the sweet spot for all of those metrics for incandescent bulbs. Technology Connections explained this well in a video about a year ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41184757,"author":"torginus","month":"2024-08","story":"East Germany invented 'unbreakable' drinking glasses","stance":"rec","why":"Running bulbs cooler extends life but hurts light efficiency.","quote":"Actually Technology Connections made a great youtube video on this","text":"Actually Technology Connections made a great youtube video on this: [VIDEO] The gist is that by running bulbs at a lower power, you can greatly prolong its life, but the downside is that it doesn't heat up as much, and since emissions spectrum correllates with temperature, ends up being much worse at converting electricity to light, which ends up being not worth it."},{"id":40703804,"author":"mmkhd","month":"2024-06","story":"How to get stuff repaired when the manufacturer don't wanna: take 'em to court","stance":"rec","why":"The bulb cartel was real, but with legitimate reasons.","quote":"Nice video from the well known channel Technology Connections","text":"Nice video from the well known channel Technology Connections: [VIDEO] => It's complicated. Yes, there was a cartel, but it was not all bad. There were legitimate reasons to go for 1000h light bulbs."},{"id":40703828,"author":"Youden","month":"2024-06","story":"How to get stuff repaired when the manufacturer don't wanna: take 'em to court","stance":"rec","why":"Shows bulb lifespan balanced light output, efficiency, and longevity.","quote":"It wasn't as simple as them wanting to make more money","text":"It wasn't as simple as them wanting to make more money: [VIDEO] Key points from an AI summary: - Incandescent bulbs had to balance factors like light output, efficiency, and lifespan - hotter filaments produced brighter, whiter light but reduced bulb lifespan. - Longer-lasting bulbs were less efficient and produced dimmer, yellower light, so they were not simply \"better\" products being suppressed. - The 1,000 hour target was a reasonable compromise that balanced these competing priorities, not necessarily a sinister plot. - Even after the Phoebus cartel dissolved, the 1,000 hour lifespan remained the industry standard for general-purpose incandescent bulbs."},{"id":37827759,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2023-10","story":"The Anatomy of a Smart Bulb","stance":"rec","why":"Thoughtful deep dive on the bulb lifetime tradeoff.","quote":"Technology Connections does a thoughtful deep dive in to this story","text":"Honestly that story is really not as simple as people suggest it is. There is a tradeoff between bulb life and brightness and standardization may have been good for the industry. Technology Connections does a thoughtful deep dive in to this story in this recent video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38063593,"author":"PaulKeeble","month":"2023-10","story":"Apple's Blue Ocean","stance":"rec","why":"History of the bulb lifetime versus brightness tradeoff.","quote":"You can watch about the history of it on Technology connections.","text":"Its a bit of a myth for ever lasting bulbs, its more complicated than that. The life time was a trade off of colour and brightness. If you made them very red shifted and quite dim then there are bulbs that are very old still around, but they don't produce much light and never did. The harder you drive the filament the better the light properties but the quicker it died. The industry choose a point for trade offs as a standard. You can watch about the history of it on Technology connections. [VIDEO] Saying that the Dubai Philips LED bulbs really do last a lot longer since they have twice the filaments and run the filaments a lot less hard as a result (as well as being more efficient). It is possible nowadays to make an LED that wont fail anytime soon although LEDs are already much better than incandescents in that regard but there are ones that will likely survive your lifetime available at a higher price but only in Dubai. Most LED bulbs are designed to fail sooner, but they are also cheaper. Those 10k recharge batteries are absolutely coming and we will have them in a few years time, li-ion as it stands today will simply be obsolete."},{"id":37046507,"author":"audunw","month":"2023-08","story":"List of the longest-lasting incandescent light bulbs","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why shorter-lived bulbs were more efficient.","quote":"TL;DW: (if I rember correctly) Bulbs with shorter lifespans are generally more energy efficient.","text":"Technology Connections talked a lot about this and the supposed planned obsolescence of light bulbs [VIDEO] TL;DW: (if I rember correctly) Bulbs with shorter lifespans are generally more energy efficient. There was good reasons to not making bulb that last forever."},{"id":46018451,"author":"artimaeis","month":"2025-11","story":"Samsung's 60% DRAM price hike signals a new phase of global memory tightening","stance":"reference","why":"Brightness versus longevity tradeoff in incandescent bulbs.","quote":"The brighter the bulb, the shorter the lifespan.","text":"The Phoebus cartel didn't collude just to make the light bulbs have a shorter lifespan. They upped the standard illumination a bulb emitted so that consumers needed fewer of them to see well. With an incandescent you have a kind of sliding scale of brightness:longevity (with curves on each end that quickly go exponential, hence the longest lasting light bulb that's so dim you can barely read by its light). The brighter the bulb, the shorter the lifespan. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40249709,"author":"dtx1","month":"2024-05","story":"Lithium-free sodium batteries exit the lab and enter US production","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> There are some original Edison bulbs that still work, yet you'd be lucky to get a year out of a modern incandescent. [VIDEO] > Same thing happened to LED bulbs. Because LED Bulbs cook your LEDs and are stupid design. Get a permanent LED fixture with decent passive cooling and they will last for decades. Adapting LEDs into the same Plug as Incandescent lighting just does not work."},{"id":38131483,"author":"roywiggins","month":"2023-11","story":"Sam Bankman-Fried and the effective altruism delusion","stance":"reference","why":"Bulbs trade efficiency against lifespan.","quote":"even lightbulbs have to make tradeoffs between efficiency and lifespan.","text":"And even lightbulbs have to make tradeoffs between efficiency and lifespan. Yeah, all else being equal the efficient bulb is better, but it's quite rare that all else actually is equal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38065294,"author":"abhinavk","month":"2023-10","story":"Apple's Blue Ocean","stance":"reference","why":"Counters the planned obsolescence myth about bulbs.","quote":"Technology Connections has an interesting video on this.","text":"> Solid state batteries with 10k charge/discharge cycles are never going to happen for the same reason incandescent bulbs are purposefully manufactured by every single lightbulb manufacturer to fail when they figured out a hundred years ago how to make them last forever. Technology Connections has an interesting video on this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36751093,"author":"eropple","month":"2023-07","story":"Infrared may no longer be a punchline, as IEEE approves 9.6Gbps wireless light","stance":"reference","why":"Clarifies what the Phoebus cartel actually was.","quote":"Which wasn't what you think it is, and is invoked by people who don't understand it.","text":"> It's been such a consistent problem for so long that it has shades of the Phoebus cartel about it. Which wasn't what you think it is, and is invoked by people who don't understand it. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uxykI30fS54",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["EpRrD58Ah3Q",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1],["-s5szfPYKY4",1],["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["hx7BXih7zx8",1],["tTBWfkE7BXU",1],["m7PVZixO35c",1],["fuFlMtZmvY0",1],["rQhzEnWCgHA",1],["AE1gaNO9nj0",1]]},"RfdZTZQvuCo":{"wy":"HN commenters share it in vaccine threads as a quick, entertaining explanation of vaccination in layman's terms.","q":{"x":"This really drives home the point of herd immunity ( in ~ 90 seconds ).","i":18583366,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18583366,"author":"rixrax","month":"2018-12","story":"The Worst Measles Outbreak In The European Union","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Drives home herd immunity in about 90 seconds.","quote":"This really drives home the point of herd immunity ( in ~ 90 seconds ).","text":"URL: [VIDEO] Penn and Teller on Vaccinations. This really drives home the point of herd immunity ( in ~ 90 seconds )."},{"id":8957747,"author":"iLoch","month":"2015-01","story":"To Protect His Son, a Father Asks School to Bar Unvaccinated Children","stance":"rec","why":"Explains vaccinations in layman's terms.","quote":"I think Penn and Teller[1] did a good job of explaining vaccinations in layman's terms","text":"I think Penn and Teller[1] did a good job of explaining vaccinations in layman's terms (though I doubt anyone here actually needs an explanation of how or why vaccinations work.) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8967926,"author":"rebootthesystem","month":"2015-01","story":"How an Amish missionary caused 2014's massive measles outbreak","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as a helpful explanation of vaccination.","quote":"Perhaps this might help","text":"The problem might very well be that our level of scientific education isn't in step with the realities of the world we live in. If someone wants to get a good grasp for the entire \"story\" of vaccination they have to have a basic understanding of evolution by natural selection. Fully 42% of the US population does not \"believe\" in evolution, which is further proof of the level of ignorance we are dealing with. I put \"believe\" in quotes because evolution by natural selection is not something one believes, that's nonsense. It is something one accepts or rejects based on over 150 years of evidence across a range of scientific disciplines. Perhaps this might help: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8391462,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2014-09","story":"CDC confirms first Ebola case diagnosed in US","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why vaccinating is better regardless.","quote":"Penn & Teller ably explain","text":"It is worse than that - even if her idiotic claims were true it would still be better to vaccinate. Penn & Teller ably explain [VIDEO]"},{"id":8080844,"author":"tokenadult","month":"2014-07","story":"“You're not allowed science any more”","stance":"rec","why":"Communicates vaccination risk and benefit ratio clearly, with strong language.","quote":"will help communicate the risk and benefit ratio of vaccination more clearly than documents written in a more clinical tone.","text":"There are a lot of comments here about the issue of how to communicate risk of different personal medical choices to patients who do not themselves have medical training. It is a tough problem. Professional stage magicians, and avid skeptics, Penn and Teller had an episode of their cable television series Bullshit report on the issue of risks and benefits of vaccination. Maybe a clip from their TV show (with language that many families would find inappropriate for viewing with their children) will help communicate the risk and benefit ratio of vaccination more clearly than documents written in a more clinical tone. [VIDEO] (short clip above, long clip below) [LINK]"},{"id":6344235,"author":"broodbucket","month":"2013-09","story":"Infectious disease doesn't care why you're unvaccinated","stance":"rec","why":"Amusing Penn & Teller clip on vaccinations.","quote":"here's an amusing Penn & Teller clip that shares your view.","text":"The paper saying that the MMR vaccine causes autism (which is where the whole concept came from) was fraudulent, so it's not just \"well, a side effect is better than the disease\", it's total misguidance. Also, here's an amusing Penn & Teller clip that shares your view. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7564491,"author":"willvarfar","month":"2014-04","story":"Tamiflu: Millions wasted on flu drug, claims major report","stance":"reference","why":"Penn & Teller on vaccines, though the commenter remains conflicted.","quote":"Here's Penn & Teller talking about Vaccines","text":"In Sweden, almost everyone was vaccinated with Pandemrix [LINK] I think this is not the same exact drug as Tamiflu, but the parallels - mass vaccination etc - are worth drawing. The scary thing about Pandemrix is that some people - mostly children and young adults - immediately got narcolepsy [LINK] Now the various investigations have all said they can find no link between narcolepsy and Pandemrix, and yet they don't really explain the suddenness of it all. Seems a hell of a coincidence. Narcolepsy is not some silly little \"sleep disorder\". This was really brought home to me - literally - by knowing someone who's child got it hours after their Pandemrix jab =( The micro-problem here is the Swedish state trying to avoid paying for care (its untreatable, as I understand it) and all that wrangling about taking responsibility. I think most parents of suffers are really out to share around the burden of responsibility, rather than caring so much about the costs of care. The macro-problem is convincing people that the next time we are told to go get a jab, we should :( Here's Penn & Teller talking about Vaccines: [VIDEO] Science isn't going to win over my wife as easily as all that. And I am very divided myself, however rational I try and be."}],"r":[["HfHgUu_8KgA",1],["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["nBNTAmLRmUg",1],["y5UT04p5f7U",1],["n63XXHY4A7I",1],["3bxcc3SM_KA",1],["sG9UMMq2dz4",1]]},"l70iRcSxqzo":{"wy":"HN commenters call it one of the best, most unbiased explanations of what actually happened to Mt. Gox and its missing coins.","q":{"x":"One of the best summaries of the Mt Gox incident, as well as the fallout and subsequent investigations that I've watched.","i":16241161,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":19399311,"author":"pakitan","month":"2019-03","story":"Mark Karpeles Gets Suspended Jail Term","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Unbiased explanation of what happened to the missing Mt Gox coins.","quote":"Kim Nilsson's work is as good and unbiased as it comes and it pretty much explains everything you need to know about the missing coins","text":"Kim Nilsson's work is as good and unbiased as it comes and it pretty much explains everything you need to know about the missing coins: [VIDEO] [LINK] Btw, with your 350 coins, I hope you've already submitted a claim and are planning to vote on the upcoming Civil Rehabilitation plan."},{"id":16241161,"author":"stuxnet79","month":"2018-01","story":"Tokyo-based cryptocurrency exchange hacked, losing $530M: NHK","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Summary of the Mt Gox incident, fallout and investigations.","quote":"One of the best summaries of the Mt Gox incident, as well as the fallout and subsequent investigations that I've watched.","text":"Kim Nilsson's group have been investigating the Mt Gox incident for years and he gave a presentation some time ago that summarized some of their findings ( [VIDEO] ). One of the best summaries of the Mt Gox incident, as well as the fallout and subsequent investigations that I've watched."},{"id":15698044,"author":"otoburb","month":"2017-11","story":"Mt. Gox Bankruptcy Wasn't Really a Bust","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nilsson's investigation and presentation on cracking MtGox","quote":"check out Kim Nilsson's great writeups[1][2] and presentation[3].","text":"If anybody wants the full sordid history from an investigative standpoint, check out Kim Nilsson's great writeups[1][2] and presentation[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26043309,"author":"Groxx","month":"2021-02","story":"Mt. Gox demise as told by a Bitcoin insider","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining outside-researcher talk tracing the missing Mt Gox money.","quote":"It's quite a rollercoaster of failures.","text":"There's also this entertaining presentation from an outside researcher (tracing down the missing money) from 3 years ago (I don't know what's up with the framerate, but really, it's worth watching): [VIDEO] It's quite a rollercoaster of failures."},{"id":15699593,"author":"jandrese","month":"2017-11","story":"Mt. Gox Bankruptcy Wasn't Really a Bust","stance":"rec","why":"MtGox finances and its poor security when Karpeles bought it","quote":"Here's a talk that discusses the financial situation at MtGox","text":"It is not unfair to say that the majority of Bitcoin exchanges to date have been either intentionally fraudulant or incompetently run. This includes MtGox. Here's a talk that discusses the financial situation at MtGox and the raging dumpster fire that its security system was when Mark Karpeles bought it. [VIDEO] It's hard to say if the poor security was due to a staggering level of inexperience and thoughtlessness, or if the previous owners had left it open so they could pilfer money as easily as possible before the whole house of cards collapsed. It's hard to imagine a financial services administrator leaving an unsecured Windows file share open to the Internet with all of the private keys on it, but somehow this was considered to be not a problem at MtGox."},{"id":24780692,"author":"jandrese","month":"2020-10","story":"Crypto scammers piggybacking Trump’s Twitter, cloning Medium, stealing crypto","stance":"reference","why":"Postmortem analysis suggesting incompetence at Mt Gox.","quote":"","text":"Nobody knows for sure, but the postmortem suggests that it might have been straight up incompetence. However, it's certainly possible to consider that the \"incompetence\" was on purpose to hide the fact that he was stealing from the company. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15960920,"author":"wu-ikkyu","month":"2017-12","story":"Bitcoin exchange Youbit shuts after second hack attack","stance":"reference","why":"Investigation into where the stolen Mt Gox coins went.","quote":"","text":">Is anyone seriously tracking where these \"stolen\" coins from these past several hacks are going? Yeah [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2Op3QLzMgSY",1],["xP5-iIeKXE8",1],["e0elNU0iOMY",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["oqu5DjzOBF8",1]]},"rvkEpstd9os":{"wy":"Boeing threads cite it as early evidence of deep cultural and quality problems at Boeing, years before the 737 MAX crashes.","q":{"x":"AlJazeera deserves respect for this excellent footage they got from inside the factory.","i":39122504,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39122504,"author":"4d4m","month":"2024-01","story":"Designed by clowns supervised by monkeys: Internal Boeing messages (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent footage from inside the Boeing factory","quote":"AlJazeera deserves respect for this excellent footage they got from inside the factory.","text":"No matter how you may feel about (all) nation-state sponsored media outlets, AlJazeera deserves respect for this excellent footage they got from inside the factory. It's a few years old, but should have raised more alarm by now: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39677693,"author":"asteroidz","month":"2024-03","story":"Boeing's woes continue as 50 injured on Australia-New Zealand flight","stance":"rec","why":"Remarkable 2014 Boeing investigation","quote":"This video (though from 2014) was remarkable","text":"Another worrisome development: [LINK] Al-Jazeera has really been on fire digging into Boeing. This video (though from 2014) was remarkable: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38981651,"author":"cjbgkagh","month":"2024-01","story":"Updates on Grounding of Boeing 737 MAX 9 Aircraft","stance":"rec","why":"Great investigation of Boeing issues years before MCAS","quote":"Al Jazeera did a great investigation ~ 9 years ago","text":"The MCAS is what brought Boeing issues into the zeitgeist, but I was aware of issues at Boeing ~ 18 years ago from friends who had worked there and Al Jazeera did a great investigation ~ 9 years ago ( [VIDEO] ). Boeing used to be the gold standard and as someone who researches such gold standards it was disheartening to see them give that up. But that happened very slowly and started a long time ago. Clearly diluting focus away from safety is an issue for whatever the reason especially when the safety of airplanes is declining. This should also be taken in context that many aspects of manufacturing have improved greatly over the past 20 years so even if everything else stayed the same the general safety should have been greatly improved. So the drag on safety by the culture is bad enough to overcome these 'free' gains and end up with even less safe planes should highlight just how bad that effect is."},{"id":21460858,"author":"piva00","month":"2019-11","story":"Boeing whistleblower raises doubts over 787 oxygen system","stance":"rec","why":"Eye-opening report on Charleston plant allegations","quote":"this report is quite eye opening.","text":"Al Jazeera has a long report on YouTube [0] covering the allegations around Charleston, I know that they can be biased against the US but this report is quite eye opening. Even more given the interview with a VP (or director, can't remember exactly the position) being cut short by the PR person involved, the reports from workers inside the factory stating they wouldn't fly a plane built there, etc. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20237863,"author":"blt","month":"2019-06","story":"Capt. “Sully” Sullenberger Slams Boeing for Inadequate Pilot Training","stance":"rec","why":"Boeing's sketchy manufacturing and QA process during 787 release","quote":"Check out this documentary on Boeing's sketchy manufacturing & QA process back in the days of the 787 release.","text":"Check out this documentary on Boeing's sketchy manufacturing & QA process back in the days of the 787 release. It seems that the company has been toxic for a while now. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19561716,"author":"bb101","month":"2019-04","story":"Lack of redundancies on Boeing 737 MAX baffles some involved in developing it","stance":"rec","why":"Interviews with ex-employees and engineers on Boeing culture","quote":"It's worth watching Al Jazeera's series on the production of the 787 Dreamliner","text":"It's worth watching Al Jazeera's series on the production of the 787 Dreamliner, and related cultural issues at Boeing after their merger with McDonnell Douglas [1]. Personally, I think it's worth taking with a grain of salt as it appears quite one-sided, but the interviews with Boeing ex-employees and head engineers are enlightening, and could go some way to explaining the lack of redundant systems (i.e. cost and time). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40059439,"author":"amiga386","month":"2024-04","story":"Boeing whistleblower says 787 fleet should be grounded","stance":"reference","why":"Revealed 787 problems including battery fires","quote":"This revealed a host of problems with the 787 Dreamliner, mainly that its battery packs catch fire","text":"You're referring to The Boeing 787: Broken Dreams published in 2014 by Al Jazeera [VIDEO] This revealed a host of problems with the 787 Dreamliner, mainly that its battery packs catch fire (which is why it was grounded by the FAA from January to April 2013 to revise the battery design). This is the plane relevant to this latest whistleblower, but not the same plane as the 737 MAX, the one with the MCAS system and the door plugs missing bolts"},{"id":39338590,"author":"eastbound","month":"2024-02","story":"How Boeing Lost Its Way [video]","stance":"reference","why":"More damning than another Boeing documentary","quote":"This documentary is not as damning as Al Jezeera’s documentary on the 2013 airframe mishaps","text":"It describes how it used providers instead of internalization to push down on the prices. However, to anyone who might expect it, it doesn’t tell that Boeing has fired 900 QAs out of 3000 back in 2021. This documentary is not as damning as Al Jezeera’s documentary on the 2013 airframe mishaps: [VIDEO] Maybe it’s a European bias, but I’d like to see more shame (and jail time) upon the Boeing management, down to the guy at the bottom who signed off the bolt work without even looking at the plane, which is criminal. But I’m fair, I’d also enjoy the management of Volkswagen going to jail, like that manager who went to holidays through a USA connection and was intercepted by the US border and is still rotting in jail."},{"id":39189367,"author":"lqet","month":"2024-01","story":"Boeing withdraws bid for safety exemption for Boeing 737 MAX 7","stance":"reference","why":"Summarizes fuselage fit problems and whistleblower retaliation","quote":"Then you really shouldn't watch this video","text":"> I love flying on the Boeing 787 Dreamliner Then you really shouldn't watch this video: [VIDEO] TLDW: Critical Dreamliner fuselage parts didn't fit together because workers at the subcontractor (Ducommun) traced a template with magic marker onto the material and cut and drilled the part by hand. Officially, the parts were manufactured by high precision CNC machinery with extremely small tolerances. At Boeing, if drill holes didn't match up in parts A and B, workers were told to just put the parts together and re-drill the hole in part B through the hole in part A, or hammer the parts into place. Internal Boeing investigators who found out and documented the issues were threatened to be sued if they spoke up. They were later fired, after the US government allegedly leaked their identities to Boeing. Here, a Boeing employee speaks about the work culture in the manufacturing plant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27659997,"author":"piva00","month":"2021-06","story":"Citing a serious flight test incident, FAA slows Boeing 777X certification","stance":"reference","why":"Investigates South Carolina plant culture versus unionized Washington","quote":"the employees themselves are caught on video stating \"I wouldn't fly on a plane made here\".","text":"There are reports about the SC plant making the 787 from Al Jazeera [1] where they investigate exactly that. And the unionisation helps a lot in the Washington plant to allow employees to speak up with no fear of reprimands. I do believe being unionised impacts a lot the work environment psychological safety, allowing employees of the plant to halt production if they don't think work is being performed up to their standards, in NC there is no such provision and the employees themselves are caught on video stating \"I wouldn't fly on a plane made here\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26435808,"author":"lqet","month":"2021-03","story":"Emirates’ Tim Clark says Boeing ‘not getting it’ on 737 Max, 787","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"many workers of the South Carolina plant would never fly a 787.","text":"According to this newer documentation, many workers of the South Carolina plant would never fly a 787. Because they know how they are being made. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24306431,"author":"piva00","month":"2020-08","story":"Boeing yanks eight 787s from service over structural issue","stance":"reference","why":"Charleston plant quality control segment","quote":"The part about the plant has an interview with an employee recording other employees worried about safety, training and other issues.","text":"Interestingly I found out about Boeing's N.C. plant quality control issues from an Al-Jazeera report on the 787 [0] way back in 2014. The part about the plant has an interview with an employee recording other employees worried about safety, training and other issues. There is a Q&A with a Boeing VP that is interrupted by some PR/legal aide when questions about quality issues in Charleston start. It's an ongoing issue for quite a long time... [0] [VIDEO] (I've timestamped to the relevant part about the Charleston plant)"},{"id":20841914,"author":"darknoon","month":"2019-08","story":"Boeing's Crashes Expose Systemic Failings","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I saw this documentary about issues with the 787 back in 2014, and it was clear that something was massively wrong at Boeing","text":"I saw this documentary about issues with the 787 back in 2014, and it was clear that something was massively wrong at Boeing: [VIDEO] The engineers doing the work know what it takes to make a good airplane. Let them do their jobs."},{"id":20221325,"author":"faeww","month":"2019-06","story":"Boeing employee: I would not put my family on a Max plane","stance":"reference","why":"Points to major problems at Boeing","quote":"The story builds up over time -- it points to major problems at Boeing.","text":"The story builds up over time -- it points to major problems at Boeing. e.g. [VIDEO] (The Boeing 787: Broken Dreams)"},{"id":20292750,"author":"BorRagnarok","month":"2019-06","story":"FAA Finds New Risk on 737 Max, Orders Boeing to Make Changes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"They've been sourcing out their own work to Boeing since the 787 Dreamliner","text":"I still don't trust the FAA at all. They've been sourcing out their own work to Boeing since the 787 Dreamliner [0]. That's seven years ago. I don't think this will make them change their practices. The 787 fiasco certainly didn't. Personally I'd be happiest if the world just scrapped all the Max-es. Just scrap 'em, let Boeing go out of business, it's what they deserve now, they shouldn't exist anymore as a company. There are enough businesses that happily create planes without putting profit before people. It's really too bad that there's no footage of either of the crashes, because if people could see what happened they would never fly one of those things again. That's why people don't fly zeppelins anymore. Not because of what people said or read, but because they saw. The Max deserves the same. Do you want to fly in a plane which was created using the deaths of over 300 people to finalize the design? [0] The Boeing 787: Broken Dreams, Al Jazeera Investigations, [VIDEO]"},{"id":15767193,"author":"curun1r","month":"2017-11","story":"Workers at Amazon's main Italian hub, German warehouses strike on Black Friday","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on Boeing's South Carolina 787 plant","quote":"The SC Boeing plant doesn't exactly come off looking very good in the Aljazeera documentary","text":"The SC Boeing plant doesn't exactly come off looking very good in the Aljazeera documentary [1] on the 787. Just having a job shouldn't necessarily be the goal...the quality of the job and the rights of the workers to advocate for the safety of workers and the public are also things the Unions have traditionally done. I don't usually advocate for or against unions...I think they make sense in some situations and less so in others, but the Boeing case doesn't seem to be a good argument against unions. Boeing prospered tremendously when working with the unions in Washington state and it seems that a lot of their recent anti-union behavior is part of a number of decisions they've made that are quite short-sighted. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["T6X2uwlQGQM",1],["vgT4Y30DkaA",1],["j2dHFC31VtQ",1]]},"AD5aAd8Oy84":{"wy":"Linked whenever people ask why a Thunderbolt cable costs so much; the CT scans show the construction difference clearly.","q":{"x":"if you're interested in why the Thunderbolt cable costs more than charging cables, some folks put one through a CT scanner recently","i":38164510,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46751735,"author":"ssl-3","month":"2026-01","story":"We X-Rayed a Suspicious FTDI USB Cable","stance":"rec","why":"Lumafield CT scans of different 18650 battery cells","quote":"It's a good watch, and I learned some new stuff","text":"Lumifield quite recently showed on Adam Savage's Tested again, with some literal insights on a reasonably-diverse array of different 18650 cells: [VIDEO] It's a good watch, and I learned some new stuff about some things that I only knew a little bit about before."},{"id":42654643,"author":"tiffanyh","month":"2025-01","story":"Thunderbolt 5 vs. Thunderbolt 4: Everything you need to know","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Thunderbolt cable is expensive by comparing construction","quote":"did a video on why Apple Thunderbolt-4 is so expensive ($130), by looking at the detail in construction vs cheap alternatives.","text":"Adam Savage (Myth Buster) did a video on why Apple Thunderbolt-4 is so expensive ($130), by looking at the detail in construction vs cheap alternatives. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42547640,"author":"lloeki","month":"2024-12","story":"EU law mandating universal chargers for devices comes into force","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the cable is better on every metric than cheap ones","quote":"The cable is simply factually better on every metric than the hand-soldered 4 wire el cheapo one","text":"[VIDEO] Maybe you could consider Apple cables to be overengineered , but I can readily understand why a cable built this way - irrespective of manufacturer - is not overpriced . And it's not just about a chip. The cable is simply factually better on every metric than the hand-soldered 4 wire el cheapo one at 15~16 min. There is no cult in that. Now if one only needs to carry USB 2.0 speeds or a limited amount of power it'd be ridiculous to buy a 100+ or even 50+ cable. That doesn't make the cable overpriced either, merely overspecced for that use case."},{"id":39247564,"author":"al_borland","month":"2024-02","story":"Vision Pro Teardown – Why those fake eyes look so weird","stance":"rec","why":"Scans of Apple cable internals versus cheap Amazon cables","quote":"this video shows scans of what is inside Apple’s cable vs cheap cables off Amazon.","text":"For those interested, this video shows scans of what is inside Apple’s cable vs cheap cables off Amazon. [VIDEO] For those who prefer to read and want to look at the scans in more detail, that can be found here: [LINK]"},{"id":38164510,"author":"wlesieutre","month":"2023-11","story":"In Defense of RAM","stance":"rec","why":"CT scan shows why Thunderbolt cables cost more","quote":"if you're interested in why the Thunderbolt cable costs more than charging cables, some folks put one through a CT scanner recently","text":"Bit of a stretch to call that a \"charging cable.\" Their 240W rated 2 meter USB-C charging cable is $30 [LINK] But if you're interested in why the Thunderbolt cable costs more than charging cables, some folks put one through a CT scanner recently: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38423083,"author":"gjsman-1000","month":"2023-11","story":"Is there an updated list for verified good USB-C cables?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows why the $130 cable is not overpriced","quote":"Apple’s $130 3’ Thunderbolt cable (the pinnacle of cables) actually is not overpriced","text":"You might want to see Adam Savage’s video about why, say, Apple’s $130 3’ Thunderbolt cable (the pinnacle of cables) actually is not overpriced. It also gives a sense as to what a quality cable is like. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46751213,"author":"shagie","month":"2026-01","story":"We X-Rayed a Suspicious FTDI USB Cable","stance":"reference","why":"CT scans of cables, with a funny Apollo computer comparison","quote":"this cable has more computing power than the Apollo guidance computer","text":"That tickled a memory of a video... and I hunted it up. Adam Savage's Tested : Look Inside Apple's $130 USB-C Cable - [VIDEO] (1 minute in \"we've been saying that our phones have more computing power than the Apollo guidance computer but I'm positive now that this cable has more computing power than the Apollo guidance computer\") That video is a look at cables (not just Apple's) with Lumafield's CT Scan."},{"id":38959034,"author":"samstave","month":"2024-01","story":"Generating SVG for the prime knots","stance":"reference","why":"Reference for advanced cable construction","quote":"See here for reference to advanced cabling","text":"As a knotable person myself, this is AMAZING. Some thoughts on application of this knowledge would be to look at the patterns as you have described as cross-sections of rope weaving with the circular looming as the individual bobbins/spinny-things in an industrial loom - so rather that just woven-sheeth and full spin style cabling, one might achieve some really incredible properties in the woven elements along a axis such as these represent. Especially if you further differentiate btwn material and woven state (Are you weaving in an already spun set of filiments? What are the materials for the various inputs, and even further - imagine you have a set of elements in the loom where youre certain threads are the static, more rigid scaffold - like woven titanium strands which then feed into another loom which is weaving in the kevlar or other materials including a core of optics which is protected by the outer woven sheath from these patterns of 2D knots stretched out along an axis - certain elements can be printed such like the articulating spine of a snake. It could make a machinable-high-tensile strength cable with an optical core with protected turn radii (titanium snake spine) See here for reference to advanced cabling: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["o0fG_lnVhHw",1],["mScpHTIi-kM",1],["6BhV-o77RqQ",1],["UF8uR6Z6KLc",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["ZfuierUvx1A",1],["oAVjF_7ensg",1],["3GVO-MfIl1Q",1],["msN87y-iEx0",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1]]},"If61baWF4GE":{"wy":"HN users shared it during the early 2022 invasion as a clear, map-driven overview of Russia's economic and strategic motives.","q":{"x":"is an excellent overview of the history that lead to this moment","i":30531931,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31351495,"author":"cycomanic","month":"2022-05","story":"Finland will seek NATO membership immediately","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Analysis of Russia's invasion motives, including military and Black Sea resource reasons.","quote":"I highly recommend watching it.","text":"RealLifeLore has an excellent analysis of the reasons for Russia's invasion [1], including the military motivations. I highly recommend watching it. In particular the reasoning around gas and oil reserves in the Black Sea have not been given the due attention in the media IMO. He doesn't talk about Finland, but I would imagine the fact that it would broaden the front for a land-based invasion is what motivates Russia's opposition. That and generally having gotten away with being a bully and getting what you want for so long. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30531931,"author":"localhost","month":"2022-03","story":"Statement from Roman Abramovich","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent overview of history behind the invasion: oil and security.","quote":"is an excellent overview of the history that lead to this moment","text":"This YouTube video titled \"Why Russia is invading Ukraine\"? [1] is an excellent overview of the history that lead to this moment. tl;dr oil and security [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30560150,"author":"fleddr","month":"2022-03","story":"Why John Mearsheimer Blames the U.S. for the Crisis in Ukraine","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rational reasons Ukraine turning west threatens Russia.","quote":"I found this video to be very insightful","text":"People take the guilt question far too personal. Consider it this way: if you stick your finger into a nest of hornets, whom is to blame if you get hurt? You can say the hornet (Russia) is always too blame. Or, you can acknowledge that hornets are dangerous and best not provoked. Willingly provoking them if there is the alternative of not doing that, can be considered irresponsible. As to why Ukraine turning western is considered a provocation, there's a lot of simplistic \"fatalistic madman\" narratives. I found this video to be very insightful: [VIDEO] It sums up a few very rational reasons that when combined, add up to a serious existential threat to Russia as a country, as a concept. Mind you, I'm no Russia apologist, I'm just trying to understand the hornet. So far the video holds up. Putin openly expressing to Macron that he will flip Ukraine entirely. Remarks like \"There is no world without Russia in it\", emphasizing that this campaign is existential, not a random aggression."},{"id":31697996,"author":"fodmap","month":"2022-06","story":"Vladimir Putin, in speech, hints at further territorial expansion for Russia","stance":"rec","why":"Well-documented explainer on why Russia is invading Ukraine.","quote":"RealLifeLore published a very well documented, and propaganda-free, video about why Russia is invading Ukraine","text":"RealLifeLore published a very well documented, and propaganda-free, video about why Russia is invading Ukraine [VIDEO]"},{"id":30530265,"author":"donclark","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: How do you cope with possible nuclear threat?","stance":"rec","why":"30-minute explanation of why Russia invaded.","quote":"Why Russia is Invading Ukraine(30mins explanation)","text":"Great video - thank you for posting. This video as well from Feb 26 2022 - Why Russia is Invading Ukraine(30mins explanation): [VIDEO]"},{"id":30542064,"author":"skadamat","month":"2022-03","story":"Russian gas flows via Yamal pipeline to Germany halt, bids remain","stance":"rec","why":"Great overview of how oil ties to Russian strategy.","quote":"this video has a great overview","text":"I never knew until recently how tied up Russia's oil strategy was to their military / political thinking. If others are interested, this video has a great overview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30564056,"author":"lowkey","month":"2022-03","story":"Yandex Provides Update on Impact of Current Developments","stance":"rec","why":"Broader context showing the conflict is more complicated than portrayed.","quote":"will leave it here for anyone who is curious about the broader context for this conflict","text":"I don't expect you to watch this full video but will leave it here for anyone who is curious about the broader context for this conflict. TL;DR: The war didn't start last week. It has been ongoing since 2014 and involves horrible acts from both sides. No, they don't average out and unfortunately we may never know what the 'truth' is here. I won't pretend to have all the answers, but I will say after watching the video, the situation appears to be much more complicated than it is being depicted on western media. Curiosity Stream: Why Russia is invading Ukraine [VIDEO]"},{"id":30764832,"author":"bambax","month":"2022-03","story":"In Praise of Memorization","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating and explanatory.","quote":"This video is fascinating and explains a lot.","text":"> Deep link to explanation: [VIDEO] This video is fascinating and explains a lot. Many thanks for this!!"},{"id":30485066,"author":"InternetPerson","month":"2022-02","story":"Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment, Feb 26, 2022","stance":"rec","why":"Argues Russia's motives are largely about fossil fuels.","quote":"I found this to be a compelling take on Russia's motivations for invading Ukraine","text":"I found this to be a compelling take on Russia's motivations for invading Ukraine: [VIDEO] The narrator argues that it's largely about fossil fuels."},{"id":30504888,"author":"milkytron","month":"2022-02","story":"Russia 'Likely' to Default on Debts","stance":"rec","why":"Summarizes oil, NATO border, and ports as motives.","quote":"I don't know if this is 100% accurate, but it seemed to help my understanding","text":"> But these sanctions are so severe that they'll probably even prevent him from rebuilding his army following this due to sanctions blocking imports to fuel his war industry. So, since he is still fighting, what is he fighting _for_? Still trying to expand Russia? I don't know if this is 100% accurate, but it seemed to help my understanding: [VIDEO] tldw - oil, a better border where Belarus cannot be surrounded by NATO, and better access to shipping ports."},{"id":30997482,"author":"klawcode","month":"2022-04","story":"Finland and Sweden set to join NATO as soon as summer","stance":"reference","why":"Information on why Russia wants Crimea.","quote":"There is a pretty decent amount of information out there as to why Russia wants Crimea (and Ukraine)","text":"There is a pretty decent amount of information out there as to why Russia wants Crimea (and Ukraine) [VIDEO] There is a lot of propaganda out there, but a hard and fast truth is that no country, not Russia nor the US nor China nor India nor anyone actually cares at all about normal citizens. People can care about other people, but countries do not and cannot. Its why Putin's claims of protecting Russians are BS. (US Claims of protecting Ukrainians too, its in the US/Europe interest to do so, its not to protect Tibetans or Afghans for example, so it does not)"},{"id":31042774,"author":"SkyMarshal","month":"2022-04","story":"Give War a Chance","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"He’s right about 9/11 and the “World-domination advocates” exploiting the event to expand the scope of the US response from self-defense (neutralizing Al Quaeda) to hegemony-expansion (invading Iraq, and subsequent regime-change efforts). It’s unpleasant at best to see them again crawling out of the woodwork, after causing so much damage to the US, Iraq, and the world, not to mention fomenting both Putin and the CCP's current aggressive expansionary posture. They defined the \"New World Order\" for the next half-century at least, as less about order and more about power. And now US appeals to a \"rules-based international order\" is falling on deaf ears because of the neocons. And the media propaganda machine ramping up war justification, where have I seen this before. On the other hand, Ukraine is, unlike Iraq, already a democracy, being invaded by an autocracy. Russia’s real objective is most likely to control Ukraine’s newly discovered natural gas reserves and maintain Russia’s energy leverage over the democratic EU [1]. Allowing an autocracy to do both of those things is equally distasteful. Kinzinger isn’t entirely wrong in his assessment that allowing Russian nuclear saber rattling to deter the West may encourage every other 2-bit autocracy to pursue nukes, but unfortunately that cat’s already out of the bag. When Gadhafi gave up his nuke program and later was regime-changed and assassinated anyway, and when Trump unilaterally backed out of the Iran nuclear deal, that showe"},{"id":30510463,"author":"lovehashbrowns","month":"2022-03","story":"Namecheap: Russia Service Termination","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There is literally no conceivable way Ukraine was ever going to remain independent as long as Putin is in control of Russia. Everything you talked about has already been done--Ukraine gained independence and, after years of Ukraine-NATO relations, began actively seeking membership in the alliance and with the EU. For example, in 2002: > \"Ukraine's total integration into NATO is dictated by the reality of European and world politics, particularly after the tragic events of Sept. 11 in the United States,\" Kuchma said in a statement. > \"We are aware that joining NATO will be a long process and we will do everything possible,\" he added. [LINK] Followed by a formal attempt in 2008: > Ukrainian President Viktor Yushchenko, Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko and Parliamentary Chairman Arseniy Yatsenyuk have signed the statement calling for consideration on Ukraine`s entry into the NATO membership action plan at the Bucharest summit\" ... [LINK] Then we get the events of 2014 in Ukraine [LINK] where Yanukovych is kicked out because of his refusal to sign a trade agreement with the EU. There are more than 2 decades of history pointing to Ukraine's willingness to join Europe and NATO. And this is all backed by the people, both by the election of their leaders and by force. So literally all of this fully agrees with the entirety of your \"solution.\" How have we ended up here? For one, Ukraine has natural gas and oil reserves--and if Ukraine can sell that to Europe, then Europe can rely on Ru"},{"id":30529451,"author":"eezurr","month":"2022-03","story":"Russia's top bank quits Europe due to sanctions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"IMO, what the West has done is shown an incredible display of power, killing multiple birds with one stone: 1.) Attempt to prevent Russia from accessing new found gas/oil reserves. [3] 2.) Continue to press Russia into a corner as the West continues to expand its influence. [1] 3.) Show and tell China the consequences of economic sanctions and economic war, and our willingness and ability to do so. [speculation] For one, the Ukraine invasion is the US's fault. This has been covered in depth by John Mearsheimer in 2015 in his lecture \"Why is Ukraine the West's Fault?\" after the Orange Revolution and Russia capturing of Crimea [1]. And there is also some rational speculation here regarding the discovery of natural resources. [3] What the US (which has major influence over Europe/NATO, so it's not clear how much of a choice Europe had here)[2] has essentially done is shown the world that we can manipulate and control the status quo (i.e. win a \"war\") without firing a shot or dropping a bomb. The new tools of war are economic control, and we're the heaviest guerilla in the room [4]. What's brilliant is the US's ability to make Putin appear much more evil than he really is. Yes, he is a dictator and isn't a good person, but he's been bringing back Russia from the dead and lifting its people out of poverty. Some of Putin's invasions occurred because we, the West/NATO, offered membership to those countries, when Russia made it clear that was a big no-no [1]. Forcing Russia to invade"},{"id":30531984,"author":"eezurr","month":"2022-03","story":"Russia's top bank quits Europe due to sanctions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I couldn't find a HN rule against reposting flagged comments, so here it is (with my edit after being flagged). I want to reiterate my point of view is entirely neutral and fact based (from what I've read so far about the conflict). IMO, what the West has done is shown an incredible display of power, killing multiple birds with one stone: 1.) Attempt to prevent Russia from accessing new found gas/oil reserves. [3] 2.) Continue to press Russia into a corner as the West continues to expand its influence. [1] 3.) Show and tell China the consequences of economic sanctions and economic war, and our willingness and ability to do so. [speculation] For one, the Ukraine invasion is the US's fault. This has been covered in depth by John Mearsheimer in 2015 in his lecture \"Why is Ukraine the West's Fault?\" after the Orange Revolution and Russia capturing of Crimea [1]. And there is also some rational speculation here regarding the discovery of natural resources. [3] What the US (which has major influence over Europe/NATO, so it's not clear how much of a choice Europe had here)[2] has essentially done is shown the world that we can manipulate and control the status quo (i.e. win a \"war\") without firing a shot or dropping a bomb. The new tools of war are economic control, and we're the heaviest guerilla in the room [4]. What's brilliant is the US's ability to make Putin appear much more evil than he really is. Yes, he is a dictator and isn't a good person, but he's been bringing back Russ"},{"id":30560501,"author":"warner_of_doom","month":"2022-03","story":"Why John Mearsheimer Blames the U.S. for the Crisis in Ukraine","stance":"reference","why":"Supports resource and geostrategic security view of the war.","quote":"","text":"This is also playing right into NATO's hands in a way. But, after reading the authors I mentioned closely, I now realize that this is a war for control/negative-control over natural resources (grain, oil, gas, fertilizers, etc.). Also, this is about geostrategic security (anchoring at the Carpathian mountains and Bessarabia Gap). See: [LINK] And: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30764435,"author":"jasode","month":"2022-03","story":"In Praise of Memorization","stance":"reference","why":"Deep link explaining Crimea's water supply.","quote":"Deep link to explanation","text":">, because Russia controls Sevastopol since it annexed Crimea in 2014; it didn't need the current war to control it more. Crimea's fresh water supply came from a canal originating in Ukraine. After Russia's annexation, Ukraine retaliated by blocking the water flow with concrete so Crimea was running out of water. Deep link to explanation: [VIDEO] So it was no surprise that on the 2nd day of the invasion, Russian soldiers used explosives to blow up Ukraine's dam to to re-open the canal: [LINK] Crimea residents' (many pro-Russian) reaction a week later on March 3: [LINK] Also, Russia controlling Ukraine's land and coastline from Donbass through Mariupol and down to north Crimea means they have a more viable \"land bridge\" for supplies than the tiny man-made bridge that links southern Crimea with Russia. Ukraine becoming allied with NATO (or a defacto \"NATO ally\" via EU membership instead of a Russia puppet) means that tiny bridge over the Kerch strait becomes a vulnerable chokepoint that threatens Russia. Deep link to that explanation: [LINK] From Russia's point of view, there are many desirable military objectives for them to take over southeastern Ukraine. EDIT reply to: >, because a naive look at the map suggests that even without Crimea, Russia already has coastline onto the Black Sea between its borders with Ukraine and Georgia... there must be a strong reason in favour of invading another country rather than developing ports on the land they already have, Russia's existing"},{"id":30764914,"author":"bambax","month":"2022-03","story":"In Praise of Memorization","stance":"reference","why":"Economic motive: Crimea oil fields and water canal.","quote":"the main motive is economical","text":"As per the video [VIDEO] ), above and beyond the geopolitical reasons for Russia's invasion of Ukraine, the main motive is economical: 1. Russia is in effect a petrostate; it lives and dies by its exports of natural gas and oil. 2. Immense fields of underwater natural gas and oil were discovered in 2012 in the Crimea maritime exclusive economic zone. 3. If Crimea stays with Ukraine, it lets Ukraine become a new petrostate and challenge Russia (take over its European customers), which to Russia is an existential threat. Hence the annexation of Crimea in 2014: not only for Sevastopol and access to the Mediterranean, but to exploit the oil fields (and/or prevent Ukraine from exploiting them). 4. But the supply of 80% of the fresh water to Crimea comes from a canal [0] that connects it to the Dnieper river in Ukraine; after the annexation, Ukraine shut the canal down, effectively thirsting Crimea to death. 5. If Russia wants to stay in Crimea (which it believes is necessary for its own survival), it needs to reopen that canal, and therefore control at least the southern part of Ukraine and possibly its whole Mediterranean coastline. (It's not just about the canal, but the canal is a very big part of it.) [0]: [LINK]"},{"id":30496364,"author":"chasil","month":"2022-02","story":"Russian Ruble just lost ~30% of its value","stance":"reference","why":"Maps of oil reserves.","quote":"This video has maps of the reserves at 12:30.","text":"The conventional oil reserves are under water in the seas to the south. There is also shale oil in the east and west. None of this is really vulnerable to radioactive contamination. This video has maps of the reserves at 12:30. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30504532,"author":"erichocean","month":"2022-02","story":"Russia 'Likely' to Default on Debts","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> the US at least attempted to invent a casus belli for Iraq Again: reward your friends, punish your enemies. Lie (\"invent a casus belli\") if you have to in order to punish your enemies. Everyone here trying to give some ideological reason why we did such and such in this case, but not in this other case, is entirely mistaken. Literally everything is spin to justify the core motivation: reward your friends, punish your enemies. Ukraine is no different. We can invade Iraq because they are our enemy. Russia CANNOT invade Ukraine because they are our friend. ---- Answering your follow up here (I'm rate-limited): > What was different about the 2014 invasion if it is strictly realpolitik friends and enemies? As I understand things, Crimea is literally the friend/enemy distinction in action (Russian perspective): Crimea, arguably (and certainly, effectively ) was/is a Russian territory, autonomous (but pro-Russian) and with an active Russian naval base, that came under the nominal control of the West after the 2014 regime change (enemy); thus Russia militarily took it back (friend), ensuring its continued independence and pro-Russian stance. Ukraine responded to Crimea's post-2014 autonomy/invasion/rescue/independence by cutting off their water supply, something Russia built for Crimea during the USSR. (I'm honestly surprised this step wasn't/isn't a Hague-level human rights violation. Maybe the Hague is also operating primarily under the friend/enemy distinction?) More detail here"}],"r":[["17oCQakzIl8",1],["5OtKEetGy2Y",1],["5pidokakU4I",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["PFc379hu--8",1],["TH9VCN6UkyQ",1],["OkmNXy7er84",1],["XFDM1ip5HdU",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["N9c7_8Gp7gI",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["2tdiKTSdE9Y",1]]},"RrHnLXMTOWM":{"wy":"Praised as a masterpiece of science education that recreates Faraday's demonstrations.","q":{"x":"is a masterpiece in science education.","i":33033024,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33033024,"author":"helsinkiandrew","month":"2022-09","story":"Michael Faraday invented the rubber balloon","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Narration of Faraday's candle lecture, a masterpiece of science education.","quote":"is a masterpiece in science education.","text":"His \"Chemical History of a Candle\" Christmas lecture is a masterpiece in science education. Narrated here by Bill Hammack - the engineerguy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16308932,"author":"JshWright","month":"2018-02","story":"The Engineering of the Drinking Bird Toy [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Highlight from Bill Hammack's series on Faraday's candle lectures.","quote":"If you haven't watched all of Bill's videos, you should put them on your list right right now.","text":"If you haven't watched all of Bill's videos, you should put them on your list right right now. His early stuff was a great mixture of goofy humor and fascinating explanations of the engineering behind various everyday objects. His more recent stuff has (unfortunately, in my opinion) dropped most of the comedy, but really stepped up the overall quality. Some highlights: His series on Faraday's Chemical History of a Candle lectures: [VIDEO] His series on Albert Michelson’s Harmonic Analyzer (a century old device that can mechanically perform Fourier transforms) [LINK] I'd also really recommend his book on R101, the last great British airship. [LINK]"},{"id":27942518,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2021-07","story":"4.6B-year-old meteorite found","stance":"rec","why":"Recreation of Faraday's demonstrations showing hypothesis, consequence, demonstration.","quote":"has a wonderful recreation of Faraday's public demonstration of these properties.","text":"How would you demonstrate that observationally? Science isn't just asking \"what if...?\" It's following up with \"... and how could I demonstrate that?\" The third step is then \"how do I communicate that to others?\" In the domain of chemistry, there's a wonderful example of that in Michael Faraday's \"The Chemical History of a Candle\". YouTube's EngineerGuy (Bill Hammack) has a wonderful recreation of Faraday's public demonstration of these properties. It seems obvious and pedestrian now, but that's because Faraday introduced us to these concepts and convinced us, collectively, of their fundamental truth. He gives us the hypothesis, he gives us the observable consequence, and he then demonstrates that consequence. Six-part series starting here: [VIDEO] I've already answered your question, as it happens: Looking out in space is looking back in time. Numerous phenomena support very strong consistency of physical constants to all observable levels. lizknope mentioned on HN recently naturally-occurring fission reactions on Earth about a billion years old or so. Those provide direct physical validation of physical constants as well. That is, we have zoomed out time, using telescopes, looking back in the Universe 13.772 billion years (9.2 billion older than the Earth's own age). And there's no observable change in any of the fundamental physical constants that we can detect over this range. But since you've repeated the question twice: why do you ask?"},{"id":19794663,"author":"bradyd","month":"2019-05","story":"The Chemical History of a Candle (1848)","stance":"rec","why":"Video series based on Faraday's candle lectures.","quote":"did a video series based on these lectures that are a very good.","text":"Bill Hammack, engineerguy on YouTube, did a video series based on these lectures that are a very good. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["st571DYYTR8",1],["g3Ud6mHdhlQ",1],["xnWKz7Cthkk",1],["uYNzqgU7na4",1],["vaCVh2SAZY4",1],["GJCF-Ufapu8",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1],["hT8GZlBBv5k",1],["hzyFZcuHmeI",1],["SFBBjynBpSw",1],["NAsM30MAHLg",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["4QXZ2RzN_Oo",1],["AqeJzItldSQ",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1],["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["HXAW2GseICs",1],["sfXn_ecH5Rw",1],["AItTqnTsVjA",1]]},"eMlx5fFNoYc":{"wy":"HN treats it as the go-to visual explanation of attention, recommended to people who find text-based transformer explainers hard to follow.","q":{"x":"what bade me finally understand the transformer architecture [0] and attention [1], are 3Blue1Brown's videos.","i":40873442,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":48321181,"author":"onlyrealcuzzo","month":"2026-05","story":"Claude Opus 4.8","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing series on how tokens become vectors and mutate through attention.","quote":"If you aren't familiar with hyper words, this is an amazing series","text":"Because it doesn't work like how you think at all. You're still thinking it works like Chain of Thought. It doesn't. And the difference is key! It works by introducing probabilistic noise, and exploring N paths fully (each with noise) in parallel (all compressed). It's reasoning at a much, much smaller (probabilistic) level than running everything through the expensive large model (deterministic) and sometimes catching that it said, \"I think 1.12 is greater than 1.9 because 12 is bigger than 9, final answer\". The easiest way to think about it is: if you understand how hyper words work, it's as if it's searching for different versions of the hyper words that probilisticslly would lead to better outcomes IF it fed them to the LLM before it even does. That's not actually how it works exactly. But I think it is close enough to be helpful to understand where the gain is, a rough idea of what's happening (searching paths), and how it can potentially have huge orders of magnitude improvements (doing so without paying the full price of exploring the paths through the expensive and huge model). And also why it is so much harder to determine what it's \"thinking\". If you aren't familiar with hyper words, this is an amazing series: [VIDEO] The general idea is that a token is a multidimensional vector to represent a word -> think like \"man\" is a [noun, singular, English, pronoun, masculine, contemporary, ...]. Each time is sees a new word, it mutates this word to mean some new token (ofte"},{"id":40873442,"author":"tomashm","month":"2024-07","story":"The Illustrated Transformer (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made the commenter finally understand transformers and attention.","quote":"what bade me finally understand the transformer architecture [0] and attention [1], are 3Blue1Brown's videos.","text":"This is good, but what bade me finally understand the transformer architecture [0] and attention [1], are 3Blue1Brown's videos. 0. [LINK] 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48510295,"author":"wren6991","month":"2026-06","story":"Can I Buy Your KV Cache?","stance":"rec","why":"Gentler introduction to attention, part of a series.","quote":"For something gentler, 3Blue1Brown","text":"If you want the actual maths instead of handwaving, I recommend: [LINK] For something gentler, 3Blue1Brown: [VIDEO] (this is part of a series)"},{"id":41926020,"author":"superkuh","month":"2024-10","story":"LLMs Aren't Thinking, They're Just Counting Votes","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how attention actually works, versus voting metaphor.","quote":"I suggest checking out 3blue1brown's videos on gradient descent","text":"> when an LLM tells us that the sun rises in the east, it's not because it truly understands astronomy or the solar system. Rather, it has seen the phrase \"sun rises in the east\" repeated so many times in its training data that this becomes the highest probability answer. It's less about understanding and more about pattern recognition. This is not too different from most human person's experience with basic education ( [LINK] ). Though it is obvious that humans can do more than that too. And while \"voting\" that may be a close-enough metaphor for the multi-layer perceptron layers, it is not what is happening in the attention layers. I suggest checking out 3blue1brown's videos on gradient descent [LINK] and \"How might LLMs store facts\" [LINK] versus how attention works [VIDEO]"},{"id":40974756,"author":"MAXPOOL","month":"2024-07","story":"The Engineer’s Guide to Deep Learning: Understanding the Transformer Model","stance":"rec","why":"Visual explanation of attention in a list of better resources.","quote":"Attention in transformers, visually explained | Chapter 6, Deep Learning","text":"There are many others that are better. 1/ The Annotated Transformer Attention is All You Need [LINK] 2/ Transformers from Scratch [LINK] 3/ Andrej Karpathy has really good series of intros: [LINK] Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out. [LINK] GPT with Andrej Karpathy: Part 1 [LINK] 4/ 3Blue1Brown: But what is a GPT? Visual intro to transformers | Chapter 5, Deep Learning [LINK] Attention in transformers, visually explained | Chapter 6, Deep Learning [VIDEO] Full 3Blue1Brown Neural Networks playlist [LINK]"},{"id":44272185,"author":"peterlai","month":"2025-06","story":"3Blue1Brown Follow-Up: From Hypothetical Examples to LLM Circuit Visualization","stance":"reference","why":"Tutorial on self-attention for next token prediction, used as comparison baseline.","quote":"I watched this 3Blue1Brown LLM tutorial","text":"About a year ago, I watched this 3Blue1Brown LLM tutorial ( [VIDEO] ) on how a model’s self-attention mechanism is used to predict the next token in a sequence, and I was surprised by how little we know about what actually happens when processing the sentence “A fluffy blue creature roamed the verdant forest.” A year later, the field of mechanistic interpretability has seen significant advancements, and we're now able to \"decompose\" models into interpretable circuits that help explain how LLMs produce predictions. Using the second iteration of an LLM \"debugger\" I've been working on, I compare the hypothetical representations used in the tutorial to the actual representations I see when extracting a circuit that describes the processing of this specific sentence. If you're into model interpretability, please take a look! [LINK]"},{"id":40037275,"author":"rahimnathwani","month":"2024-04","story":"Visualizing Attention, a Transformer's Heart [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Notes techniques that are out of scope for the video.","quote":"He lists Ring Attention and half a dozen other techniques, but they're not within the scope of this video","text":"He lists Ring Attention and half a dozen other techniques, but they're not within the scope of this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40448714,"author":"lolinder","month":"2024-05","story":"YouTube seems to once again be rolling out its widely hated new web redesign","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Taking one example, 3Blue1Brown's Deep Learning series, episode 6.","text":"Eh, insightful and thoughtful videos tend to have better comments than the low brow videos, but they're still not useful . The best of them read like a decent-but-not-great subreddit's comment thread. Taking one example, 3Blue1Brown's Deep Learning series, episode 6. Here are the top few comments [0]: > I'm a university lecturer with a PhD in AI, and I cannot compete with the quality of this work. Videos like this put the entire higher education system to shame. Fantastic! This is a nice comment, but doesn't contribute anything to my day. I'm sure the creator is glad to see it and I'm glad someone said it, but I don't need to read it. > Are you kidding me? ONE WEEK FOR 2 MASTERPIECES?! > Thank you so much! Same story. > I've got to say - \"Attention Is All You Need\" is an incredible title for a research paper. And now we're down to the reddit comments. > As a graduating PhD student working in Natural Language Processing, I still found that video to be extremely beneficial. Awesome! Nice. > 3b1b is the only content producer whose videos I start by first making coffee, then upvoting, then hitting the play button. Nice. > Attention existed before the 2017 paper \"Attention Is All You Need\". > The main contribution was that attention was... all you needed for sequence processing (you didn't need recurrence). Self-attention specifically was novel though. Finally a comment that contributed something. Not much, mind you (the video doesn't actually claim otherwise), but something . Unf"}],"r":[["wjZofJX0v4M",2],["ScEPu1cs4l0",1],["aircAruvnKk",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["IHZwWFHWa-w",1],["7J7X7aZvMXQ",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1]]},"sw0VfmXKq54":{"wy":"Repeatedly called essential viewing for game development history and clever work under memory constraints.","q":{"x":"Essential viewing for anyone interested in game development history.","i":45383182,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48500887,"author":"mscdex","month":"2026-06","story":"How we made hit video game Prince of Persia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"War Stories episode on making Prince of Persia","quote":"If you haven't already seen it, I highly recommend watching the","text":"If you haven't already seen it, I highly recommend watching the \"War Stories\" video on the making of Prince of Persia: [VIDEO] On a related note, I also highly recommend the \"War Stories\" video for the making of Crash Bandicoot: [LINK]"},{"id":48502173,"author":"stefanos82","month":"2026-06","story":"How we made hit video game Prince of Persia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Creator explains the tiny details that made the game","quote":"Just watch it yourself, you will be in awe!","text":"I have watched the creator explaining to us in great detail how he came up with so many tiny details that made it a perfect game for its time! Just watch it yourself, you will be in awe! [VIDEO]"},{"id":45383182,"author":"barishnamazov","month":"2025-09","story":"A platform-jumping prince – History of Prince of Persia's 1990s Ports","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on the making of Prince of Persia","quote":"Essential viewing for anyone interested in game development history.","text":"> \"For the first time I felt what it's really like to play Prince of Persia when you're not the author and don't already know by rote what's lurking around every corner.\" This perfectly captures why code reviews by someone who didn't write the original are so valuable. You can't unsee your own assumptions. I remember seeing the following short but extremely interesting documentary about makings of the game as well: [VIDEO] - Essential viewing for anyone interested in game development history."},{"id":29713383,"author":"ajdoingnothing","month":"2021-12","story":"Prince of Persia in JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"Mechner on developing Prince of Persia","quote":"If you haven't seen Jordan Mechner talking about the development of Prince of Persia yet (definitely recommended)","text":"If you haven't seen Jordan Mechner talking about the development of Prince of Persia yet (definitely recommended): [VIDEO]"},{"id":25408972,"author":"softwhale","month":"2020-12","story":"Examples of games developed by a single developer?","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with Prince of Persia creator Jordan Mechner","quote":"Arstechnica has a great interview with Jordan Mechner, the creator of this game.","text":"Arstechnica has a great interview with Jordan Mechner, the creator of this game. A true technical and artistic wizard: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45407365,"author":"throwaway31131","month":"2025-09","story":"The Death of Utilitarian Programming","stance":"reference","why":"Example of crazy engineering around memory constraints","quote":"It was a huge task just to get our programs to run at all not very long ago.","text":"> no more engineering for engineering’s sake I'm not sure many successful engineering orgs did much of that but also the environment our creations live in is much different now. It was a huge task just to get our programs to run at all not very long ago. When I first started coding I was working on some code for a VLSI problem and I spent like a month just figuring out how to get the problem to fit on disk (not main memory, but on disk!). Now there are similar things that run on a laptop [0]. And it's not like I'm unique in having to come up with crazy solutions to problems tangent to the primary problem in this space. [1] Now the infrastructure for our code is amazing and the machines that execute them abound in resources, especially in the cloud. Now that the yoke has been cast off it makes sense more time is spent on the solution the actual problem you set out to solve in the first place. [0] [LINK] [1] How Prince of Persia Defeated Apple II's Memory Limitations [VIDEO]"},{"id":40397113,"author":"clusmore","month":"2024-05","story":"Prince of Persia – how jumping animation was made","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with the poster of the jumping animation","quote":"you can see more in this Ars Technica interview with the X poster","text":"The link doesn't work for me as I don't have an X account, but you can see more in this Ars Technica interview with the X poster: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["izxXGuVL21o",1]]},"DShDaJXK5qo":{"wy":"Recommended as a classic documentary on military training and the psychology of going to war.","q":{"x":"This documentary explains in great detail the nature of military basic training and its psychological component","i":6430136,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":15297730,"author":"bitexploder","month":"2017-09","story":"The Ballad of the Green Beret","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic documentary on war and training","quote":"I think the classic PBS documentary \"anyone's son will do\" is great.","text":"Yeah. It is interesting though. I think the classic PBS documentary \"anyone's son will do\" is great. [VIDEO] I think war in the industrialized world is almost purely political. Wars in the modern day are arranged by old men and politicians and fought by young men. I don't blame the young men, but I think it goes back to some aspects of human biology and how young men see themselves and are manipulated through patriotism and other political forces to volunteer (or be conscripted) into a military to serve political ends. In a time before two tribes could communicate and apply some reason to things, force was the only way to resolve things. Force and violence is still a crude, simple, and effective path to changing human behavior so it of course is used, but if it is ever used between super powers the world is basically over. Anyhow, I digress."},{"id":9394137,"author":"htormey","month":"2015-04","story":"Why Young Men Go to War","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Episode on how basic training transforms recruits into soldiers","quote":"A great documentary miniseries about this topic is War: A Commentary by Gwynne Dyer.","text":"A great documentary miniseries about this topic is War: A Commentary by Gwynne Dyer. In particular, the episode anyone's son will do: [LINK] [VIDEO] The episode covers what goes into training young people who enter the marine core. It features candid interviews with drill sergeants and the recruits themselves about their motivations. Well worth a watch. One of the main points the episode makes is that the job of being a soldier is ultimately about killing and dying, something that doesn’t come naturally to most people. The method for turning young men into soldiers is basic training. According to Dyer the secret of basic training is that its not really about teaching people, its about changing people. He claims that if you want to change people quickly and radically, you put them in a place where the only right way to think and behave is the way you want them to. You isolate them and then apply enormous physical and mental pressure. i.e basic training. Essentially he claims that basic training is all about brain washing. In the linked interview Sebastian Junger talks a lot about what it means to be a man. In particular: “In that kind of environment,” Junger continues. “If you dare talk about what it means to be a man, what you’re saying is men are different from women.” and “We aren’t asking, ‘What does it mean to be a human being?’” he says. “‘What’s it mean to be a man?’ Once you have that conversation you offend a lot of academics.” and “Men are clearly wired for [war],” h"},{"id":6430136,"author":"digitalengineer","month":"2013-09","story":"WikiLeaks Leaks 'Fifth Estate' Screenplay","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on military basic training and its psychological component","quote":"This documentary explains in great detail the nature of military basic training and its psychological component","text":"\"Anybody's son will do\" [LINK] This documentary explains in great detail the nature of military basic training and its psychological component . It's on Youtube: [VIDEO]"}]},"l6XQUciI-Sc":{"wy":"HN links it for Cantrill's entertaining epoll versus kqueue rant and his stories, such as bricking a machine with rm -rf.","q":{"x":"There's a good (and wildly entertaining) discussion/rant about it in this BSD Now episode","i":12344552,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":16303741,"author":"beefhash","month":"2018-02","story":"Talks I have given","stance":"rec","why":"Casual podcast episodes featuring Cantrill","quote":"I also found the somewhat more casual BSDnow podcasts that featured Mr. Cantrill quite insightful","text":"I also found the somewhat more casual BSDnow podcasts that featured Mr. Cantrill quite insightful[1,2,3]. He shows up around 15 or 30 minutes in. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":12344552,"author":"Gracana","month":"2016-08","story":"“Node.js is one of the worst things to happen to the software industry” (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining rant on why Linux epoll gets it wrong","quote":"There's a good (and wildly entertaining) discussion/rant about it in this BSD Now episode","text":"This sounds like something straight out of the Unix Hater's Handbook. :) But epoll is not the fault of Unix. Linux gets it wrong, but others do not. There's a good (and wildly entertaining) discussion/rant about it in this BSD Now episode: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10568377,"author":"myztic","month":"2015-11","story":"Run containers on bare metal already [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with Cantrill on his background and Sun in the 90s","quote":"Quite a funny and smart guy ;)","text":"Amusing Interview with Bryan Cantrill (regarding his background and he also shares some stories) [VIDEO] Also very informative (about what happened at Sun in the 90s, dtrace, ...) Quite a funny and smart guy ;)"},{"id":10353083,"author":"protomyth","month":"2015-10","story":"Things you can do with ZFS [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Follow-up viewing on ZFS and licensing","quote":"The video is very good and you can follow-up with","text":"Probably not, since that would have allowed someone to add GPL-only code to it which couldn't be distributed with the whole. The video is very good and you can follow-up with [VIDEO]"},{"id":42128856,"author":"eduction","month":"2024-11","story":"Way too many ways to wait on a child process with a timeout","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill's famous epoll/kqueue rant","quote":"famous epoll/kqueue rant","text":"He mentions Bryan Cantrill in there and I can’t resist posting his famous epoll/kqueue rant: [VIDEO] I know this is related but maybe someone smarter than me can explain how closely it relates (or doesn’t) to this issue which seems more general (iirc Cantrill was talking about fs events not child processes generally)"},{"id":29246028,"author":"eqvinox","month":"2021-11","story":"The Demikernel Datapath OS Architecture","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for kqueue being better than epoll","quote":"The BSDs have kqueue which according to some people is actually better","text":"Well, not a question. POSIX and Linux are not the same. There is no epoll in POSIX, it's a Linux addition. The BSDs have kqueue which according to some people is actually better [cf. [VIDEO] ]. But kqueue isn't POSIX either. Only poll() and select() are..."},{"id":18332188,"author":"protomyth","month":"2018-10","story":"Maintaining 65k open connections in a single Ruby process","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill on epoll semantics","quote":"Brian Cantrill on epoll","text":"[LINK] While a best effort has been made to mimic the Linux semantics, there are some semantics that are too peculiar or ill-conceived to merit accommodation. In particular, the Linux epoll facility will -- by design -- continue to generate events for closed file descriptors where/when the underlying file description remains open. For example, if one were to fork(2) and subsequently close an actively epoll'd file descriptor in the parent, any events generated in the child on the implicitly duplicated file descriptor will continue to be delivered to the parent -- despite the fact that the parent itself no longer has any notion of the file description! This epoll facility refuses to honor these semantics; closing the EPOLL_CTL_ADD'd file descriptor will always result in no further events being generated for that event description. Brian Cantrill on epoll [VIDEO]"},{"id":16268432,"author":"Annatar","month":"2018-01","story":"LinuxBoot: Linux as Firmware","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"watch it to the end.","text":"[VIDEO] - watch it to the end. How long \"people have been doing it\" has nothing to do with the quality, it's a fallacy. They can't code if they can't even get the basics like polling or startup / shutdown correctly."},{"id":13739527,"author":"gizmo","month":"2017-02","story":"Epoll is fundamentally broken","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill explains why epoll behavior is broken","quote":"a straightforward implementation behaves in a way you never want","text":"The linked interview with Bryan Cantrill gives a good explanation of why it's broken: a straightforward implementation behaves in a way you never want, with bizarre behavior where the kernel can wake up a thread with an accept() on a fd that has already been closed. linux should just have adopted kqueue or an IOCP model instead. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11392119,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2016-03","story":"Microsoft and Canonical partner to bring Ubuntu to Windows 10","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill's earlier argument for the LX zones approach","quote":"we want as many other systems as possible take this approach.","text":"Exciting stuff! This is (very?) similar what with LX branded zones in SmartOS[1][2] -- and, as I've said before[3] -- we want as many other systems as possible take this approach. That said, we also know how long a haul this is, and how detail-intensive it is. So I've got a bunch of questions. ;) First, is the source available? Second, how much of the One Hundred Language Quine Relay[4] runs successfully? (For us, running that sucker to completion was an important early milestone.) Assuming that runs, my next questions are all common apps/programs that we knew to be especially thorny for one reason or another: Go, strace, tcpdump, systemd, etc. Anyway, it's great to see the SmartOS approach so broadly validated by Microsoft -- and hope that they both finish the job and open source it all! [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":11009011,"author":"myztic","month":"2016-02","story":"No POST after rm -rf /","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill tells the story of bricking a machine with rm -rf","quote":"(quite entertaining)","text":"Reminds me of Cantrill, more so his story when they completely bricked a machine of a fellow worker once and then took a close look at the standard[1]. # rm -rf / Among other things, it will delete the current directory. In the standard it does not say what to delete first, In their implementation it will try to remove the current directory first -> undefined behaviour -> it fails. The logic behind it: When is it really your goal to delete your entire machine, mostly never, you don't type it out by accident, but shell scripts with unset variables might do it. And regarding Poettering's response[2] (not trying to start a fight): It's Poettering, what do you expect? You can hate or love systemd, but part of why people hate it is his intellectual arrogance in everything he does. [1] He tells the story somewhere in here [VIDEO] (quite entertaining) [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11153871,"author":"protomyth","month":"2016-02","story":"Linux 4.5-rc5: efivarfs fixed to avoid “rm -rf /” bricking UEFI","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill on Solaris refusing rm -rf /","quote":"I believe Solaris did the right thing and made it not work.","text":"For the few use cases where a system admin wants to \"rm -rf /\", there are hundreds of bad scripts that can screw up a system. I believe Solaris did the right thing and made it not work. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Tv6aJRGpz_A",1],["Ya6h2zKlpaQ",1],["GnBbhXBDmwU",1]]},"nnR8fDW3Ilo":{"wy":"Recommended as a spectacular example of bio-inspired robotics, and a pointer to Festo's other work.","q":{"x":"Their Smart Bird is spectacular.[2]","i":12385088,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":28966043,"author":"_Microft","month":"2021-10","story":"Feasibility of a Dune Ornithopter","stance":"rec","why":"Festo's earlier flapping bird model with rigid wings","quote":"Go and check out their other stuff","text":"The German industrial control and automation company Festo [0] created a bionic swift [1] as an engineering demo this year. The flapping wings are no longer as rigid as in their earlier flapping bird model [2]. Go and check out their other stuff (e.g. a dragonfly: [3], a flying fox or aquatic animals), it's great! Here is a Youtube search for Festo Animals for your convenience [4]. [0] [LINK] [1] BionicSwift: [LINK] [2] SmartBird: [VIDEO] [3] Dragonfly: [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":15810022,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2017-11","story":"Mercedes-Benz conducting the biggest test using drones to ship everyday items","stance":"rec","why":"Flapping-wing robot bird demonstration","quote":"You should definitely watch this","text":"You should definitely watch this: [LINK] and [VIDEO] Also, while birds are really good at using their wings to glide and provide propulsion flapping designs that need to lift much more than their own weight have so far not worked very well. So I don't really see them as viable for packages."},{"id":12385088,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-08","story":"The first autonomous, entirely soft robot","stance":"rec","why":"Festo's Smart Bird shows advanced bio-inspired robotics","quote":"Their Smart Bird is spectacular.[2]","text":"This is \"autonomous\" at the windup toy level. Festo, the German robotics company, is way ahead in autonomous soft robots. See their \"Air Ray\" fly around[1] Each year, Festo makes motion controllers and robots, especially high-precision pneumatic devices. Each year, they build something awesome. They do have some rigid components, but if they wanted to build one with fluidic logic, they certainly could. Their Smart Bird is spectacular.[2] Festo is a big, family-owned engineering company. 18,700 employees. founded in 1925. Annual sales €2.64 billion. One of the most innovative companies in mechanical engineering, yet a totally different business model than Silicon Valley's. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8433799,"author":"blhack","month":"2014-10","story":"Fluid Actuators from Disney Research Make Soft, Safe Robot Arms","stance":"rec","why":"Festo's robotics work is really cool","quote":"I highly recommend looking at the stuff that Festo","text":"If you're interested in this, I highly recommend looking at the stuff that Festo (a german industrial automation company) is doing: [LINK] (This is from 2008). And not necessarily related, but really cool anyway: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4065740,"author":"blhack","month":"2012-06","story":"The DOD had two \"better-than-Hubble\" space telescopes just sitting around.","stance":"reference","why":"Festo built this robot bird just for a trade show","quote":"This is made by Festo, a German industrial automation company.","text":"Uh...look at this thing: [VIDEO] This is made by Festo, a German industrial automation company. They did this for a trade show. It was \"for fun\". Imagine what we're capable of building for spying on China. I doubt we're having problems with it."},{"id":2381731,"author":"tintin","month":"2011-03","story":"Quadrocopters juggle balls cooperatively","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I'm more scared of this robot bird","text":"When it comes to unpleasant thoughts I'm more scared of this robot bird: [VIDEO] When you see a quadcopter hovering you know you are being watched. But with the bird it's a different story."}],"r":[["0fKBhvDjuy0",2],["PhhC_N6Bm_s",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["Imquk_3oFf4",1],["NGFhc8R_uO4",1],["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["muCPjK4nGY4",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["Uj-rK8V-rik",1],["3UxZDJ1HiPE",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["JOLtS4VUcvQ",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["nsJZ_kKjXcE",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1],["obmt_PkIfBE",1]]},"3FOzD4Sfgag":{"wy":"Recommended as a short, eye-opening piece of film analysis whenever physical or visual comedy is discussed.","q":{"x":"short, easily digestible, and well-produced YouTube videos about this stuff that are eye-opening.","i":8985322,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":8985322,"author":"wmeredith","month":"2015-02","story":"New Jurassic World Trailer Hides Improved CGI in Plain Sight","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eye-opening short film-analysis videos on visual comedy","quote":"short, easily digestible, and well-produced YouTube videos about this stuff that are eye-opening","text":"Everything on screen matters. Modern Cinema is a HIGHLY evolved art form. If you have any interest in this at all, YouTube user Every Frame A Painting publishes, short, easily digestible, and well-produced YouTube videos about this stuff that are eye-opening. Here are a few of my favorites: Edgar Wright - How to Do Visual Comedy: [VIDEO] The Silence of the Lambs - Who Wins the Scene? [LINK] Michael Bay - What is Bayhem? [LINK]"},{"id":32135933,"author":"mimimi31","month":"2022-07","story":"Rowan Atkinson: Visual Comedy (1992) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains visual comedy with examples from different movies","quote":"They give and explain some great examples from different movies.","text":"Reminds me of some of the videos from Every Frame a Painting about visual comedy in film. Specifically \"Edgar Wright - How to Do Visual Comedy\" [1] and \"Buster Keaton - The Art of the Gag\" [2]. They give and explain some great examples from different movies. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":21327786,"author":"vangale","month":"2019-10","story":"The Demise of Physical Comedy","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay on the loss of physical comedy","quote":"I love this video from \"Every Frame a Painting\" which also mourns the loss of physical comedy","text":"I love this video from \"Every Frame a Painting\" which also mourns the loss of physical comedy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39029420,"author":"dan_hawkins","month":"2024-01","story":"The tyranny of the algorithm: why every coffee shop looks the same","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of Edgar Wright's visual comedy","quote":"Take a look at \"Every Frame a Painting: Edgar Wright - How to Do Visual Comedy","text":"I'm really not sure about the humor. Take a look at \"Every Frame a Painting: Edgar Wright - How to Do Visual Comedy\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":38591551,"author":"latexr","month":"2023-12","story":"The actors who are the best (and worst) at their job","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is incredibly flawed in many respects. It’s not comparing actors, but the movies they starred in with different metrics for success. Those do not correlate. A mediocre actor featuring in Oscar-bait will fare better than a great actor who only works on low budget independent productions. > In general, critics seem to have a bias against comedy films. Rather, this article is very US-centric and those comedies are largely generic, unfunny, low effort movies of talking heads. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UWEjxkkB8Xs",1],["2THVvshvq0Q",1],["l_F9jxsfGCw",1]]},"HXAW2GseICs":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a practical, home-bakable sourdough pizza method that gets close to stone-oven quality.","q":{"x":"This is the best video on making neapolitan style at home with an cast iron pan and oven","i":22967177,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22967177,"author":"cmod","month":"2020-04","story":"How to make pizza like a Neapolitan master","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Neapolitan pizza at home with cast iron and oven.","quote":"This is the best video on making neapolitan style at home with an cast iron pan and oven","text":"This is the best video on making neapolitan style at home with an cast iron pan and oven: [VIDEO] I've used this method a few times to superb results."},{"id":26153164,"author":"leoncvlt","month":"2021-02","story":"The Physics of baking good Pizza [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Cooking pizza in an iron skillet at home.","quote":"it gets decently close the the quality of a stone oven","text":"No love for iron skillets? I've had some pretty good results cooking pizzas in a home settings with it. It sounds weird but it gets decently close the the quality of a stone oven, something to do with the heat conductivity of cast iron: [VIDEO] You cook it on the hob for a few minutes to cook the bottom, constantly shuffling the skillet round to get that random charring, then a few more minutes in a pre-heated oven as close to the broiler as possible. Great for deep dish pizza as well, if you're into that."},{"id":22974097,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2020-04","story":"How to make pizza like a Neapolitan master","stance":"rec","why":"Practical home-bakable sourdough pizza recipe.","quote":"For a practical, home-bakable, sourdough recipe I can vouch for","text":"For a practical, home-bakable, sourdough recipe I can vouch for: [VIDEO] Top comment: \"There is more drama in this video than in the entire Transformers franchise.\""},{"id":18852270,"author":"crispyambulance","month":"2019-01","story":"Pizza Dough","stance":"rec","why":"Wordless demonstration of high-heat pizza technique.","quote":"without words, better than I can describe","text":"> what part of the rest of the pizza-making process can you do better than the restaurant? A determined home pizzaiolo can make a perfectly decent pizza. But you are right about the temperature. Household ovens simply can't reach the temperatures used in a proper pizza oven, about 700F. I have had success with heating a large cast iron pan on the stove top to ~700F, dropping the dough in that, adding toppings when it's ready and then finishing it in a broiler or well preheated oven. These guys show how it's done, without words, better than I can describe: [VIDEO] It is pretty damn good technique. They're using 00 flour, high protein (available at Italian markets in any big city). One thing you can do at home better than the pizza shop, however, is to use crazy good artisinal toppings, dialed in exactly how you like it."}],"r":[["vJG698U2Mvo",1],["-6rWqJhDv7M",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["sfXn_ecH5Rw",1],["AItTqnTsVjA",1],["6h3RJhoqgK8",1],["wlR5gYd6um0",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["VnvGwFegbC8",1],["jh8supIUj6c",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1]]},"QpDn4-Na5co":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a favorite synthwave album, good for programming and long drives.","q":{"x":"My favourite album from that genre is Lazerhawk's debut album Redline, awesome for programming IMO","i":24361507,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24361507,"author":"scns","month":"2020-09","story":"Bardcore","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite synthwave album, great for programming.","quote":"My favourite album from that genre is Lazerhawk's debut album Redline, awesome for programming IMO","text":"My favourite album from that genre is Lazerhawk's debut album Redline, awesome for programming IMO: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28976557,"author":"noir_lord","month":"2021-10","story":"Gunship “Tech Noir” Breakdown","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter's intro to the synthwave genre.","quote":"Redline - (my intro to the genre closing on a decade ago).","text":"Also Lazerhawk Skull and Shark - [LINK] Redline - [VIDEO] (my intro to the genre closing on a decade ago). Trevor Something - Trevor Something Does Not Exist [LINK] Hands down my favourite in that genre."},{"id":9088209,"author":"mzs","month":"2015-02","story":"“Just the Beginning of What We Can Do on Super Nintendo”","stance":"rec","why":"Lazerhawk album good for long drives.","quote":"Drove straight from NY to IL one day with that on repeat.","text":"MM is good, I'm a fan of Lazerhawk too myself: [VIDEO] Drove straight from NY to IL one day with that on repeat."},{"id":12178522,"author":"InclinedPlane","month":"2016-07","story":"The Typography of ‘Stranger Things’","stance":"reference","why":"One example of synthwave among several.","quote":"","text":"Perhaps you should become acquainted with the music genre \"synthwave\", \"new retro wave\", or \"outrun\". Some examples: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["LZgyVadkgmI",1],["EOwxxsPaogY",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1],["esPRsT-lmw8",1]]},"tKTZoB2Vjuk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a fast, accessible way to start learning Python.","q":{"x":"recommend it to anybody that thinks they need to learn programming","i":16531423,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16531423,"author":"therockspush","month":"2018-03","story":"Google's Python Class","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accessible lectures that make low-level programming concepts understandable.","quote":"recommend it to anybody that thinks they need to learn programming","text":"I watched the original Nick Parlante videos that this material is based off 7 years ago, and recommend it to anybody that thinks they need to learn programming. First Lecture. [VIDEO] I took a couple C classes in college that were geared for people who already knew how to program and hated it. Never wanted to think about it again. Those videos helped abstract a lot of the low level muck away and build real stuff. Once I was able to understand some of these concepts and why you would actually use them I could go back and learn all the things that were too deep in the weeds to understand. Its a bit dated now but still some of the best training videos I've seen."},{"id":4843467,"author":"willholloway","month":"2012-11","story":"ShowHN: I learned to code and got a job in less than a year. Here is my ebook.","stance":"rec","why":"Python class for those with basic programming familiarity.","quote":"for anyone getting started with Python that has a familiarity with basic programming concepts","text":"I found the post worthwhile and I do not doubt its veracity whatsoever. I think you attack the author unfairly. I clicked to read because I followed a similar path to the author, except I never attended any kind of bootcamps. I started writing my first django app and one month later I had my first coding job. It was difficult at first, I was often baffled by the intentions of the codes author who I took over for, but I made the product work and it changed my life forever. I just started coding professionally January 15th of this year and already I've fixed and enhanced two large complicated projects and built two of my own. So anyone saying this isn't possible is absolutely wrong. I recommend watching Google's Python Class [1] for anyone getting started with Python that has a familiarity with basic programming concepts. [1] : [VIDEO]"},{"id":3334787,"author":"PythonDeveloper","month":"2011-12","story":"Ask HN: Ideas For Implementing A Simple Web App?","stance":"rec","why":"Intro Python course for learning quickly.","quote":"you can have an app up in 10 minutes","text":"The best tool for the job is the tool that lets you finish and ship a product. Try Python ( [LINK] ). It's free, easy to learn, uses the latest in methodologies, has massive community support, and you can have an app up in 10 minutes. You can also add django if you want a very well thought out framework with scaffolding, enhanced models, etc. Check it out on YouTube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3513353,"author":"sumukh1","month":"2012-01","story":"Google’s Python Lessons are Awesome","stance":"reference","why":"First lecture of Google's Python class.","quote":"the link to the lessons is a good link to keep handy","text":"Readability PDF: [LINK] Site seems to be down (PDF posted above) but the link to the lessons is a good link to keep handy. Link to class: [LINK] Link to video 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3513383,"author":"Natsu","month":"2012-01","story":"Google’s Python Lessons are Awesome","stance":"reference","why":"Google's Python intro lecture videos.","quote":"the material it links to seems pretty good","text":"The article is very short. It just tells you that Google's Python intros are awesome, then gives you the links below: Lessons: [LINK] YouTube videos: [VIDEO] Downloadable puzzles: [LINK] You won't miss much if you skip the article entirely. That said, the material it links to seems pretty good. I've been trying to learn Python lately and working through the interpreter at first seems to be a good way to get a sense of how Python does things. And there are some neat libraries out there. For example, you're not much more than a \"from pygr import worldbase\" away from wandering through the human (or mouse, or yeast...) genome if you want to. That said, I still need to set up a proper vim config for Python."}]},"Bd2bbHoVQSM":{"wy":"Cited in threads about hard-to-cancel subscriptions and timeshare scams as an informative overview of the sketchy exit industry.","q":{"x":"I think this Last Week Tonight episode is very informative on the topic","i":41650857,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41650857,"author":"VTimofeenko","month":"2024-09","story":"Timeshare owner? The Mexican drug cartels want you","stance":"rec","why":"Informative episode on sketchy timeshare exit industry","quote":"I think this Last Week Tonight episode is very informative on the topic","text":"I am very sorry to hear that and I hope they can properly escape this situation. Maybe my opinion is biased (the media generally does not publish \"everythibg is a-OK\" articles), but my perception of the timeshare escape industry is that they are sketchy too. I think this Last Week Tonight episode is very informative on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36032247,"author":"dixie_land","month":"2023-05","story":"Roblox: Ten-year-old spent £2,500 of mum's money without her knowing","stance":"rec","why":"Segment arguing timeshare exit companies are bigger scams","quote":"John Oliver had an interesting segment on timeshares and argues timeshare exit companies are even bigger scams","text":"John Oliver had an interesting segment on timeshares and argues timeshare exit companies are even bigger scams: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35277042,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2023-03","story":"The FTC wants to ban those tough-to-cancel gym and cable subscriptions","stance":"rec","why":"Covers timeshare exit scams that double-scam owners","quote":"John Oliver just did a Last Week Tonight on Timeshares. He goes over that in the video.","text":"Yeah, John Oliver just did a Last Week Tonight on Timeshares. He goes over that in the video. Also there are scam companies that exist that promise to take your money to help you exit your timeshare agreement, then disappear. So you get double-scammed. And somehow this is technically legal? Link to the Last Week Tonight video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39059971,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2024-01","story":"Americans are spending billions on stuff they forget to cancel","stance":"reference","why":"Example of businesses that are hard to exit","quote":"Just go look at timeshares [1].","text":"Whenever this subject comes up I'm disheartened to see how many are willing to blame the victim: the consumer. We have allowed businesses to exist whose only really business model is duping customers into a subscription and then making it difficult or impossible to cancel. Just go look at timeshares [1]. I would dearly love to give money to some quality news publications to support their work but I don't. Why? Because I absolutely don't ever want to deal with their subscriptions department should I ever want to cancel. Ever tried to cancel an Equinox gym membership? I've seen half a dozen sign up constulants standing around at the entrance, someone call up to cancel and being told there's no specialist available. Cancelling a service should be no more difficult than pressing a button on an app or a website. We passed the CAN-SPAM Act to force compliance in communications for businesses [2]. The same should apply to taking their money. Speaking of gyms, it's an anti-user business model. The perfect gym customer is one who pays for a membership and never uses it. The more members a given gym can \"support\", the more profitable it is. If everyone used it 2 hours a day they'd go out of business. Planet Fitness took this one step further and removed their most popular equipment to dissuade people from going and charging so little that people usually can't be bothered to cancel it. All US cable/Internet start with an introductory rate that quietly gets jacked up over time. You can g"}],"r":[["x6AOwDttBsc",1],["Fiu9GSOmt8E",1],["qPb_0JZ6-Rc",1]]},"CM0aohBfUTc":{"wy":"HN cites it in discussions of sponsored science content and Veritasium's credibility, usually as the go-to critique.","q":{"x":"Tom Nicholas did a good job breaking down the problems with it","i":36565882,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38779199,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2023-12","story":"Veritasium: The SAT Question Everyone Got Wrong [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Critique of Veritasium that changed the commenter's view.","quote":"You may be interested to see this critique of Veritasium which cooled my views on the channel significantly","text":"You may be interested to see this critique of Veritasium which cooled my views on the channel significantly since I saw it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36565882,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2023-07","story":"Entropy: The Most Misunderstood Concept in Physics [video by Veritasium]","stance":"rec","why":"Breaks down problems with Veritasium's sponsored self-driving video.","quote":"Tom Nicholas did a good job breaking down the problems with it","text":"I was quite disappointed by his video on self-driving cars. It presented a very one-sided view and felt like a puff piece (and, indeed, it was sponsored by Waymo). Tom Nicholas did a good job breaking down the problems with it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30800676,"author":"alexb_","month":"2022-03","story":"Kodak factory tour: How does Kodak make film? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Worthwhile critique of YouTube sponsorship propaganda.","quote":"This video may be worth a watch","text":"This video may be worth a watch: [VIDEO] Side note: I don't really agree with a lot of his other videos. Yet I am sharing this one, because I believe it's worthwhile and am not taking his other videos into account for a context that doesn't matter :)"},{"id":32859685,"author":"ummonk","month":"2022-09","story":"Did GoogleAI just snooker one of Silicon Valley’s sharpest minds?","stance":"reference","why":"Critique of sponsored Waymo content and reviewer experiences.","quote":"It was critiqued by Tom Nicholas","text":"Veritasium's video in particular: [LINK] It was critiqued by Tom Nicholas: [VIDEO] Most notable was Snazzy Labs' own comment in the replies to Tom Nicholas' video which descriped their experience participating in the Waymo sponsored reviews: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32043128,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2022-07","story":"“EVs aren't the only answer”: Toyota scientist on the future of cars","stance":"reference","why":"Critique of Veritasium's Google-sponsored self-driving video.","quote":"was criticised for filming a propaganda video for Google[0]","text":"> I'm hardly an expert on this subject What are your opinions based on? I've seen some some \"propaganda science\" videos, which were essentially Tesla fanboys defending Tesla's choice for some reason. IMHO, nothing ever is that one dimensional. Obviously those who invested in PHEV or something else will defend that point and I can't see how one can be correct and the other can be the wrong choice when all come with pros and cons. Did Intel made the wrong choice x86? Did Apple made the correct choice with ARM? Isn't it possible that at different stages of maturity of the tech and the market the \"correct choice\" can be different? Lately I'm very annoyed of the science and tech propaganda on Youtube. One of my favourite YouTubers, Derek Muller of Veritasium, was criticised for filming a propaganda video for Google[0] and I'm afraid that the critics have a point. Company positions in the skin of scientific discussion are not new but with YouTube is seems like we are having hard time to identify them. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32223515,"author":"foerbert","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: YouTube Channels for the Intellectually Curious","stance":"reference","why":"Context in a debate about sponsored science channels.","quote":"Making a living is one thing. But that isn't my problem.","text":"> I don't know what to tell you if you believe Thunderf00t's \"debunking\" is more valuable than your own viewing of any given channel. I wasn't very inclined to view Matt Ferrel's videos much in the first place. I was too leery of the way they were presented to consider it worth my time to investigate further. I was wondering if I was missing out, but never decided to really try to find out first-hand as it just wasn't worth it to me. It was at that point that I saw Thunderf00t's video, and I decided to see what his take was. As you note, I'm a bit wary of Thunderf00t as well. I don't watch his videos often and when I do I ensure to remain a little more actively critical. Based on what I saw there, I didn't feel inclined to further wonder if I was missing anything by avoiding Ferel's videos. I'm sure that video, and the follow up, were probably some of the weakest from Ferel. However, it was less the actual content and rather the approach that didn't fit what I'd want to watch. Uncritical readings of pie-in-the-sky press releases isn't my thing, and the follow-up in particular made it clear that that was not where Ferel thought the problem was. > Regarding Veritasium, that is an even weaker argument IMO. If we're going to try to pillory everyone trying to make a living off communicating STEM topics I'm not sure who we'll have left in the space. Making a living is one thing. But that isn't my problem. I wouldn't recommend avoiding his channel if it was just a matter of him runn"},{"id":29610722,"author":"runarberg","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Case study of sponsorship influence on Veritasium.","quote":"Veritasium lost a bunch of his credibility capital when he made the sponsored content for the self driving cars.","text":"For me Veritasium lost all his credibility capital by the way he responded to Tom Nicholas’ video about YouTube sponsorships using Veritasium as a case study[1]. In stead of approaching the debate calmly (or better yet, simply ignoring it) he came to the comment section way too defensive, like a lot, basically calling Tom a lair and a bad actor. Actually that is not quite true, Veritasium lost a bunch of his credibility capital when he made the sponsored content for the self driving cars. I never watched that video as I spotted it was basically and ad and was reluctant to watch his new stuff after that (though I did). 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28975538,"author":"emsign","month":"2021-10","story":"SmarterEveryDay's 4privacy can, and cannot, meet its goals","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They're getting hired by companies to basically produce an ad in the style of their regular videos.","text":"> Where I get uneasy is how quickly SmarterEveryDay seems to have > switched from a YouTube channel about explaining things into a > YouTube channel about stoking \"Big Tech\" fears as a way to sell > apps. The latest video is title \"Is Your Privacy An Illusion? > (Taking on Big Tech) - Smarter Every Day 263\". This has been a trend with YouTube science communicators and educators for a while now. They're getting hired by companies to basically produce an ad in the style of their regular videos. Physics Girl did a video in cooperation with Toyota praising Toyota's hydrogen powered test car, telling her audience that hydrogen as a passenger car fuel has a future while ignoring the fact that every car manufacturer including Toyota has given up on it and favoring Lithium battery packs instead. [LINK] It was an odd move from her to hype Hydrpgen cars but her audience caught on to it quickly in the comments. Which prompted her to make a follow up video: [LINK] where she's trying to save what's left to save for the hydrogen cause. Veritasium did it too. In which Derek Muller created nothing short of a propaganda piece for driverless cars and not even just an ad for the Waymo service in particular. [LINK] This gives me the same vibe as those doctors promoting cigarettes smoking against coughs in the 1950s and 1960s: Here's a professional often someone who is highly regarded as being objective and impartial who is basically leaving out crucial information to push something either a prod"}],"r":[["uxykI30fS54",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["EpRrD58Ah3Q",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1],["zb7Bs98KmnY",1],["-s5szfPYKY4",1],["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["hx7BXih7zx8",1],["tTBWfkE7BXU",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1],["4PHT-zBxKQQ",1],["dPHPNgIihR0",1],["EiZhdpLXZ8Q",1],["jQIHqkudgNY",1],["NOAgplgTxfc",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1]]},"LfascZSTU4o":{"wy":"Offered as an example of someone who publicly predicted the financial crisis ahead of time, in arguments about economic forecasting.","q":{"x":"There have been people like Peter Schiff warning about the recession and specifically saying when it will happen and how long it will last","i":3459489,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":6355012,"author":"cpursley","month":"2013-09","story":"Low pay and the rise of the machines","stance":"rec","why":"Schiff predicting the financial crisis and housing bubble.","quote":"these people and groups predicted the financial crisis and housing bubble","text":"+1 on Mises Institute and Peter Schiff. Say what you want about Austrian / free market economic schools, but these people and groups predicted the financial crisis and housing bubble: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3459489,"author":"nextparadigms","month":"2012-01","story":"Inside the Fed in 2006: A Coming Crisis, and Banter","stance":"rec","why":"Schiff warned of the recession beforehand.","quote":"There have been people like Peter Schiff warning about the recession and specifically saying when it will happen and how long it will last","text":"There have been people like Peter Schiff warning about the recession and specifically saying when it will happen and how long it will last, and even politicians like Ron Paul warning about the housing bubble since 2003. They just refused to believe it, and I guess everyone in the Fed fell prey to group think. They couldn't admit to themselves that their strategy was failing, and even worse, that they were responsible for the economic failure through their policies over the past few years. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":425566,"author":"natmaster","month":"2009-01","story":"The Two Flaws Of Libertarian Economics","stance":"rec","why":"Peter Schiff predicting the economic collapse in 2006.","quote":"I tend to trust the scientists that have models that can accurately predict the future","text":"I stopped reading when he misstated everything about Libertarianism: \" 1. Corporations are more “efficient” than the government because of the “natural selection” of our capitalist system. 2. Corporations are therefore better than the government at everything. 3. Corporations should be the government, or at least in control of it.\" This guy obviously not only misunderstands what Libertarism is, or Austrian economics. But also grossly misunderstands the cause of our economic collapse. I tend to trust the scientists that have models that can accurately predict the future: [VIDEO]"},{"id":425577,"author":"natmaster","month":"2009-01","story":"The Two Flaws Of Libertarian Economics","stance":"rec","why":"Schiff's accurate prediction of the crisis.","quote":"Most scientists (myself included) tend to prefer models that accurately predict the future","text":"My thoughts: Most scientists (myself included) tend to prefer models that accurately predict the future: [VIDEO] What Zed describes is in no way libertarianism. What the following to understand exactly what it is (HINT: It's the opposite of Libertarianism.) [LINK]"}],"r":[["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["wq5D43qAsVg",1],["7Q25-S7jzgs",1],["nBiJB8YuDBQ",1]]},"OsFEV35tWsg":{"wy":"HN uses it to argue that systems rather than bad apples drive cruelty, and for its section on how anonymity changes behavior.","q":{"x":"Here's Zimbardo's TED talk on what makes people do evil","i":4146595,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":39189663,"author":"Rochus","month":"2024-01","story":"The Value of Downvoting, Or, How Hacker News Gets It Wrong (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how anonymity changes people's behavior.","quote":"If you're interested in the effects of anonymity to people, watch this part of Zimbardo's very interesting lecture","text":"> the most notable difference between Hacker News and Reddit is that it's impossible to downvote anything on Hacker News. [..] It's an interesting design decision, but ultimately a bad one, in my opinion. That's not true (as the author realized himself). And I don't agree with the author; letting people downvote is unfortunate in my opinion, and a bad design decision. The possibility to downvote encourages misuse. I don't see any reason for the majority of downvotes I come across; mostly it's just other opinions that don't suit a few people; often it's even the correct presentation of the facts that has been downvoted. Most people on HN are anonymous. Downvoting is even more anonymous; there is usually no record from who the downvotes come. If you're interested in the effects of anonymity to people, watch this part of Zimbardo's very interesting lecture: [VIDEO] . The anonymity of the web, or the masses, gives these people a power that obviously overwhelms them. There were experiments which demonstrated how it can play out when ordinary people suddenly gain power (see e.g. [LINK] ). The possibility to downvote promotes the bad in people. And it is not even necessary. If you like a comment or story for whatever reason, you can upvote. That's already enough to promote popular content, and demote unpopular content. And if a comment violates site rules, it can be flagged. Of course also flagging can be misused (as it is very popular e.g. on Lobsters); this can be mitigated in tha"},{"id":37032287,"author":"Rochus","month":"2023-08","story":"In Defense of Programming Languages","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how anonymity changes people's behavior.","quote":"If you're interested in the effects of anonymity to people, watch this part of Zimbardo's very interesting lecture","text":"That's the usual naggers club you can meet all over the web today. And it's getting worse, not better. What these people say is an end in itself to make themselves important and does not help the matter. It undoubtedly has something to do with anonymity. Most people on these forums are anonymous, also the quoted people are obvious fantasy pseudonyms. Downvoting is even more anonymous; there is usually no record from who the downvotes come. If you're interested in the effects of anonymity to people, watch this part of Zimbardo's very interesting lecture: [VIDEO] . The anonymity of the web, or the masses, gives these people a power that obviously overwhelms them. There were experiments which demonstrated how it can play out when ordinary people suddenly gain power (see e.g. [LINK] )."},{"id":4146595,"author":"RyanMcGreal","month":"2012-06","story":"Woman Who Couldn’t Be Intimidated by Citigroup Wins $31 Million","stance":"rec","why":"Zimbardo argues systems, not bad apples, make people do evil.","quote":"Here's Zimbardo's TED talk on what makes people do evil","text":"> The argument that gets repeated ad nauseum, that things like this are the fault of \"a few bad apples\", is just plain false. Philip Zimbardo, the architect of the Stanford Prison Experiment and author, more recently, of The Lucifer Effect , argues that it's not \"bad apples\" that spoil the barrel, but a \"bad barrel\" that spoils the apples. Here's Zimbardo's TED talk on what makes people do evil: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8726251,"author":"rasz_pl","month":"2014-12","story":"US Senate Report on CIA Detention and Interrogation Program","stance":"reference","why":"Cited regarding how one person can resist wrongdoing, as at Abu Ghraib.","quote":"It usually takes only one man","text":"Remember Abu Ghraib? there was only ONE guy not ok with what was happening. It usually takes only one man [VIDEO] in case of US concentration camps there also was one man [LINK] he is still in prison, because ratting on your fellow men is against the law There usually are no 'good cops' either, because doing something in the face of evil marks you as a traitor"}],"r":[["2Z43y94Dfzk",1],["Qg0mHFcB510",1],["5SaFTm2bcac",1]]},"j2dHFC31VtQ":{"wy":"HN people link it in threads on big vehicles and road safety as a comparison of US and Dutch fire department approaches.","q":{"x":"shows quite well the major difference in approach between fire departments in the USA vs the Netherlands","i":44708296,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44708296,"author":"piva00","month":"2025-07","story":"Why does a fire truck cost $2m","stance":"rec","why":"Compares US and Dutch fire department approaches","quote":"shows quite well the major difference in approach between fire departments in the USA vs the Netherlands","text":"I think this video from the Not Just Bikes channel shows quite well the major difference in approach between fire departments in the USA vs the Netherlands (which is quite similar to many other European countries): [VIDEO] Fire engines in Europe are as well equipped as the American/Canadian ones while not depending on these massive and expensive bespoke rigs."},{"id":42375975,"author":"viraptor","month":"2024-12","story":"Fire risk assessment of battery home storage compared to general house fires","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant look at fire department requirements","quote":"There's a good video from Not Just Bikes about it","text":"Depending on which requirement they mean, that case is actually interesting. There's a good video from Not Just Bikes about it [VIDEO] (\"How American Fire Departments are Getting People Killed\")"},{"id":41421541,"author":"CHB0403085482","month":"2024-09","story":"Americans' love affair with big cars is killing them","stance":"rec","why":"Oversized fire trucks force bad road design causing collisions","quote":"forcing bad road design to accommodate giant fire trucks cause more car collisions","text":"Also \"How USA Fire Departments Are Getting People Killed\". Summary: forcing bad road design to accommodate giant fire trucks cause more car collisions [VIDEO]"},{"id":44707911,"author":"ropable","month":"2025-07","story":"Why does a fire truck cost $2m","stance":"reference","why":"American fire trucks have grown larger over time","quote":"American fire trucks have gotten progressively larger over time","text":"I recently watched a YouTube video that described how American fire trucks have gotten progressively larger over time, which may help explain some of the cost increase: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43646360,"author":"nayuki","month":"2025-04","story":"The Difference Between Municipal Fire Trucks and Airport Fire Trucks","stance":"reference","why":"Large fire trucks create wide roads and speeding","quote":"large fire trucks create the need for wide roads, which enables normal car drivers to go at dangerously high speeds.","text":"Somewhat related, Not Just Bikes / Jason Slaughter talked about how large fire trucks create the need for wide roads, which enables normal car drivers to go at dangerously high speeds. [VIDEO] (27m24s) [2024-08-11]"},{"id":42720480,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2025-01","story":"43K fewer drivers on Manhattan roads after congestion pricing turned on","stance":"reference","why":"Shows emergency vehicles can work in car-light cities","quote":"And less-car-centric areas ≠ no-vehicles-allowed-ever areas.","text":"> How do you propose we handle logistics or emergency vehicles? Find the the width of said emergencies vehicles, and then build bike lanes that are a little wider than that: the emergencies vehicles can drive down them quite easily (and cyclists can get out of the way quite quickly). Alternatively, if a city has trams, created segregated rights of way, and the emergency vehicles can use them. * [VIDEO] And less-car-centric areas ≠ no-vehicles-allowed-ever areas."}],"r":[["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["OUNXFHpUhu8",1],["6c_LeIXrzAk",1],["v6jhjjVy5Ls",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["q58dLWjRTBE",1],["7Hlb8YX2-W8",1],["rvkEpstd9os",1]]},"x2-rSnhpw0g":{"wy":"Recommended as the introduction to C4 when people ask how to visualize and communicate software architecture.","q":{"x":"If C4 is new to you too, I recommend their intro video","i":37230817,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37230817,"author":"SnowProblem","month":"2023-08","story":"Architecture diagrams enable better conversations","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to C4 as a light ruleset for system diagrams","quote":"If C4 is new to you too, I recommend their intro video","text":"If C4 is new to you too, I recommend their intro video: [VIDEO] . It feels like a very light ruleset over what we would do naturally when explaining a system to another engineer. That's great. Some of the ideas that stood out were: 1. Allow flexibility in the notation (shapes and color) as long as the abstractions are good. 2. When drawing arrows, make them unidirectional to show the main intent. 3. Hide details to express the main story (@ 27min in the video) 4. Don't just give names to components. Give short descriptions too. 5. Don't document the lowest levels. Code is better here."},{"id":31576308,"author":"qrian","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: Visualizing software designs, especially of large systems (if at all)?","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces the promising C4 model","quote":"which seemed very promising.","text":"Recently stumbled upon [C4 Model]( [VIDEO] ), which seemed very promising. It builds on the failure of adopton of UMLs, and aim to be practical and communicative."},{"id":29093855,"author":"tmountain","month":"2021-11","story":"Beyond Whiteboarding: Creating Expert System Architecture Diagrams","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces C4 model for coherent architecture diagrams","quote":"I recommend the C4 model.","text":"These fail my litmus test, which is, \"can a third-party look at the diagram and make sense of it without verbal explanation?\" For a more formal approach to coherent diagrams and white boarding, I recommend the C4 model. It's conceptually simple and results in better system documentation than the free form method that's commonly used. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34345854,"author":"chrsig","month":"2023-01","story":"Architecture diagrams should be code","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by the author of structurizr","quote":"the author of structurizr (Simon Brown) was recently linked in another HN thread.","text":"I've looked at mermaid & planetuml before. structurizr is new to me and looks interesting. PlanetUML has the advantage of gitlab support, iirc -- allowing rendering of diagrams in README files. I'd like to see more options for that. edit: interestingly, the author of structurizr (Simon Brown) was recently linked in another HN thread. HN thread - [LINK] YT link - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ThjvMReOXYM",1]]},"7Hlb8YX2-W8":{"wy":"Recommended as informal, long-form live coding to watch, though some commenters criticize his style.","q":{"x":"The guy's a certified legend.","i":31303390,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31303390,"author":"pingeroo","month":"2022-05","story":"Half a million people watch me study on TikTok","stance":"strong_rec","why":"11-hour live implementation of SLAM","quote":"The guy's a certified legend.","text":"I love occassionaly putting on George Hotz's streams while programming. Here's an 11 hour stream of him implementing SLAM from a closet: [VIDEO] The guy's a certified legend. Sadly he 'quit' streaming but the hundreds of hours in the archive should suffice."},{"id":24671983,"author":"viraptor","month":"2020-10","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite YouTube channel to learn advanced programming?","stance":"rec","why":"Informal long-form live coding sessions","quote":"you can find saved streams from geohotz / comma.ai.","text":"It's a different kind of watching, but you can find saved streams from geohotz / comma.ai. Those contain things like learning about blockchain contracts and finding a security bug in one of them in a very informal 8h live stream. Or building a toy computer vision project from scratch for a recorded dashboard footage. [VIDEO] It's an experience... Try watching at higher speed when it gets boring."},{"id":24944392,"author":"donquichotte","month":"2020-10","story":"Pygame 2.0","stance":"reference","why":"Hotz using pygame for visualization in SLAM prototype","quote":"","text":"I am working at a company that is tracking vehicles using GNSS antennas. When designing the vehicle dynamics model for the Kalman Filters, I found it useful to have a graphical representation (including error ellipses, velocity vectors etc). Turns out that pygame was a great fit for that type of visualization. Fast forward to two days ago, when I was idly watching George Hotz design a monocular visual SLAM prototype in 11h. Much to my surprise, one of the first things he fires up is pygame for visualization [1]! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18158320,"author":"lostmsu","month":"2018-10","story":"George Holtz codes 11 hours straight live stream [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sorry to be holy-wary, but that was outrageous: just watched random pieces of that video until found one where he actually codes, basically vim Python editing. So I opened somewhere around this moment: [VIDEO] And, first, he copy-pastes some code, and then spends about a minute figuring if he got tabs converted to spaces, including googling a vim command. Then he proceeds to make a loop over some collection next to the loop over an adjacent collection. And he makes that stupid mistake of renaming a variable of the loop, but no renaming in the body. Man, if that's how professional vim users work, this piece of video will be the only proof I will ever need to prevent zealots from even wasting IDE users time to try vim."},{"id":17485506,"author":"natemurthy","month":"2018-07","story":"All the things I hate about Python","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Note his reaction at 5:55","text":"Hi everyone, so I'm the guy who wrote this article. I'm sharing a video recording of George Hotz writing some Python code. Note his reaction at 5:55 [VIDEO] His outburst is an emotional manifestation of the different sources of frustration I've had working with Python that I've tried to document. A common theme I've seen in responses is that many claim that I've mistaken Python as a weakly-typed language countering that it is indeed a strongly-typed language -- obviously an argument of semantics. I'll define a \"strongly-typed\" language as one where it is trivial to identify the type signature and definition of any function or variable in a program. In Python, this is non-trivial. FYI, this article is also cross-posted on: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["j2dHFC31VtQ",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["OUNXFHpUhu8",1],["6c_LeIXrzAk",1],["v6jhjjVy5Ls",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["q58dLWjRTBE",1]]},"Ocq3NeudsVk":{"wy":"Recommended as an incisive look at the real potential and common misuse of data sonification, often brought up in threads on space sonifications.","q":{"x":"Tantacrul has a typically incisive video [1] about the real potential of sonification as a powerful technique for understanding data","i":32590627,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":23527338,"author":"jw1224","month":"2020-06","story":"MuseScore 4: Moving from notation software to composition software","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Creating sound from data","quote":"","text":"As soon as I saw this pop up on HN, I thought “hey, MuseScore — isn’t that the notation software Tantacrul loves to hate?”. I’m not a composer, and I’d never heard of let alone used MuseScore before stumbling on his videos a couple of weeks ago. What a great surprise to see they’ve been listening to their critics! After finding his channel I slowly worked my way through watching all his videos. One of my favourites is about “sonification”, or creating sound from of data. Might be quite interesting to the HN crowd: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32590627,"author":"omnicognate","month":"2022-08","story":"New NASA Black Hole Sonifications with a Remix","stance":"rec","why":"Real potential of sonification versus dishonest publicity use","quote":"Tantacrul has a typically incisive video [1] about the real potential of sonification as a powerful technique for understanding data","text":"Tantacrul has a typically incisive video [1] about the real potential of sonification as a powerful technique for understanding data, and its real artistic possibilities, vs the dishonest and arbitrary way it gets used to generate publicity. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42008387,"author":"richbell","month":"2024-10","story":"Hear the sounds of Earth's magnetic field from 41,000 years ago","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on making music from data","quote":"","text":"I'm reminded of \"Sonification & The Problem with Making Music from Data\" by Tantacrul. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["MFzDaBzBlL0",1],["KbgvSi35n6o",1]]},"_gXiVOmaVSo":{"wy":"HN points to Bret Victor's segment as a recent, concrete demonstration of Dynamicland's ideas applied to a specific scientific domain.","q":{"x":"It shows just how far the Dynamicland concept can be pushed into a hyperspecific feature set customized to fit a single domain","i":34488120,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36596680,"author":"StevenOtto","month":"2023-07","story":"Bret Victor: Update July 2023","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Biomolecular design in Realtalk showcasing Dynamicland strengths","quote":"Really impressive stuff, as usual.","text":"His latest demo of Realtalk, Biomolecular design in Realtalk, showcases the strenghts of Dynamicland. Really impressive stuff, as usual. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34488120,"author":"jimmySixDOF","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone fully attempted Bret Victor's vision?","stance":"rec","why":"Dynamicland applied to protein visualization with physical objects","quote":"It shows just how far the Dynamicland concept can be pushed into a hyperspecific feature set customized to fit a single domain","text":"This is the latest thing I know of from him and was recorded at the 2022 Foresight Designing Molecular Machines Workshop talk called \"Nanoscale Instruments for Visualizing Small Proteins\" & Bret Victor comes in about 14:14 [1] It shows just how far the Dynamicland concept can be pushed into a hyperspecific feature set customized to fit a single domain -- because they can deconstruct the user experience to that level of detail. Extending a Tangible UI out to the actual OS itself has been the thing at Dynamicland from the get go but here we see it finally as physical 3d objects participating with realtime digital feedback in the built environment !! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34491078,"author":"jimmySixDOF","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone fully attempted Bret Victor's vision?","stance":"rec","why":"Bret Victor's latest demo at about 14:14","quote":"Please see his latest demo you might need to update those priors","text":"Please see his latest demo you might need to update those priors (Bret Victor on stage aprox 14:14) [VIDEO]"},{"id":37527946,"author":"tgbugs","month":"2023-09","story":"Artificial intelligence can revolutionise science","stance":"reference","why":"Example of lab automation and instrumentation work","quote":"For automation and instrumentation of labs the vision is on point","text":"We are still quite far from being able to implement these kinds of things at scale. For the literature review piece the key problem is that LLMs are exquisitely bad at working with even the simplest kind of scientific evidence: citations [1, 2]. They will get better, but it is not clear that LLMs can deal effectively with the very sparse kind of evidence that appears in the literature. Also, generating hypotheses isn't exactly the rate limiting step, the bigger issue tends to be when you get people with pet projects/hypotheses in positions of power that dictate funding priorities (e.g. the decades long Alzheimer's Aβ disaster). For automation and instrumentation of labs the vision is on point and there is interest, and active work, if not large amounts of funding, to bring that vision to reality [3, 4, 5]. However, we simply don't have the tooling needed to be able to express the full complexity of experimental protocols in a way that can be verified. Sure you can write a python script to control a robot, but it is exceptionally difficult to extract the scientific meaning from that. My PhD work was to develop a formal language for scientific protocols, and I'll be continuing to develop it, but there is still a long way to go. 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. [VIDEO] 4. [LINK] 5. [LINK]"},{"id":34026938,"author":"maegul","month":"2022-12","story":"When Paper Beats the Screen","stance":"reference","why":"Bret Victor's latest Realtalk application","quote":"Incidentally, just watched a recent Bret Victor talk about his latest application of his RealTalk/Dynamicland system","text":"A bit wishy-washy, but the general point holds. Since the 90s, I’ve been somewhat surprised to see the PC technology fail to really learn from how well humans have leveraged printed information that is embedded in our environment. I figure screens and projectors are simply harder and more expensive than what would be required for the digital to get paper like. But still, it’s disappointing. Incidentally, just watched a recent Bret Victor talk about his latest application of his RealTalk/Dynamicland system: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fOGdb1CTu5c",1],["Y65FRxE7uMc",1]]},"gcS04BI2sbk":{"wy":"Recommended as the best introduction to self-management and new management paradigms.","q":{"x":"I can highly recommend watching this video if you're interested in self-management and the idea of a new management paradigm","i":8662770,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8662770,"author":"joelgascoigne","month":"2014-11","story":"Ask HN: Do you know other firms like Valve or GitHub?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Introduces self-managing organization paradigm","quote":"I can highly recommend watching this video if you're interested in self-management and the idea of a new management paradigm","text":"At Buffer we've started to move in this direction. I can highly recommend watching this video if you're interested in self-management and the idea of a new management paradigm: [VIDEO] . Frederic's book by the same name also goes into further details and shares several example companies: Buurtzorg, AES, Morning Star, FAVI, RHD and others."},{"id":9790813,"author":"bcx","month":"2015-06","story":"Reinventing organizations (2014) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Good summary of the Reinventing Organizations book","quote":"You can watch a pretty good summary of the book here","text":"The author of this book wants readers to pay what they feel the book was worth to them. [LINK] So you if you end up downloading and reading the direct PDF link (the security through obscurity method is clearly failing here) I recommend paying what feels right to the author: You can watch a pretty good summary of the book here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8673838,"author":"s3nnyy","month":"2014-11","story":"Mean People Fail","stance":"rec","why":"Evidence that less mean management leads to success","quote":"Particularly the example of Buurtzorg, a Dutch healthcare provider, is quite convincing","text":"I agree with PG on this trend. In \"Reinventing Organizations\" by Frederic Laloux you'll get more evidence that lack of meanness leads to success: [VIDEO] Particularly the example of Buurtzorg, a Dutch healthcare provider, is quite convincing: They went from 0% to 80% market-share by basically being less mean to their employees and clients. (This Ask-HN discussion is also related to this: [LINK] )"},{"id":24383909,"author":"schwax","month":"2020-09","story":"Are you an anarchist? The answer may surprise you (2000)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A question I'm asking myself is: \"What would it take for anarchism to work?\" (See also: \"What would it take for capitalism to work for everyone?\") Some ideas to consider: 1. @visakanv on assholes: > Assholes make up about 1% of most groups, cause about 75% of the damage, and ruin everybody’s experience. [1] 2. Dale Pendell on a long-term formulation of anarchism he calls \"Horizon Anarchism\": > I want to see the president, when it comes time to sign a bill, even if the bill may be necessary, to recognize—instead of being proud of every piece of legislation that’s passed—to recognize that it represents a failure of our collective social nature. And, instead of giving away all these pens with fanfare, he should light a stick of incense and say, “My fellow Americans, it is with deep regret that I must announce to you that, because we could not solve this problem on our own, we have had to enact another piece of legislation. Let us pray we can recover our senses and repeal it as soon as possible.” [2] 3. Another question to ask is \"Why are some people willing to cooperate, but not others?\" Theories of developmental stages give us useful models to understand the prerequisites. What cultural, psychological, and physical needs must be meet? I've found a good starting point in this area in Reinventing Organizations by Frederic Laloux [3], which draws heavily on Ken Wilber's integral theory and other research on developmental stages that came before it [4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [V"}],"r":[["9dKiLclupUM",1]]},"hhMAt3BluAU":{"wy":"Commenters point to it as a well-researched explainer of the history and culture behind China's current position, despite the political title.","q":{"x":"Kraut has a fantastic video on the topic","i":27186838,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27186838,"author":"honkdaddy","month":"2021-05","story":"China increases spending 500% to influence America","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains Chinese history and culture behind its present position, despite Trump-titled name.","quote":"Kraut has a fantastic video on the topic","text":"Kraut has a fantastic video on the topic - [VIDEO] While its title relates to Trump, the video is much moreso about Chinese history and culture and what brought them to the position they're in today."},{"id":37097137,"author":"hotdogscout","month":"2023-08","story":"We Need Scientific Dissidents Now More Than Ever","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis done before COVID that explains the mechanics discussed.","quote":"Please take a look at this analysis if you can.","text":"Please take a look at this analysis if you can. It was miraculously done before COVID yet it explains those exact mechanics you exemplified. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28440713,"author":"Aunche","month":"2021-09","story":"Missing businesswoman urges ex-husband not to publish book critical of China","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as backing for China's effective international PR.","quote":"","text":"China is hilariously bad at HN and Reddit PR, which they never cared about to begin with. They are tremendously effective at international PR. By providing economic support to smaller EU nations, they've been able to veto efforts to limit their influence. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24289685,"author":"forgot_account","month":"2020-08","story":"Belarus turned off the internet – citizens hot-wired it","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"TDS Sufferer: \"Trump is a dictator!\" [1] Trump lets the States exercise their Constitutional authority [2] TDS Sufferer: \"Trump is a disappointingly-WEAK dictator!\"[3] It really is bizarre. As you say, there are things he's legitimately punting into the stands, such as his ham-fisted counter to the rise of China (great concept, abysmal execution).[4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"}]},"oLkfnc_UMcE":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear introduction to structured concurrency and as an explanation of subtle asyncio bugs that Trio avoids.","q":{"x":"one of the best explanations I've seen about what subtle bugs are easy to make with asyncio","i":28882968,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28882968,"author":"acidbaseextract","month":"2021-10","story":"A viable solution for Python concurrency","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains subtle asyncio bugs and how trio addresses them","quote":"one of the best explanations I've seen about what subtle bugs are easy to make with asyncio","text":"The video (or blog post) below is one of the best explanations I've seen about what subtle bugs are easy to make with asyncio, why it's easy to make them, and how the trio library addresses them. But yes, consider alternatives before you pick asyncio as your approach! Talk: [VIDEO] Blog post: [LINK]"},{"id":34667714,"author":"kzuberi","month":"2023-02","story":"Structured Concurrency Definition","stance":"rec","why":"Useful introduction with live-coded concurrency problem","quote":"A useful introduction to the ideas is in Nathaniel's PyCon 2018 talk on trio","text":"A useful introduction to the ideas is in Nathaniel's PyCon 2018 talk on trio [1], where he live-codes a solution to a concurrency-spaghetti problem. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24322660,"author":"ric2b","month":"2020-08","story":"Don't trust default timeouts","stance":"reference","why":"Happy Eyeballs concept for multiple requests","quote":"I think it's usually called Happy Eyeballs","text":"Great list. That last suggestion (Multiple requests) can be tough to implement correctly, I think it's usually called Happy Eyeballs: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K_q-xnYRugQ",1],["cQWlnTyOSig",1],["gSKzgpt4HBU",1],["Pi7l8mMjYVE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1]]},"q3OuCR0EpGo":{"wy":"HN commenters who used it remember it as an unusually fast and accurate touchscreen text input method and a fascinating alternative to QWERTY.","q":{"x":"It was absolutely, hands down without question, the most accurate way to quickly enter text into a phone using only a screen.","i":31907657,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31907657,"author":"horsawlarway","month":"2022-06","story":"The PocketReform is a made-in-Berlin Linux handheld","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gesture keyboard for Android enabling fast accurate typing","quote":"It was absolutely, hands down without question, the most accurate way to quickly enter text into a phone using only a screen.","text":"> Sad thing is, that if someone would spend a ton of research and thinking on making something usable and comfortable, most people would completely poopoo it as a freaky unusable layout, just because it would look unfamiliar, lol. Absolutely. 8-Pen was this for Android. (example video here: [VIDEO] ) It was absolutely, hands down without question, the most accurate way to quickly enter text into a phone using only a screen. Seriously - I could type at about 25-35 wpm on my phone screen at 100% accuracy, one handed. They'd done a ton of work to place letters based on frequency and likely combinations to make typing smooth and relatively fast. No fucking disaster of auto-complete that I have to click to get the word I want, or when you actually have a vocabulary larger than 150 words, but auto-complete is fucking dumb and always picks the most basic option from the path, or when the word you want isn't in the phone dictionary, no hiccups if you want to misssspell something for impact. It was genuinely just the best keyboard I have EVER used on a phone. The downside? It takes about 3 weeks of practice to actually learn, and most people just refuse to try for longer than 10 seconds. I still find it incredibly frustrating that qwerty, of all god-damned things, is the input formula we've picked for phones. It's fucking dumb."},{"id":34377370,"author":"Tijdreiziger","month":"2023-01","story":"The iPhone Keyboard – Make It or Break It (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating alternative touchscreen input method","quote":"I’ve always found the 8pen [1] fascinating.","text":"The physical ones (on flip phones) were also 12-key (0-9, *, and #), so I suppose it was just swiping on the keys that was novel. It definitely does leave one to wonder whether that innovation was inspired by this prototype. Since we’re on the topic of input methods: I’ve always found the 8pen [1] fascinating. I sadly never had the opportunity to try it for myself, since I was using an iPhone rather than Android at the time (and it’s since been delisted). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43598057,"author":"grishka","month":"2025-04","story":"Show HN: iPhone 2005 weird \"Blob Keyboard\" simulator","stance":"reference","why":"Concept inspiring ideas for better touchscreen input","quote":"","text":"I honestly never understood why on earth QWERTY became the standard for touchscreen text input. It's a good keyboard layout but only when the keys are physical and large enough that you can type with two hands without looking at the thing. But it's absolutely godawful when you have to use it with one finger or two thumbs and no feel. All those dictionary-based crutches that were piled on top of it over time don't help, they only exacerbate the problem, especially considering how laughably poorly they work with languages whose grammar is more complex than English. I've been using touchscreen phones since 2011 yet I somehow still make typos all the time. This suggests that the problem isn't me. An ideal touchscreen text input method should embrace the strengths of the medium. Touchscreens allow swipes and gestures, so use that to your advantage! Like in the OP, yes. I myself experimented with a similar concept, except it was a 3x4 grid of square buttons that each can input one of 5 characters, one for tap and the other four for each of the directions, inspired by Japanese keyboards. I tried to use it for a day, it worked, but it felt somewhat unnatural. Maybe I haven't given myself enough time to get used to it, or maybe it was a terrible idea to begin with, or maybe the character layout wasn't optimal (I arranged them alphabetically). Another idea I have is to take the 8pen[1] concept but add more lines, make it 6 or 8, so it's practical for Russian which has 33 letters. Haven"}],"r":[["vI5pbDFDZyI",1]]},"qPb_0JZ6-Rc":{"wy":"HN commenters post it in climate threads as a clear explanation of overshoot and declining carrying capacity.","q":{"x":"you should really spend some 30 mins here","i":36861151,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":24739523,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2020-10","story":"More than half of all whale and dolphin species are of conservation concern","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows humanity already far past the planet's biocapacity limit.","quote":"I found this video incredibly illuminating on that point","text":"If we wipe out the vast majority of the animal and plant life and drinkable water on the planet, how likely do you think we will keep our population at an all-time high? I think it's certainly possible you're right that population will keep increasing for the next, well, 10 years at least, maybe not 20, but meanwhile we're cratering our biocapacity, and we are not going to have anywhere near enough resources left on the planet to sustain that population if we keep going on our current trajectory. There's quite a few experts that say it's already too late, we've already depleted our biocapacity too much, the only way to correct for it is to undergo some form of rapid decline at some point in the future (collapse). I found this video incredibly illuminating on that point (I honestly thought we were still under the limit before watching it, not way past it already): [VIDEO] Extinction may be a bit extreme, humans might be able to survive somehow, but the vast majority will not. Here's one article that discusses the possibility of human extinction. Key quote: \"I don’t want to claim that we can’t survive the loss of many, many species. We’ve already proved that we actually can. We’re very adaptable. But I think the bottom line is, you wouldn’t want to find out.\" [LINK]"},{"id":36820924,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-07","story":"There’s a heatwave in the sea and scientists are worried","stance":"rec","why":"Explains declining carrying capacity and overshoot clearly.","quote":"nicely explained here","text":"No, our real problem is \"overshoot\" ( [LINK] ) and the the \"Great Acceleration\" ( [LINK] ). ‘Overshoot’ occurs when humanity consumes bio-resources faster than ecosystems can regenerate and waste production exceeds nature’s assimilative capacity. Overshoot is a meta-problem: climate change; plunging biodiversity; pollution of land, air and waters; tropical deforestation; soil/land degradation etc., etc., are all co-symptoms of overshoot. Climate change is an excess waste problem — CO2 is the greatest waste by weight of modern techno-industrial economies. We cannot solve any major symptom of overshoot in isolation. Indeed, the mainstream approach to emissions reductions will not only fail to subdue climate change but, by promoting material growth, will exacerbate overshoot ( [LINK] ). Almost everything we undertake follows a pattern resembling a hockey stick, growing exponentially long after we've already surpassed safe boundaries. This has been happening for several decades now, at least since 1970. The carrying capacity of our environment has been deteriorating for decades, and recently, the rate of its decline has escalated. It is on the verge of entering free fall, if it hasn't already (nicely explained here [VIDEO] ). We should have stopped using fossil fuels yesterday and started changing our diets, reforming agriculture, reforesting, restoring habitats and biodiversity, reforming financial systems, promoting degrowth and implementing UBI to address inequality, etc., etc"},{"id":36826707,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-07","story":"Climate records tumble, leaving Earth in uncharted territory – scientists","stance":"rec","why":"More information on declining carrying capacity of the environment.","quote":"I'd suggest this for more info","text":"> when the alternative is decarbonizing using cleaner technologies There (might not be) enough fossil fuels left, and EROI is falling fast, so the question is whether we'd able to do this before the energy runs out and our economy will be forcibly reduced by nature. > carbon capture once that technology matures I'm worried that's just a technological pipedream. No such technology will probably exist for decades at the scale needed. We'd need millions of such factories. In fact, we already have such \"technology\", it's called forests ... but we're not doing that either. > Carrying capacity isn't fixed No, it's not. We've extended it in the past, but now it seems that it started decline ... and might enter freefall soon. I'd suggest this for more info: [VIDEO] > energy isn't fixed either Yes, and it's heating the planet and oceans 100x more than what we're consuming now. 5 atomic bombs every second now, is it? I'm aware that 254x254 km2 of solar panels would be enough for all our energy needs (except storage) ... but we're far from that. Even in the most optimistic scenarios we're targeting 25-30% of renewable energy in 2050 ... not really enough. > science fiction scenario where we become a type 2 civilization I'd like us to get there. But we might as well be at the end of the runway, looking at the great filter with our own eyes right now."},{"id":36841198,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-07","story":"How, Mathematically, We’re Fucked: Exponential growth on a finite planet","stance":"rec","why":"Argues collapse is the only realistic scenario given inaction.","quote":"Collapse: The Only Realistic Scenario - Arthur Keller","text":"> Burn it all down and start over We won't be the ones doing that. > We're going to have to figure this sustainability thing out though It may be already late. I wish it wasn't so. I think we could have chance at repairing it ... if only we would do what's necessary. But we're not doing what's necessary, we never started even discussing it, and I'm starting to doubt that we'll ever do. We're talking only about climate change, but that's only one of the symptoms of the overshoot. Even people @ hacker news mostly don't have a clue. > we can't just ignore the problems We've been ignoring them for 50-70 years. The limits to growth / club of Rome was in 1972? So far ... we did nothing but ignored the problems. We may cross the 1.5C threshold in 5 years. Not in 2050 as previously expected, but in 5 years. And we're still pursuing economic growth. Have you noticed any evidence to the contrary lately? > it's going to be a big challenge, and things may get ugly I recommend few videos for you. I must warn you ... it may hamper your ability to enjoy parties. Collapse: The Only Realistic Scenario - Arthur Keller [VIDEO] How to Enjoy the End of the World - Dr. B. Sidney Smith [LINK] Arithmetic, Population and Energy - Dr. Albert A. Bartlett [LINK] Why Societies Collapse And What it Means for Us - Joseph A. Tainter [LINK] Revealing the Naked Emperor – Paris, 2° & Carbon Budgets - Kevin Anderson [LINK]"},{"id":36861151,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-07","story":"Deadly global heatwaves undeniably result of climate crisis, scientists show","stance":"rec","why":"A 30-minute explanation of overshoot to correct misconceptions.","quote":"you should really spend some 30 mins here","text":"Quickly: > Or seed the atmosphere with sulfur dioxide That's not solving the situation, that's temporarily mitigating some effects with poisons for a short term and at the same time emitting a ton of emissions from a lot of flights. > global warming is bad (but manageable) Warming, or better climate change, is only one of the symptoms of the overshoot. We've managed almost zero, nil, zilch in past 50 years (coal is still at 82%). > but pre-industrial society is far, far worse Calling mitigation strategies return to pre-industrial society ... i don't have words for that. But if we don't start solving it, and fast (yesterday was late), the nature will solve the problem for us. One way is better than the other. If you're interested where else you're wrong, you should really spend some 30 mins here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37229645,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-08","story":"We Are Witnessing the First Stages of Civilization’s Collapse","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Collapse - the only realistic scenario?","text":"> So in other words, we don't know what their elites did. They didn't do enough. > And I'm not denying climate change. Climate change is just one symptom of the overshoot of the environment's carrying capacity (planetary boundaries). > Is it obvious? Isn't it the subject of intense scientific research? [LINK] Climate change, biodiversity loss and deforestation (land use change) are our biggest problems, I'd say. > This article just seems like it's taking advantage of peoples anxieties to harvest clicks No, it's just an early bird in the mass media. We'll hear about it more often going forward. Earlier collapse of Anthropocene ecosystems driven by multiple faster and noisier drivers [LINK] Climate Endgame: Exploring catastrophic climate change scenarios [LINK] Trajectories of the Earth System in the Anthropocene [LINK] Ecological doom-loops: Why ecosystem collapses may occur much sooner than expected [LINK] The Human Ecology of Overshoot: Why a Major ‘Population Correction’ Is Inevitable [LINK] Chances Of Societal Collapse In Next Few Decades Is Sky High, Modelling Suggests [LINK] Ecological Collapse Could Happen Much Sooner Than Expected, Study Finds [LINK] Deep Adaptation: A Map for Navigating Climate Tragedy [LINK] Collapse - the only realistic scenario? [VIDEO]"},{"id":37309937,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-08","story":"Exxon says world set to fail 2°C global warming cap by 2050","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Collapse of what? Civilization? I was referring to the collapse (significant degradation) of the environmental carrying capacity. In such a scenario, our civilization would implode on its own. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] > But feel free to try and change my view It's a complex topic ... I only can give you a few links. The system we live in is very complex ... and as in every complex system even a minor error can cripple the system. Just remember how much damage to the economy was caused by just one ship blocking the Suez. I don't think the system is able to handle large-scale agricultural failures, prolonged droughts or abrupt sea level rises. Everything seems to be changing more rapidly than predicted - from air and sea temperatures to thawing, droughts, biodiversity loss, etc. [LINK] Climate Collapse Could Happen Fast - As temperature and weather records fall, Earth may be nearing so-called tipping points. [LINK] Catastrophic climate 'doom loops' could start in just 15 years, new study warns [LINK] Chances Of Societal Collapse In Next Few Decades Is Sky High, Modelling Suggests [LINK] Climate Endgame: Exploring catastrophic climate change scenarios [LINK] The Human Ecology of Overshoot: Why a Major ‘Population Correction’ Is Inevitable [LINK] Trajectories of the Earth System in the Anthropocene [LINK] Earlier collapse of Anthropocene ecosystems driven by multiple faster and noisier drivers [LINK] UN warns Earth ‘firmly on track toward an unlivable world’ [LINK] Limits to Growt"},{"id":36828836,"author":"grobby","month":"2023-07","story":"Resilience in Complex Systems (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Offers a systems perspective on challenges facing global civilization.","quote":"","text":"I found the part \"Humans in Complex Systems\" quite interesting. My understanding of it is that technology could fall short of being an adequate solution to a more systemic problem wrt climate change. In addition to that work, I've been digging around the perspective of system dynamics [1] on the issues the global civilization is facing [2]. I feel the crowd here is more biased toward a solutionist view on those issues - I might be very wrong. Anyway, I was curious about different perspectives on this topics. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36839526,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-07","story":"How, Mathematically, We’re Fucked: Exponential growth on a finite planet","stance":"reference","why":"Covers exponential growth versus environmental carrying capacity.","quote":"Exponential growth vs. carrying capacity of the environment","text":"No paywall: [LINK] Great acceleration graph: [LINK] Exponential growth vs. carrying capacity of the environment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36862685,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-07","story":"Deadly global heatwaves undeniably result of climate crisis, scientists show","stance":"reference","why":"Presented as covering the classic viewpoints on the crisis.","quote":"You have one of the 4 classic viewpoints, pretty understandable, but not really realistic.","text":"> There were 3 billion people in the world in 1960, now we are 8 billion. And some of them consume very little, and a western minority consumes much more than in 1960. [LINK] > Why do I pay almost double for 'bio' foodstuff? Why do people today choose to buy ultra processed instead of simple foods Why is healthy food so expensive in America? Blame the Farm Bill that Congress always renews to make burgers cheaper than salad [LINK] The 2023 Farm Bill is projected to spend $700 billion over the next five years, with powerful industry lobbyists directing funds to enrich themselves at the expense of agricultural communities, human health, animal welfare, and environmental sustainability. Most Americans have never heard of this massive omnibus bill, which Congress reauthorizes every five or so years, yet it impacts us every day. It shapes our food system–from subsidizing factory farms to funding food and nutrition programs, and it is why burgers are artificially cheap and salads cost more than they should. > with votes for politicians that push for innovative solutions Where did that got us in the last 50 years? Still business as usual. > actively trying to develop new technologies to mitigate the strategy You have one of the 4 classic viewpoints, pretty understandable, but not really realistic. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28473096,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: What are your thoughts on the r/collapse community","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I check and comment on the subreddit on an almost daily basis, and have for about a year now. It takes a while to believe that it can happen, for sure. I didn't start seriously believing it for at least six months of checking the subreddit periodically. But when you see how much damage we're doing to our oceans (National Geographic warns there might be no (human) seafood at all by 2048[1], ocean acidification, coral bleaching, etc), how more and more regions are getting into emergency situations with water (which will lead to people getting more and more desperate to get that water, and possibly wars being waged over it), positive feedback loops from global warming that can shoot us way past 1.5c if we get that far (I've seen speculation that it could get up to 12c, which would likely wipe out humanity), and know how terrible humans are at dealing with exponential and compounding escalating situations (as proven by our current pandemic), and yeah, I'm pretty sure it will happen. The only question is how soon. It's also looking like even if we could do enough about it, we'd have to dedicate almost 100% of our efforts towards it, and basically everyone on the planet are being distracted by petty shit, or even other very important issues, just not as threatening to our existence and society like climate change is (including myself, it's hard to avoid the distractions with our monkey brains). So instead we're doing basically nothing but lip service towards these issues, still, af"}],"r":[["UcN7SGGoCNI",1],["gSPNRu4ZPvE",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["G0R09YzyuCI",1],["o0VrZPBskpg",1],["sI1C9DyIi_8",1],["5WPB2u8EzL8",1],["Bd2bbHoVQSM",1],["x6AOwDttBsc",1],["Fiu9GSOmt8E",1]]},"wq5D43qAsVg":{"wy":"Cited in copyright and patent debates to argue that creative works and inventions evolve by building on earlier ones.","q":{"x":"Then watch this rather insightful video titled 'Everything is a Remix'","i":2934871,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2934871,"author":"kubrickslair","month":"2011-08","story":"Apple changed everything? Yeah, right.","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insightful big-picture view of copying and creativity","quote":"Then watch this rather insightful video titled 'Everything is a Remix'","text":"Parent and grandparent, take some deep breadths and relax. Please don't quibble. Then watch this rather insightful video titled 'Everything is a Remix' [VIDEO] And pardon me, for coming off as arrogant, but I hate to see trivial fights here when you are missing the bigger picture."},{"id":3626148,"author":"nextparadigms","month":"2012-02","story":"If Android is a \"stolen product,\" then so was the iPhone","stance":"rec","why":"How inventions evolve through copying","quote":"This video describes very well how inventions get created and then evolve for the benefit of everyone","text":"Look around in any industry you want. How would you define competition, if nothing but very similar products to the original product of that type in that particular market? This video describes very well how inventions get created and then evolve for the benefit of everyone: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16835836,"author":"cromwellian","month":"2018-04","story":"In a Leaked Memo, Apple Warns Employees to Stop Leaking Information","stance":"reference","why":"Companies improving on others' designs","quote":"I favor Kirby Ferguson's take on this","text":"No, everyone is accused of copying Apple, and Apple gets a pass when they come out with something that other companies already did. But the least similarity to anything Apple does is often labeled a copy, sometimes even requiring time-travel as the supposed copy was released first. Take for example the idea that other companies copied Apple's thin bezels. The predecessors for that were the Sharp Aquous, Xiaomi Mix, Samsung Galaxy S8, and Essential Phone. Samsung even had \"True Tone\" long before Apple started marketing it, but were accused of copying it. I favor Kirby Ferguson's take on this ( [VIDEO] ), and we are better served by companies taking designs from others and improving on them, and open publishing and sharing, and the incessant cry of \"Redmond Start Your Copiers\" is really wearing thin."},{"id":4883337,"author":"mtgx","month":"2012-12","story":"Staffer axed by Republican group over retracted copyright reform memo","stance":"reference","why":"Copyright argument tied to remix idea","quote":"","text":"You're right. You do sound like a copyright hawk. Now. Why is the first point about the size of the industry relevant? Unless you're talking about their ability to bribe...I'm sorry...\"lobby\" Congress to pass the laws they like? That shouldn't happen anyway, regardless of their size. Laws should be passed on common sense and what's good for the people at large, not based on how big is your bank account. What is dubious about his argument about copyright? First off, copyright is not \"property\", and shouldn't be treated as property. It's more like a permit. The government allows you to use a certain idea for a \"limited time period\" as it says in the Constitution. Unfortunately, because of the bribing..sorry, again, I meant lobbying...the \"limited time period\" turned from 14 years to almost 10x more. That doesn't sound limited at all to me. Copyright was meant as an \"incentive\" system - not as a welfare system. Ideas can and should be reused. Whoever gets something copyright, most definitely got \"inspired\" or copied parts of someone else' ideas. That's why there isn't really something like \"intellectual property\", because nobody owns an idea 100%. And since you used someone else' ideas, you have to get paid for whatever you added only for a limited period of time, and then allow others to benefit from it, too, and expand the public knowledge. The whole point of the copyright system was to benefit the \"public\". It doesn't say \"the creators\" in the Constitution. The fact that you "},{"id":3463744,"author":"nextparadigms","month":"2012-01","story":"What everyone in the SOPA debate is missing: IP is not a fundamental right","stance":"reference","why":"Creative works build on prior works","quote":"to allow other people later on to use those works, for the betterment of the whole society","text":"I think the current copyright laws are already too overreaching and need to be overhauled. They are basically owning a work forever now, and that's not how copyright was supposed to work. Copyright was supposed to protect the creator for a while, and then release it in the public domain, so other creators can make variations of it, and further enrich the culture, which was actually the ultimate goal of copyright. Protecting the creator was just a means to an end, not the very end itself, which is how MPAA and RIAA are acting right now - like they own everything and forever (with the perpetual 20 year extensions), and are entitled to laws that further protect it, no matter what other rights they abuse to do that (case in point: SOPA, Internet disconnections, ISP monitoring for piracy, etc). And no, \"intellectual property\" is not just like real property. Why? Because you can't just take someone's property and make variations of it, but you can do it with creative works, and it has been done since the beginning of time. And again, this was the ultimate goal of copyright, to allow other people later on to use those works, for the betterment of the whole society: [LINK] [VIDEO] So don't assume that just because they named it Intellectual Property now, it's exactly the same as real property. It's actually more unlike real property than it is like it. IP is more of a misnomer in this case. It's the same thing with patents. It's probably safe to assume that they changed it to that na"},{"id":3463948,"author":"nextparadigms","month":"2012-01","story":"Is That Pinterest? Nope, No It’s Not","stance":"reference","why":"Everything is copied","quote":"It's just the nature of the business, and it has always been like that.","text":"People live in a fantasy world if they think you can build something and you won't be copied - regardless if laws exist against it or not. It's just the nature of the business, and it has always been like that. The only solution is to be the one that truly understands his customers, leads the market, and is always one step ahead of his competition, and you will be rewarded for it. Every single competitor in every single industry is more or less a clone of the \"originator\" (which probably based his product on something else, too) and then they \"copy\" each other's features. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["LfascZSTU4o",1],["7Q25-S7jzgs",1],["nBiJB8YuDBQ",1]]},"-t1_ffaFXao":{"wy":"HN points to it as a detailed explanation of Wolfram's physics hypothesis, and for Wolfram's evident passion, though some find it long.","q":{"x":"He has three long interviews on Lex Fridmans youtube channel! I greatly enjoyed watching those those.","i":30941374,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":38057518,"author":"block_dagger","month":"2023-10","story":"The Concept of the Ruliad","stance":"rec","why":"Wolfram discusses ruliads in this appearance.","quote":"Wolfram is always fascinating on Lex even if he goes on a bit too long","text":"Wolfram is always fascinating on Lex even if he goes on a bit too long (it’s Wolfram after all). He talks ruliads in his second appearance: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30941374,"author":"Pilottwave","month":"2022-04","story":"We’ve got a science opportunity overload: Launching the Wolfram Institute","stance":"rec","why":"Long interview showing Wolfram's passion for fundamental physics.","quote":"He has three long interviews on Lex Fridmans youtube channel! I greatly enjoyed watching those those.","text":"Lots of comments about his \"arrogance\" but i just see someone passionate, judge for yourself! He has three long interviews on Lex Fridmans youtube channel! I greatly enjoyed watching those those. 1:[Stephen Wolfram: Cellular Automata, Computation, and Physics]( [LINK] ) 2:[Stephen Wolfram: Fundamental Theory of Physics, Life, and the Universe]( [VIDEO] ) 3: [Stephen Wolfram: Complexity and the Fabric of Reality ]( [LINK] )"},{"id":27230846,"author":"meowface","month":"2021-05","story":"Physicists identify the engine powering black hole energy beams","stance":"reference","why":"Detailed explanation of Wolfram's physics hypothesis.","quote":"Here's a detailed explanation of the hypothesis in a Lex Fridman podcast episode","text":"Although I'm skeptical of the correctness of Wolfram's grand new theory/hypothesis of everything, there are many intuitively appealing aspects of it, and I wouldn't be surprised if some aspects of the theory may help find or understand future discoveries. (Though maybe not; I'm definitely not an expert.) Here's a detailed explanation of the hypothesis in a Lex Fridman podcast episode: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4-SGpEInX_c",1],["ez773teNFYA",1]]},"8r1CZTLk-Gk":{"wy":"Posted constantly in threads about tech complaints as a reminder to appreciate how remarkable modern technology is.","q":{"x":"Maybe we need to watch this tube to see things in a more balanced way","i":4536739,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4512468,"author":"batista","month":"2012-09","story":"iPhone 5","stance":"rec","why":"Reminds complainers how remarkable the device is","quote":"You might want to see this","text":"Plus, \"simply a taller screen and newer processor\", really? New screen, new processor, new innards, new machining and construction, new connector, new iOS, new camera, improved battery life AND lighter. NFC and wireless charging? Who promised you that? And who delivers those at the moment? You might want to see this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4536739,"author":"tomaszkubacki","month":"2012-09","story":"Everything's broken and nobody's upset","stance":"rec","why":"Gives a more balanced view of complaining","quote":"Maybe we need to watch this tube to see things in a more balanced way","text":"Everythings Amazing & Nobodys Happy Maybe we need to watch this tube to see things in a more balanced way: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2235593,"author":"smiler","month":"2011-02","story":"Why I stopped travelling to the US and largely stopped doing business in the US","stance":"rec","why":"Reminder to appreciate modern air travel","quote":"And please watch","text":"Refuse? Refuse? You can fly to the US - some 4000 miles away in 7.5 hrs for the East Coast and 11 hours for the West coast. You then may have a little bit of a wait at immigration (or not as the case may be). I've gone through in 5 minutes and one time it was 45 minutes. Seeing as you state you're in the UK, you also eligible for the VWP which means you can travel to the US whenever you like. Just consider what it was like to travel before the invention of the airplane, or the steam ship. Please appreciate that you live in a time when travelling the world is very very easy. And please watch [VIDEO]"},{"id":4396403,"author":"calinet6","month":"2012-08","story":"I Have 50 Dollars","stance":"reference","why":"Compared to Louis CK's ultra-realist observational comedy","quote":"","text":"> The communist anti-capitalism rhetoric Black and white much? This idea that everything that's anti-capitalist or anti-stupid-ways-we-spend-money is communist is absurd. The anti-capitalism rhetoric of the \"mid-1900's\" was a full-on propaganda campaign put out by government agencies and supported by the very real threat of nuclear total annihilation. Comparing a complaint about the stupid ways we spend money and organize our priorities to the Cold War is a bit of a stretch. I fear communist comparisons are becoming the new Godwin's law. In reality, the guilt mentality discussed above is much more similar to the ultra-realist perspective of the comedy of Louis CK— [VIDEO] —in that it is ridiculous that we have the immediate concerns that we do, but is, in fact, entirely true, and it's observant and useful to point that out. It is not, however, communist."},{"id":4183529,"author":"ericHosick","month":"2012-07","story":"Brutally honest presidential speech (video)","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as closing note on technological progress","quote":"","text":"Honest about what? Guilt? Should we, as humans, crawl back into our caves and claim resource limitations as the cause? Claim greed was our downfall? Claim that this was all just too much for the human race? I might be a romantic, but it seems to me that focusing on ways to make all peoples lives better is what we should continue to do. We should be technologically solving that which has plagued humanity the most: limited energy and resources. We shouldn't be spending our time focusing on conservation. We should be focusing on how to make conservation un-necessary. Only then, will poverty go away. Maybe, just maybe, the ideas like mining asteroids, fusion, nano-technology, and AI, just to name a few, are silly. However, they are a lot less silly than creating artificial centrally planed markets like carbon offsets to alleviate poverty. That being said I leave this: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":4072008,"author":"utlanning","month":"2012-06","story":"Buttons that morph out of the surface of the device.","stance":"reference","why":"Quoted line summarizing the complaint culture","quote":"","text":"I think your post was tongue-in-cheek, and it is maybe one of my favorite HN posts ever. That said, I get a little bummed that there are no Back to the Future 2-style hoverboards, that I don't have a personal flying car, and that I still have to shower every day. I'd love a polymorphic phone screen, and am bummed that this display isn't that. Just out of curiosity, I rubbed some Vaseline in my ear just now, and must admit that it is more of a bummer than the lack of polymorphic phone screens. \"Everything is amazing, and nobody's happy\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":3860759,"author":"jcc80","month":"2012-04","story":"Time To Get Past Facebook And Invent A New Future","stance":"reference","why":"Applies the line to complaints about tech innovation","quote":"I think Louis CK said it best","text":"I think Louis CK said it best, \"Everything is amazing and nobody's happy.\" [VIDEO] Sorry but Social mobile local (or as people cooler than me are calling it, SoMoLo) is still pretty new. You're just going to have to \"suffer\" through a little more. I get the author's point that it seems like the same things are getting made and funded over and over. But with only about 1/2 the U.S. pop. owning smartphones, you can't blame people too much for trying to stake their claim. Anything more innovative might be too early anyways."},{"id":3613040,"author":"codesuela","month":"2012-02","story":"Splash screens == sloth ","stance":"reference","why":"Louis CK rant on impatience with technology","quote":"this rant from Louis CK","text":"when I read this I instantaneously thought about this rant from Louis CK [Everythings Amazing & Nobodys Happy] [VIDEO] Linux Mint and Windows 7 take 10 seconds to boot on my laptop with a SSD. That's amazing. Just a few months ago I would go make a sandwich while my PC would boot. If you have a problem with splash screens alt tab to the browser of your choice. If you think your PC boots to slow take out your smartphone and play with it. This is not to say developers should be wasteful with users time. Especially web developers know how import page speed is. But it's a complex program just give it a few SECONDS. Also one should mention Steam (the game distribution platform). If you are bored in between rounds you just bring up the steam panel and use the embedded browser to browse the web or chat with friends. But those at times minute long delays. Sure you could embed a email client in the OS the comes up long before the main GUI is loaded but you wont get to read much in 10 seconds."},{"id":3482183,"author":"bad_user","month":"2012-01","story":"With Today's Protests, SOPA Becomes a Mainstream Issue","stance":"reference","why":"Comedian captures how spoiled people are about technology","quote":"This comedian, Luis CK captures it much better than I can","text":"The word you're looking for is not \" stupid \" but rather \" spoiled \". People in first-world countries are spoiled to their bones. They take EVERYTHING for granted. Which is why they don't have the patience to read explanations why Wikipedia is down. They just want Wikipedia and they want it right now. This comedian, Luis CK captures it much better than I can: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2880498,"author":"sliverstorm","month":"2011-08","story":"San Francisco Subway Muzzles Cell Service During Protest","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for appreciating amazing technology","quote":"Too few see the world this way.","text":"It's awesome that cell phones work in the Bart tunnels at all Upvote this man (and give me some downvotes if it balances things out for you). Too few see the world this way. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2755832,"author":"ddw","month":"2011-07","story":"Netflix introduces new plans and price changes","stance":"reference","why":"Applies the bit to complaints about Netflix prices","quote":"","text":"I clicked Comments to say the exact same thing. They have access to thousands of films and whole seasons of TV shows and people are complaining because it's going to cost a little more. Unbelievable. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2513203,"author":"michael_dorfman","month":"2011-05","story":"Dear Record Labels: Focus on Buyers, Not Thieves","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates first-world complaints about iPod capacity","quote":"for the reason Louis CK illustrates in this video","text":"I think in this case, the term was justified, for the reason Louis CK illustrates in this video: [VIDEO] Here we've got someone who has a magical device not much bigger than a wristwatch that holds 16 days worth of music, and he's complaining that it's not enough. For comparison, I was in college when Sony released the first Walkman, and we thought it was the coolest thing since sliced bread-- a device the size of a paperback book that allowed you to listen to 90 minutes worth of music while walking around. So, yeah: 30GB capacity on an iPod is a first-world problem, in my book."},{"id":1995580,"author":"siculars","month":"2010-12","story":"Web Programming Is Hard ","stance":"reference","why":"Reminder of how much has to go right for technology to work","quote":"I am constantly reminded of this by Louis CK","text":"When people complain to me about so and so technology related I tell them to relax, breath and consider the millions of things that need to go right in order for you to watch the latest Lady Gaga video on youtube. I am constantly reminded of this by Louis CK [VIDEO]"},{"id":1818153,"author":"ithkuil","month":"2010-10","story":"Stonebraker:  Clarifications on the CAP Theorem and Data-Related Errors","stance":"reference","why":"Louis CK on impatience with technology","quote":"","text":"Berkely DB is NoSQL under some, let's say literal, definitions of NoSQL. neo4j currently is also such a db, a non sql db, a NoSQL. Normally, though, people expect something more from this NoSQL \"movement\". People want \"scaling\", and elasticity. We live in the world where today you have 2 users, tomorrow you have 2 million of users, the day after you have only 1000. We also live in a world were people are expecting everything to be working always, and are pretty pissed of if things doesn't respond in seconds (watch Luis C.K [VIDEO] ) It's not a surprise that all this hype about NoSQL came out when a number of db implementation were developed which handled replication, sharding, dynamic resizing (add remove nodes) etc Now put a little bit aside the issue with the word \"SQL\" per se. Let's focus on the \"partition tolerance\" feature. I was always frustrated with the fact that no matter how great my product could be, how perfect the implementations would be, how great my db would be; if my machine/rack/datacenter or section of datacenter wen't down, switches break, network connectivity goes down etc. the users of my application are not able to use it, for them it's down. This kind of NoSQL, the one that handles partition tolerance, gives you the hope that eventually you will be able to make great software, resisting to this kind of events. I'm not sure whether by these tools (cassandra, riak etc) I would be able to write an application that actually works better, even in the other "},{"id":1662558,"author":"timf","month":"2010-09","story":"There is a Horse in the Apple Store","stance":"reference","why":"Louis CK on adjusting to marvels too quickly","quote":"Louis CK has a funny bit about it","text":"When the tiny pony is a negative thing, many call it Creeping Normalcy [1]. Sounds like the same phenonmenon to me: adjustments that come slowly enough do not get appreciated. Louis CK has a funny bit about it [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":1614958,"author":"jamesbritt","month":"2010-08","story":"Why are so many people in their 20s taking so long to grow up?","stance":"reference","why":"Louis CK on being pampered and impatient","quote":"","text":"\"If we're into social commentary, I'd point out that as we become more and more pampered, we become more and more infantile. I expect to push the button on the iPhone and have the pizza show up 15 minutes later. I expect to be in contact with all of my friends instantly via text message. I expect to have college paid for and a warm house with the folks if things don't work out.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":1319136,"author":"swombat","month":"2010-05","story":"Did your boss thank you for coding yourself to death?","stance":"reference","why":"Louis CK bit on how people are unhappy despite amazing things","quote":"","text":"I hate to agree with you but... Wait, no, I don't hate to agree with you. You're absolutely right. \"Died from too much hard coding\"? Yeah right. Coding is hard intellectual work. And, as you point out, if you're not happy with the trade-offs at work, you can always start your own business. It's really an awesome situation to be in, compared to what we could have been doing a hundred years ago. Everything's amazing but nobody's happy: [VIDEO] PS: I doubt the coding is what killed \"John\"."},{"id":4143546,"author":"whit537","month":"2012-06","story":"How not to get acquired","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That Louis C. K. bit helps me out in such situations. :-)","text":"That Louis C. K. bit helps me out in such situations. :-) [VIDEO] \"And then when you ran out of money, you'd just go, 'Well, I can't do any more things now.'\""},{"id":1662385,"author":"jedwhite","month":"2010-09","story":"The Funded’s Adeo Ressi Arrested After Virgin America Flight Incident","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Queue the Louis CK Everything's Amazing & Nobody's Happy clip :)","text":"Queue the Louis CK Everything's Amazing & Nobody's Happy clip :) [VIDEO] Me, I think they're just buses in the sky... except for all that economic and personal friction we pay to ride them."},{"id":1523283,"author":"pkaler","month":"2010-07","story":"I have seen Antennagate, and it is us","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Stuff like this always reminds me of this Louis CK clip","text":"Stuff like this always reminds me of this Louis CK clip: [VIDEO] \"Will you give it a second! It's going to SPACE!\""}]},"HE6rSljTwdU":{"wy":"Offered as a concise summary of Russian security concerns and why Russia reacts as it does to NATO and Europe.","q":{"x":"has a few errors but is overall a good summary.","i":21856859,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":30452764,"author":"pseudostem","month":"2022-02","story":"Estonia, allies to trigger NATO Article 4","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Russian reaction to NATO expansion","quote":"","text":"I usually avoid posting here as I see far more intelligent people on this forum. Since you asked for conjecture... I'd recommend Caspian Report's video [1] first. Secondly, if we look at Cuban missile crisis, US had a strong reaction to missiles around them. I see the Russian reaction pretty similar. They do not want NATO expansion and they do not want missiles in Poland. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21856859,"author":"CapricornNoble","month":"2019-12","story":"Russia up in arms over Chinese theft of military technology","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of Russian security concerns and mindset","quote":"has a few errors but is overall a good summary.","text":">>Russia is wasting its time being an agitator. They could, with time, integrate with the west and coexist That's a fundamental failure to understand the Russian character, their geographic position, and their legitimate security concerns. Caspian Report's video on the subject has a few errors but is overall a good summary.[1] We've had bilateral military training exercises with the Russians in the past.[2] Their military used to be focused on the Caucasus and the largely-Islamic Chechen insurgency. They were restructuring their military towards a brigade-based organization to facilitate fighting those sorts of small wars. But they were faced with continual expansion of NATO eastwards, contrary to a tacit agreement (if not necessarily formalized) with the US. I think the straw that finally broke the back of Russian paranoia was the establishment of Anti-Ballistic Missile sites in Poland, to protect against non-existent Iranian missiles that would supposedly target Europe.[3] Things came to a head in 2007. [4] After that, the Russians began to re-align their military to discouraging another high-intensity war with the west -- in the past 250 years, all three of the existential threats to the Russian nation have been invasions from the west (Napoleon, WW1, WW2). All of that is why they eventually reconstituted the Guards Tank Army[5]. And all of that is why they are currently painted as an \"agitator\". [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":29935554,"author":"guerrilla","month":"2022-01","story":"FSB arrests REvil ransomware gang members","stance":"reference","why":"Geography shaping Russia-Europe relations","quote":"geography will dominate[1]","text":"That's never going to happen if Europe and Russia aren't allowed to integrate their economies further. Until they do, geography will dominate[1] and NATO and Russia will continue to press each other. 1. [VIDEO]"}]},"h2cs8QLnxlU":{"wy":"Shared as a delightful example of a famous media hoax.","m":[{"id":18423826,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2018-11","story":"How Vladimir Lenin Became a Mushroom (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Part one of the Lenin mushroom hoax","quote":"Haha that's awesome!","text":"Haha that's awesome! Part I: [VIDEO] As a kid I read a book which seriously made the case that Jesus was a magic mushroom - the English archaeologist and Dead Sea Scrolls scholar John M Allegro's The Sacred Mushroom and the Cross (1970) \"Allegro's book The Sacred Mushroom and the Cross (1970) argued...that stories of early Christianity originated in an Essene clandestine cult centred around the use of Psilocybin mushroom, and that the New Testament is the coded record of this shamanistic cult. ...that the authors of the Christian gospels did not understand the Essene thought..[that] the Christian tradition is based on a misunderstanding of the scrolls. ...that Jesus in the Gospels was in fact a code for a type of hallucinogen, the Amanita muscaria, and that Christianity was the product of an ancient 'sex-and-mushroom' cult.\"[0] Well, I learnt since that Santa Claus is a mushroom myth[1], and the soma of the Vedas (the earliest Hindu texts) sure sounds like mushrooms.. edit: Added last paragraph and..Ok downvoter, care to explain? Maybe you have some good reason, but it just makes me dislike HN when people downvote for apparently no reason but..well, who knows. [0] [LINK] [LINK] [1] I read about that in Scientific American 20ish years ago. Some interesting leads here: [LINK]"},{"id":11953060,"author":"maheart","month":"2016-06","story":"Lenin was a mushroom","stance":"rec","why":"Kuryokhin's Lenin-was-a-mushroom TV hoax","quote":"Haha, hilarious, thanks for sharing.","text":"Haha, hilarious, thanks for sharing. I had to see this for myself. Source: Video, part1: [VIDEO] Video, part2: [LINK] The \"revelation\" occurs at 2m36s: [LINK]"},{"id":15875063,"author":"wimagguc","month":"2017-12","story":"How Vladimir Lenin Became a Mushroom","stance":"reference","why":"Prior HN discussion and working links to the video","quote":"the Youtube links still work too!","text":"I learned about this video from HN last year or so. The relevant discussion is here: [LINK] ...and the Youtube links still work too! [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1]]},"oP1Oq3JLNbc":{"wy":"HN recommends the channel for short, wry videos on obscure topics, and this one is cited as an example of gaming rules via a loophole.","q":{"x":"HAI makes interesting, short, sarcastic videos about obscure topics that HNers will probably enjoy.","i":30501767,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35778032,"author":"AntonyGarand","month":"2023-05","story":"Absurd 200-Foot ‘Railway’ Demolished After Court Closes Shipping Loophole","stance":"rec","why":"Short video on the loophole railway.","quote":"I liked this short video from","text":"I liked this short video from \"Half as interesting\" on Youtube on the railway itself if you're looking for a video on the subject. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30501767,"author":"krallja","month":"2022-02","story":"200-foot-long railway to nowhere is a brilliant shipping loophole","stance":"rec","why":"Short sarcastic video on the railway to nowhere.","quote":"HAI makes interesting, short, sarcastic videos about obscure topics that HNers will probably enjoy.","text":"There was a video earlier this month on Half As Interesting about this railway: [VIDEO] HAI makes interesting, short, sarcastic videos about obscure topics that HNers will probably enjoy."},{"id":33835050,"author":"bombcar","month":"2022-12","story":"Europe Makes Shipping Vessels Pay for Carbon Emissions","stance":"reference","why":"Example of gaming rules via a shipping loophole.","quote":"","text":"It'll be interesting to see how it's gamed, but that doesn't mean we shouldn't try - you will get things like this: [VIDEO] but they can be corrected for later. One solution would be to switch the tax to a tariff on goods that can be avoided if you prove reduced carbon travel. So if your goods are from China, you get the 100% whatever it is, but if you can show that they travelled from China on whatever is considered acceptable, it drops to 20% or 0% or similar."},{"id":30019431,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-01","story":"Adding unusable RAM for tax reasons","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Someone figured out it was cheaper then to import unprocessed meat from Spain to Norway, turn the truck around as soon as they were customs cleared and drive the 3000 km or so back to Spain for curing. Ahh, those Norwegians and their customs evasions. [VIDEO] (do note that I'm ascribing this to Norwegians completely in jest - tone is difficult to get across in text) ... Which recently had an update. [LINK]"}],"r":[["P_fHJIYENdI",1]]},"suo_aUTUpps":{"wy":"People link it as the quick answer to how Google builds 3D maps, and as evidence that planes are still used to capture the imagery.","q":{"x":"Here there is a video on how Google creates the 3D for Google Earth","i":24126252,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24126252,"author":"Hokusai","month":"2020-08","story":"AI Creates Detailed 3D Renderings from Thousands of Tourist Photos","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how Google creates 3D for Google Earth.","quote":"Here there is a video on how Google creates the 3D for Google Earth","text":"I have been spending some time on the Virtual Reality version of Google Earth, and it is amazing to see the world in 3D from inside. Here there is a video on how Google creates the 3D for Google Earth: [VIDEO] I hope to see more of this technologies as it is great to visit places without leaving home."},{"id":15967069,"author":"angch","month":"2017-12","story":"Google Maps' Moat","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how planes with cameras produce 3D imagery.","quote":"Fly plane. Zig zag. 5 cameras, then photogrammetry. StreetView, from planes.","text":"They fly their own planes these days. [LINK] Too bad it's a video. Fast forward about 2 minutes. Or: [VIDEO] Fly plane. Zig zag. 5 cameras, then photogrammetry. StreetView, from planes."},{"id":29811203,"author":"reportingsjr","month":"2022-01","story":"Google deploying planes over cities for 3D maps (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Source showing airplanes still used for 3D maps imagery.","quote":"they were still using Airplanes to gather imagery for the 3D maps","text":"Hmm, I'm curious about your first claim! I can't find anything more recent than ~2017[0], but they were still using Airplanes to gather imagery for the 3D maps. I'm pretty sure this is still the case, as urban area resolution is way higher than available non military satellite imagery. 0: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JEYh5WACqEk",1],["ecIWPzGEbFc",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1]]},"8bT6txm4RpA":{"wy":"HN finds it fascinating as a window into 1930s factory work, often contrasting its hand labor with modern assembly ergonomics.","q":{"x":"I find this half-hour movie from Chevrolet-making in 1936 absolutely fascinating","i":9261576,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9261576,"author":"ptaipale","month":"2015-03","story":"Why do Americans not drive diesels?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating 1936 look at a large car factory.","quote":"I find this half-hour movie from Chevrolet-making in 1936 absolutely fascinating","text":"Whatever you think of GM today, I find this half-hour movie from Chevrolet-making in 1936 absolutely fascinating: [VIDEO] This was slightly before the great strikes in Flint, Michigan, and those working conditions are obviously both hazardous and extremely inconvenient. But it shows what an amazing enterprise a large car factory was already then, and the world has come far since those days."},{"id":21167359,"author":"dugditches","month":"2019-10","story":"The GM strike is really about the switch to electric cars","stance":"reference","why":"Shows heavy forge work in old auto plants versus modern ergonomics.","quote":"These fellas workin the forges and lifting those blocks certainly went home more sore","text":">Working in an auto plant is hard, backbreaking work that will leave you physically broken by retirement Maybe 80 years ago. These fellas workin the forges and lifting those blocks certainly went home more sore than someone operating a pneumatic driver all day. [VIDEO] There is so many ergonomic and Health/Safety stuff now. Maybe it's hyperbole, but I wouldn't call auto assembly factory work 'backbreaking' by any stretch."},{"id":14371006,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-05","story":"Tesla factory employees describe grueling work conditions","stance":"reference","why":"Shows tires lifted by hand, contrasted with modern assembly ergonomics.","quote":"To see tires lifted entirely by hand in an auto plant, you have to go back to this famous Chevy film from 1936.","text":"That's why workers need a union. If there's an assembly line station that's too hard, that's a union grievance. There's a way to work out a solution, with the worker being in a strong position. Happens all the time in a union plant. Expecting someone to lift tires overhead for mounting on a production line is way out of date. Here's how it's done in a Ford plant. First, they show the old way, where they had a platform at just the right height to roll the wheel into position. The worker then pushes it onto the lugs and hub. Then, they show the fully robotized operation.[1] Here's an older, manual system, but with a counterbalanced lifting aid to do the heavy part.[2] Simple setups like that are routine. If anybody is lifting anything heavy on an assembly line, you're doing it wrong. There are many simple ways to fix that. To see tires lifted entirely by hand in an auto plant, you have to go back to this famous Chevy film from 1936.[3] That was before the big GM strike. Tesla is still learning how to run an auto plant. Tesla isn't as good at this as they pretend to be. Their head count in Fremont is quite high for the number of vehicles produced. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Sm5xF-UYgdg",1]]},"pyMNIFZTQkg":{"wy":"Cited as a vivid illustration of autonomous weapons and machines that keep running after their creators are gone.","q":{"x":"I love watching this short every couple years","i":23424895,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23424895,"author":"blitmap","month":"2020-06","story":"The case for declaring armed and fully autonomous drone swarms as WMD","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dystopian short film rewatched every couple years.","quote":"I love watching this short every couple years","text":"I'm not sure how contributory to the discussion this is, but I love watching this short every couple years: [VIDEO] (I love it in the sense that I'd love a dystopian scifi movie, but not a reality like this)"},{"id":35836897,"author":"throw_nbvc1234","month":"2023-05","story":"Geoffrey Hinton leaves Google and can now speak freely about his AI concern","stance":"reference","why":"Fiction showing AI continuing attacks after humans are incapacitated.","quote":"where humans are incapacitated and AI just continue on like nothing has happened","text":"identify a target decide to attack the target implement the attack determine the result of the attack If humans are required for the decision, then humans have an inherent hook to prevent more attacks. Otherwise you run into scenarios like [VIDEO] where humans are incapacitated and AI just continue on like nothing has happened. It makes the failure modes more sane. It's not a catch-all but a good agreeable step to take."},{"id":26550527,"author":"bartmika","month":"2021-03","story":"DARPA now has AI-controlled F-16s working as a team in virtual dogfights","stance":"reference","why":"Sci-fi short depicting AI-driven war outliving humanity.","quote":"Here are two science fiction short videos of an artist depicting such a future","text":"> What puzzles me is that as of yet, there doesn't seem to be any talk of using \"AI\" (ie Neural Net stuff) for planning and strategy, which seems like a MUCH more powerful application than this stuff. It'll probably be so effective, it'll still be fighting long after all of humanity has destroyed itself and left an irradiated environment. Here are two science fiction short videos of an artist depicting such a future: \"LAST DAY OF WAR\" - by Dima Fedotov | TheCGBros - [LINK] \"Fortress/Крепость\" - by Dima Fedotof | TheCGBros - [VIDEO] (edit: typofix)"},{"id":22503602,"author":"blitmap","month":"2020-03","story":"Philips Hue Bridge v1 online services will be shut off after April 2020","stance":"reference","why":"Dystopian CGI short on abandoned autonomous machines.","quote":"This is very dramatic, but imagine something like this","text":"For the most part, at least we don't have to worry about forgotten, rogue IoT devices. The overriding mission has been to connect it to shitty online services. Watch these companies go out of business or deprecate APIs. This is very dramatic, but imagine something like this: [VIDEO]"}]},"CuoER1DwYLY":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference for how noisy raw real-time ray tracing is and how it compares with traditional faked lighting.","q":{"x":"this extremely interesting video show state of the art techniques used to fake global illumination","i":21179682,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21179682,"author":"LoSboccacc","month":"2019-10","story":"An Inside Look at Microsoft’s Newest Flight Simulator","stance":"rec","why":"Techniques faking global illumination compared to ray tracing.","quote":"this extremely interesting video show state of the art techniques used to fake global illumination","text":"this extremely interesting video show state of the art techniques used to fake global illumination without using ray-tracing and compares the result with the ray-traced variant [VIDEO] long story short moving object color casting light is the main visible difference that cannot be replicated with traditional techniques (like baking the light reflection in the scene at built time, but can be faked convincingly); ray-tracing is also not limited at reflecting elements that are on the scene like what you'd get from screen space reflection, which is less noticeable of an effect but is there if you pay attention and know what to look for."},{"id":21080686,"author":"missosoup","month":"2019-09","story":"Ray Tracing Denoising","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that current RTX looks worse than existing techniques.","quote":"","text":"And yet I cite 3 unrelated sources that all corroborate what I said with detailed analysis and you cite.. Opinion? > assuming that's what you mean, since you keep using the term \"RTX\" and it's unclear what you talk about, whether it's ray tracing or DLSS etc. I use the term the same way NVIDIA uses it. RTX is anything an RTX core accelerates. Still confused? I think that might have been the intention of their marketing team. > very few people seem to contest that scenes where ray tracing is properly artistically used, have superior aesthetic quality This is quantifiable bs. Ray tracing as a technique is superior to rasterisation, but only with sufficient flops. And the current generation of hardware does not yield that critical number. So we get 'ray tracing', but so subdued and limited that existing approaches just flat out look better and also perform better. [VIDEO] Or if you want a more approachable comparison of RTX vs. not-RTX. consider Minecraft+RTX[1] vs. Minecraft+PTGI[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":19322735,"author":"rolleiflex","month":"2019-03","story":"Tensorflow 2.0.0-Alpha0","stance":"reference","why":"Compares standard global illumination with RTX noise levels.","quote":"Here is a comparison of the regular global illumination vs RTX real-time raytracing","text":"My impression from the analyses I've seen so far is that it barely improves the visual quality, since the game designers learned to fake it so well over the last two decades, and it comes at a significant cost to frame rate. In particular, good ray-tracing with acceptable noise levels comes at around 500 rays a point. RTX cards are capable of 2 rays a point. Here is a comparison of the regular global illumination vs RTX real-time raytracing, particularly take look at noise levels generated by RTX in the background. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19461694,"author":"kuzehanka","month":"2019-03","story":"Quake II RTX: Re-Engineering a Classic with Ray Tracing Effects on Vulkan","stance":"reference","why":"Shows raw noisy RTX output without smoothing.","quote":"Here's what RTX+DLDN actually looks like if not dialled down and smoothed","text":"Path tracing has huge wow factor. The problem with RTX is that on current gen hardware it runs at laughably low sample rates and the result is very noisy even after DLDN. Games are handling this by dialling the amplitude of RTX effects way down and having a smoothing pass. After all that is done, the wow effect is gone and you're just left with a more dynamic version of the same or worse aesthetics that we're already used to. I expect it'll truly take off on next gen hardware, whenever that rolls out. Here's what RTX+DLDN actually looks like if not dialled down and smoothed: [VIDEO] My takeaway is yes that quake 2 demo looks really cool, but you just know that they used every black magic hack in the book to get there, and most of it can't be replicated in a modern high fidelity game. It's definitely not a case of flicking an 'RTX on' switch."}]},"G32NYQpvy8Q":{"wy":"Pointed to as a clear look at what is inside a smart meter, including anti-tamper switches and the cellular module.","q":{"x":"One of the best explorations I've seen of this is Big Clive's tear down of a smart meter.","i":24553413,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24553413,"author":"jon-wood","month":"2020-09","story":"Bypassing ESP32 Encrypted Secure Boot","stance":"rec","why":"Teardown showing anti-tampering switches and last-gasp modem capacitor","quote":"One of the best explorations I've seen of this is Big Clive's tear down of a smart meter.","text":"> You probably shouldn't leave a sensitive device unattended anyway I find this area fascinating in terms of how you protect devices that are both sensitive, and need to be left unattended in someone else's possession. One of the best explorations I've seen of this is Big Clive's tear down of a smart meter. The thing is absolutely full of anti-tampering measures, including several switches that will cut off the power supply if the casing is removed, but most interestingly to me the cellular modem being connected to a big capacitor designed to give it just enough power to send out a last cry for help even if the entire radio module is pulled off the meter. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39067956,"author":"michaelt","month":"2024-01","story":"What's that touchscreen in my room?","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown showing GPRS module in a UK smart meter","quote":"You can see a UK smart meter being taken apart here [1] with the GPRS module shown at around the 2 minute mark.","text":"In the UK, yes. You can see a UK smart meter being taken apart here [1] with the GPRS module shown at around the 2 minute mark. And you can look at meter datasheets [2] which list GPRS WAN as a feature. Smart meters often send a reading every 30 minutes. Some energy companies will then show a breakdown on their website that purports to show how much you're spending on lighting, fridges, appliances and things like that [3]. I suspect they use a lot of guesswork to arrive at that breakdown, given the limited input data. Although it's probably fairly easy to recognise certain multi-hour-and-distinctively-large loads, like EV charging and heat pumps. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":37984286,"author":"bayindirh","month":"2023-10","story":"Millions of smart meters will brick it when 2G and 3G turns off","stance":"reference","why":"Source for meter communication details","quote":"The video is at","text":"The one I saw used BT4.2 to talk with the meter, and cellular network to push data to cloud (IIRC). The video is at [VIDEO]"}]},"II2QF9JwtLc":{"wy":"HN cites it as a firsthand example of search engines rewarding AI-flavored content over genuine writing.","q":{"x":"where he had Gemini replace his 10+ year carefully curated content with AI slop and he instantly shot (back) up to the top of the rankings.","i":47641318,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49381471,"author":"efilife","month":"2026-08","story":"Consumer Rights Wiki","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Rossmann used AI-written SEO content on his site.","quote":"Look for the video on his channel, it's not as bad as it looks.","text":"he explained why he does that. In short, he was fighting to be displayed in search results by having legitimate content on the site for months, but the traffic was dying day by day and killing his business. He then asked Gemini to write him some bullshit SEO and went back to the 1st spot on google. Look for the video on his channel, it's not as bad as it looks. [VIDEO] I found this but keep in mind there were more videos about it. Let me leave the description of the video here: Google delisted my business for almost a year. Professional SEO couldn't fix it, creating a new site didnt help. What finally worked was having AI rewrite my authentic content into corporate-friendly bunk. I hate that it worked. This video took me a month to make because I felt ashamed of my decision."},{"id":48115954,"author":"em-bee","month":"2026-05","story":"Restore full BambuNetwork support for Bambu Lab printers","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why the site looks LLM generated.","quote":"basically louis found that not using AI to design his website drastically reduced the hits he would get from google.","text":"That’s an LLM generated website if I’ve ever seen one the explanation for that is here [VIDEO] basically louis found that not using AI to design his website drastically reduced the hits he would get from google."},{"id":47641318,"author":"gibsonsmog","month":"2026-04","story":"12,000 AI-generated blog posts added in a single commit","stance":"reference","why":"Gemini-rewritten content put his site back at top of rankings.","quote":"where he had Gemini replace his 10+ year carefully curated content with AI slop and he instantly shot (back) up to the top of the rankings.","text":"Louis Rossman recently posted a video ( [VIDEO] ) where he had Gemini replace his 10+ year carefully curated content with AI slop and he instantly shot (back) up to the top of the rankings. They're very clearly favoring their own generated generic content rather than any sort of organic, well written or well informed entries. Shame."},{"id":46935000,"author":"masswerk","month":"2026-02","story":"(AI) Slop Terrifies Me","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann forced to convert advocacy content into AI-friendly text.","quote":"Louis Rossmann finding himself forced to convert his genuine advocacy for repair into textual convenience.","text":"Actually Louis Rossmann finding himself forced to convert his genuine advocacy for repair into textual convenience. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47077426,"author":"nickjj","month":"2026-02","story":"AI makes you boring","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann's story of AI rewriting restoring his search ranking.","quote":"He told his story here .","text":"Look at the world Google is molding. Here's a guy who has had an online business dependent on ranking well in organic searches for ~20 years and has 2.5 million subs on YouTube. Traffic to his site was fine to sustain his business this whole time up until about 2-3 years where AI took over search results and stopped ranking his site. He used Google's AI to rewrite a bunch of his articles to make them more friendly towards what ranks nowadays and he went from being ghosted to being back on the top of the first page of results. He told his story here [VIDEO] . NOTE: I've never seen him in my YouTube feed until the other day but it resonated a lot with me because I have a technical blog for 11 years and was able to sustain an online business for a decade until the last 2 years or so. Traffic to my site nose dived. This translates to a very satisfying lifestyle business to almost $0. I haven't gone down the path of rewriting all of my posts with AI to remove my personality yet. Search engines want you to remove your personal take on things and write in a very machine oriented / keyword stuffed way."}]},"MUdro-6u2Zg":{"wy":"HN points to it as a cool demonstration of cheap precision laser measurement and the origin of hacks based on self-mixing.","q":{"x":"a pretty cool video where he demonstrates how a single laser diode can be used to measure miniscule vibrations","i":41231088,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41231088,"author":"birktj","month":"2024-08","story":"Open source laser microphone picks up laptop keystrokes","stance":"rec","why":"A single laser diode measuring miniscule vibrations","quote":"a pretty cool video where he demonstrates how a single laser diode can be used to measure miniscule vibrations","text":"Related to this Applied Science on YouTube has a pretty cool video where he demonstrates how a single laser diode can be used to measure miniscule vibrations. I wonder if this would be sufficient for a laser microphone? [VIDEO]"},{"id":45323109,"author":"amelius","month":"2025-09","story":"Lidar, optical distance and time of flight sensors","stance":"reference","why":"Near-example of cheap sub-micron laser measurement","quote":"This guy gets close","text":"Slightly offtopic, why is it so difficult to find a cheap and compact laser interferometer that can do sub-micron measurements? This guy gets close: [VIDEO] But why isn't something cheap and small like this commercially available as an integrated system?"},{"id":20531722,"author":"colechristensen","month":"2019-07","story":"Kern Microtechnik: Accurate CNC Machines [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the idea for hacking laser self-mixing sensing","quote":"The idea for hacking on these things comes from Ben Krasnow","text":"There is a cool effect where a laser reflecting back into itself will cause interference inside the laser diode and affect the power required to drive it in a measurable way and with it you can extract a surprising amount of information (distance, rotation, angle, speed, etc.) I was ... hasty ... with my first laser diode and burned it out in about 30 seconds. I like to tell myself the money wasted was spent on learning lessons. The second is coming in the mail. The upshot with this method is that it can be extremely low cost and give you access to write code that interfaces directly with physics instead of somebody else's device. The idea for hacking on these things comes from Ben Krasnow [VIDEO] and several research papers."}]},"R35UuntQQF8":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the most impressive 256-byte production and as an example of how much graphics code can fit in a tiny binary.","q":{"x":"The most impressive to date is probably Puls","i":2858947,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2858947,"author":"psykotic","month":"2011-08","story":"Low-level x86 code compression for 64k intros","stance":"rec","why":"Most impressive 256-byte production to date.","quote":"The most impressive to date is probably Puls","text":"Neat hack! But stacking sine waves with a bit of modulation isn't exactly earth-shattering. You might like to try some of these: [LINK] . The most impressive to date is probably Puls: [VIDEO] . The top three 256b productions on Pouet are all doing the same basic thing of ray marching against an isosurface. The sophistication of the geometry has increased a lot since Lattice, which used something simple like cos(x)^2 + cos(y)^2 + cos(z)^2 = f(t) where f(t) is an oscillatory function of time that controls the thinning and fattening of the lattice."},{"id":8436246,"author":"userbinator","month":"2014-10","story":"Intel Underestimates Error Bounds by 1.3 Quintillion","stance":"reference","why":"Example of compact x87 code in size-optimised graphics intros.","quote":"size-optimised graphics intros, where tremendous accuracy is not essential","text":"> You really don't want to use x86 hardware transcendental instructions ...unless you're doing size-optimised graphics intros, where tremendous accuracy is not essential and the stack-based nature of x87 makes for some extremely compact code: [LINK] [VIDEO] I think the main reason why this issue hasn't been so significant is that the majority of code that uses those instructions isn't dependent on extreme accuracy - code that does would likely use their own routines and/or even arbitrary-precision arithmetic."},{"id":7556998,"author":"chengsun","month":"2014-04","story":"Evolutionary algorithm to find Quake III's fast inverse square hack with Python","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the coder's 256-byte raycaster.","quote":"See, for example, this 256-byte raycaster","text":"Řrřola (a famous demoscene coder [1]) found a far better constant than the original (a 2.7-fold accuracy improvement) by using an optimisation algorithm over all three constants present in the original inverse square hack. His algorithm and the result (along with a plot of relative error) can be found on his blog [2]. [1]: See, for example, this 256-byte raycaster [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["ZsRRCnque2E",1]]},"RPdB_zdyMbM":{"wy":"Shared as an example of great Amiga demoscene work.","q":{"x":"Two Amiga demos that I like from The Black Lotus (TBL)","i":25246849,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25246849,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2020-11","story":"Little Things That Made Amiga Great","stance":"rec","why":"An Amiga demo by The Black Lotus from 2006.","quote":"Two Amiga demos that I like from The Black Lotus (TBL)","text":"The Amiga has a very impressive demoscene... Two Amiga demos that I like from The Black Lotus (TBL): - Eon (2019): [LINK] (4:16 is my favorite part) - Starstruck (2006): [VIDEO]"},{"id":45163387,"author":"dejobaan","month":"2025-09","story":"The demo scene is dying, but that's alright","stance":"reference","why":"Part of a playlist of favorite Amiga demoscene demos.","quote":"Some of my favorite demoscene demos were Amiga ones","text":"I was just walking down demoscene memory lane yesterday. Some of my favorite demoscene demos were Amiga ones (playlist: [VIDEO] ), which lead me to \"The Greatest Video Game Tech Demo Ever,\" Shadow the Beast: [LINK] That video's great because it breaks down how Psygnosis managed to get 12 layers of parallax scrolling at 50fps and 128 colors on-screen. I never really loved the game as much as (say) Blood Money, but it was an awesome accomplishment in the same way demoscene demos were!"},{"id":10913857,"author":"Mithaldu","month":"2016-01","story":"The Amiga Demoscene Mixtape Vol. 1 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of what the active Amiga programming community produces.","quote":"","text":"I've owned one, though i never programmed for it. But from what i know, i guess the answer is that the amiga is very close to a modern computer, in that it has specialized chips for graphics and sound (Denise and Paula), making many tasks quite easy, but is still weak enough to force most programs to be relatively simple. At the same time there's also still a very active competitive amiga programming community², making things like these: [LINK] -- [LINK] -- [VIDEO] -- [LINK] -- [LINK] ² [LINK]"}],"r":[["vgT4Y30DkaA",1],["iD9xk3SDSYc",1],["3Fx5Q8xGU8k",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["Xe5AE-qYan8",1],["8pBPZMUcsh0",1],["P4VBqTViEx4",1],["yFIa-Mc2KSk",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["PdO3IP0Pro8",1]]},"XGbPHp6QfkQ":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on touchscreens versus physical buttons in cars, as an example of an enthusiast's critique of VW's UX.","q":{"x":"Here's a good video from Jason Cammisa about the new (at the time) mk8","i":38689155,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38689155,"author":"deckard1","month":"2023-12","story":"VW Is Putting Buttons Back in Cars Because People Complained Enough","stance":"rec","why":"Good Cammisa review of the Mk8 GTI","quote":"Here's a good video from Jason Cammisa about the new (at the time) mk8","text":"Here's a good video from Jason Cammisa about the new (at the time) mk8: [VIDEO] I had a mk7 and it is the best interior + UX I've seen in a car. Everything is as simple and ergonomic as possible. The volume adjustment is a knob, with soft rubber knurling and indents for feedback. It's not some awful feeling smooth afterthought that you see on certain Toyotas or Hondas. It's not a push button (beep beep beep beep) you find on old Pioneer stereos from the '90s. It's exactly what you want to adjust the volume. The AC is similar. Heated seats are a button with 3 LEDs indicating high/med/low. Everything just makes sense. And then VW got cheap and greedy. The mk8 is almost the complete opposite of what the mk7 was."},{"id":29129633,"author":"DavidPeiffer","month":"2021-11","story":"Some new BMWs won't have touchscreens thanks to chip shortage","stance":"reference","why":"Review noting awful touchscreen interfaces","quote":"the interfaces are often straight up awful","text":"Unfortunately I think it's more driven by cost motivations. As noted in the Mk8 VW GTI review [1], the interfaces are often straight up awful, this being a particularly bad design. They're the biggest source of complaints/issues shortly after buying, and three years later. Additionally, distractions are already abundant in a vehicle. A crappy user interface, which most are, is a safety concern. At minimum, lag between screens loading should be extremely minimal. Staring at a screen for an extra second or two while it registers that you pressed a button and updates the screen is incredibly dangerous and has surely lead to some number of deaths. Two years ago Mazda announced they were moving away from touchscreens, which was very well received on HackerNews. [2] And I can't find the thread right now, but 1-3 years ago there was a really good discussion on here about an eye tracking study in a variety of car models. Touchscreen-only interfaces in cars are kind of like electronic-only voting machines. Technical people who know about computers breath funny thinking about it and see issues left and right while large swaths of people really want it. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":28485413,"author":"29083011397778","month":"2021-09","story":"Our fridge just emailed us to say we opened its door too many times","stance":"reference","why":"Cammisa criticizes VW's capacitive buttons","quote":"Watching Jason Camisa dunk on VW's UX was an exercise in vindication","text":"> my father-in-law's Mazda... the touch screen doesn't work while driving Sounds like it's an older model, and I can tell you from personal experience Mazda, at least, has learned from their mistakes (regarding touchscreens). I know the 2020 models, and I believe much earlier, have all gone back to physical, tactile buttons. Real buttons, not Volkswagon's stupid, stupid capacitive buttons. Watching Jason Camisa dunk on VW's UX was an exercise in vindication for having avoided that horseshit [0]. They are clicky, settings are remembered, and it does what you tell it to do. Frankly, that's all it took for me to insist our new car be a Mazda. And while I apologize for the rant, finding a new car that doesn't tell you to use a touchsceen at 110km/h is far harder than it should be - and what a sad state of affairs that is. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38679059,"author":"dharmab","month":"2023-12","story":"Volkswagen Admits Failure, Will Bring Back Physical Buttons","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It was objectively worse than physical buttons, without any advantage at all!","text":"The bizzare thing is that the soft buttons had hardcoded symbols on them! [VIDEO] It was objectively worse than physical buttons, without any advantage at all!"}],"r":[["3Ea3pkTCYx4",1],["P4VBqTViEx4",1]]},"YGIvWT-NBHk":{"wy":"HN points to it as a rare, practical look at how a game studio tests its games and engine.","q":{"x":"Here's also a good overview how Croteam (creators of Serious Sam and The Talos Principle) approach automated testing","i":32808316,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32808316,"author":"senko","month":"2022-09","story":"I learned to love testing game code","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of Croteam's approach to automated testing in games.","quote":"Here's also a good overview how Croteam (creators of Serious Sam and The Talos Principle) approach automated testing","text":"Here's also a good overview how Croteam (creators of Serious Sam and The Talos Principle) approach automated testing: [VIDEO] The talk is from 2018 and references some of the trends in the industry towards more automated testing."},{"id":32871816,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What interesting problems are you working on?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I haven’t watched them yet, but I added these relevant videos to my YouTube backlog just this week","text":"I worked at an MMO game company and the only testing we had was manual QA for functional testing and bot clients for server stress testing. Not a great environment for building quality software. (Our max server uptime was about 24 hours.) The client had a secret level with all game objects for testing and debugging. But reading your post makes me see that we didn’t think ahead far enough: the secret level could have been the foundation for automated unit testing game objects and battling characters. I haven’t watched them yet, but I added these relevant videos to my YouTube backlog just this week: * Automated Testing of Gameplay Features in 'Sea of Thieves' (GDC 2019): [LINK] * Taking CI and automated testing seriously (2018): [VIDEO]"},{"id":27549654,"author":"dsego","month":"2021-06","story":"TDD from the Factorio Team","stance":"reference","why":"Case studies of CI and testing pipelines for Talos Principle and Serious Sam.","quote":"Not TDD but here is a talk about automated testing at Croteam","text":"Not TDD but here is a talk about automated testing at Croteam Continuous integration and testing pipelines in games - case studies of The Talos Principle and Serious Sam [VIDEO]"},{"id":25559037,"author":"senko","month":"2020-12","story":"Raytracing won't simplify AAA real-time rendering","stance":"reference","why":"Croteam talk on how they test their games and engine.","quote":"Here's a talk from Croteam on how they test their games and engine","text":"> From what I've seen game industry has terrible software engineering practices. You'd be surprised. Here's a talk from Croteam on how they test their games and engine: [VIDEO] I'd wager all major engines are exhaustively tested. Trouble is, there's combinatorial explosion of game state, user input, assets, scripted behaviour and engine, so there's a huge area to cover."}],"r":[["q9Golcxjpi8",1]]},"cvZjhWE-3zM":{"wy":"HN points to it as the go-to accessible video whenever nuclear rocket engines come up.","q":{"x":"Scott Manley does a great video talking about nuclear rocket engines[2]","i":28045877,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28045877,"author":"barbacoa","month":"2021-08","story":"SpaceX installed 29 Raptor engines on a Super Heavy rocket last night","stance":"rec","why":"Scott Manley on nuclear rocket engines","quote":"Scott Manley does a great video talking about nuclear rocket engines[2]","text":"For those interested: As absurd as it sounds nuclear powered aircraft engines are a thing.[1] They have also been proposed for spacecraft, Scott Manley does a great video talking about nuclear rocket engines[2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35187453,"author":"st_goliath","month":"2023-03","story":"Project Orion","stance":"reference","why":"Scott Manley on the nuclear salt-water rocket","quote":"Obligatory Scott Manley video","text":"An other quite interesting concept (also a little less \"out there\" and actually, successfully tested) is the NERVA engine, also linked at the bottom of the article[1]. It uses a nuclear reactor to heat up a single propellant (liquid hydrogen) which then rapidly expands as it goes out the nozzle at the end, resulting in quite high specific impulse and is more efficient than a chemical rocket. If we want to go really far out in terms of crazy rocket designs, there is also the concept of the nuclear salt-water rocket[2][3]. This one uses enriched plutonium or uranium salts, dissolved in water, inside a neutron-absorbent fuel tank design. The water is pushed out through a pipe, where an uncontrolled chain reaction starts. If it goes fast enough, it explodes right inside the engine bell, pushing the rocket. The proposed design in the original paper is supposed to generate hundreds of gigawatts of thermal power. This is comparable to the Chernobyl reactor #4 when it exploded, but unlike Chernobyl (which only exploded once) or the pulsed Orion engine, it could run continuously . [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] Obligatory Scott Manley video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34512101,"author":"coenhyde","month":"2023-01","story":"NASA, DARPA Will Test Nuclear Engine for Future Mars Missions","stance":"reference","why":"Scott Manley covers the nuclear salt water rocket","quote":"Scott Manley video on the subject","text":"Can we develop a nuclear salt water rocket already? [LINK] Scott Manley video on the subject: [VIDEO] Basically a controlled thermo nuclear reaction blasting out the back. \"One design would generate 13 meganewtons of thrust at 66 km/s exhaust velocity (or 6,730 seconds ISP compared to ~4.5 km/s (450 s ISP) exhaust velocity for the best chemical rockets of today).\" You certainly wouldn't want to use this to take off from Earth. But we could use it for deep space travel."}]},"fS4X1JfX6_Q":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in discussions of punctuation, tone and emoji in chat as a short intro to how internet writing norms developed.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott has a nice intro video about this","i":24831377,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24831377,"author":"cuddlybacon","month":"2020-10","story":"A case for using punctuation in Slack","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to how generations of internet users developed casual writing conventions.","quote":"Tom Scott has a nice intro video about this","text":"Tom Scott has a nice intro video about this: [VIDEO] The book he mentions, Because Internet, goes into more detail. But to over-simplify, there were a few major generations of people joining the internet as well as a generation that never fully joined, and each one developed its own conventions for casual writing on their own. Some used the same mechanism to mean different things."},{"id":41600837,"author":"Wingy","month":"2024-09","story":"Gaining access to anyones Arc browser without them even visiting a website","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"lowercase without caps reads with a warmer, informal tone","text":"I use it to indicate tone. Proper capitalization and punctuation reads with a formal, cold tone. lowercase without caps reads with a warmer, informal tone there’s a Tom Scott Language Files video documenting it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29361355,"author":"mac-chaffee","month":"2021-11","story":"The melancholy decline of the semicolon","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Since periods can indicate a serious or angry tone[1]","text":"I use them a lot to control the tone of what I write. Since periods can indicate a serious or angry tone[1], I often need some other way to indicate a separation between two complete sentences. For example: \"It looks like you forgot to do X; that's needed to do Y.\" Using a period could be perceived as anger, but using a comma would make that sentence a comma splice. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27810160,"author":"graynk","month":"2021-07","story":"I Stopped Using Emojis","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the message bubble _is_ the full stop.","text":"As Gretchen McCulloch put it: the message bubble _is_ the full stop. It did happen organically, and it _is_ an alarming event when someone who usually doesn't use full stops in chat suddenly decides to use one. Tom Scott made a video on that [0], which references Gretchen's book - Because Internet [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["UiS27feX8o0",1]]},"mvrE2ZGe-rs":{"wy":"Cited as a concrete look at Forth in action, including its snapshot/restore dictionary model.","q":{"x":"Sam Falvo demonstrates writing a blog engine in Forth.","i":20554187,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":45332817,"author":"thomascountz","month":"2025-09","story":"Easy Forth (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Fun video to see Forth in action","quote":"I recommend the video.","text":"This is a great resource and running in the browser is great for fast feedback. Thanks for sharing! I just started learning Forth a month or so ago, and I found this video from Andreas Wagner[1] fun to watch. If anyone goes through OP's book and find yourself wanting to see Forth in action, I recommend the video. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20554187,"author":"pointfree","month":"2019-07","story":"Levels of code in Forth programming (2002)","stance":"reference","why":"Sam Falvo writes a blog engine in Forth","quote":"Sam Falvo demonstrates writing a blog engine in Forth.","text":"\"Over the Shoulder 1 - Text Preprocessing in Forth\" [VIDEO] - Sam Falvo demonstrates writing a blog engine in Forth."},{"id":15605192,"author":"brandonbloom","month":"2017-11","story":"Dueling Rhetoric of Clojure and Haskell","stance":"reference","why":"Forth's dictionary snapshots and restores instead of mutation","quote":"Instead of mutation, offers snapshots and restores of the “dictionary”. See this video","text":"Clojure is following in the tradition of lisps that do this right. The important bit is the extra indirection on top-level names. I wrote more about it here a long time ago: [LINK] Also interesting is the other end of the spectrum: Forth. Instead of mutation, offers snapshots and restores of the “dictionary”. See this video: [VIDEO]"}]},"nAsrsMPftOI":{"wy":"Used to illustrate that the standard targets consistency rather than taste, and to discuss what standardization is for.","q":{"x":"TL;DR is that the standard is used for the most consistent tea, not most tasty/enjoyable tea","i":17655074,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":36092226,"author":"crzyman","month":"2023-05","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"Standardized items aren't intrinsically enjoyable but still valuable.","quote":"standard/pure/repeatable items aren't intrinsically \"good\" or \"enjoyable\".","text":"I find this to be a close cousin of Tom Scott's \"Making an international standard cup of tea\" video ( [VIDEO] ). And very related to Tom Scott's \"The US government will sell you freeze-dried urine\" ( [LINK] ) and Veritasium's \"The world depends on a collection of strange items. They're not cheap\" ( [LINK] ) They bring forward the idea that standard/pure/repeatable items aren't intrinsically \"good\" or \"enjoyable\". But that doesn't make them not useful or valuable in other contexts. It also offers some food for thought when it comes to what metrics we decide to put value in. We may like the idea of a particular metric, or what we think it represents, but that doesn't make it worth optimizing for. A metric without context is useless, and taking a holistic view of what makes something worth doing allows us to make more informed decisions."},{"id":26401532,"author":"dharmab","month":"2021-03","story":"ISO-8601 date format reference not publicly available","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how ISO standards are funded.","quote":"","text":"That's how the ISO standards body works- you pay access for the spec to fund the ISO's development of standards. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17655074,"author":"mrguyorama","month":"2018-07","story":"HTTP 418 I'm a Teapot","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the standard targets consistent tea, not tasty tea.","quote":"TL;DR is that the standard is used for the most consistent tea, not most tasty/enjoyable tea","text":"An elaboration by Tom Scott: [VIDEO] TL;DR is that the standard is used for the most consistent tea, not most tasty/enjoyable tea"}]},"uAdmzyKagvE":{"wy":"HN cites it as a fascinating explanation of a real Dutch auction, in threads about auctions and ticket sales.","q":{"x":"Fascinating to see how it worked then and how it works now.","i":33668130,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33668130,"author":"poopsmithe","month":"2022-11","story":"How to sell tickets fairly","stance":"rec","why":"How the Dutch flower auction clock worked","quote":"Fascinating to see how it worked then and how it works now.","text":"YES! I love this concept. The big clock featured in the post was described in a Tom Scott video[1] earlier this year. It's the Royal FloraHolland's flower auction in Aalsmeer. Fascinating to see how it worked then and how it works now. Same concept, just computerized and accessible remotely by bidders. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37630082,"author":"somat","month":"2023-09","story":"Why Scalpers Can Get Olivia Rodrigo Tickets and You Can't","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the big auction clock","quote":"Unfortunately I don't think the flower auctions use the big clock anymore.","text":"Have you seen how the dutch flower auctions do it? Those guys have something like 30 seconds max to hit their mark. Now those are professional buyers. I think for your amateur crowd you would have to provide a bit more time. perhaps a week. just make it clear what is happening. And that big clock, have you seen the big clock? if I ever made a dutch auction ticket site A big clock would feature predominantly in the ui. Unfortunately I don't think the flower auctions use the big clock anymore. which is a shame. [VIDEO] (tom scott)"},{"id":33929342,"author":"Nican","month":"2022-12","story":"$850 USD to renew your own .dev domain which is owned by Google, insane","stance":"reference","why":"Famous example of a Dutch auction","quote":"","text":"Price decreases as time passes? It is called an Dutch auction. [1] The most famous example I can think of is the flower auction/Amsterdam clock.[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"-dAjCeMuXR0":{"wy":"HN uses it as a source for what Harris actually said, in arguments about vaccine politics.","m":[{"id":30478503,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2022-02","story":"How American conservatives turned against the vaccine","stance":"reference","why":"Clarifies what Harris actually said about the vaccine.","quote":"","text":"There is a video of Kamala Harris declining to get a vaccine if it is developed under Trump administration. I’ll try to find it in a bit. Good morning folks! Edit: Actually she said she would take it if doctors say so, but not if Trump tells her to get one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30143524,"author":"0xy","month":"2022-01","story":"Operation Warp Speed: A new model for industrial policy","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source of Harris's statement.","quote":"","text":"The Trump admin closed the borders with China in late January and was accused of being racist and exaggerating the risk by news media and the Democrats. In fact, they're the only people who took it seriously at all. Then OWS was partly responsible for shipping vaccines in record time while the media said it was impossible to do. As for undercutting experts, the current Vice President literally said she wouldn't take the vaccine, implying it couldn't be trusted because Trump was engaged in the rollout [1] while the current President said the vaccine was rushed and that people shouldn't trust it. [2] Numerous \"experts\" threw FUD at the vaccines, calling them rushed repeatedly. [3] Dr Fauci himself said a vaccine's approval was \"rushed\" by regulators in some markets. [4] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":28842334,"author":"jmeister","month":"2021-10","story":"Covid lesson: trust the public with hard truths","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of Harris's vaccine statement.","quote":"","text":"Kamala Harris is on record(last summer) saying she wouldn’t take the vaccine if Trump told her to take it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28277337,"author":"seriousquestion","month":"2021-08","story":"FDA Approves First Covid-19 Vaccine","stance":"reference","why":"Quote of Harris on vaccine skepticism.","quote":"","text":"You don't even have to imagine. Kamala Harris: \"But if Donald Trump tells us to take it, I’m not taking it.\" [VIDEO] The same thing was broadly expressed on twitter at the time too, she was far from alone in that sentiment."},{"id":27153474,"author":"antientropic","month":"2021-05","story":"Vaccinated Americans now may go without masks in most places, the CDC said","stance":"reference","why":"Source for what Harris actually said about the vaccine.","quote":"","text":"> People have memories. And sometimes they have false memories. For example, regarding: > 8 Months ago Kamala Harris herself said she would NOT get Trump's Vaccine. What she said was that she would take the vaccine if the professionals said it was safe, not if Trump told her to take it [1]. A rather understandable level of skepticism given Trump's peddling of miracle cures such as hydroxychloroquine. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28024868,"author":"0xy","month":"2021-08","story":"Sky News Australia has been suspended from YouTube after a review of old videos","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here's the mainstream media stoking vaccine misinformation repeatedly, including attacking the safety of the vaccine, masks and travel bans. [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] [7] Dr Fauci: \"A vaccine that you make and start testing in a year is not a vaccine that's deployable\" [8] Andrew Cuomo: \"I think it's going to be a very skeptical American public about taking the vaccine, and they should be.\" [9] Kamala Harris: \"If Donald Trump Tells Us To Take It, I’m Not Taking It\" [10] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK] [8] [LINK] [9] [LINK] [10] [VIDEO]"}]},"5PmphkNDosg":{"wy":"Cited as an outsider's critical look at Musical.ly and TikTok and the child-safety problems of video platforms.","m":[{"id":24067501,"author":"hn_check","month":"2020-08","story":"Facebook launches its TikTok rival, Instagram Reels","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I knew musical.ly only from Paymoneywubby","text":"\"Everyone calls me stupid every time I bring up the fact that Twitter had a better TikTok before TikTok in Vine\" The view that \"Vine was tiktok before tiktok\" is extremely common. Vine was very popular, was a trend beachhead, but it just wasn't something Twitter was really interested in so they shanked it. Speaking of which, I didn't realize that musical.ly morphed into TikTok. I knew musical.ly only from Paymoneywubby - [VIDEO]"},{"id":19209617,"author":"css","month":"2019-02","story":"On YouTube, a Network of Paedophiles Is Hiding in Plain Sight","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Which is similar to the recent videos PayMoneyWubby has been exposing","text":"> Enter “twister girl” and autocomplete suggests “little girl twister in skirt” Jesus… Credit where credit is due, I learned about this from MattsWhatItIs: [LINK] Which is similar to the recent videos PayMoneyWubby has been exposing: [VIDEO] and [LINK] It reminds me of a darker version of [LINK] ."},{"id":20343116,"author":"lm28469","month":"2019-07","story":"TikTok being investigated in the U.K. for handling of children’s data and safety","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This platform is at the very least extremely disturbing.","text":"Every time I read \"tiktok\" I have flashbacks from one of paymoneywubby's video [0]. This platform is at the very least extremely disturbing. It's mild but I'd tag it as NSFW. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18094190,"author":"sorenjan","month":"2018-09","story":"Bytedance said to be valued at over $75B in new round","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It seems like a lawsuit waiting to happen","text":"I've never used Musica.ly myself, and I don't have any kids of my own, but I came across this video [0] where a Youtuber looked at clips from it. It seems like a lawsuit waiting to happen, I can't see how it could be worth that much money. I wouldn't want any of my future kids anywhere near there. [0] [VIDEO] (NSFW language)"},{"id":17733061,"author":"burger_moon","month":"2018-08","story":"Anatomy of Tik Tok, the Viral Video App from China's Bytedance","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's a video someone made recently explaining it better","text":"They left off the part that gets brought up anytime someone asks what musical.ly is/was. Which is an app filled with young teens being advertised to old men. I haven't used the app yet, I was interested in checking it out and maybe I'll play around with it but every discuss about tiktok and musical.ly comes back to it just being a weird platform that seems to exploit young teens and showcase the videos to old men. Here's a video someone made recently explaining it better [VIDEO]"}]},"5nKk_-Lvhzo":{"wy":"Only brought up as a joke about encabulator-style nonsense jargon.","m":[{"id":39014741,"author":"stephenr","month":"2024-01","story":"Show HN: Shelly: Write Terminal Commands in English","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Essentially Turbo/Retro/Hyper Encabulator[1], in text.","text":"I guess LLMs in general could be thought of as just a faster way to copy and paste bad S/O code, so perhaps it's just the next logical step of \"fail fast\" for people who need to fail faster than they can copy and paste. /s On a serious note: the one thing I think an LLM would be perfectly suited for, I haven't seen anyone make yet: producing real-enough sounding copy text, as a better replacement for Lorem Ipsum (aka Lipsum) text. The point of Lipsum is that the text is gibberish and thus should be ignored, so the focus is on the important factors: layout, colours, design, functionality. But in 20 years I've never once seen a non-technical person who could grasp that concept, so it's defeats the whole purpose it's being used. Of course it's also inevitable that some Lipsum will end up in the final product sometimes too, which confuses non-technical people and highlights an obvious failure to technical people. LLM powered text waffling on about the topic would be perfect. It doesn't matter if it hallucinates shit, it just needs to seem correct at a casual glance and it's already a massive improvement. Essentially Turbo/Retro/Hyper Encabulator[1], in text. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38424624,"author":"datameta","month":"2023-11","story":"Rockwell Retro Encabulator [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"What a legacy. Practically pioneered a new field single-handedly.","text":"What a legacy. Practically pioneered a new field single-handedly. And a few decades later they did it again! [VIDEO] Some will say the toroidal de-synchronizer firmware didn't need a significant refactor but those folks are probably still using memory unsafe languages with their 1978 V1.1 differential vortex limiter module."},{"id":33832696,"author":"jkqwzsoo","month":"2022-12","story":"The Turbo Encabulator's long, weird and funny history","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Finally, an Encabulator for the modern cybersecurity environment.","text":"What a coincidence, I just watched SANS ICS’s promo for their new HyperEncabulator last night. Finally, an Encabulator for the modern cybersecurity environment. [VIDEO]"}]},"5rXPrfnU3G0":{"wy":"HN cites it as the key evidence of what Chelsea Manning leaked and as an example of civilian deaths in war, with some early commenters disputing how to read the footage.","q":{"x":"For those unfamiliar with the still unprosecuted war crimes exposed by Manning (and published by Assange)","i":19875300,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":29097659,"author":"dotancohen","month":"2021-11","story":"Israeli spyware company NSO Group placed on US blacklist","stance":"reference","why":"Example of US forces engaging civilians.","quote":"Unfortunately the US is well-known for actually actively engaging civilians.","text":"Unfortunately the US is well-known for actually actively engaging civilians. [VIDEO] Whether or not that is an isolated incident is irrelevant. The US has a reputation for shooting civilians. The US media doesn't help either, often portraying soldiers shooting civilians even in fiction."},{"id":23686951,"author":"stickfigure","month":"2020-06","story":"India bans TikTok, WeChat, and dozens of other Chinese apps","stance":"reference","why":"Notes the video is still available on YouTube.","quote":"Doesn't look very banned to me.","text":"Do you mean this video, \"Collateral Murder\", which is currently available on Youtube? [VIDEO] Doesn't look very banned to me."},{"id":20178172,"author":"EForEndeavour","month":"2019-06","story":"Julian Assange: Home Secretary Signs US Extradition Request","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes soldiers dismissing children's presence on the battlefield.","quote":"Physically sickening.","text":"Physically sickening. [Voice 1] Well it's their fault for bringing their kids into a battle. [Voice 2] That's right. ( [VIDEO] \"Battle\" my ass."},{"id":19875300,"author":"StanislavPetrov","month":"2019-05","story":"Chelsea Manning released from jail with expiration of grand jury","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the unprosecuted war crimes Manning exposed.","quote":"For those unfamiliar with the still unprosecuted war crimes exposed by Manning (and published by Assange)","text":"I think its pretty clear that she refused to testify out of principle. None of the war criminals exposed by Manning's leaks have been charged or tried for their crimes. The government's refusal to charge the war crimes, which are central to the entire case, completely undermine any pretense of \"justice\". As usual, the government is using its infinite power to punish those who exposed wrongdoing, while completely ignoring the actually wrongdoing that was exposed. I find it quite admirable that Manning refuses to be used as a tool to further this charade of injustice, at great personal expense. Being held in \"contempt of court\" is no vice when the court, and the prosecutors, have done everything possible to earn that contempt. For those unfamiliar with the still unprosecuted war crimes exposed by Manning (and published by Assange): [VIDEO]"},{"id":16601944,"author":"mmjaa","month":"2018-03","story":"Adrian Lamo has died","stance":"reference","why":"The video Manning released that made her the commenter's hero.","quote":"","text":"She's a hero if someone says she is, and that's all there is to it. I think she was a brave, selfless person in doing what she did, and I'm glad that the US got its ass handed to it in this way. American military crimes are heinous, and the entire nation is doing nothing effective about it. There are millions of people suffering because the American public are too cowardly to confront their own military. So by sticking her neck out and releasing the Collateral Murder videos, she is most definitely my hero. (EDIT: s/Damage/Murder/ - this video: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":13421957,"author":"etjossem","month":"2017-01","story":"Obama Commutes Bulk of Chelsea Manning’s Sentence","stance":"reference","why":"Shows journalists killed in an Apache strike.","quote":"After watching journalists get annihilated by an Apache missile strike, there is not a lot of room for confusion.","text":"> no idea what was in any of the information being leaked After watching journalists get annihilated by an Apache missile strike, there is not a lot of room for confusion. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13424792,"author":"enraged_camel","month":"2017-01","story":"Obama Commutes Bulk of Chelsea Manning’s Sentence","stance":"reference","why":"Shows firing on a van picking up wounded and eager gunner.","quote":"they fired on a van despite realizing that it had come to pick up the wounded.","text":">>This is war. The video clearly shows that soldiers made a decision made on an honest, although tragic, mistake. Um, they fired on a van despite realizing that it had come to pick up the wounded. Geneva Conventions clearly state that hospitals, both fixed and mobile, ambulances, hospital ships, medical aircraft, and medical personnel are not to be fired upon. [VIDEO] (You'll also note the trigger-happy attitude of the gunner. He kept begging for permission to engage, and at one point got pretty frustrated that he was having to wait. Like the OP said: chilling.)"},{"id":12578063,"author":"eth0up","month":"2016-09","story":"Chelsea Manning's 14 days in solitary for suicide attempt is 'cruel and inhuman'","stance":"reference","why":"What Manning exposed.","quote":"It still sears my mind that someone could be locked in a cage by our own government for exposing this","text":"It still sears my mind that someone could be locked in a cage by our own government for exposing this: [VIDEO] (Collateral Murder video) People actually died in that example. I am unaware of anything but hypotheticals regarding anyone killed as result of Manning's \"leaks\"."},{"id":8796053,"author":"_nedR","month":"2014-12","story":"Julian Assange: Why I Founded WikiLeaks","stance":"reference","why":"The shortened version of the footage.","quote":"Shortened version","text":">> He did not publish raw source material, he published his narrative: He published both. Full Unedited Footage : [LINK] Shortened version : [VIDEO]"},{"id":8119944,"author":"atmosx","month":"2014-08","story":"Big Data Firm Says It Can Link Snowden Data To Changed Terrorist Behavior","stance":"reference","why":"Counterexample to claim civilians are never targeted.","quote":"They look pretty much civilians to me.","text":"> But innocent civilians are NEVER OUR TARGET. I'm getting for granted that you are the USA. So in order to avoid never-ending discussions here you go: [VIDEO] They look pretty much civilians to me. ps. You know, the guy who released this video... Is hiding in an Embassy in the UK because he is terrorised by someone who call him a terrorist , food for thought!"},{"id":7733614,"author":"vfclists","month":"2014-05","story":"Rand Paul: Show Us the Drone Memos","stance":"reference","why":"Example of military killing children without evidence.","quote":"","text":"Could you kindly provide evidence of the threats are you referring to? You are simply saying that the officials should be taken on trust alone? An American citizen who open admits committing a crime gets more due process than a harmless child in some third world country. Do American military men ever have to produce evidence before killing children, as in [VIDEO]"},{"id":6726312,"author":"gasull","month":"2013-11","story":"Secret Trans-Pacific Partnership Agreement","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The Collateral Murder video was most likely watermarked.","text":"The Collateral Murder video was most likely watermarked. I think the low quality of the video is because the quality had to be degraded so the watermarks can't be seen/scanned. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6095819,"author":"falk","month":"2013-07","story":"Snowden asylum still under review, stays in airport for now","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as example of US killing journalists.","quote":"","text":"\"I mean, Putin straight kills journalists.\" So does the United States of America. [VIDEO] The United States government wants to make an example out of Snowden. There's no way he would serve just two years in prison."},{"id":6125356,"author":"monsterix","month":"2013-07","story":"Bradley Manning verdict to be announced Tuesday","stance":"reference","why":"Shows what Manning whistleblew on.","quote":"Seen collateral murder [1]?","text":"> What exactly was Bradley Manning trying to whistleblow about? Seen collateral murder [1]? > Its hard to say that that is not treason. Really? It's so easy to kill the messenger. Not to bring in the emotional aspect of this typical power structure vs. single human issue here, but then treason would be the last word I'd want to use for the guy who uncovered killing of innocents and wrong doings of some (I think most soldiers are not trigger-happy rather good people who love peace) stupid crazy soldiers in our army (who should be behind bars instead!). Let's not forget that we pay the bills for all this. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5312813,"author":"derrida","month":"2013-03","story":"Bradley Manning’s Statement","stance":"reference","why":"Shows US troops shooting unarmed civilians and journalists.","quote":"Here are some US troops shooting at unarmed civilians, children and Reuters journalists.","text":"> What wrong-doing did Manning uncover; what crimes? Here are some US troops shooting at unarmed civilians, children and Reuters journalists. There was never a gun held, or fired except by the US troops... [VIDEO] This is the reality of asymmetric warfare, where doing your job walking down the street in Baghdad can get you killed. There are 1000's of such incidents we know about thanks to Manning. This happens to be the most graphic and easily understood because it is a video. Other stories are contained in the giant databases and are a couple of hundred words of text. Personally I uncovered a case in Cablegate where the US Ambassador to Nigeria asked for someone to be rendered to the US without going through the Nigerian Courts. This constitutes extraordinary rendition, moreover, this was pre-911 before extraordinary rendition was known to be widespread. If anybody actually looks at the material, you'll grasp its significance immediately. Moreover, there is a good chance with 250,000 cables and countless war logs if you see something significant, you are the only person in the world to have recognised the significance of that piece of information."},{"id":5315074,"author":"derrida","month":"2013-03","story":"Bradley Manning’s Statement","stance":"reference","why":"Exact second a freeze-frame was taken, contradicting military claims.","quote":"Here is the exact second that freeze-frame above is taken in the collateral murder video","text":"Provably false. Here is the exact second that freeze-frame above is taken in the collateral murder video [VIDEO] Compare with the picture that the US military claims is of an armed person [LINK] That person isn't carrying anything let along a weapon. At the exact time the US troops say \"that's a weapon\" (around or just after 3:13) and thus fix the belief that the group is carrying weapons, from the Apache all that is visible in shot are 2 camera men, with cameras. What amazes me is that who ever decided to make this picture didn't go to the effort to at least make a claim that wasn't provably false from the available evidence. It's really hard to prove a negative existential statement, so that would have been on their side."},{"id":3316367,"author":"VladRussian","month":"2011-12","story":"Red Cross Wants Real Life Laws Enforced Within Virtual Worlds","stance":"reference","why":"Example of soldiers eager to shoot.","quote":"","text":"killing virtual people in a virtual world - violence, bad. At the same time each and every of us oppresses, brutalizes, tortures and kills in real-life through our hired-for-money agents - governments at all levels. \"Come on, let us shoot\" [VIDEO] Games are to blame? Or hysterical propaganada dehumanizing arabs, Afgans, etc... and blessing violence against them."},{"id":1242542,"author":"shaddi","month":"2010-04","story":"Wikileaks gun camera video of civilians shot in Baghdad 07/12/07","stance":"reference","why":"Previous content of a link, now changed.","quote":"In case the link is changed, this is what was previously there","text":"I believe this is the full, unedited helicopter camera footage. [LINK] In case the link is changed, this is what was previously there: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1242386,"author":"Skeuomorph","month":"2010-04","story":"Wikileaks gun camera video of civilians shot in Baghdad 07/12/07","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The man appears to \"engage\" the helicopter.","text":"Right, because photo stills from \"collateralmurder.com\" are intended to convey the ease of identification from a moving helicopter during a live situation? There are other form factors for RPGs: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] I wouldn't want to be wrong on the fly about whether it was an RPG or a black telephoto barrel that's sticking around the corner aiming at my crew. Presumably I'd use other cues to help decide. The site leaves out stills showing the key decision-triggering factor - surreptitious behavioral cues. Here is one they left out: [LINK] The site talks about the cameraman shooting a picture of \"whatever is occurring further down the street\" but the video, even in slow replay (that the airmen didn't have in the moment), suggests the man is taking cover then popping out to aim at the helicopter. The man appears to \"engage\" the helicopter. Thinking in context of 2007, that was incredibly dumb: [VIDEO] Want a picture of an American chopper while you're walking in a hostile zone with militia carrying what even \"collateralmurder.com\" admits \"might be a weapon\" and \"appears to be a weapon\" -- ok, stand in the middle of the street with your camera, wave your obvious press card while wearing your obvious press jacket, and take your picture. Don't duck around corners then share the intel with your gun carrying group. Troops made a call about the RPG vs camera (though certainly doesn't look like a white Canon lens as shown in the website, looks black, and that tube and the way he aim"},{"id":1270343,"author":"pbhjpbhj","month":"2010-04","story":"WikiLeaks plans to post video showing US massacre of Afghani civilians","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"see the guy at 3:45 swinging around?","text":"[VIDEO] see the guy at 3:45 swinging around? [LINK] makes it clearer. Also the US mil report from the time (not a response to Wikileaks, from 17 July 2007, 5 days after the event) - They did have AKM/AK47 and loaded RPG + extra rounds and were hidden around the corner watching, one of their party took pics, [LINK] of an APC whilst their friends were apparently readying an RPG to blow them up with. And though it's not proof of anything specific in this case the photog in question, who died, was known for getting \"victory\" shots of insurgents handiwork."}]},"C7TwBUxxIC0":{"wy":"HN cites it as the debunking behind news stories about radioactive anti-5G pendants and wellness trinkets sold on Amazon.","q":{"x":"the maker went to debunk the negative ion energy bands, only to discover some of them had highly dangerous powdered thorium coating them.","i":29603851,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":45014857,"author":"IlikeKitties","month":"2025-08","story":"The unlikely revival of nuclear batteries","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you knew how bad it really is: [VIDEO] Anyway here's where you can order bulk capsule of americium: [LINK] Thorium oxide powder [LINK] That should get you started repeating the atomic boyscouts experiments straigt from china. If the thorium oxide isn't to your liking in bulk, it's been sold as a \"negative ion\" product to gullible esoteric idiots and widely available. Edit: you might also need some beryllium for the neutron gun, you can get that on ebay [LINK] Anyway, i'm on a whole lot of fbi lists now"},{"id":44172745,"author":"e2le","month":"2025-06","story":"Plutonium Mountain: The 17-year mission to guard remains of Soviet nuclear tests (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Quantum wands filled with thorium dioxide powder","quote":"The \"quantum wands\" as shown in the YouTube video are filled with a sizeable quantity of thorium dioxide powder","text":"Not entirely related. Some scam health products are made with radioactive material[0,1] (thorium dioxide) and can be found in wands, baby clothing, pendents, cards, wristbands, face cream, toothbrushes, and more. Even more concerning given that these products will shed that radioactive material into the environment and be ingested by humans. The \"quantum wands\" as shown in the YouTube video are filled with a sizeable quantity of thorium dioxide powder and is possible to forcibly open. They are illegal but continue to be sold. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":29593948,"author":"Nextgrid","month":"2021-12","story":"'Anti-5G' necklaces are radioactive and dangerous, Dutch nuclear experts say","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here's a video about the subject","text":"Here's a video about the subject - as usual, Amazon was (and might still be involved) in selling such products: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29603851,"author":"posnet","month":"2021-12","story":"Necklaces worn to 'protect' against 5G are radioactive experts warn","stance":"reference","why":"Maker debunks negative ion bands and finds powdered thorium coatings","quote":"the maker went to debunk the negative ion energy bands, only to discover some of them had highly dangerous powdered thorium coating them.","text":"Reminds of this video [VIDEO] where the maker went to debunk the negative ion energy bands, only to discover some of them had highly dangerous powdered thorium coating them."},{"id":29607367,"author":"derefr","month":"2021-12","story":"Lead exposure from routine consumption of coffee and tea from ceramic mugs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> If you can't trust the origins of pottery on Amazon, why can you trust the origins of anything on Amazon? Because many (most?) categories of goods are consolidated into an effective oligopoly of reputable brands, where these brands push competitors out of the market in basically every respect — in brand-recognition, in advertising payments, in number of reviews, in review score, in \"link juice\", etc. So even when there are garbage Shenzhen e-waste products being marketed on the same website, in the same category as the thing you're looking for, you won't see them if there are real products to be had, because the first five pages are all the real products. (Unless you're doing very specific searches, using very unusual keywords that the well-known brands would never think to buy.) This is why the experience of shopping on Amazon is a different experience than shopping on a site like Wish or AliExpress: Amazon has both the e-waste and the stuff from well-known brands, and if you stay \"on the happy path\" of using the site, you'll end up mostly seeing stuff from well-known brands. The only time this falls down is when there are no reputable brands in a category. Such as in the category of kitsch stoneware. For those categories, Amazon and Wish/AliExpress become effectively the same site. > And if you can't trust the origins of anything, you can't count on safety regulations and standards that you take for granted. I mean... most of the things I buy on Amazon, I'm not putting in"},{"id":29620113,"author":"Groxx","month":"2021-12","story":"People Are Wearing Dangerous Radioactive Pendants to Ward Off 5G Radiation","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"These and similar products have been around for years, and can often be purchased through Amazon","text":"These and similar products have been around for years, and can often be purchased through Amazon: [VIDEO] 5G is just the new excuse."},{"id":29620581,"author":"colanderman","month":"2021-12","story":"Anti-5G “quantum pendants” are radioactive","stance":"reference","why":"Expose of such junk products, leading to some being pulled from Amazon","quote":"the former of which apparently resulted in several of the products being pulled from Amazon","text":"The product pictured featured in an exposé of such junk in a pair of Thought Emporium videos a year ago [1] [2], the former of which apparently resulted in several of the products being pulled from Amazon (as reported in the second video). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27919623,"author":"6r1d","month":"2021-07","story":"Even if you’re paying, you’re still the product","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"last radioactive items for healing appeared, oh well, on Amazon.","text":"> This is how a free market works. Said free market tends to create weird customs that are not necessarily good for the most of us. Someone once said radiation has healing properties and last radioactive items for healing appeared, oh well, on Amazon. [VIDEO] and before that, there's a museum of the same stuff [LINK] Note that I am not saying the regulator can not or will not abuse their rights. This is a big issue and I'm not competent enough to say where I'm leaning there. I only disagree with \"free market will lead to the best solution by itself\"."},{"id":23205244,"author":"0zelot","month":"2020-05","story":"DIY Particle Detector","stance":"reference","why":"Cloud chamber footage showing alpha particle tracks from radon","quote":"If you look closely in thought emporiums video (link below), the thick clouds","text":"Yes, radon is pretty hilarious and spreads in very unexpected ways. Completely wrapping sources of radon like uranium or thorium minerals (or these bracelets containing some of that), only holds back the diffusion process for some time unless the shielding is really thick and very dense. That's why radon accumulates so well in household cellars, it penetrates concrete walls easily. If you look closely in thought emporiums video (link below), the thick clouds - representing each the full paths of individual alpha particles (from the point of decay until full absorption in the air) - don't originate right at the surface of his shielding materials (paper, chicken skin... whatever). Instead, the alpha particle clouds stand by themselves in free space and can only really stem from radon that decided to decay at those positions somewhere outside of the shielding (transforming to \"solid\" polonium in that process and sticking itself to the next closest lump of molecules/dust). If the alpha particles would penetrate the shieldings (which they can't because of too much material/density), we would see clouds stemming directly from the shielding surfaces which I don't see happening: [VIDEO] You may find and like my comment there, maybe it helps to fight some misconceptions about natural radioactivity. ;-)"},{"id":22400324,"author":"dimnsionofsound","month":"2020-02","story":"Which of these Amazon Prime purchases are real?","stance":"reference","why":"Negative ion trinkets that turn out to be radioactive","quote":"negative ion” trinkets that are actually radioactive","text":"Mildly related, here’s another instance of harmful things you can buy on Amazon: “negative ion” trinkets that are actually radioactive [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"G6D1YI-41ao":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reference to bad translation and phrasebooks.","m":[{"id":38043895,"author":"buildsjets","month":"2023-10","story":"Learn a Foreign Language Before It's Too Late (By Douglas Hofstadter)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That’s a old Monty Python sketch.","text":"That’s a old Monty Python sketch. It’s called “Dirty Hungarian Phrasebook”. My hovercraft is full of eels. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6178065,"author":"quchen","month":"2013-08","story":"How sexist can a web developer job ad get?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"In case you were thinking the spelling mistakes come from a dirty phrasebook","text":"In case you were thinking the spelling mistakes come from a dirty phrasebook [1]: the German part of the website is just as awful. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2666685,"author":"stan_rogers","month":"2011-06","story":"Translation technology may let humans speak with dolphins","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"with an automated Hungarian Phrasebook[1], we'll eventually learn to get by.","text":"Oh, I'm sure there would be a few unfortunate \"drop your panties, Sir William; I cannot wait until lunchtime\" incidents, but with an automated Hungarian Phrasebook[1], we'll eventually learn to get by. More to the point, though, we have no reason to assume that dolphins, any more than, say, apes, have the ability to \"chat\" -- to communicate about things displaced from the here and now. It's worth a go, of course, but our expectations shouldn't be too high going in. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":383121,"author":"ObieJazz","month":"2008-12","story":"Ask HN: Review my webapp","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here's the Monty Python sketch I'm referring to","text":"This is the beginning of one of the essays: \"I love icecream very much. Normally people wuld like to eat icecream in summer, few people were seen eating icecream in winter. But I will, and I found that is very excited to eat icecream in winter.\" There are grammatical errors and misspellings all over this. Here's the Monty Python sketch I'm referring to: [VIDEO] Maybe the problem is that people are writing essays in the language they are learning instead of in their native language like it suggests on the home page. If that's that case, then this could be a good tool for essay writers to get feedback on their work. It needs to be clear, though, whether people should write essays in their native language or in the language they're learning. Maybe there could be different sections for each type of essay? Then you could also read essays in your native language written by students of the language and give them feedback, or you could read essays written by native speakers of the language you're learning."}]},"GaD8y-CGhMw":{"wy":"HN quotes it as a joke about the ad and marketing industry in threads about tracking, ads and data use.","m":[{"id":41299367,"author":"eesmith","month":"2024-08","story":"Procreate's anti-AI pledge attracts praise from digital creatives","stance":"reference","why":"Bill Hicks rant against advertising and marketing people.","quote":"By the way, if anyone here is in advertising or marketing... kill yourself.","text":"Bill Hicks, quoting [LINK] with original video at [VIDEO] : > By the way, if anyone here is in advertising or marketing... kill yourself. [audience laughs and claps] ... there's no rationalization for what you do and you are Satan's little helpers, okay? Kill yourself, seriously. You're the ruiner of all things good, seriously. ... > I know all the marketing people are going, \"he's doing a joke...\" there's no joke here whatsoever. ... I know what all the marketing people are thinking right now too, \"Oh, you know what Bill's doing, he's going for that anti-marketing dollar. That's a good market, he's very smart.\" [audience laughs] Oh man, I am not doing that, you fucking evil scumbags. > \"Ooh, you know what Bill's doing now, he's going for the righteous indignation dollar. That's a big dollar. A lot of people are feeling that indignation. We've done research. Huge market. He's doing a good thing.\" Goddammit, I'm not doing that, you scumbags. Quit putting a goddamn dollar sign on every fucking thing on this planet! > \"Ooh, the anger dollar. Huge. Huge in times of recession. Giant market. Bill's very bright to do that.\" God, I'm just caught in a fucking web."},{"id":19393760,"author":"renholder","month":"2019-03","story":"Facebook Loses Top Executives, Including Chris Cox","stance":"reference","why":"Bill Hicks comedy bit on marketing.","quote":"","text":"> The ad dollars come from knowing you, not concealing you. Advertising dollars are far more lucrative if you think you can trust where you're sharing your deepest, darkests... Imagine what people would share between themselves if it wasn't \"public for the world to see\". Imagine how many more data points that would produce for targeted advertisements on the Facebook platform, alone. Edit: Including necessary reference to Bill Hicks[0]. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":19129986,"author":"cookieBaker","month":"2019-02","story":"A growing disconnect between consumers and businesses over use of customer data","stance":"reference","why":"Short Bill Hicks clip attacking marketing and advertising.","quote":"Bill Hicks said it best","text":"Before internet became what it is today, Bill Hicks said it best: [VIDEO] (2mn clip) /s ...( or is it? ) --edit for context."},{"id":13409036,"author":"bigbugbag","month":"2017-01","story":"Google and Facebook ad traffic is 90% useless","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"1. are you aware that newspaper were dying before the web ? they were mostly financed by advertising which owned them, also they pretty all been bought by rich people or giant corporations. Then out of the content available on the web (I'm not talking of the internet because I've never seen a journal publish on newsgroups or other parts of the internet that are not the web) newspaper are but a really tiny tiny fraction of the content available. The web was designed as a non-commercial sharing space, so it's no surprise that people expect it to still be used that way. At some point advertisers and marketers (who should suck on tailpipes [1]) invaded the web by the selling the idea that you could make money out by hijacking the traffic of a website. The internet and the web built on top changed the rules of the game and your failed business model is not my problem is the attitude you have to deal with from now on. That said on the matter of independent (read no advertising) investigative journalism publishing online the number of available spots are limited and the first to move will seize the better place on the market. Look at how mediapart.fr established itself in France and how many stories they get out compared to the other newspapers. They are a success established on initial funding giving no power to the funder and now entirely paid by the readers. Lesson here is there are people willing to pay and support a truly independent online newspaper, the demand is here and the"}]},"IaO69CF5mbY":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on psychology replication and weak research as Feynman's critique of pseudo science.","q":{"x":"Feynman himself speaks about it","i":3650768,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":9820147,"author":"dpc_pw","month":"2015-07","story":"Study shows psychotherapy becoming less effective","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on social sciences.","quote":"R. P. Feynman on social sciences","text":"R. P. Feynman on social sciences: [VIDEO] Psychology, economics, climatology. World is literally run by \"almost-sciences\" claiming to have an authority of \"full-science\"."},{"id":4625771,"author":"FrojoS","month":"2012-10","story":"Daniel Kahneman calls for ‘daisy chain’ of psychology replications","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on pseudo science.","quote":"Richard P. Feynman on pseudo science","text":"Richard P. Feynman on pseudo science: [VIDEO] BTW, I come from the field of robotics and there, hardly any published result is replicated either."},{"id":3650768,"author":"zerohp","month":"2012-03","story":"Collection of Richard Feynman talks on the scientific method, learning, and CS","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on what counts as real science.","quote":"Feynman himself speaks about it","text":"Actually, people still say that sociology, and other social \"sciences\" are not science. You're assuming that the scientific method is science, but its more than that. Many modern fields of endeavor call themselves sciences mostly because of the success of science. They only superficially emulate science. This includes Computer Science, which is really mathematics and engineering. Feynman himself speaks about it: [VIDEO]"}]},"JrdEMERq8MA":{"wy":"Posted as a joke or consolation in threads about career setbacks, being lost or existential worry.","m":[{"id":48069670,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2026-05","story":"Rumors of my death are slightly exaggerated","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Look at it from the bright side; Aaaaannnd.. aaaalways look on the bright side of life! Always look on the light side of life... ... For life is quite absurd And death's the final word [VIDEO]"},{"id":34520140,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What are we even chasing?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Life's a piece of shit (Oooh) When you look at it Life's a laugh and death's a joke, it's true (Oooh) You'll see it's all a show (Oooh) Keep 'em laughin' as you go Just remember that the last laugh is on you (Oooh) [VIDEO]"},{"id":20949420,"author":"hevi_jos","month":"2019-09","story":"Ask HN: Fired from my first job – how do I talk about it during interviews?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well, you will become the people you surround yourself with. You will not change them but they will change you. \"Always talked and about things he hated or how things sucked. Rarely anything positive.\" I don't know if you realize everything you talk about your first job is negative. I would focus on the positive and minimize exposure if it really was that bad, which I doubt, because your manager told you reasonable things. I am c programmer myself, with lots of experience programming lots of other languages, and programming raw c is extremely inefficient use of your time, most of the time. With less than 1 year of experience, your suggestions are of very little value, and if you get experience working with a bad team, you are not improving but learning bad habits. In an interview, tell the truth, but you had a bad emotional experience and are highly biased against your old job. Look at it as a neutral third person looking from outside. Be grateful for it, and start looking at the good side of it. Write it down. Forgive them, you decided to work with them so you were as responsible as they were. It is your fault as much as it is theirs. Forgiving them is forgiving you. [VIDEO] On future projects and endeavors start focusing on what you what to do, not on what you don't want to do. Companies love people that is proactive and efficient, not people that blames others, complains and put excuses. But it should not be about passing an interview. Your attitude should change."},{"id":8177610,"author":"bussiere","month":"2014-08","story":"I'm 25 years old and I am lost","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's my point of view but why center your life around your job ? You already have done one impressive things for your age. My advice would be do what you please, maybe take a job and learn things asides and live. With such a resume you will not have problemes to find a job, why not just live ? Travel , meet people , try to learn things and skills. And there is two things in life , things that you control and things that you can't control. Don't worry about the second part. Enjoy yourself and don't forget to laugh and have fun. Life is hort. Even i you are the best in something, you may not achieve renown because of unluck or politic. Find something / someone that you like and do it. An other things is that as long people that you respect, respect you tell the others to f * off. You don't have anything to prove to anyone. Once you will realise this you will be more peaceful. Life is short enjoy , go out your circle of confort. I worked in a lot of menial job (doorman was the worst for some aspect, having some drunk rich kids spent your monthly wage in one night) but you may find in something interesting in it (for me it was networking my contacts and practice undercover hypnotism on asshole drunk people). Live. and don't forget to laugh : For life is quite absurd And death's the final word You must always face the curtain with a bow Forget about your sin - give the audience a grin Enjoy it - it's your last chance anyhow. So always look on the bright side of death... (Whistle) "}]},"Mo-ddYhXAZc":{"wy":"HN commenters invoke it as a punchline when someone confidently opines on something they may not have answers for; it is a running joke, not a recommendation.","m":[{"id":39479927,"author":"ycombinete","month":"2024-02","story":"AI is a 'Glorified Tape Recorder,' Says Theoretical Physicist Michio Kaku (2023)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This type of thing always reminds me of Dave Chappelle’s bit on Ja Rule","text":"This type of thing always reminds me of Dave Chappelle’s bit on Ja Rule being asked for his opinion on September 11th: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34402107,"author":"jollofricepeas","month":"2023-01","story":"CNN is considering hiring a comedian to host one of its prime time shows","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the living example of his famous “What does Ja Rule say?” joke.","text":"Might as well. Apparently, Dave Chappelle has become America’s moral authority. He doesn’t even realize the irony of him a rich, sanctimonious celebrity coming full circle to become the living example of his famous “What does Ja Rule say?” joke. [VIDEO] News has always been filled with biased entertainment and humor going back beyond the days of yellow journalism to the time of the Greeks. Let the comedians joke and fiddle while Western Democracy burns as fascism regains its footing."},{"id":26144345,"author":"mtalantikite","month":"2021-02","story":"Clubhouse’s Inevitability","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"reminded me of the classic Dave Chappelle \"I want some answers that Ja Rule might not have right now","text":"I'd really like to see what their engagement and retention numbers are, because after a few days on Clubhouse I just deleted the app. Just a bunch of nonsense conversations, many of which were led by people that sell the snake-oil of how to become successful in business and life, whereas their major accomplishment seems to be being known for selling that snake-oil. Sometimes there was a random celebrity talking about things they couldn't speak all that eloquently about (reminded me of the classic Dave Chappelle \"I want some answers that Ja Rule might not have right now\" [1]). At least Twitter and Instagram felt fun when they started. Clubhouse just felt boring. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22838408,"author":"Zenbit_UX","month":"2020-04","story":"First look at Apple/Google contact tracing framework","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"reminds me of this [Chappelle Show skit]","text":"No clue who/what a moxie is (presumably some guy) and it makes this threads title seem even more absurd. OP feeling like we all need to know what moxie thinks about this reminds me of this [Chappelle Show skit]( [VIDEO] ) about getting Ja Rule's hot take on current events."}]},"SyD4p8_y8Kw":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference to illustrate frustration with confusing mathematical jargon.","q":{"x":"For a rant on this see \"Hitler Learns Topology","i":19818316,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":27666744,"author":"rwetv","month":"2021-06","story":"Show HN: Anki alternative with integrated notes and import/export","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Memorization is crucial for a subject like math. In a highly technical branch of math like, say, topology, definitions fly at one like bvllets from a m@chine gun. Just to solve a problem you might need immediate access in your head to some 12 definitions, 5 lemmas, 3 theorems, 4 corollaries and 6 prototype/canonical examples meaning you want to keep them all(along with their proofs as proofs carry important techniques and ideas) at the tip of your tongue and top of your head. Just because you \"understood\" them once in the past doesn't mean much if you cannot recall them immediately right where you need them. Obviously, you can look them up one by one (granted you know what you're looking for). Along the way, you'd have to re-understand them cause understanding is often fleeting in math, but this will make you lose time, momentum and get/keep you stuck in a thick quagmire making the original problem massively more difficult than it is. Many very important results in math (that have become staples) are made up of extremely specific techniques that are very hard to reproduce even after 5 minutes one has read (and presumably understood) them. Even understanding in math comes in waves. To get a deeper understanding of an idea you'd need to have some preliminary grasp on it. I often see the sentiment \"in math if you understand something, you don't need to memorize it\" on English speaking side of Internet. Contrast that with the old Soviet school (& modern Indian one?) philosophy wh"},{"id":19818316,"author":"tzs","month":"2019-05","story":"Mathematical Jargon Failures","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"For a rant on this see \"Hitler Learns Topology","text":"> Often brought up as an example are the topological notions of “open” and “closed” sets. It sounds as if they should be exclusive and exhaustive — surely a set that is open is not closed, and vice versa? — but no, there are sets that are neither open nor closed and other sets that are both For a rant on this see \"Hitler Learns Topology\" [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16203404,"author":"eru","month":"2018-01","story":"Linus Torvalds: “Somebody is pushing complete garbage for unclear reasons.”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"For a very memorable performance, see","text":"A leader getting angry is not a guarantee of getting good governance. For a very memorable performance, see [VIDEO]"}]},"TkX-TPaodoM":{"wy":"Referenced in threads about layoffs and career choices for its depiction of firing and giving up on dreams.","m":[{"id":46525846,"author":"alexpotato","month":"2026-01","story":"I wanted a camera that doesn't exist – so I built it","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"How much did they pay you to give up your dream?","text":"This post, where someone from one field decides to do something they love but in another field, reminds me of the below: At my kids' elementary school there is a yearly \"Dad's Night\" show where the dads get up and do skits, dance, sing and/or make funny videos. You get to see dads who sell insurance or are lawyers do dance numbers that look professionally choreographed or make music videos that look like they could have been on MTV. It's a reminder that \"The Sort\" pulls people very strongly into certain fields but there is always that question, from the movie Up In The Air and asked by George Clooney, \"How much did they pay you to give up your dream?\" [0] Part of me is VERY excited to see AI/LLMs help facilitate this for the people who always thought \"I have always wanted to write a piece of software but didn't know how and now I can!\" 0 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":34724580,"author":"ta1243","month":"2023-02","story":"Gitlab to lay off 7% of staff","stance":"reference","why":"Clip from film about firing people with real reactions","quote":"The film included a lot of people who had really been fired and what their honest reactions were","text":"I've been reminded of the \"Up in the Air\" film from the last recession, where George Clooney flew around the country firing people [VIDEO] The film included a lot of people who had really been fired and what their honest reactions were"},{"id":34301885,"author":"RonaldOlzheim","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Tech After Chemotherapy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Please watch these videos in the right order: 1 Please watch this video for why: [LINK] 2 Please watch this video for how: [LINK] 3 Please watch this video for what: [VIDEO] 4 Please watch this for when: [LINK] Final note: I believe in the good so much all the time, but it is hard to keep believing in the good all the time. Please watch this when you are having a hard time believing: [LINK] I hope you receive courage, wisdom, temperance and moderation. *Let´s connect on Linkedin. (You can message me anytime) Ron."},{"id":33327778,"author":"RonaldOlzheim","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: How do you prioritize Job offers by money and interest?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Simple: Quotes 1: 30 Put Yourself First Quotes ( [LINK] ) Video 2: Up in the Air (3/9) Movie CLIP - How Much Did They Pay You to Give Up on Your Dreams? (2009) HD ( [VIDEO] ) Music 3: Without Me · Halsey ( [LINK] ) Contact me through linkedin if you need more inspiration."}]},"VAFd4FdbJxs":{"wy":"Quoted to argue that aligned elites act in concert without explicit collusion.","q":{"x":"you don't need a formal conspiracy when interests converge.","i":38256823,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":44216470,"author":"mandmandam","month":"2025-06","story":"The US is turning into a mass techno-surveillance state","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin quote about elites sharing interests without meetings","quote":"They don’t need to call a meeting—they already know what’s good for them.","text":"> When interests converge, formal conspiracies aren’t necessary. These people went to the same universities, joined the same fraternities, and sat on the same boards and country clubs. They share the same interests. They don’t need to call a meeting—they already know what’s good for them. - [VIDEO]"},{"id":40766202,"author":"mandmandam","month":"2024-06","story":"Richard Stallman, su, and the 'wheel' group (2004)","stance":"reference","why":"Powerful people with aligned interests need no entity dictating","quote":"Just powerful people whose interests align; who all went to the same schools, are in the same clubs","text":"> I'm sure you didn't mean to imply it but the way your post is worded could be misunderstood as if you were saying that some powerful entity is trying to mute rms through defamation I said what I said, though, you should understand there doesn't need to be some 'entity' making dictates. Just powerful people whose interests align; who all went to the same schools, are in the same clubs, and have the same fucked up views [0]. 'Muting' isn't the word I'd use; I'd go with 'marginalize'. Close enough though. RMS' vision of free software is an existential threat to big tech, and to the ecosystem of mass surveillance they enable. Much as Assange's autism was weaponized to paint him as 'arrogant' in corporate media, RMS' quirks have been magnified to mark him as 'crazy' despite the fact that it's possible no one alive, ever, has had such a clear view on software freedom. > However, the website you cite points out that the beginning of the whole sex offender thing was a single MIT alumna who wrote a blog post. Whereever the initial spark came from, it was carefully nurtured into a blaze, most obviously by Vice. Vice deliberately twisted RMS' words, craftily removing clear and vital context to make RMS look as creepy as possible. Then, corporate media took that steaming turd of a Vice article and repeated it ad nauseum, without talking to Stallman, without ever adding context, and without any apology or retraction after the truth slowly filtered through. Here's [1] a great and short a"},{"id":38520258,"author":"godelski","month":"2023-12","story":"Harvard gutted team examining Facebook Files following $500M Zuckerberg donation","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin joke on aligned interests without explicit collusion","quote":"you don't have explicitly collude when you all come from the same backgrounds.","text":"There's a joke from a comedian I forget if it is L̶o̶u̶i̶s̶ ̶C̶K̶ George Carlin o̶r̶ ̶n̶o̶t̶. But basically is along the lines that you don't have explicitly collude when you all come from the same backgrounds. The interests are aligned already. It's pointing out that representatives need to actually be from representative populations of people. But he did it in a funny way, this comment really isn't. Edit: thanks lo_zamoyski. Here's a video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38256823,"author":"richbell","month":"2023-11","story":"McDonald's, Chipotle increases prices after California raises fast-food min wage","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's point that converging interests need no formal conspiracy","quote":"you don't need a formal conspiracy when interests converge.","text":"> What's being proposed is a grand conspiracy theory. That across most companies across all the states in the country they've all decided to be evil and greedy together... To quote the late George Carlin \"you don't need a formal conspiracy when interests converge.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":36618876,"author":"mabbo","month":"2023-07","story":"“Greedflation” is a nonsense idea","stance":"reference","why":"Converging interests need no formal conspiracy","quote":"You don't need a formal co","text":"A simpler explanation of 'greedflation' is simply that the lack of actual competition by market leaders in most consumer goods markets has lead to massive profits and higher prices, without increased wages. \"Greed, for lack of a better word, is good\" - a fantastic quote from a fun movie, but it can in fact be true too. If we use greed as a means to drive competitors in a market, competing against each other, resulting in them creatively undercutting one another, driving down prices so that they can sell the most, then the consumer wins out. But that isn't happening anymore. The largest corporations in each market have gained dominance through mergers, acquisitions, etc. They can reduce their own costs because of their massive scale, more than anyone newcomer can. The result is less and less competition for even basic things like groceries. Here in Canada, there are dozens of different grocery store brands- and they're all owned by 3 companies, giving the illusion of choice. If there were lots of competing retailers for common goods, they'd also be competing for labour, offering better salaries and benefits to their employees, which would help alleviate the cost of living crisis we're in now. But that isn't happening either, again because there's fewer and fewer companies to work for. All the while, the leaders of the world's large corporations are taking hundreds of millions of dollars in salaries, and giving their shareholders billions in profits. \"You don't need a formal co"}]},"Vpwkdmwui3k":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence in arguments about European dependence on Russian gas and NATO spending, especially after Russia's 2022 invasion of Ukraine.","q":{"x":"Trump and NATO secretary-general and former Norwegian PM Jens Stoltenberg argue on camera","i":32605702,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":48310232,"author":"0xDEAFBEAD","month":"2026-05","story":"Canada to order military plane fleet from Sweden in shift from US suppliers","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing Trump's prescient statements about Europe and Russia.","quote":"statements about Europe's relationship with Russia that look incredibly prescient with the benefit of hindsight","text":">Remember that Trump also had a term before Biden where he undermined cross-atlantic relations. Sure, tell me about Trump's first term where he made statements about Europe's relationship with Russia that look incredibly prescient with the benefit of hindsight: [VIDEO] >Before Obama you had Bush Jr's wars, with especially the Iraq war being very controversial. Yes, I'm an isolationist. I say the US should end all defense commitments and warmongering. Starting with Europe. US should withdraw from NATO, because Europe complains about US foreign policy the most. What's good for the goose is good for the gander."},{"id":39539218,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2024-02","story":"Europe probes Microsoft's €15M stake in AI upstart Mistral","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as Trump telling NATO to spend more in 2018.","quote":"Trump was lambasted by the usual bien-pensant suspects when he told NATO this in 2018","text":">until they elect a president that throws allies under the bus (I.e. NATO, NAFTA, etc) Based on recent news, Trump has done more for NATO rearmament than any US president in history. Gee, maybe sometimes drastic steps are needed when 20 years of three other presidents doing everything but stand on their heads while begging other NATO members to spend more on their own defense failed. Trump was lambasted by the usual bien-pensant suspects when he told NATO this in 2018 < [VIDEO] >. He was right when at the UN he warned against Western European dependence on Russian energy, for which the German foreign minister and others laughed at him < [LINK] >. And remember, Trump isn't even in office now; he's already done more for Western collective defense than a) the putative current president and b) an actual land war in Europe! >and gives state secrets to enemies Repeat this often enough and maybe it'll come true one day!"},{"id":36526446,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2023-06","story":"Vassalization: How Russia’s War on Ukraine Transformed Transatlantic Relations","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of Trump and Stoltenberg arguing about Russia dependence.","quote":"Trump and NATO secretary-general and former Norwegian PM Jens Stoltenberg argue on camera","text":"If opposing \"dictators and warmongers\" is so important, why were European NATO members so unprepared for the 2022 war? Poland, the Baltics, and other ex-Warsaw Pact countries by and large did their best to wean themselves off the Russian oil and gas teat they fed from during the Cold War, even while Germany and other far wealthier countries did their best to suckle ever more from the same teat. Since energy is fungible, the far higher prices they are now having to pay affect them and the rest of the world, even those like the US and UK that never relied on Russian energy themselves, but at least Poland and the Baltics tried their best. It's also entirely possible that greater European investment in defense might have dissuaded Russia from the February invasion. Or, at the least, be able to contribute now more than the relatively paltry sums and supplies they have so far sent. Again, the ex-Communist countries by and large both met the 2% GDP spending guideline that their far wealthier NATO fellow members have mostly ignored, and are sending as much gear as they can without endangering their own defense. Sweden and Finland's militaries were and are far better prepared than the average NATO member despite (or, perhaps, because of) not being part of the alliance. Consider two moments five years ago: 1) Trump and NATO secretary-general and former Norwegian PM Jens Stoltenberg argue on camera (video < [VIDEO] >, article < [LINK] >) about dependence on Russia. Who turned out to be "},{"id":32552702,"author":"thepasswordis","month":"2022-08","story":"Fake News and Democracy","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of Trump criticizing European defense spending and Russian gas dependence.","quote":"","text":"This is exactly the problem with fake news. It sticks around, and you've been caught by it. It seems likely that this fake news has influenced your ability to make rational decisions about how you vote, etc. This is a huge problem for democracy. No he did not withhold military aid from Ukraine. In fact in the initial offensive, the javelin missiles that they were effectively using against Russian tanks came from the Trump administration. And in fact Trump was highly critical of the previous administration for \"only sending blankets\", which was something he was called a hawk for. AND NOT ONLY THAT but one of the major points of Trump's presidency was criticizing European/NATO nations for not spending enough on their defense; specifically warning that Russia was going to use their dependence on Russian natgas, and lack of military spending, to Russia's advantage. Here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32564431,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2022-08","story":"How messed up was Germany's energy policy?","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as evidence in argument about NATO spending and Russia.","quote":"","text":">He was saying the USA should leave NATO. Ah yes, the other line that people pull when questioned on this topic. First, Trump did not say that the US should leave NATO. He publicly insisted/cajoled/prodded with that signature Trump charm and style that Germany and other NATO members who don't meet the 2% of GDP spending requirement—and showed zero inclination of actually meeting the 2026 deadline (itself pushed back repeatedly after first being set in 2006)—needed to do so. >In our day and age you should be arrested as traitor for saying that. Beyond the strange notion that an American should be \"arrested as a traitor\" for his words, a \"traitor\" to whom? Who benefits more from NATO membership, the US or Germany? Who benefits more, the US or Belgium? The US or Norway? The US or Italy? Would a Russian conquest of all of Ukraine endanger US security? Not really. It is Poland that would have Russian troops on hundreds more miles of its borders. It is Poland and the Baltic states that now face the risk being cut off from the rest of NATO at the Suwalki Gap. The funny thing is that Poland and the Baltics do meet the 2% spending requirement, and have so for years. It is the likes of the far wealthier Germany, Belgium, Norway, Italy that do not. When you say that a US president should have been \"arrested as a traitor\" for questioning the value (\"obsolete\", I believe he said) of a military alliance in which the US funded 69% of total military spending while the wealthiest other member"},{"id":32605702,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2022-08","story":"Life Is Good in America, Even by European Standards","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of Trump and Stoltenberg arguing about Russia dependence.","quote":"Trump and NATO secretary-general and former Norwegian PM Jens Stoltenberg argue on camera","text":">now suffering from the Russian invasion of Ukraine As mantas said, the energy/economic issues Europe faces from the Ukraine War is 100% self-inflicted. First, Poland, the Baltics, and other ex-Warsaw Pact countries have by and large done their best to wean themselves off the Russian oil and gas teat they fed from during the Cold War, even while Germany and other far wealthier countries did their best to suckle ever more from the same teat. Since energy is fungible, the far higher prices they are now having to pay affect the rest of the world, even those like the US and UK that never relied on Russian energy themselves. Second, it's entirely possible that greater European investment in defense might have dissuaded Russia from the February invasion. Or, at the least, be able to contribute now more than the relatively paltry sums and supplies they have so far sent. Again, the ex-Communist countries by and large both met the 2% GDP spending guideline that their far wealthier NATO fellow members have mostly ignored, and are sending as much gear as they can without endangering their own defense. Consider two moments four years ago: 1) Trump and NATO secretary-general and former Norwegian PM Jens Stoltenberg argue on camera (video < [VIDEO] >, article < [LINK] >) about dependence on Russia. Who turned out to be right? Who turned out to be completely, totally, 100% wrong? 2) Trump at the UN warns that Germany is endangering itself by increasing dependence on Russia. German envoys la"},{"id":31614181,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2022-06","story":"Classified specs leaked on War Thunder forum for third time","stance":"reference","why":"Trump and Stoltenberg arguing about German dependence on Russian gas","quote":"Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera (< >).","text":">And think that Trump wanted USA to leave NATO. Talk about russian assets.. Speaking of Russian assets, there are still people that say that Merkel was always against German dependence on Russian gas but could not do anything about it. For 16 years! There is more \"evidence\" that Merkel is a long-term Russian intelligence asset, recruited from her youth in East Germany,[1] than that of Trump being the same. One guess on which claim is incessantly repeated by the bien-pensants of the chattering classes. Bonus: Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera (< [VIDEO] >). Who turned out to be right? Who turned out to completely, totally, 100% wrong? [1] Something else never talked about is how her parents moved from West to East Germany when she was a baby"},{"id":31746902,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2022-06","story":"US quietly urges more use of Russia fertilizer to ease food crisis","stance":"reference","why":"Trump and Stoltenberg arguing about energy dependence on Russia","quote":"Trump and Jens Stoltenberg of NATO argue on camera (< >).","text":">I remember that on TV Trump was telling German chancellor that they should seek energy sources outside of Russia, and the media was mocking him for being populist or for being anti globalization (or for being paranoid as Russian would need buyers? I can't remember exactly). Is the orange man so bad that whatever he touches is a taboo? Is there any truth in what he does, and the executive branch can be less political but focus more on execution? Trump and Jens Stoltenberg of NATO argue on camera (< [VIDEO] >). Who turned out to be right? Who turned out to completely, totally, 100% wrong?"},{"id":31746998,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2022-06","story":"US quietly urges more use of Russia fertilizer to ease food crisis","stance":"reference","why":"Trump and Stoltenberg exchange on NATO and Russian energy","quote":"Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera (< >).","text":">> Pushing NATO members to meet their defense spending obligations ended up being damn-near prescient >This was not “pushing” so much as “ineffective trash talking”. Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera (< [VIDEO] >). Who turned out to be right? Who turned out to completely, totally, 100% wrong? If Trump was so bad for NATO and Ukraine's defense, why didn't the current war didn't begin in 2017-2020? Let me guess: You think that the current state of the Ukrainian military (in terms of training and weaponry) that enabled it to survive the initial Russian invasion in February when few believed it could, somehow magically began in January 2021. It is a fact that the Trump administration continued and accelerated the training and arming of Ukrainian military by the US that his predecessor began. Let me also guess: You are one of those gullible who say that Merkel was always against German dependence on Russian gas but could not do anything about it. For 16 years! There is more \"evidence\" that Merkel is a long-term Russian intelligence asset, recruited from her youth in East Germany,^1 than that of Trump being the same. One guess on which claim is incessantly repeated by the bien-pensants of the chattering classes. ^1 Something else never talked about is how her parents moved from West to East Germany when she was a baby"},{"id":31747095,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2022-06","story":"US quietly urges more use of Russia fertilizer to ease food crisis","stance":"reference","why":"Trump and Stoltenberg exchange on NATO and Russian energy","quote":"Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera (< >).","text":">If by diplomacy you mean kowtowing to everything Russia does, I'm not sure that's much better. Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera (< [VIDEO] >). Who turned out to be right? Who turned out to completely, totally, 100% wrong?"},{"id":31924222,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2022-06","story":"TikTok updated privacy policy to collect faceprints and voiceprints (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence in political argument about Trump and NATO.","quote":"Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera","text":"Trump proven right yet again. Another example: Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera (< [VIDEO] >). Who turned out to be right about NATO and Russia? Who turned out to completely, totally, 100% wrong?"},{"id":31359641,"author":"TMWNN","month":"2022-05","story":"Finland will seek NATO membership immediately","stance":"reference","why":"Trump and Stoltenberg arguing about German dependence on Russian gas","quote":"Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera (< >).","text":"Don't forget Merkel. There are still people that say that Merkel was always against German dependence on Russian gas but could not do anything about it. For 16 years! There is more \"evidence\" that Merkel is a long-term Russian intelligence asset, recruited from her youth in East Germany,[1] than that of Trump being the same. One guess on which claim is incessantly repeated by the bien-pensants of the chattering classes. Bonus: Trump and Stoltenberg argue on camera (< [VIDEO] >). Who turned out to be right? Who turned out to completely, totally, 100% wrong? [1] Something else never talked about is how her parents moved from West to East Germany when she was a baby"},{"id":30522164,"author":"ComradePhil","month":"2022-03","story":"Nord Stream 2 declares bankruptcy","stance":"reference","why":"Trump scolding NATO chief over European energy ties to Russia","quote":"2018 Jul - Trump scolds NATO General Secretary over European energy relations with Russia","text":"To those who are being exposed to this only because of recent events, here's what has happened before all this: - 2011 - Nord Stream 1 nearing completion and Nord Stream AG starts thinking about two more lines (which would later be named Nord Stream 2) - 2015 Jun - Decision to build Nord Stream 2 was made [LINK] - 2017 Apr - Financing agreements signed [LINK] - 2018 Jan - Germany officially grants permit [LINK] - 2018 May - US diplomats start talking about sanctions [LINK] - 2018 Jul - Trump scolds NATO General Secretary over European energy relations with Russia [VIDEO] - 2019 Jan - US ambassador to Germany threatens sanctions [LINK] - 2019 Dec - US sanctions Nord Stream 2 [LINK] - 2020 Dec - US continues to protest, Germany continues to build [LINK] - 2021 Jan - Trump sanctions the Russian ship building Nord Stream 2 on his last day at the office [LINK] - 2021 Jan - EU sanctions it too [LINK] - 2021 May - US waives the sanctions on Nord Stream 2 [LINK] - 2021 Jun - First line construction is complete [LINK] - 2021 Sep - The full construction of Nord Stream 2 is complete [LINK] - 2022 Jan - Nord Stream 2 registers German subsidiary as a part of the certification process [LINK] This is a result of over a decade of planning and construction with governments, corporations and companies of Germany, UK, France, Austria, Switzerland, Finland, Estonia, Sweden, Russia... and probably more countries to provide cheap and reliable and climate friendly energy source to Europe... and it "},{"id":30462055,"author":"__blockcipher__","month":"2022-02","story":"Twitter based map of Russian troop movements","stance":"reference","why":"Trump criticizing German reliance on Russian energy at NATO","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] If Trump is Putin's puppet why did he publicly criticize Germany for being overly reliant on Russian pipelines for their energy needs?"}]},"a_Vy6fgaBPE":{"wy":"Cited in debates over PCR cycle thresholds, as a place where Fauci addressed them directly.","q":{"x":"Here's Fauci talking about it a little","i":25480683,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":26623597,"author":"sleepysysadmin","month":"2021-03","story":"Yes, experts will lie to you sometimes","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as Fauci statement in a debate over PCR cycle counts.","quote":"","text":">That's not a false positive. False positive happens when there are no viral particles in the sample, but the test comes back positive. Those are very rare with rt-PCR and can usually be tracked down to cross-contamination. Cycle count has nothing to with those. You're not arguing with me. You are arguing with Fauci. [VIDEO] If you could explain to me if you don't mind. Why are you saying Fauci is wrong? Was he lying again? >If there is no SARS-CoV-2 genetic material in the sample, there's nothing for rt-PCR to amplify. I made my entire post literally Fauci and not me at all. I made no comments or assertions."},{"id":25480683,"author":"tertius","month":"2020-12","story":"FDA grants authorization to Moderna's Covid-19 vaccine","stance":"reference","why":"Fauci discussing PCR cycle thresholds.","quote":"Here's Fauci talking about it a little","text":"PCR positive doesn't mean infected. You'll need an high amount in PCR, i.e. low cycle threshold for the probability to be high. Antigen tests will show if there was/is an infection. PCR does not show infection only presence. (WHO has now put out guidance about this. They don't have to it's well known.) If you're European this may be strange to you but I'm the U.S. we don't receive the cycle count when the RNA for the virus is detected. Here's Fauci talking about it a little: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25127493,"author":"jorge-fundido","month":"2020-11","story":"South Dakota nurse says many dying patients still insist Covid-19 'not real'","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for claim about PCR cycle thresholds.","quote":"","text":"Why are cases important? Assuming PCR, can get whatever result you want with the cycle threshold (discussion begins around minute 3:50): [VIDEO] Want more positive cases? Use a higher threshold. Want to scare the population into compliance? Use a higher threshold. Want to demonstrate that person X or policy Y was effective against stopping spread? Use a lower threshold."}]},"ad_higXixRA":{"wy":"Linked to show that a billionaire's price guesses are reasonable, usually in threads about out-of-touch wealthy people.","m":[{"id":41582704,"author":"fwip","month":"2024-09","story":"The Double Irish Dutch Sandwich: End of a Tax Evasion Strategy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"this video of Bill Gates guessing grocery prices on the Ellen show","text":"Likely that they conflated it with this video of Bill Gates guessing grocery prices on the Ellen show: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22542687,"author":"RandomBacon","month":"2020-03","story":"Google recommends all North America employees work from home","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I could only find Bill Gates guessing grocery item prices","text":"If you're going to ask people to watch something, it would be nice of you linked to it. Maybe this is what you wete refering to, but I could only find Bill Gates guessing grocery item prices: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19977072,"author":"JeremyBanks","month":"2019-05","story":"When Boris Yeltsin Went Grocery Shopping in Clear Lake","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Gates' actual guesses were pretty reasonable","text":"That was a scene from the fictional TV show Arrested Development. > Lucille: Don’t you judge me. You’re the selfish one. You’re the one who charged his own brother for a Bluth frozen banana. I mean, it’s one banana, Michael. What could it cost, ten dollars? Gates' actual guesses were pretty reasonable: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34662007,"author":"pcthrowaway","month":"2023-02","story":"I hired 5 people to sit behind me and make me productive for a month","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't think that's what people are \"objecting\" to about this (well, not even objecting in many cases, just... flabbergasted). The author appears to do very little work, while he pays someone to sit there and be his surrogate mother, so that he can be a bit more productive. It seems somewhat attention-seeking (I personally believe it's honest, though it does invoke Poe's Law). That's fine too, people seek attention on HN, but they usually offer something insightful in return. This is more the equivalent of the Tiktok user who posted her \"day in the life of an employee at (Meta? Airbnb?)\" that involved very little work. Also fine, work isn't the point of life. But when you're showing off a flamboyantly eccentric \"productivity hack\" that got you to work 80 hours in a month instead of 25, so you could spend more time starting blog posts, breakdancing, doing yoga, going to the gym, \"unironically starting a company (it's in stealth, sorry!)\", doing online courses (at least this is at least somewhat related to the job they are presumably actually paid to do)... many people reading are going to be amused at the very least. It seems like they have time management issues that make this \"experiment\" only potentially useful to people who also struggles to get anything actually done due to battling severe ADHD while balancing working at a very casual pace, and shooting off in 10 different directions at once all the time. They would very likely benefit from some combination of a reality "},{"id":21793965,"author":"umvi","month":"2019-12","story":"Uber and Lyft Suggest the Days of Cheap Rides Could Be Over","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's not like speeding tickets are inevitable. I've been driving for over 17 years and I've never been pulled over once (knock on wood). But sure, I don't mind proportional speeding tickets (I never get them), but I think it's a slippery slope of socialism where you start thinking everything should have proportional costs. Why should rich and poor both pay $3 for a gallon milk? Shouldn't the rich pay $30 for a gallon of milk? Heck, they are so out of touch with food costs anyway they probably wouldn't notice.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"dSj6kvv7_Sg":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence of early lights-out manufacturing and of Steve Jobs's factory automation roots in threads on automation and US manufacturing.","m":[{"id":43787516,"author":"scrlk","month":"2025-04","story":"Apple Computers Used to Be Built in the U.S. It Was a Mess (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Video of NeXT's automated factory in action","quote":"There are videos of these factories in action","text":"Steve invested heavily in automation for the Mac and NeXT factories. There are videos of these factories in action: We Are Manufacturing (Apple): [LINK] The Machine to Build the Machines (NeXT): [VIDEO] Infamously, he caused a ton of issues for both factories when he demanded that the machinery be painted to colours of his liking. > When Jobs took a tour, he ordered that the machines be repainted in the bright colors he wanted. Carter objected; this was precision equipment, and repainting the machines could cause problems. He turned out to be right. One of the most expensive machines, which got painted bright blue, ended up not working properly and was dubbed “Steve’s Folly.” Finally Carter quit. “It took so much energy to fight him, and it was usually over something so pointless that finally I had enough,” he recalled. [0] > Jobs demanded that all the robots in the NeXT manufacturing plant be painted in coordinated shades of gray and black. 2 of his top manufacturing engineers worked through a weekend to paint the assembly line, repeating the process 4 times until they got the finish just right. [1] Ultimately, even that significant automation investment couldn't overcome the core challenges that other commenters have pointed out. I find the culture aspect ironic. Deming was American, yet American manufacturers largely ignored him until the Japanese applied his methods and began outcompeting everyone. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":24547416,"author":"ChickeNES","month":"2020-09","story":"New Steve Jobs recordings from the '80s released","stance":"reference","why":"Automated PCB assembly line demo","quote":"At about 35 minutes in there was a video demonstrating their automated PCB assembly line","text":"At about 35 minutes in there was a video demonstrating their automated PCB assembly line, and luckily it's on YouTube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23751316,"author":"foobiekr","month":"2020-07","story":"Playstation's secret weapon: a nearly all-automated factory","stance":"reference","why":"Early lights-out production at NeXT","quote":"Early lights out production.","text":"Every discussion of highly-automated factories brings to mind this: [VIDEO] Early lights out production. The problem became obvious a little later, for NeXT, which was that these things only make sense at very high scale for products that change infrequently."},{"id":12161776,"author":"tpowell","month":"2016-07","story":"Apple Taps Bob Mansfield to Oversee Car Project","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's roots in factory automation","quote":"don't forget their roots","text":"I wouldn't underestimate them with their war chest and supply chain expertise, and don't forget their roots: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16704631,"author":"IBM","month":"2018-03","story":"Analysts argue that over-automation is to blame for Tesla problems","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Sounds like Steve Jobs... in 1986.","text":">sounds like one of those sad stories where the engineers fell in love with the stack and forgot about the product. Sounds like Steve Jobs... in 1986. [VIDEO]"}]},"e9dZQelULDk":{"wy":"Commenters reference it as a bleak visual shorthand for the rat race and consumerist unhappiness.","q":{"x":"I liked that \"Happiness\" cartoon","i":34459926,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":45933430,"author":"metadope","month":"2025-11","story":"Being poor vs. being broke","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates American unhappiness and wanting more","quote":"explains American unhappiness and tendency to complain","text":"Steve Cutts < [LINK] > explains American unhappiness and tendency to complain (even though we've had it better than almost everyone ever). The Pursuit Of Happiness is humanity's universal right, but our culture and the economy depends upon everybody always wanting more, and better... [VIDEO]"},{"id":34459926,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2023-01","story":"Tell HN: Sometimes you don't realise how bad something is until you leave","stance":"reference","why":"Cartoon about the rat race","quote":"I liked that \"Happiness\" cartoon","text":"> That and my age made me feel really depressed and unwanted. In my case, my age was the \"elephant in the room,\" at my interviews. I feel as if the original in-house recruiters (different beast from the \"outhouse\" recruiters, though), and even a couple of managers, had no issue with my age. As soon as even one tech person became involved, however, the temperature dropped twenty degrees. They were usually quite brusque, and I knew that I was screwed. After that, the company usually \"ghosted\" me. I stopped following up, because even the enthusiastic and supportive recruiters and managers wouldn't talk to me. It was very depressing. It was not my imagination. They really didn't want me, and they didn't care whether or not I knew it. No blowing sunshine up my butt, or stringing me along. They wanted me gone, ASAP. The few companies that seemed interested, did so, on a pretty nasty footing. They made it clear that they would be doing me a huge favor, by offering me a job, and that I'd be treated like garbage, if I took it. That was why I threw in the towel, and just decided to retire. I was more than willing to do a pretty awesome job, and take chances with risky companies, for a fairly low wage, but this did not seem to matter (indeed, it seldom even got to that point). But it's all actually been for the best. Nowadays, I can't even imagine going back to the rat race (I liked that \"Happiness\" cartoon, that a previous post linked[0]). It was very sick, and I never want to go back "},{"id":32590791,"author":"meitham","month":"2022-08","story":"Korea shatters its own record for world’s lowest fertility rate","stance":"reference","why":"Art visually resembling the rat race","quote":"I have just came across this art which visually resembles what you described","text":"> by refusing to participate in the rat race That does indeed sounds like a rat race! I have just came across this art which visually resembles what you described [VIDEO]"},{"id":21111116,"author":"Something1234","month":"2019-09","story":"Are We Living in a Post-Happiness World?","stance":"reference","why":"Dark animation by Steve Cutts about the pursuit of happiness","quote":"They are crazy depressing.","text":"Anybody ever watch Steve Cutts, especially the animations he did for Moby? They are crazy depressing. I feel like happiness is exactly what Denis Leary explianed it as. It's a brief moment, like a smoke or a beer. But on to the videos: [happiness]: [VIDEO] [moby are you lost]: [LINK]"}]},"eC1BAcOzHyY":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an example of expert memory built on context, such as Carlsen identifying players, tournaments and moves from positions.","q":{"x":"They put a number of mid-game positions on the board, and Magnus was able to guess the players, tournament, game number, who won","i":32991103,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33834419,"author":"ninethirty","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Is your memory as bad as mine?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of expert memory built on care and context.","quote":"","text":"Not enough context. Do you only watch football once every four years? Probably you don't care enough and have enough context of strategic moments to create memories. The more you care + context, the better the memory will be, see LeBron + Magnus. { [LINK] , [VIDEO] }"},{"id":32991103,"author":"lysp","month":"2022-09","story":"Dear Chess World","stance":"reference","why":"Carlsen identifies players, tournaments and next moves from positions.","quote":"They put a number of mid-game positions on the board, and Magnus was able to guess the players, tournament, game number, who won","text":"I saw a video a while ago. [VIDEO] They put a number of mid-game positions on the board, and Magnus was able to guess the players, tournament, game number, who won, what the next few moves were. Who was playing on the table next to him. What their moves were."},{"id":29056669,"author":"matsemann","month":"2021-10","story":"How long does it take ordinary people to get good at chess?","stance":"reference","why":"Carlsen instantly recognizes positions and their games.","quote":"There are videos of Magnus Carlsen being shown various positions, and he can instantly tell which games they are from.","text":"There are videos of Magnus Carlsen being shown various positions, and he can instantly tell which games they are from. Like their brain has an index on postions or something. Not only that, but also can tell how the game continues. One example [VIDEO]"}]},"f3PJF0YE-x4":{"wy":"Referenced as a well-known routine when threads discuss offensive words and who gets to use them.","m":[{"id":18706745,"author":"nugga","month":"2018-12","story":"Hate Speech on Patreon","stance":"reference","why":"Routine cited to explain how a commentator defended his word use","quote":"","text":"Sargon claims he is using the word in the way described in this stand up comedy routine where Chris Rock (a black person) talks about the difference between 'Black people and niggers' [VIDEO] Sargon said something like he was denigrating the 'alt right / nazi trolls' for behaving the way the 'nazis' claim the people they hate behave in."},{"id":9926662,"author":"Nadya","month":"2015-07","story":"The Evolution of Profanity","stance":"reference","why":"Chris Rock routine as example of a long-standing line of reasoning","quote":"","text":"That line of reasoning the mother used isn't uncommon. I can date it back to at least '96 and know that line of reasoning existed well before then. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4198519,"author":"JonnieCache","month":"2012-07","story":"PHP is much better than what you think","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This post reminds me of the infamous Chris Rock routine","text":"This post reminds me of the infamous Chris Rock routine: [VIDEO] \"I take care of my kids! I ain't never been to jail!\""}]},"gECj1AE9D2c":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as inspiration for large eInk displays and their own DIY projects.","q":{"x":"Some guy created a rotating newspaper frame from it[3]. Looks quite amazing, and it has a 1 year battery life.","i":28667353,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":34324337,"author":"sho_hn","month":"2023-01","story":"Raspberry Pi's Camera Module 3 adds autofocus and new Sony sensor","stance":"reference","why":"Inspiration for the commenter's own project","quote":"My inspiration is this","text":"My inspiration is this: [VIDEO] But I actually only watched the first 30 seconds and then got hooked on the idea. The impl is mine :-)"},{"id":29665446,"author":"mynameisash","month":"2021-12","story":"PineNote Developer Edition – E-Paper Tablet","stance":"reference","why":"Inspired the commenter's wish for a large e-ink photo display","quote":"I was inspired by this[0]","text":"I really want an 8.5x11 or bigger, very low power e-ink device that I can use as essentially a photo display. I was inspired by this[0], but as I'm not a hardware guy at all, trying to make one is out of my reach. Hopefully this PineNote will become that device sometime. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28667353,"author":"zius","month":"2021-09","story":"Turning an eInk screen into a monochrome art gallery","stance":"reference","why":"Rotating newspaper frame built from an eInk display","quote":"Some guy created a rotating newspaper frame from it[3]. Looks quite amazing, and it has a 1 year battery life.","text":"Joan’s parent company, Visionect[1], also produces the Place & Play, a 13” or 36” eInk display[2]. Some guy created a rotating newspaper frame from it[3]. Looks quite amazing, and it has a 1 year battery life. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] blog: [LINK] and video: [VIDEO] and code: [LINK]"}]},"hNuu9CpdjIo":{"wy":"HN uses it as a recurring joke about people skills, managers and CEOs, and about the value of non-technical skills in tech.","m":[{"id":48735471,"author":"palmotea","month":"2026-06","story":"Claude Code Is Steganographically Marking Requests","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Hello, Tom Smykowski. You have people skills!","text":"> I just don't agree that it's a false equivalency. I see them both as \"tools I use to get the job done\". For me, the job is not \"writing code\" - it is \"deliver feature\", \"fix bug\", and the accountability, responsibility, and communication that comes with it. Hello, Tom Smykowski. You have people skills! [VIDEO]"},{"id":48026487,"author":"jimbokun","month":"2026-05","story":"Three Inverse Laws of AI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I HAVE LLM SKILLS! I'M GOOD AT DEALING WITH THE LLMS!","text":"These engineers are becoming the real life equivalent of this Office Space scene: [VIDEO] \"I HAVE LLM SKILLS! I'M GOOD AT DEALING WITH THE LLMS!\""},{"id":46415792,"author":"sallveburrpi","month":"2025-12","story":"CEOs are hugely expensive. Why not automate them?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The people in this thread coming to the defense of their CEOs sound like Tom Smykowski in Office Space desperately trying to save his job: “I already told you: I deal with the god damn customers so the engineers don't have to. I have people skills; I am good at dealing with people.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":41805067,"author":"015a","month":"2024-10","story":"Scrum's “Product Owner” Problem","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"For starters: The old notion that engineers aren't good with people so don't let them talk to stakeholders [1]; so you end up with PMs/POs/EMs holding a monopoly on information gathering from stakeholders, which is at-best inefficient and more realistically less accurate. It becomes \"viral\" (it spreads) when it becomes institutionalized; when leaders say \"no we want you focused on the code, so go into the basement and we'll talk in our 1-1 about your progress\". People skills are a skill; its right there in the name, it needs to be developed and exercised. If you don't let your engineers out of the basement, you get engineers with bad people skills, and the prophecy writes itself. Those engineers with bad people skills prioritize the skills they have in themselves when hiring others, and so on, and so forth. This isn't me arguing that these PM/EM/PO roles shouldn't exist, to be clear; its more-so things that bad individuals in those roles may do. That being said, I do feel that the healthiest, most productive software teams I've been on had a few things in common: Less technical EMs, combination EM+PMs, or sharing a PM across multiple related teams in the same department. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41447226,"author":"kragen","month":"2024-09","story":"Greppability is an underrated code metric","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> look! i already told you! i deal with the god damned customers so the engineers don't have to! i have people skills! i am good at dealing with people! can't you understand that? what the hell is wrong with you people? (office space, [VIDEO] ) look, lucumo, i'm sure you have excellent people skills. which is why you're writing five-paragraph power-trip-fantasy comments on hn about laughing in people's faces as you demonstrate your undeniable dominance over them, then take pity on them. but i'm not sure those comments really represent a contribution to the conversation about code greppability; they're just ego defense. you probably should not have posted them"},{"id":41524917,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2024-09","story":"Learning to Reason with LLMs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't want to lean into negativity here, and I'm far from an \"AI Doomer\". But... I will say I think the question you ask is a very fair question, and that there is, indeed, a LOT of uncertainty about what the future holds in this regard. So far the best reason we have for optimism is history: so far the old adage has held up that \"technology does destroy some jobs, but on balance it creates more new ones than it destroys.\" And while that's small solace to the buggy-whip maker or steam-engine engineer, things tend to work out in the long-run. However... history is suggestive, but far from conclusive. There is the well known \"problem of induction\"[1] which points out that we can't make definite predictions about the future based on past experience. And when those expectations are violated, we get \"black swan events\"[2]. And while they be uncommon, they do happen. The other issue with this question is, we don't really know what the \"rate of change\" in terms of AI improvement is. And we definitely don't know the 2nd derivative (acceleration). So a short-term guess that \"there will be a job for you in 1 year's time\" is probably a fairly safe guess. But as a current student, you're presumably worried about 5 years, 10 years, 20 years down the line and whether or not you'll still have a career. And the simple truth is, we can't be sure. So what to do? My gut feeling is \"continue to learn software engineering, but make sure to look for ways to broaden your skill base, and position "},{"id":40642958,"author":"015a","month":"2024-06","story":"Apple unveils 'Passwords' manager app at WWDC 2024","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This point of view essentially reduces to the same place libertarians are at: Institutions are bad, Apple is bad, Google is bad, we should refuse to support institutions, or maybe even institutions should not exist, depending on how severe the FOSSism is. And look, I don't feel that libertarians (or, let's kill the analogy, FOSSers) are always wrong. Of course they're right about some things; they're just wrong about so much more than they're right about, its like a 90/10 split, its not close. I think the cognitive dissonance is something similar to chesterton's fence: FOSSers don't respect the massive profit-motivated and closed-source companies and systems which, at best, make pockets of productive, awesome open source possible; but more realistically and worse those pockets are just the software version of \"buy a Subaru because we donate money to cancer research\", they're free labor/recruiting/tax writeoff/community goodwill campaigns by gigacorps, and its all just profit at the end of the day. Nerds who can see the inside of the machine and are aware that this sort of thing happens is literally just stating in different terms the stereotype type-As assign to nerds: that they don't understand anything but the technology [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35150886,"author":"ForHackernews","month":"2023-03","story":"Am I still relevant as a programmer now that there's AI?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Turns out Tom[0] has the laugh after all. The goddamn people skills will be what counts in the end. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31685224,"author":"sky-kedge0749","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Please share opinions on coding post “singularity” event","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Look, I already told you. I deal with the goddamn customers so the AI doesn't have to. I have people skills. I am good at dealing with people. Can't you understand that? What the hell is wrong with you people?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":30284224,"author":"skywal_l","month":"2022-02","story":"Peloton – A call for action [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Office Space\" reference for anyone wondering[0]. Another Mike Judge's gift to us. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12858899,"author":"cmdrfred","month":"2016-11","story":"Why C.E.O.s are getting fired more","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Honestly, this sort of non-quantifiable magic reminds me of Office Space. \"I have people skills! I am good with dealing with people can't you understand that! WHAT THE HELL IS WRONG WITH YOU PEOPLE.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"lMChO0qNbkY":{"wy":"Used as a punchline in privacy and opt-out threads, mostly as a joke that reality has caught up to the satire.","m":[{"id":22339549,"author":"heartbeats","month":"2020-02","story":"Treat Facebook like something between a telco and a newspaper, says Zuckerberg","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well, let's put it this way: \"The market can stay irrational longer than you can stay solvent.\" You can stay away from Facebook and then feel really smug ten years from now when it eventually collapses. But until then, you'd have missed out on a lot of important things. The only winning move is to realize that you do not have a choice, and that the terms of service are just an illusion. For the record, I think Facebook's existence is regrettable. But the notion that you have any ability, choice, freedom, right, or opportunity to opt out from it is laughable. As for the idea of the social contract, I think you are actually spot on: > Social contract arguments typically posit that individuals have consented, either explicitly or tacitly, to surrender some of their freedoms and submit to the authority [...] in exchange for protection of their remaining rights or maintenance of the social order. Individuals have: [?] consented explicitly [x] consented tacitly [x] to surrender some of their freedoms [x] to submit to the authority [Facebook] [ ] in exchange for protection of their remaining rights [x] in exchange for maintenance of the social order In fact, it could even be argued that this de-facto state of affair ought to be made de-jure. Certainly, it is the case already today that many of my local government agencies are more active on Twitter or Facebook than they are over phone or e-mail. In fact, they rarely use e-mail for regulatory reasons. If someone is suicidal, has an e"},{"id":44480469,"author":"roxolotl","month":"2025-07","story":"The force-feeding of AI features on an unwilling public","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Onion has a great response to this from 2009","text":"The Onion has a great response to this from 2009: [VIDEO] Of course you can opt out. People live in the backwoods of Alaska. But if you want to live a semi normal life there is no option. And absolutely people should feel entitled to a normal life."},{"id":43959002,"author":"roxolotl","month":"2025-05","story":"Avoiding AI is hard – but our freedom to opt out must be protected","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of the wonderful Onion piece about a Google Opt Out Village.","text":"Reminds me of the wonderful Onion piece about a Google Opt Out Village. [VIDEO] I appreciate the frustration that, if not quite yet, it’ll be near impossible to live a normal life without having exposure to GenAI systems. Of course as others say here, and the date on the Onion piece shows, it’s not sadly not a new concern."},{"id":28734496,"author":"lozenge","month":"2021-10","story":"Beware Centralization","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"When Apple killed Flash, it killed Flash for you too. Now if Facebook promotes division and misinformation... just because you don't log onto Facebook, doesn't mean it has no impact on your life. [VIDEO] vibes"},{"id":27422998,"author":"wongarsu","month":"2021-06","story":"Apple pays out millions to student after repair techs shared her personal images","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That sounds a bit like the Onion sketch","text":"> If that's your kink, then Polaroid cameras and camcorders exist. That sounds a bit like the Onion sketch \"Google allows you to opt-out of tracking by moving to remote mountain village\" [1]. This case proves that the criminal justice system works to protect your personal images, we just need a better way to be notified when it happens (the kind of action you would expect Apple to take if they cared, instead of \"we continued to strengthen our vendor protocols\") 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18323233,"author":"mindcrash","month":"2018-10","story":"Google’s smart city dream is turning into a privacy nightmare","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this vid from The Onion made a few years ago has become reality.","text":"So it's pretty safe to say this vid from The Onion made a few years ago has become reality. [VIDEO] What a time to be alive."},{"id":12447689,"author":"miesman","month":"2016-09","story":"The privacy wars are about to get a whole lot worse","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"If you want to opt out that is fine. You can just toil in the hinterlands, and die young\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11338674,"author":"readams","month":"2016-03","story":"Snowden Warns World Against Trusting Privacy to Tech Giants","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Your argument reminds me of this Onion video","text":"Seriously? You can't imagine why people might want to take advantage of modern technology while simultaneously wanting privacy protections? Your argument reminds me of this Onion video: [VIDEO]"}]},"mr9kK0_7x08":{"wy":"Mostly used as a reference clip, as a view of a modern factory or evidence in an argument, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":38676702,"author":"chrisco255","month":"2023-12","story":"Where Johnny Cash Came From","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a modern factory","quote":"And here's a modern factory","text":"> The window where labor unions made mill, mine, and factory jobs something livable was quite short--1960 to 1980, roughly. I'm sorry, what? What do you think 2023 mills, mines, and factories look like in the United States? Here's a tour of a wheat mill: [LINK] Here's a modern coal mine: [LINK] And here's a modern factory: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38384161,"author":"machdiamonds","month":"2023-11","story":"Sam Altman's been fired before. The polarizing past of OpenAI's reinstated CEO","stance":"reference","why":"Factory tour used to rebut a claim","quote":"there's a lot of yellow in this factory tour he did","text":"You guys just believe whatever you read. Things that can easily be debunked with common sense. For example, there's a lot of yellow in this factory tour he did: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17811811,"author":"melling","month":"2018-08","story":"Internal documents reveal the grueling way Tesla hit its 5,000 Model 3 target","stance":"reference","why":"Recent interview with Elon on Tesla production","quote":"Elon seems to be doing fine in this recent interview","text":"Tesla is almost at 6000 Model 3’s a week. [LINK] And I believe that they’re building almost 2,000 Model X,S cars a week. At almost 400,000 cars a year, Tesla has got to be doing better than many expected. Elon seems to be doing fine in this recent interview: [VIDEO]"}]},"pjvQFtlNQ-M":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reply, typically when someone cites an unreliable or only-sometimes-true claim.","m":[{"id":33615552,"author":"daveslash","month":"2022-11","story":"Vast Majority of People Who Invest in Bitcoin Inevitably Lose Money, Study Shows","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"They've done studies you know. 60% of the time, it works every time. [0] I am shocked. SHOCKED!!!... Well, not that Shocked. [1] Darmok and Jalad at Tanagra. Shaka, when the walls fell. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":32482969,"author":"supermatt","month":"2022-08","story":"Custom Mouse","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> However, this first version does 100% work 90% of the time, You mean: \"90% of the time it works EVERY time!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11158657,"author":"billyhoffman","month":"2016-02","story":"If you hate telemarketers, you’ll love this robot designed to waste their time","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> This definitely works - [LINK] not always though ... so... it doesn't work? \"They've done studies you know. 60% of the time, it works every time\" [VIDEO]"}]},"q3NQQ7bPf6U":{"wy":"HN references it as a demonstration of Doom's original multi-monitor support and as retro computing content.","q":{"x":"There's (at least) one video on Youtube showing it in action with 3 monitors plus a fourth one with the map","i":45969354,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45969354,"author":"badocr","month":"2025-11","story":"How Quake.exe got its TCP/IP stack","stance":"reference","why":"Doom running across multiple monitors","quote":"There's (at least) one video on Youtube showing it in action with 3 monitors plus a fourth one with the map","text":"Yes, Doom with multi-monitors! There's (at least) one video on Youtube showing it in action with 3 monitors plus a fourth one with the map: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35211612,"author":"dasil003","month":"2023-03","story":"The early 90s tech scene that created L0pht, the legendary hackerspace","stance":"reference","why":"Example of strong retro computing nostalgia","quote":"retro computing and nostalgia for the era has never been stronger or more accessible","text":"As a teenager at the time, there was a palpable feeling of being part of a counter-culture that was on the bleeding edge of an inevitable future which adults simply could not grok. That world definitely ain't coming back, in large part because global internet connectivity has rendered quaint the very notion of counter-culture. On the other hand, retro computing and nostalgia for the era has never been stronger or more accessible: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34847673,"author":"earksiinni","month":"2023-02","story":"Doom the Way It Was Meant to Be Played – v1.1 Multi-Monitor","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"But what's with the left monitor's screen overlapping with his face? Looks kinda greenscreen-y","text":"I have no reason to doubt that this is real, and it looks amazing. But what's with the left monitor's screen overlapping with his face? Looks kinda greenscreen-y: [VIDEO]"}]},"qaHLd8de6nM":{"wy":"Cited repeatedly in threads on wokeness, cancel culture and outrage as an authoritative voice from the left criticizing call-outs.","q":{"x":"If all you're doing is casting stones, you're probably not going to get very far","i":26614264,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":42689503,"author":"normalaccess","month":"2025-01","story":"The Origins of Wokeness","stance":"reference","why":"Obama calling out woke gotcha politics","quote":"","text":"Even Obama is not a fan of \"woke\" culture and calls it out for the hollow vapid gotcha politics that it is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42690106,"author":"gitfan86","month":"2025-01","story":"The Origins of Wokeness","stance":"reference","why":"Obama criticizing call-out behavior, countering claim it is right-wing","quote":"","text":"You are dismissing the issue by implying it is a right wing thing. Obama is using the term and criticising people who do it in this clip. I in no way consider him to be right wing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42690146,"author":"gitfan86","month":"2025-01","story":"The Origins of Wokeness","stance":"reference","why":"Obama explaining what woke is","quote":"","text":"You obviously didn't read the article. He calls out how virtue signallers quickly change what the rules are around which word are OK. Here is someone who you may or may not consider to be a far right bad actor explaining what woke is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33356715,"author":"incomingpain","month":"2022-10","story":"The Problem with Polling Might Be Unfixable","stance":"reference","why":"Obama cited as evidence for social pressure on public opinions","quote":"Obama is smart.","text":"They show a general bias toward democrats in the article. However, if you look at 2011 and earlier, it does seem balanced. Only since 2011 is there a problem. This aligns with the democrat shift in political polarization. [LINK] >But what if the problem is that Republican voters are the type of people that don’t talk to pollsters? And the few Republicans that do talk to them are unrepresentative of the party itself? If this were the case, then there would be no clear fix I think it's less this problem, a few paragraphs later they self-refute this point. Polling was relatively reliable before 2009. It's something new, and it's obvious what it is. Republicans vote in their secret ballot differently than they admit to pollsters. Why can they not publicly admit their opinion anymore? [VIDEO] Obama is smart. What I believe happened is social sciences fell on its face. It's unclear to me if STEM excluding social sciences left the field with lesser talent or if the revelations that caused social sciences to collapse bore out STEM. Ultimately this is what spawned jordan peterson. In most cases what he says is just what the social sciences say. The problem is what he says is a tough medicine to take. The democrats would rather stop him from speaking than confront what social sciences say."},{"id":30981367,"author":"incomingpain","month":"2022-04","story":"Heresy","stance":"reference","why":"Obama on call-out culture, as starting point for religion analogy","quote":"Obama is a very smart dude.","text":"[VIDEO] Obama is a very smart dude. 2 years ago he saw it building and he misidentified this as activism. John Mcwhorter I believe better identified this as a religion: [LINK] As an atheist you see the same pattern often. Here's a hour long video of sam harris and john talking about it: [LINK] This new religion has the christians, muslims, atheists, and others all worked up. It's unusual for a new global religion to form, nothing there to really prevent it. The big difference this time is they got really big while remaining undercover. I guess my prediction as well. You could become a preacher for this new religion, set for life to serve. Just need to figure out how to. If you do try this, make sure you are following the golden rule every day. Make sure you're 100% positivity. Try to merge religions, I'm pretty sure 'coexist' is a big part of this."},{"id":30105862,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2022-01","story":"Spotify Removing Neil Young's Music","stance":"reference","why":"Obama's remark applied to criticism of a boycott","quote":"","text":"It is incomprehensible for me to understand why people call this \"principled\". It is bullying in the words of President Obama - \"That's not activism\" [1]. If Neil wants to go and debate, he should show up on JRE and put his points forth. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29688883,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2021-12","story":"A Man from the Future – John von Neumann’s nuclear vision","stance":"reference","why":"Obama on call-out culture, cited to support anti-woke argument","quote":"","text":"This culture, whatever it is, has had a personal impact on me. Many great people that I truly and deeply admired growing up, the reason why I am the person that I am today is because of historical figures. People need to learn how to forgive, look for the best in people and let go of decades old misgivings of a person. They changed the world. They inspired a whole generation. Anything that they did in their personal lives pales in comparison. If you want to go with this route, literally every person's great great grandfather was sexist in 1920s by today's standards. People need to understand the era, the context under which they operated. I am pretty sure my thoughts today are going to be outdated after 50 years. This is a dangerous slippery slope of wokeness that needs to be strongly codemned IMO. It's one thing to raise awareness, another to deflate historical figures because of some shit they said 70 years ago. It is toxic, futile and against your cause. \"That's not activism\" - President Obama: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28927387,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2021-10","story":"Against ‘Diversity Statements’","stance":"reference","why":"Obama criticizing call-out culture, cited as authority against woke ideology","quote":"","text":"President Obama on woke ideology pointing out to what his daughters are learning in our education systems: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28958906,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2021-10","story":"The War over Academic Freedom","stance":"reference","why":"Obama calling call-out culture not activism","quote":"Here is President Obama speaking about this so called wokeness in Academia","text":"\"This is not activism\". Here is President Obama speaking about this so called wokeness in Academia: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28983519,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2021-10","story":"Show HN: Pimp My Readme","stance":"reference","why":"Obama saying call-out culture is not activism","quote":"As President Obama says [1], it is not activism.","text":"The term for this is wokeness. It is typified by overly sensitive, agenda-pushing, irrational behavior that uses offense as a tool to further their political agenda, oppress those who oppose and exemplify eggregious levels of false moral-superiority (and virtue signal to their mob). As President Obama says [1], it is not activism. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26614264,"author":"anonu","month":"2021-03","story":"Addiction to Outrage (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Obama criticizing the super woke generation's stone-casting.","quote":"If all you're doing is casting stones, you're probably not going to get very far","text":"Obama called out the \"super woke\" generation a few years back: [VIDEO] \"If all you're doing is casting stones, you're probably not going to get very far\"..."},{"id":23761797,"author":"Reedx","month":"2020-07","story":"A Letter on Justice and Open Debate","stance":"reference","why":"Obama's 2019 talk on call-out culture.","quote":"That was good, but too late.","text":"Do you have examples? The only one I'm aware of is this talk in 2019: [VIDEO] That was good, but too late. It seemed like he mostly got \"Ok, boomer\" responses from his target."},{"id":23505584,"author":"renaudg","month":"2020-06","story":"Facebook fires employee for publicly scolding a colleague","stance":"reference","why":"Obama on call-out culture not being activism.","quote":"","text":"“I do get a sense sometimes now among certain young people, and this is accelerated by social media, there is this sense sometimes of: ‘The way of me making change is to be as judgmental as possible about other people, and that’s enough.” “Like, if I tweet or hashtag about how you didn’t do something right or used the wrong verb, then I can sit back and feel pretty good about myself, cause, ‘Man, you see how woke I was, I called you out.’” “That’s not activism. That’s not bringing about change. If all you’re doing is casting stones, you’re probably not going to get that far. That’s easy to do.” - Barack Obama [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23552440,"author":"Reedx","month":"2020-06","story":"For black CEOs in Silicon Valley, humiliation is a part of doing business","stance":"reference","why":"Obama warned against enforced lockstep on the left.","quote":"Obama warned about this too, but unfortunately wasn't well heeded","text":"Casting those who question dogma as the other (conservative or \"alt-right\", commonly) is an effective silencing and compliance technique. \"You're not one of us if you don't stay in lockstep\" has a real chilling effect and is doing serious damage to the left. Obama warned about this too, but unfortunately wasn't well heeded: [VIDEO] It's also intellectually dishonest. Most (all?) of the cited authors are liberals. James Lindsay, liberal professor. John McWhorter, liberal professor. I'm pretty sure Jon Haidt and Paul G are liberal-minded. For random commenters on HN, you don't know what their leaning is nor does it automatically mean disqualification. And it's worth noting that John McWhorter specializes in linguistics and has written books on language and race relations. He noticed the religious aspect of this years ago. Here he is on CNN back in 2015 making the point: [LINK]"}]},"uwajp0g-bY4":{"wy":"Cited to show who Go was designed for, often in critiques of the language's simplicity.","m":[{"id":39245582,"author":"wging","month":"2024-02","story":"Let futures be futures","stance":"reference","why":"Rob Pike on who Go is for","quote":"","text":"It's Go. The description fits well, and it's pretty much confirmed by the Rob Pike quote in the sidebar. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41247008,"author":"stephenr","month":"2024-08","story":"Tell HN: Google OAuth consent screen issue could be costing you signups","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It's more likely that the person re-implementing the SSO flow is making a mistake The thread has other people confirming the same behaviour - and the description of the issue (preventing a double-submit of a non-idempotent action) is something most web-focused developers learn in their first year. I don't understand why people assume that the people working at Google/etc are inherently incapable of producing bugs. Go literally invented a new programming language to make their staff less likely to fuck up because they don't have much real world experience: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38334763,"author":"thwora23__","month":"2023-11","story":"Practical nil panic detection for Go","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here you go: [VIDEO] I think Go could still work without completely neutering the type system. Algebraic Data Types and pattern matching are not difficult... python already has them."}]},"wzkAeopX7P0":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as a sound sample when discussing data-over-audio projects that resemble RTTY.","m":[{"id":43177208,"author":"nickcw","month":"2025-02","story":"Ggwave: Tiny Data-over-Sound Library","stance":"reference","why":"What RTTY sounds like, similar bitrate to ggwave.","quote":"Here is what that sounds like","text":"It sounds quite nice. It is also about the same bitrate as RTTY which was invented in 1922 and is still in use by radio amateurs round the world. Here is what that sounds like [VIDEO] Not as musical but quite similar"},{"id":34513962,"author":"hugs","month":"2023-01","story":"Mystery Signal from a Helicopter (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of distinct sound of RTTY digital mode.","quote":"Same with with RTTY","text":"I don't have the skills of the person who decoded the signal... however, if you get your ham radio license and start playing with the various digital modes, you'll start to recognize the various sounds of different modes. An APRS data packet [1] has a very unique sound. Same with with RTTY [2]. Our brains are really good at pattern matching if they're exposed to a pattern enough times. Obviously, you'd still need a computer to decode those signals, but the \"sound\" of the signals can give you very good headstart. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26896231,"author":"tdeck","month":"2021-04","story":"Show HN: I made a tool to communicate data using the PC speaker","stance":"reference","why":"Sound sample of radioteletype modulation.","quote":"Wow it sounds like RTTY (radioteletype)","text":"Wow it sounds like RTTY (radioteletype): [VIDEO] I suppose there are only so many ways to modulate a data signal."}]},"x2ZLS1V3iMw":{"wy":"Cited in threads about sugary drinks, alcohol and advertising as a famous example of a star publicly rejecting a sponsor's product.","q":{"x":"Ronaldo's Coke/water moment gave me a vast amount of respect for him","i":42648804,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42648804,"author":"ANewFormation","month":"2025-01","story":"Collection: More Doctors Smoke Camels","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Ronaldo's Coke/water moment gave me a vast amount of respect for him","text":"I don't understand why you think there would have been 0 evidence. The countless respiratory illnesses associated with smoking are readily obvious by taking even a small sample of longterm smokers - a decent chunk of them will sound like smokers, be occasionally hacking up a lung, and so on. People obviously knew smoking was unhealthy, but chose to do so anyhow. And companies naturally worked to trt to strengthen that cognitive dissonance. Same thing today, but it's slightly more subtle. For instance in a typical Coke ad you'll generally see people that look nothing like regular Coke drinkers - health, fitness, and good body weights abounds. Ronaldo's Coke/water moment gave me a vast amount of respect for him - [VIDEO] It's how the sort of people Coke gets to pose with their product actually feel about it."},{"id":36578398,"author":"paganel","month":"2023-07","story":"Against Alcohol","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"For me good alternatives are good coffee and plain or sparkling water. About this latter choice I have to give an unironical shootout to /hydrohomies and to Cristiano Ronaldo [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48067832,"author":"merely-unlikely","month":"2026-05","story":"I Want to Live Like Costco People","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> you can drink Coke, too I drink water. Which means I'm just like Cristiano Ronaldo[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"zicGxU5MfwE":{"wy":"Used mainly as a political reference in arguments over what Trump actually said and in criticism of him, not as something to learn from.","m":[{"id":43577198,"author":"mcoliver","month":"2025-04","story":"US Administration announces 34% tariffs on China, 20% on EU","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Watch the whole thing for yourself","text":"Watch the whole thing for yourself [VIDEO] He talks about light for the first two things (cover the body and put it under the skin) and then switches to disinfectant. Was clearly talking about some kind of chemical to \"clean the lungs\" like you would clean you bathtub."},{"id":28480672,"author":"loonster","month":"2021-09","story":"Biden to mandate coronavirus vaccine for federal workers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"He spoke about putting UV light and disinfectants inside the body.","text":"He never talked about putting bleach in your veins. He spoke about putting UV light and disinfectants inside the body. Both are obscure, but real treatments. Edit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28174491,"author":"camhart","month":"2021-08","story":"WHO inquiry chief 'had concerns' about lab close to first Covid cases","stance":"reference","why":"Used to argue Trump only said officials would look into it.","quote":"Watch the video for yourself (and take note at the video title)","text":"He said they were going to look into it. He didn't break trust with your example because he didn't make a statement of fact that it was a cure. He said they'd look into it. Now if all you do is read the title of articles or videos from left leaning news site, you may walk away with the belief that he's telling people to inject it. But he's not. He says they're going to look into it. Watch the video for yourself (and take note at the video title): [VIDEO] . I want to add--I'm in no way indicating the right leaning news sites don't do the same. They've both screwed the country up by failing to report news without some bias slapped all over everything."},{"id":44219199,"author":"strangattractor","month":"2025-06","story":"Self-hosting your own media considered harmful according to YouTube","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Do you remember him suggesting to drink bleach or exposing yourself to UV? [0] Let's say for example the President keeps his yap shut about hydroxychloroquine or drinking bleach [0] etc and 10 people listening to Alex Jones die taking it because they are frightened. Now lets say POTUS goes on televised News and suggest doing dangerous things to 100 times the size of Alex Jones's audience and we end up with 1000 needless deaths of people that may not get COVID anyway. I expect Alex Jones to be an irresponsible sleaze bag but POTUS should have some restraint/intelligence. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43675535,"author":"jonplackett","month":"2025-04","story":"Trump exempts phones, computers, chips from ‘reciprocal’ tariffs","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The guy is a full on moron who thinks he is a full on genius.","text":"I beg to differ. Quote from Trump: “I see the disinfectant that knocks it out in a minute ... is there a way we can do something like that by injection inside, or almost a cleaning?\" \"Because you see it gets inside the lungs and it does a tremendous number on the lungs, so it would be interesting to check that” [LINK] Edit: found the actual video. Enjoy! [VIDEO] The guy is a full on moron who thinks he is a full on genius."}]},"zn7-fVtT16k":{"wy":"Shared as a joke in threads about Einstein, physics and famous scientists.","m":[{"id":33700865,"author":"thinky_thoughts","month":"2022-11","story":"Albert Einstein vs. Robert Oppenheimer (1933)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I find their rap battle performances more compelling","text":"I find their rap battle performances more compelling: Einstein (v Stephen Hawking): [VIDEO] Oppenheimer (v Thanos): [LINK]"},{"id":31162195,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2022-04","story":"Yo-Yo Ma Performs Richard Feynman’s Ode to the Wonder of Life, Animated","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This one is funny, if anyone hasn't seen it","text":"This one is funny, if anyone hasn't seen it: Albert Einstein vs Stephen Hawking. Epic Rap Battles of History [VIDEO]"},{"id":19032177,"author":"j1vms","month":"2019-01","story":"The Man Who Invented Information Theory (2017)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"here's Einstein vs. Hawking (120 million views)","text":"It's probably the case that there are no scientists besides maybe Einstein (E = mc^2) and Newton (apple falling from the tree -> gravity) that the general public would be able to identify by name and refer to one thing that very roughly approximates what they did. Who knows though, given the public's enthusiasm for modern tech and its stars (Jobs, Musk, Gates, Bezos, etc.) maybe one day scientists' work might make a \"Casey's Top 40\"-like popular countdown if presented the right way! Till then there's always Epic Rap Battles of History, here's Einstein vs. Hawking (120 million views): [VIDEO]"}]}});
